                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "About this record: the course was written by AI agents from a source dossier of 342 entries compiled for it, in three rounds of research and writing, then rewritten for this release; the working notes, the dossier, the chapters as written and the scripts that recorded the examples are kept with the course's sources, not in the library. This record condenses them: the sources below are those the steps cite, and the activities say how the course was checked."@en ,
                 "The course was checked by machine (the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators, its course and Markdown checks) and by AI agents against the cited sources and the recorded runs; it has had no human review."@en ,
                 "Sources: every step was written from the sources its draft cites, 248 documents in all: the Solid specifications (the Solid Protocol, WAC, ACP, Solid-OIDC, the WebID profile, notifications, type indexes and others), the W3C standards around RDF, IETF RFCs, the servers' and libraries' documentation and code, governance records, papers and articles. Each source's comment below quotes the evidence for its licence, says what the course took from it, if anything, and gives how much weight the dossier says it can carry and what it was used for; most were read on 2026-10-07, and their licences on 2026-10-08. The facts that change with new versions were checked again, all of them, in the third round, and the course says \"as of October 2026\" where a fact may date."@en ,
                 "Structure: 17 chapters of 193 steps in all. Each step is a short theory text, with code, requests and tables where they help, and one or more multiple-choice questions on it; each chapter has review questions (101 in all) that combine its steps and are asked only in its final review. Every question has one correct option (the card's back) and three wrong options (its distractors) of similar length and form, each with a note on why it is wrong. Questions that show code have the code in the question; an option that is code has wrong options that are code too."@en ,
                 "Selection: the course as planned: why Solid exists, linked data, RDF and its syntaxes, semantics and vocabularies, pods and containers, reading and writing resources, identity and authentication, Web Access Control, sharing with ACP and app permissions, notifications, data-oriented design and interoperability, building Solid apps, security and backends, a minimal app read piece by piece, Solid Memo as a worked example, Solid's difficulties and open problems, and Linked Web Storage. Its drafts' hands-on parts (a lab pod, exercises run in a terminal, and a capstone project with a starter and a checker) are left out, since a course in the library has to stand on its own: what they showed is given as examples recorded on a server, and the capstone was dropped."@en ,
                 "Licensing: the course is CC0 1.0, like most of the library. The dedication covers what is the compiler's own: the theory, questions, options and notes, which state the facts of the sources in the course's own words, the examples written for the course, and the excerpts of Solid Memo's code in Chapter 15, whose copyright is the compiler's. Each source's comment below quotes the evidence for its licence and says what the course took from it; most were used to verify facts only. What the course took is of three kinds. First, code and examples from sources whose licences let them be republished: the Solid terms vocabulary and the early Solid specification repository, both CC0; the Solid Protocol editor's draft, whose code snippets are CC0; and Solid Community Group reports, whose contributors grant everyone a licence to reproduce, adapt, distribute and sublicense them under the W3C Community Contributor License Agreement. Second, a few lines of what Community Solid Server 7.2.0 and node-solid-server 6.0.0 wrote and answered in the recorded runs (Steps 6.2, 8.6, 12.3 and 14.13, among others), both servers MIT-licensed and the lines not a substantial portion of either. Third, short passages, each with its source named in the text, quoted for teaching as the right of quotation allows (Berne Convention article 10(1); 22 § of the Swedish Copyright Act), among them three examples abridged from the Linked Web Storage drafts; these stay their authors' and are not part of the dedication."@en .

<#recorded-runs>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-08T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://github.com/CommunitySolidServer/CommunitySolidServer/releases/tag/v7.2.0> ,
              <https://github.com/nodeSolidServer/node-solid-server/releases/tag/v6.0.0> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    rdfs:label "Recorded runs on Community Solid Server 7.2"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the requests, responses and server behaviour the course shows as recorded (\"On CSS 7.2 …\"), run by scripts on 2026-10-07 and 2026-10-08 against a local Community Solid Server 7.2.0, mostly, and node-solid-server 6.0.0 for comparison, with the libraries the steps name (such as @inrupt/solid-client 4.0.0). Some examples leave out headers that do not matter to the step, or write the local server's address as https://pod.example/. The scripts and their full transcripts are kept with the course's sources, not in the library."@en ,
                 "Runs: Primary observations made 2026-10-07; http WebIDs and a local CSS; WAC-Allow on GET; N3 Patch with blank nodes; NSS 6, ETag and If-Match; Notifications JSON-LD context URLs; A minimal app end to end on CSS 7.2; Chapter 14's notes app on CSS 7.2; The notes app on NSS 6 and on CSS 7.2 with ACP; The Try-it list for Chapters 5-10 and 13; Solid Notes, round 3: npm test on CSS 7.2 with WAC and ACP; Solid Notes, round 3: browser run (Playwright 1.63.0); Chapter 14 social Your-turn solutions on CSS 7.2; A linked external WebID on two CSS 7.2 servers; CSS 7.2 with a pod quota; Chapter 12 Try-its (Steps 12.3 and 12.4) on CSS 7.2; Snippet tests: the chapters' code run verbatim; Umai and Media Kraken against a CSS 7.2 pod (Step 12.14); Offline Try-its of Steps 3.7, 3.8 and 4.10; Every Try-it box of Chapters 5-10, 13 and 16 on CSS 7.2; Social Try-its on CSS 7.2 (Steps 9.7-9.10); First-hand HTTP check of https://shapetrees.org/TR/specification/; Chapter 5 Try-it transcript on CSS 7.2; Chapter 6 Try-it on CSS 7.2; File naming and formats on NSS 6; Chapter 7 login and profile tests on CSS 7.2; Owner lock-out and acl:agentGroup on CSS 7.2; Chapter 10 Try-it transcript on CSS 7.2; Local check of the safeFetch sketch; Owner rescue on two CSS 7.2 configs; ACP sharing by insert-only N3 Patch on CSS 7.2."@en ,
                 "Outcome: the transcripts are the evidence for every example the course gives as recorded, and the release review (review 4) compared the examples with them. Other servers, or other versions, may behave otherwise, and the course marks such behaviour with ⚠."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the whole release, with the library checker (npm run library:check), which validates it against Solid Memo's shapes (library deck format 5, card format 5, chapter, step and distractor format 1), DCAT-AP and SKOS, with the reference data, checks the course's outline by its rules and its Markdown by the rules for a release."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The release conforms to every shape, profile and rule the checker applies to it."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-07T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Completeness (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the drafted chapters, their research notes and the dossier, reviewed by an AI agent for whether a beginner who completes the course could build a Solid app, and for contradictions, broken references and unsupported claims; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: every citation resolved in the dossier. Missing topics (sharing as a workflow, the session lifecycle, security, working code), contradictions and broken cross-references were found; the second round of writing addressed them."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-07T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Second review and primary-source checks (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the revised chapters, reviewed again by an AI agent with the same question, and the claims the first review doubted checked against the primary sources and on local servers; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: five contradictions or stale claims were found, and that the minimal app did not exercise everything the later chapters teach; the third round of writing addressed them, and its review found the gaps the second had named closed."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-08T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Third review and version recheck (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: every chapter, the dossier and the recorded runs, reviewed a third time by an AI agent; every version, date and address the course gives as of October 2026 re-read from the live page, registry or repository on 2026-10-07; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: two dates had moved and two statements were made more exact; two small correctness problems were found and fixed in the text, each fix checked against the transcripts and steps it concerned."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-08T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Release text: facts, questions and examples (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: each chapter as rewritten for this release (hands-on parts turned into recorded examples, questions made to stand alone), reviewed by an AI agent against the recorded transcripts, the research notes and the chapter as drafted; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: the findings (such as unqualified version details, examples that did not match their transcript, options whose length or wording gave the answer away, and notes that relied on another chapter) were fixed in the text, or kept with a reason, and recorded in the course's working notes."@en .

