# Chapter 1: Why Solid.
<#ch-why-solid>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    dcterms:title "Why Solid"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Why Solid exists: the data-silo problem, the idea of pods with apps as views on them, where Solid came from and who shapes it today. Ends with what a Solid app is made of and which spec governs each piece."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-why-solid-r01> ,
                              <#q-why-solid-r02> ,
                              <#q-why-solid-r03> ,
                              <#q-why-solid-r04> ,
                              <#q-why-solid-r05> ,
                              <#q-why-solid-r06> .

<#ch-why-solid-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-why-solid> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Data silos and the coupled stack

Think of a typical web app, say a photo-sharing site. One company runs the user interface, the database, the login system and the rules about who may see what. Your photos and tags live in that company's database, in its own schema, behind its own login. An early Solid paper, a demonstration at the WWW 2016 conference, called services built this way "data silos": each app controls its own data and often has its own login and access control.

The paper names three consequences:

1. Users cannot easily switch to a similar app and keep their data.
2. Users cannot move their data from one storage service to another.
3. Developers are tied to one platform's API, so writing an app that works across platforms is hard.

The W3C charter for Linked Web Storage (2024) describes the same problem: the storage, how you log in, who may access what, and the app provider are coupled, so changing one means changing all of them, "sometimes at the price of all past data". Solid's starting point is to pull these pieces apart."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-why-solid-1a> ,
                         <#q-why-solid-1b> .

<#q-why-solid-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Maya wants to leave her photo app for a better one, but her albums and tags exist only in the first app's database. Which feature of the coupled web-app stack causes this?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "One company controls both the app and her data"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "In a coupled stack the app provider also owns the database, so leaving the app means leaving the data behind."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-1a-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Photo files are too large to move between services"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The problem is who controls the data and its format, not file size."@en .

<#q-why-solid-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The new app is written in another programming language"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The language an app is written in does not stop it reading data it can reach."@en .

<#q-why-solid-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Her photos are locked by the first app's encryption"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Even unencrypted, the data sits in a database only that company controls; the obstacle is control, not encryption."@en .

<#q-why-solid-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "From a developer's point of view, what does the \"data silo\" model of web apps, the problem Solid sets out to solve, make hard?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Writing one app that works with data from many platforms"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Each silo exposes its own API, so a cross-platform app needs a separate integration for every platform."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-1b-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Keeping user data private from other users of the same app"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Each silo already controls who sees what inside its walls; the hard part is crossing between silos."@en .

<#q-why-solid-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Storing app data in a relational database"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Silos make that easy; it is exactly what each silo does."@en .

<#q-why-solid-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Hosting an app's front end on a content delivery network"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Front-end hosting has nothing to do with where user data is locked up."@en .

<#ch-why-solid-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-why-solid> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Separation creates choice

When one company holds both your data and the app, switching apps is expensive: you lose your history, your contacts, your settings. Splitting data, apps and identity across independent providers, instead of one company, is what Solid means by **decentralisation**.

Ruben Verborgh, a long-time Solid contributor, argues that in the app-centric model companies compete on how much user data they collect, which builds dependency and switching costs. If data and apps are separated, "separate competitions for storage and apps emerge". Tim Berners-Lee made the same market argument in 2009: separating storage from apps "separates the market for storage from the market for applications", which should stimulate both. In 2019 Berners-Lee also criticised ad-based models that reward clickbait; an app that does not hold your data can no longer compete by hoarding it.

So decentralisation is not only about privacy. Verborgh puts it as "enabling choice, by breaking up artificially coupled decisions into individual options": you pick your storage, your apps and your identity separately."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-why-solid-2a> ,
                         <#q-why-solid-2b> .

<#q-why-solid-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "In the argument for Solid, why would separating data storage from apps create more competition?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Storage and apps can each be chosen and replaced separately"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Once data does not belong to the app, users can switch apps without losing data and switch storage without losing apps, so each market competes on its own."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-2a-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Users can install more apps on the same device"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "How many apps fit on a device has nothing to do with it; separation means every app works on the storage you chose."@en .

<#q-why-solid-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Apps no longer store any data, so they cost less to build"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Apps still read and write data; they just do it in the user's storage."@en .

<#q-why-solid-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "One provider can then offer storage and apps more cheaply together"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is the coupled model the argument wants to move away from."@en .

<#q-why-solid-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A colleague says, \"Solid's decentralisation is just a privacy feature.\" What is the better summary of the Solid view?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It is mainly about choice: you pick each part yourself"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Privacy matters, but the core argument is about breaking up coupled decisions so users can choose storage, apps and identity independently."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-2b-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It is mainly about speed: many small servers load faster"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Performance is not the argument; spreading data across servers can even make it slower."@en .

<#q-why-solid-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It is mainly about cost: users stop paying for online services"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Someone still has to host storage; the point is that you choose who."@en .

<#q-why-solid-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It is mainly about anonymity: users never reveal who they are"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid apps usually know who you are, because you log in with an identity."@en .

<#ch-why-solid-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-why-solid> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Portability is not interoperability

Isn't this already solved by law? In the EU, Article 20 of the GDPR gives people the right to receive their personal data "in a structured, commonly used and machine-readable format", and to have it sent directly to another company "where technically feasible".

But that is a **one-off export**: you download a file, the new app may or may not understand it, and every later change has to be exported again.

Solid aims for **interoperability**. The data stays in one place, the user's storage, and many apps read and write it there, using shared data formats (RDF, Chapters 2-3) and shared protocols. If a calendar app and a to-do app understand the same data format, both work on the same appointment. When one updates it, the other sees the change the next time it reads the data, or at once if it subscribes to notifications (Chapter 10).

| Portability (GDPR Art. 20) | Interoperability (Solid's goal) |
|---|---|
| Copy data out, once | Data stays put |
| Each app keeps its own copy | Many apps share one copy |
| Format must be "machine-readable" | Format and protocol are shared standards |"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-why-solid-3a> .

<#q-why-solid-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Why is a data export under Article 20 of the EU's GDPR (data portability) not enough to give users the benefits Solid aims for?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Each app ends up with its own copy, and the copies drift apart"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Portability gives you a snapshot; Solid wants many apps to work on one copy of the data where it lives."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-3a-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Exports are not machine-readable, so no app can import them"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Article 20 requires a structured, commonly used and machine-readable format."@en .

<#q-why-solid-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Exports work, but only between apps from the same company"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Article 20 allows sending the data directly to another company where technically feasible."@en .

<#q-why-solid-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An export includes the app's code, which the new app cannot run"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "An export holds your personal data, not the app's software."@en .

<#ch-why-solid-4>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-why-solid> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## The Solid idea: pods and apps as views

Solid's answer is to give each person a **pod**: a personal online data store. The 2016 paper puts it this way: users' data is managed independently of the apps that create and use it; each user stores data in a pod, "can have one or more pods from different pod providers, and can easily switch between providers". That is the design goal; in practice moving a pod is still hard (Step 1.9).

Apps then become **views** on that data. Verborgh describes apps as "views on top of our data pod and those of others". An app reads and writes data where it lives, but only the parts the user has allowed. The Solid website explains this with a rucksack: your storage is like a rucksack with separate pockets, and each app can open only the pockets you allow. The Solid website also lists "AI agents" among those you can let read and edit your data: an AI agent is one more app asking to open a pocket.

The name comes from **So**cial **Li**nked **D**ata. "Linked Data" is the way the data is written so that different apps can understand it, the topic of Chapter 2."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-why-solid-4a> ,
                         <#q-why-solid-4b> .

<#q-why-solid-4a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid, what does it mean to call an app a \"view\" on your data?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The app works on the data in your pod, not its own copy"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The data lives in the pod and outlives any one app; apps are interchangeable windows onto it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-4a-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-4a-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-4a-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-4a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The app can only display data and may never change it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid apps can write data too, if the user grants write access."@en .

<#q-why-solid-4a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The app is a web page rather than an installed mobile program"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A view in this sense can be any kind of app, web or native."@en .

<#q-why-solid-4a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The app downloads your whole pod once and then works offline"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Apps read the parts they are allowed, where the data lives."@en .

<#q-why-solid-4b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "The Solid website compares your pod to a rucksack with separate pockets. What do the pockets stand for?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Parts of your data you can open to some apps but not others"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Access is granted per part of your data, so each app only reaches the pockets you open for it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-4b-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-4b-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-4b-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-4b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Separate pod providers that each keep a full copy of your data"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Pockets are parts of one storage, not duplicate providers."@en .

<#q-why-solid-4b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Different file formats that Solid can store side by side"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The metaphor is about permission, not formats."@en .

<#q-why-solid-4b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Backups that protect your data if one of them is lost"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The pockets illustrate selective access, not redundancy."@en .

<#ch-why-solid-5>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-why-solid> ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Origins: older ideas, hard adoption

The ideas are older than the name. In August 2009 Berners-Lee wrote two notes in **Design Issues**, his personal series of notes on web architecture. **Read-Write Linked Data** recalled that the web was meant as "a collaborative space", described writing data with HTTP, and noted that "one does not want a data wiki in which anyone can write". **Socially Aware Cloud Storage** proposed separating apps from storage, identifying people with URLs, and controlling who may access what.

The work took shape at MIT, in the **Crosscloud** project, with the Qatar Computing Research Institute; the 2016 demonstration paper came out of it. That paper listed open questions, such as how apps agree on what data means and how they notify each other of changes. In this course's assessment, agreeing on data formats is still hard today (Chapters 4 and 16).

Solid was not the first attempt. The 2016 paper cites Diaspora, Musubi and WebBox, and notes that "none of these proposals has been widely adopted yet". Good ideas are not enough; adoption is hard."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-why-solid-5a> ,
                         <#q-why-solid-5b> .

<#q-why-solid-5a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Which idea did Tim Berners-Lee's 2009 Design Issues note \"Socially Aware Cloud Storage\" propose, foreshadowing Solid?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Keep users' data in storage that is separate from the apps"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The note proposed separating apps from storage, identifying people with URLs and controlling who may access what, which became Solid's core idea."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-5a-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-5a-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-5a-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-5a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Let anyone write to any data on the web, like a wiki"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "His \"Read-Write Linked Data\" note of the same month says one does not want a data wiki anyone can write to."@en .

<#q-why-solid-5a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Give each app its own private database for better privacy"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is the data silo the note wanted to move away from."@en .

<#q-why-solid-5a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Replace HTTP with a new protocol designed for writing data"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "His 2009 notes reuse HTTP for writing data."@en .

<#q-why-solid-5b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Diaspora, Musubi and WebBox were earlier decentralised-data projects that a 2016 Solid paper cites. What does their history suggest for Solid?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Technical design alone does not guarantee that people adopt it"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The 2016 paper notes that none of these proposals had been widely adopted yet, a reminder that Solid must win users, not just be well designed."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-5b-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-5b-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-5b-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-5b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Decentralised systems are impossible to build with web technology"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Those systems were built; the problem was adoption, not feasibility."@en .

<#q-why-solid-5b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Solid is a renamed version of those three projects"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The 2016 paper cites them as earlier, separate attempts."@en .

<#q-why-solid-5b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Social networks cannot store data outside a company's servers"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The projects show it can be done; they just were not widely used."@en .

<#ch-why-solid-6>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-why-solid> ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Who's who: Inrupt, the W3C Solid CG, the ODI and LWS

Anyone can start a W3C **Community Group (CG)**, and the reports it publishes are not standards. Only a chartered **Working Group (WG)** can take a document along the **standards track** to a **W3C Recommendation**, which is what a W3C Standard is.

| Body | Since | Role |
|---|---|---|
| **Inrupt** | 2018 | A company co-founded by Berners-Lee that sells Solid software, such as the Enterprise Solid Server (ESS) |
| **W3C Solid Community Group (CG)** | Oct 2018 | Writes and maintains the Solid specifications as CG reports |
| **Open Data Institute (ODI)** | Oct 2024 | Solid's steward: organisational home, events, roadmap and adoption |
| **W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) Working Group** | Sep 2024 | Puts Solid's ideas on the standards track |

The Solid Protocol 0.11.0 says plainly that "it is not a W3C Standard nor is it on the W3C Standards Track". The LWS Working Group uses it as its main input. As of October 2026 its LWS Protocol 1.0 is a Working Draft. In practice you build against the Solid Protocol stack today, which existing servers implement, and watch LWS as the standard to come (Chapter 17)."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-why-solid-6a> ,
                         <#q-why-solid-6b> .

<#q-why-solid-6a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A friend says, \"Solid is Inrupt's product.\" What is the more accurate description?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Solid is open specs; Inrupt sells one implementation"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Solid is open specs plus a community, stewarded by the Open Data Institute; Inrupt is one company among several implementers."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-6a-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-6a-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-6a-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-6a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Solid is Inrupt's spec; other vendors license it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The specs are written in a W3C Community Group and are open to everyone."@en .

<#q-why-solid-6a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Solid is an ODI product; Inrupt resells it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The ODI is a steward, not a product owner, and the specs are open."@en .

<#q-why-solid-6a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Solid is a W3C Standard; Inrupt hosts it for the W3C"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The specs are Community Group reports, not W3C Standards, and many servers are not Inrupt's."@en .

<#q-why-solid-6b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "As of 2026, your manager will adopt only specs on the W3C standards track. Which group's documents should you follow for Solid's ideas?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The LWS Working Group"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Only a chartered W3C Working Group produces standards-track documents; for Solid's ideas that is the Linked Web Storage WG, chartered in 2024."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-6b-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-6b-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-6b-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-6b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The Solid Community Group"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Community Groups publish reports, but not on the standards track."@en .

<#q-why-solid-6b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The Open Data Institute"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The ODI is Solid's steward, not a standards body."@en .

<#q-why-solid-6b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Inrupt's spec team"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A company cannot put documents on the W3C standards track on its own."@en .

<#ch-why-solid-7>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-why-solid> ;
    schema:position 6 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Built from web standards

Solid does not invent a new internet. It reuses ordinary web standards:

- **HTTP** to read and write data (GET, PUT, POST, PATCH, DELETE),
- **URIs** to name people, documents and the things in them,
- **RDF** (Linked Data) as the shared data format,
- **OpenID Connect** for login, through the Solid-OIDC spec.

Login is the part that changed most. The 2016 platform used WebID-TLS, which relied on certificates in the browser; Solid later moved to Solid-OIDC.

Why reuse? Any HTTP client, web server or browser can already read and write pod data, and developers start from skills they already have. It is also why Solid is not built on a blockchain (more on blockchains in Step 1.9). The Solid Protocol "connects a set of specifications" into one stack; Step 1.12 maps them."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-why-solid-7a> .

<#q-why-solid-7a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Why does Solid build on HTTP, URIs and RDF instead of a new technology stack?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Existing web tools and apps already speak them"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Building on the existing web keeps the bar low for developers and lets Solid data be reached with ordinary web requests."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-7a-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-7a-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-7a-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-7a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "RDF and HTTP are faster than a purpose-built protocol"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The choice is about reach and reuse, not speed."@en .

<#q-why-solid-7a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Pods run in the browser, so only web tech works"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Pods are servers, and Node.js apps and scripts use them too; reuse is a design choice."@en .

<#q-why-solid-7a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Web standards make every pod's data public by default"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "HTTP serves private data behind access control too; reuse is about reach."@en .

<#ch-why-solid-8>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-why-solid> ;
    schema:position 7 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## A first look at a profile

A **WebID** is a URL that names you, such as `https://alice.example/profile/card#me`. Fetching it gives your profile document (Chapter 2 explains the `#me`).

Here is a small profile in Turtle, written with the terms of today's Solid WebID Profile and Solid-OIDC specs:

```turtle
@prefix foaf:  <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/> .
@prefix pim:   <http://www.w3.org/ns/pim/space#> .
@prefix solid: <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#> .

<https://alice.example/profile/card#me>
    a foaf:Person ;
    foaf:name "Alice" ;
    pim:storage <https://alice.example/> ;
    solid:oidcIssuer <https://idp.example/> .
```

It says Alice is a person, links to her storage with `pim:storage`, and names the server she logs in with through `solid:oidcIssuer`. Profiles usually link to storage this way, but not all do: on Community Solid Server (CSS) 7.2, as of October 2026, a freshly created pod's profile has no `pim:storage` triple, and apps find the storage another way (Step 5.5). Finding a pod by following links from a profile goes back to the 2016 platform; these exact terms came later. Don't worry about the syntax yet: Chapter 3 teaches it, and Chapter 7 covers login."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-why-solid-8a> .

<#q-why-solid-8a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app knows only Alice's WebID, such as `https://alice.example/profile/card#me`. How does it usually find where her data is stored?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It fetches her profile and follows its storage link"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The profile is an ordinary RDF document on the web, and `pim:storage` typically links from it to Alice's pod; where it is missing, as on CSS 7.2 in 2026, the app walks up the path instead."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-8a-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-8a-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-8a-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-8a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It asks a central Solid registry for her pod address"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "There is no central registry; the profile itself links to the storage."@en .

<#q-why-solid-8a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It looks up her pod address on a public blockchain"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid uses HTTP and RDF documents, not a blockchain."@en .

<#q-why-solid-8a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It assumes her data is on the app's own default server"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The user chooses the pod provider, so the app must discover it."@en .

<#ch-why-solid-9>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-why-solid> ;
    schema:position 8 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Common misconceptions

Two mix-ups were covered in Step 1.6: Solid is not Inrupt, and it is not yet a W3C standard. Three more come up often.

**"Solid is Web3."** No. Solid uses HTTP, URIs, RDF and OpenID Connect. In 2022, asked whether he aligned with Web3, Berners-Lee answered "Nope", and said of building such systems on a blockchain: "it just doesn't work".

**"Solid means running a server at home."** No. Pods can be hosted by any provider, and the Flemish government makes pods available to its citizens. Self-hosting is possible, not required. Switching providers is a design goal, but moving a pod is still hard in practice (Chapter 16).

**"Data in a pod is public."** No. A pod holds private data too, and you decide which people, apps and agents may read or edit each part (Chapters 8-9)."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-why-solid-9a> ,
                         <#q-why-solid-9b> .

<#q-why-solid-9a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Sam wants to try Solid but has no spare computer to run a server. What should you tell him?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "He can get a pod from a hosting provider; self-hosting is optional"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Solid separates the choice of storage from the choice of apps; a hosted pod is a normal choice."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-9a-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-9a-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-9a-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-9a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "He needs a home server, because pod data must stay on his device"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Pods can be hosted by any provider; the data need not be on your device."@en .

<#q-why-solid-9a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "He must buy a pod from Inrupt, the only official provider"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "There are several providers and servers, and none is the \"official\" one."@en .

<#q-why-solid-9a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "He can join a blockchain network that stores pods for everyone"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid pods are web servers, not entries on a blockchain."@en .

<#q-why-solid-9b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Lena stores her medical notes in her Solid pod. Which statement is right?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Only the people and apps she grants access to can read them"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A pod is not a public website: each part of it can be opened only to the people, apps and agents its owner chooses. Access control governs requests to the pod, though, so the provider that stores the data must still be trusted."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-9b-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-9b-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-9b-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-9b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Anyone who knows the notes' URL can read them, as on any web page"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A URL names the data; the server still checks who may read it."@en .

<#q-why-solid-9b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Her pod provider decides which apps may read them"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The provider stores the data; Lena decides who gets access."@en .

<#q-why-solid-9b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "They must be encrypted on her phone before Solid can store them"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid protects data with access control on the server; it does not require client-side encryption."@en .

<#ch-why-solid-10>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-why-solid> ;
    schema:position 9 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Solid in the real world

Solid is used beyond research, mainly in pilots and public-sector projects:

- **Flanders (Belgium).** The government created **Athumi**, a public company that acts as a "utility company for data". According to Inrupt's case study, it makes pods available to the region's 6.5 million citizens, running on Inrupt's ESS. Uses include sharing diplomas with recruiters or only selected payroll data with a landlord.
- **BBC.** Inrupt's case study describes the **BBC Together + Data Pod** from BBC Research and Development, a "watch party" for iPlayer: detailed viewing data stayed in each user's pod, the BBC could not see it unless the user gave permission, and the pods ran on Inrupt's ESS.
- **NHS Greater Manchester.** The ODI lists NHS pilots in Greater Manchester among Solid's real-world projects.

A caution: these accounts come from Inrupt, the vendor, or the ODI, the steward. They are case studies, not independent evaluations. Several flagship projects also run on one vendor's server, an uncomfortable fit for a project about avoiding lock-in. In principle the data could move to another conforming server; in practice that is hard (Step 1.9)."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-why-solid-10a> ,
                         <#q-why-solid-10b> .

<#q-why-solid-10a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "In Flanders (Belgium), the public company Athumi makes Solid pods available to the region's citizens. What does this deployment show about who can host pods?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "An organisation such as a government can host pods for many people"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A \"personal\" data store does not have to be self-hosted; a trusted organisation can host it while the person controls access."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-10a-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-10a-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-10a-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-10a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Hosting by a government means the government controls access"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The host stores the data; the person decides who may see it."@en .

<#q-why-solid-10a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Pods only work when each citizen runs their own server at home"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Athumi makes pods available to Flanders' 6.5 million citizens."@en .

<#q-why-solid-10a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Pods can only hold public data, not personal records like diplomas"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Flanders uses pods for personal data such as diplomas and payroll."@en .

<#q-why-solid-10b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "How should you treat the published Solid success stories from Flanders and the BBC, written by Inrupt or the Open Data Institute?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "As case studies by interested parties, not neutral tests"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Case studies by an interested party are useful evidence that Solid is used, but not neutral proof that it works well."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-10b-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-10b-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-10b-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-10b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "As peer-reviewed studies proving Solid improves privacy"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "They are published by Inrupt or the ODI, not peer-reviewed research."@en .

<#q-why-solid-10b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "As proof that Solid is ready for any production use"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "They describe particular pilots, written by interested parties; they do not show readiness everywhere."@en .

<#q-why-solid-10b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "As official W3C test reports showing which servers conform"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "They are case studies, not conformance results."@en .

<#ch-why-solid-11>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-why-solid> ;
    schema:position 10 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Anatomy of a Solid app

A Solid app is an ordinary web or Node.js application built from three pieces:

1. **An authenticated `fetch`.** The user logs in at the identity provider named in their profile (Step 1.8); the app gets a `fetch` that proves who the user is on every request, using **tokens** (short-lived proofs of who the user is; Solid-OIDC, Chapter 7).
2. **Plain HTTP to the pod.** The app reads and writes documents with GET, PUT, POST, PATCH and DELETE.
3. **An RDF library** that turns Linked Data into values for your code.

Usually there is **no backend database** for user data: it stays in the pod, where any app the user allows can open it. (Backends that act on their own are Chapter 13.)

```text
 Your app --login--> Identity provider --tokens--> Your app
 Your app --authenticated HTTP--> Pod server --RDF/files--> Your app
```

The **identity provider** issues tokens; the **pod server** stores the data and checks who may access it. One product often plays both roles, and the user picks the provider, not you."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-why-solid-11a> ,
                         <#q-why-solid-11b> .

<#q-why-solid-11a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "In a typical Solid app, where does the user's data live?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "In the user's pod, read and written over HTTP"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A Solid app works directly on the pod over HTTP, so any other app the user authorises can open the same data."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-11a-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-11a-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-11a-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-11a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In the app's own database, synced to the pod nightly"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid apps usually have no backend database of their own for user data; they work on the pod directly."@en .

<#q-why-solid-11a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In the identity provider, next to the user's login"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The identity provider issues tokens; it does not store app data."@en .

<#q-why-solid-11a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In the browser's localStorage, uploaded on logout"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The pod is where the data lives; apps read and write it there over HTTP."@en .

<#q-why-solid-11b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "What does the identity provider contribute to a Solid app?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Tokens that prove who the user is to the pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The identity provider authenticates the user and issues tokens; the pod server checks those tokens against its access rules."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-11b-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-11b-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-11b-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-11b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The list of documents stored in the user's pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The pod server stores the documents and lists them; the identity provider only logs the user in."@en .

<#q-why-solid-11b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The access rules for each of the user's documents"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The pod server stores and checks the access rules."@en .

<#q-why-solid-11b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A cached copy of the user's data for faster reads"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The identity provider holds no pod data; it only authenticates."@en .

<#ch-why-solid-12>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-why-solid> ;
    schema:position 11 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## The specs behind each piece

Each piece of a Solid app follows a specification, and the **Solid Protocol** ties them together. The map below shows which spec governs each piece, its latest version as of October 2026 with its status, and where the course teaches it. None of the Solid specs is a W3C Standard: they are Community Group reports or drafts.

| Piece | Spec (latest version and status, October 2026) | Chapter |
|---|---|---|
| The data: Linked Data and RDF | RDF 1.1 (W3C Recommendation, 2014) | 2-4 |
| Pods, containers, reading and writing over HTTP | Solid Protocol 0.11.0 (Draft CG Report) | 5, 6 |
| Your identity document | Solid WebID Profile 1.0.0 (Draft CG Report, not under /TR) | 7 |
| Logging in, the authenticated `fetch` | Solid-OIDC 0.1.0 (2022); a 2026 Draft CG Report adds requirements | 7 |
| Who may access what, and sharing | WAC 1.0.0 (Draft CG Report) or ACP 0.9.0 (Editor's Draft): two alternatives | 8, 9 |
| Live updates | Solid Notifications Protocol 0.3.0 (Draft CG Report) | 10 |
| Data design, tools, security and backends | (practice, not one spec) | 11, 12-13 |
| A minimal app, read end to end | (all of the above) | 14 |
| A real app, open problems, the standard to come | (practice); LWS Protocol 1.0 (Working Draft) | 15-17 |

Spec versions move; check the current one before relying on a detail."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-why-solid-12a> .

<#q-why-solid-12a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Your Solid app must show changes another app makes to a pod resource, without the user reloading the page. Which Solid spec covers that?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Solid Notifications Protocol"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The Solid Notifications Protocol lets an app subscribe to a resource and be told when it changes; the Solid Protocol requires servers to support it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-12a-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-12a-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-12a-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-12a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Solid WebID Profile"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The WebID Profile describes the document a WebID points to; it says nothing about changes to other resources."@en .

<#q-why-solid-12a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Web Access Control (WAC)"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "WAC decides who may read or change a resource; it tells no one when the resource changes."@en .

<#q-why-solid-12a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Solid-OIDC"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid-OIDC logs the user in and proves who is asking; it says nothing about changes."@en .

<#q-why-solid-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "An AI assistant wants to read your calendar stored in your Solid pod. In Solid terms, how should this be handled?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Like any other app: it gets access only to what you allow"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Solid's model treats an AI agent as one more app that needs permission to open specific parts of your pod."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-r01-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It first copies your pod into the AI company's own database"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That recreates the data silo Solid tries to avoid."@en .

<#q-why-solid-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It needs no permission, because AI agents are trusted by default"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "No app or agent is trusted by default; access must be granted."@en .

<#q-why-solid-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only an agent built by Inrupt may read Solid data"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Any developer can build an app or agent for Solid."@en .

<#q-why-solid-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A notes app and a task app both understand the same appointment format and use the same appointment in your Solid pod. You edit it in one app. What happens in the other?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It sees the change on its next read, since there is one copy"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Keeping one copy in the user's storage is what turns portability into interoperability."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-r02-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing changes until you export and import the data again"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That describes one-off portability, not shared data in a pod."@en .

<#q-why-solid-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It receives the change at once, pushed automatically to every app"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "An app sees it when it next reads, or at once only if it subscribes to notifications."@en .

<#q-why-solid-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It shows an error, because each app needs a pod of its own"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "One pod can serve many apps."@en .

<#q-why-solid-r03>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Which pair matches a problem of today's web apps with its Solid answer?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Data locked in each app's database → a pod many apps share"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The data silo is answered by moving data into user-chosen storage that apps access with permission."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-r03-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-r03-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-r03-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-r03-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Ad-funded incentives → a cryptocurrency that pays users for data"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid has no token or payment system and does not use a blockchain."@en .

<#q-why-solid-r03-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Hard-to-switch storage → one global Solid server for everyone"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid lets users choose among many providers, not one server."@en .

<#q-why-solid-r03-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Each app has its own login → each app keeps a copy of your password"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "You log in through an identity provider you choose, so apps never see your password."@en .

<#q-why-solid-r04>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Maya's photo albums live in her photo app's own database. Which change gives her both a choice of photo apps and a choice of where her albums are stored?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Keeping her data in a pod, separate from any app"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Separating storage from apps turns one coupled decision into two free choices: the chain from silo to separation to choice."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-r04-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-r04-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-r04-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-r04-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Getting a GDPR export of her albums every month"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Exports are one-off copies; each app still keeps its own data."@en .

<#q-why-solid-r04-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Asking her photo app to encrypt all of her albums"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Encryption does not change who controls the storage."@en .

<#q-why-solid-r04-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Moving all her apps to one larger, more trusted provider"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is still a coupled stack, just a bigger one."@en .

<#q-why-solid-r05>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "According to Inrupt, Flanders' citizen pods and the BBC's pilot ran on Inrupt's Enterprise Solid Server (ESS). Why is that not lock-in in principle, and what is the catch?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Any conforming server could hold the data; the catch: moving is hard"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Open specs mean any conforming server could host the same data, but moving a pod is still hard in practice. That both ran on ESS is Inrupt's own claim."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-r05-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-r05-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-r05-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-r05-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "ESS is open source, so anyone can run it; the catch is the licence fees"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "ESS is a commercial product sold by Inrupt; other servers are separate projects."@en .

<#q-why-solid-r05-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The W3C guarantees free migration; the catch is that it takes weeks"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "No W3C rule guarantees migration, and the Solid Protocol defines no way to move a pod."@en .

<#q-why-solid-r05-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The data sits on a blockchain; the catch is the transaction costs"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid pods are web servers, not a blockchain."@en .

<#q-why-solid-r06>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app is given only Alice's WebID. Which sequence lets it read her private notes?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Read her profile, log in at its `solid:oidcIssuer`, find and fetch her storage"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The profile names the identity provider and usually the storage; logging in there gives the app an authenticated `fetch`, which it then uses on the pod server."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-why-solid-r06-d1> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-r06-d2> ,
                          <#q-why-solid-r06-d3> .

<#q-why-solid-r06-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Send her password to her pod server, then read her notes from it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Apps never handle the password; she logs in at the identity provider her profile names."@en .

<#q-why-solid-r06-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Ask a central registry for her pod, then log her in at that registry"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "There is no central registry; her profile names her identity provider and usually links to her storage."@en .

<#q-why-solid-r06-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Log her in at the app's own server, then copy her pod to its database"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Her profile names the identity provider, and copying the pod recreates the silo."@en .

# Chapter 2: Linked Data.
<#ch-web-of-data>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    dcterms:title "Linked Data"@en ;
    dcterms:description "The idea underneath every Solid standard: Linked Data, a set of principles for naming, finding and connecting data with URIs, HTTP and RDF. It covers global identifiers, dereferencing, hash URIs and the triple as the unit of data."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-web-of-data-r01> ,
                              <#q-web-of-data-r02> ,
                              <#q-web-of-data-r03> ,
                              <#q-web-of-data-r04> .

<#ch-web-of-data-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-web-of-data> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## The four Linked Data rules (2006)

In 2006 Tim Berners-Lee wrote down four rules for putting data on the web:

1. Use URIs as names for things.
2. Use HTTP URIs, so that people can look those names up.
3. When someone looks up a URI, provide useful information, using the
   standards (such as RDF).
4. Include links to other URIs, so that they can discover more things.

Rule 1 is about *things*, not only web pages: people, books and ideas get
names too. Rule 2 makes a name something you can fetch. Rule 3 says that what
you get back should be machine-readable. Rule 4 turns separate datasets into
a web: from one piece of data you can follow links to more.

A Solid pod follows all four. Every resource in a pod has an HTTP(S) URL
(rules 1 and 2). Fetching a data document returns machine-readable data in a
standard RDF format such as Turtle (rule 3). RDF is the W3C's data model for
Linked Data, taught in Step 2.4. And documents point to other documents and
other pods: a WebID profile links to the owner's storage with `pim:storage`
(rule 4)."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-web-of-data-1a> ,
                         <#q-web-of-data-1b> .

<#q-web-of-data-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Why does the second of Berners-Lee's Linked Data rules (2006) ask for *HTTP* URIs rather than any kind of URI?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "So a program can fetch the name and get data about it"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "An HTTP URI is a name you can dereference: put it in a GET request and you receive information about the thing it names."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-web-of-data-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-1a-d3> .

<#q-web-of-data-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "So the data is encrypted while it is in transit"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Encryption comes from HTTPS/TLS, and rule 2 is about lookup, not secrecy."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "So the name is guaranteed never to change"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Keeping a name stable is a separate concern; an HTTP URI breaks if its owner stops serving it."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "So the server can choose which format the name uses"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A URI is a name; format choice happens later, when the name is looked up."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid profile document contains `pim:storage <https://alice.example/>`. Which of Berners-Lee's four Linked Data rules does that line mainly put into practice?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Rule 4: link to other URIs so more can be discovered"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A link from the profile to the storage lets an app discover a pod it did not know about, which is exactly rule 4."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-web-of-data-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-1b-d3> .

<#q-web-of-data-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Rule 1: use URIs as names for things, not only pages"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The line uses a name, but its purpose is to point the reader onward to another resource, which is what rule 4 asks for."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Rule 2: use HTTP URIs so names can be looked up"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Rule 2 is about which kind of URI to use, not about linking one resource to another."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Rule 3: return standard data when looked up"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Rule 3 is about what a server sends back when a URI is fetched, not about what a document links to."@en .

<#ch-web-of-data-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-web-of-data> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## 5-star open data, and why Linked Data need not be open

In 2010 Berners-Lee added a 5-star scale for publishing *open* data:

| Stars | What you publish | Typical format |
|---|---|---|
| ★ | Anything on the web, with an open licence | PDF |
| ★★ | Machine-readable structured data | Excel |
| ★★★ | A non-proprietary format | CSV |
| ★★★★ | Open W3C standards, with URIs to identify things | RDF |
| ★★★★★ | Links to other people's data for context | RDF linking to other datasets |

In the words of 5stardata.info, at one and two stars the data is "locked up
in a document"; at three it is data *on* the web; at four it is data *in* the
web, and at five it is data in the web *linked to* other data.

The first star asks for an open licence, so this is a scale for open data.
The Linked Data rules themselves don't require openness. Global names,
lookups and links work just as well for data that only some people may read.
Solid is the main example: most pod data sits behind access control, and it is
still Linked Data."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-web-of-data-2a> ,
                         <#q-web-of-data-2b> .

<#q-web-of-data-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "On Berners-Lee's 5-star open data scale, a city publishes bus timetables as CSV files under an open licence. Which single change would move it from three stars to four?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Republish it as RDF with a URI for each stop"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Four stars means using open W3C standards such as RDF, with URIs that identify things."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-web-of-data-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-2a-d3> .

<#q-web-of-data-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Republish the same tables as Excel spreadsheets"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Excel is proprietary, which is the two-star level, so this would be a step down."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Add links to another city's timetable PDFs"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Linking is the five-star step, and it needs data that uses URIs (four stars) first."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Add a column of stop web-page URLs to the CSV"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The file now contains URLs but is still CSV; four stars needs an open W3C standard such as RDF, where URIs name the stops themselves."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid pod holds medical notes that only their owner and her doctor can read. Are those notes Linked Data?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Yes; naming and linking don't require openness"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Linked Data is a way of naming and linking data, and it works the same for private, access-controlled pod data."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-web-of-data-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-2b-d3> .

<#q-web-of-data-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No; Linked Data must be published under an open licence"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The open licence belongs to the 5-star open data scale, not to the four Linked Data rules."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No; data protected by a login can't have URIs"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A protected resource still has a URL; access control decides who may fetch it."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only if they also earn five stars on the open data scale"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The star scale rates open publishing and does not decide what counts as Linked Data."@en .

<#ch-web-of-data-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-web-of-data> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Global names: URI, URL, IRI

Linked Data names things with web identifiers, and those names are *global*.
In RDF, the same identifier in two places means the same thing, so anyone can
reuse your identifier to talk about the same thing. As the W3C's Web
architecture (2004) puts it, "global naming leads to global network
effects". If two apps that have never met both use Bob's WebID, their data is
about the same Bob.

You will see three names for these identifiers:

- **URI** (RFC 3986) is the general kind of identifier, such as `https://…`
  or `urn:isbn:…`.
- **URL** is a URI that also tells you how to *locate* the resource, for
  example over HTTP. `urn:isbn:…` is a URI that is not a URL.
- **IRI** (RFC 3987) generalises a URI so that it may contain Unicode
  characters, such as `https://example.org/résumé`. Mapping it to a URI writes
  those characters as escaped bytes: `r%C3%A9sum%C3%A9`.

RDF compares IRIs as strings, character for character. So `http` versus
`https`, `#` versus `/`, or a letter's case each make a different IRI: copy
IRIs exactly. RDF specs say IRI and Solid specs say URI or URL. In Solid code
you often parse them with the JavaScript `URL` class, but `URL` normalises what
it parses: it lower-cases the scheme and host, percent-encodes Unicode (`résumé`
becomes `r%C3%A9sum%C3%A9`) and adds `/` to an empty path. So compare IRIs as
the strings that appear in the data, not as `new URL(…).href`."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-web-of-data-3a> ,
                         <#q-web-of-data-3b> ,
                         <#q-web-of-data-3c> .

<#q-web-of-data-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Two apps that have never met both use the IRI `https://bob.example.org/profile#me` in their RDF data. What may a reader conclude?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Both apps are talking about the same thing"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "In RDF, two appearances of the same IRI denote the same resource, which is what makes global names useful for linking."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-web-of-data-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-3a-d3> .

<#q-web-of-data-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing, because each app's data is separate"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "IRIs have global scope in RDF, so the same IRI means the same resource wherever it appears."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "They mean the same thing only if they share a server"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Where the data is stored does not change what an IRI denotes."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The second app has copied the first app's data"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Reusing a global name is normal and expected; it says nothing about copying."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-3b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "An RDF graph contains both `https://alice.example/profile/card#me` and `http://alice.example/profile/card#me`. Does RDF treat them as the same IRI?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "No: RDF compares IRIs character for character"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "RDF compares IRIs as plain strings, so a different scheme, case or separator gives a different IRI; copy IRIs exactly."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-web-of-data-3b-d1> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-3b-d2> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-3b-d3> .

<#q-web-of-data-3b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes: the scheme is ignored when IRIs are compared"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The scheme is part of the string, and RDF compares the whole string."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-3b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes: both lead to the same document on one host"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "RDF never fetches IRIs to compare them; where they lead does not matter."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-3b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only if the server redirects one to the other"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Server behaviour does not change IRI identity in RDF; the strings differ, so the IRIs differ."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-3c>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "How is an IRI (RFC 3987) different from a URI?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "An IRI may contain Unicode characters"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "RFC 3987 defines IRIs as URIs extended with Unicode; every URI is also an IRI, and an IRI maps back to a URI by percent-encoding."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-web-of-data-3c-d1> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-3c-d2> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-3c-d3> .

<#q-web-of-data-3c-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An IRI always starts with `http` or `https`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "IRIs can use any scheme; being restricted to HTTP is a Solid habit, not part of the IRI definition."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-3c-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An IRI can't contain a `#` fragment"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "IRIs follow the same general syntax as URIs, fragments included."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-3c-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An IRI names a thing, while a URI names a document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Both can name anything; the difference is only in the allowed characters."@en .

<#ch-web-of-data-4>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-web-of-data> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Triples and graphs

RDF, the data model behind Linked Data, stores every fact as a *triple*: a
subject, a predicate and an object. Think of it as an arrow with a label: the
subject points to the object, and the predicate is the label.

```turtle
<https://alice.example/profile/card#me>
    <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/knows>
        <https://bob.example.org/profile#me> .
```

Here Alice *knows* Bob. An *RDF graph* is a **set** of triples. Since it is a
set, it has no order, and stating the same triple twice still gives one
triple.

The terms in a triple come in three kinds: IRIs (global names, Step 2.3),
*literals* (data values such as `"Alice"`) and *blank nodes* (nodes with no
global name, Step 2.8). Not every kind can go in every position:

| Position | Allowed terms |
|---|---|
| Subject | IRI or blank node |
| Predicate | IRI only |
| Object | IRI, blank node or literal |

So a literal such as `"Alice"` can never be a subject, and a predicate is
always an IRI, never a plain key like `name`. (RDF 1.2 adds one more kind of
object, covered in Chapter 3.)"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-web-of-data-4a> ,
                         <#q-web-of-data-4b> .

<#q-web-of-data-4a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Two apps both add the RDF triple \"Alice knows Bob\" to the same RDF graph. How many triples does the graph now hold about that fact?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "One, because a graph is a set"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "An RDF graph is a set of triples, so duplicates collapse into one and order carries no meaning."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-web-of-data-4a-d1> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-4a-d2> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-4a-d3> .

<#q-web-of-data-4a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Two, one for each app that added it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A graph is a set of triples, so an identical triple can't appear twice."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-4a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Zero, because the second add cancels the first"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Adding a triple that is already there changes nothing, and nothing is removed."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-4a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Two, but readers see only the latest one"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A graph keeps no order or history, so there is no \"latest\" copy, only one triple."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-4b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "In RDF, which combination of term kinds forms a valid triple?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "IRI subject, IRI predicate, literal object"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Subjects are IRIs or blank nodes, predicates are always IRIs, and only objects may be literals."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-web-of-data-4b-d1> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-4b-d2> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-4b-d3> .

<#q-web-of-data-4b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Literal subject, IRI predicate, IRI object"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A literal may only be an object, never a subject."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-4b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "IRI subject, literal predicate, IRI object"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A predicate must always be an IRI."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-4b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "IRI subject, blank node predicate, literal object"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Blank nodes may be subjects or objects, but never predicates."@en .

<#ch-web-of-data-5>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-web-of-data> ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Dereferencing and content negotiation

To *dereference* a URI is to fetch a representation of the resource it names,
for example with an HTTP GET. One URI can have several representations, and
the client says which it prefers in the `Accept` header. An optional `q`
weight from 0 to 1 ranks the choices; no weight means 1:

```http
GET /profile/card HTTP/1.1
Host: alice.example
Accept: text/turtle, application/ld+json;q=0.9
```

```http
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: text/turtle

@prefix foaf: <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/> .
<#me> a foaf:Person ; foaf:name "Alice" .
```

The body states two triples about `<#me>`, the `#me` inside this document
(Step 2.7): Alice is a person, and her name is "Alice". This client prefers
Turtle and will accept JSON-LD. The response's `Content-Type` says which
format it got. Under the Solid Protocol (v0.11, 2024), a server must be able
to return an RDF document either as Turtle (`text/turtle`) or as JSON-LD
(`application/ld+json`), depending on what the client asks for. Both carry
the same data; only the syntax differs. Chapter 3 teaches both formats."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-web-of-data-5a> ,
                         <#q-web-of-data-5b> .

<#q-web-of-data-5a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A client sends `Accept: application/ld+json, text/turtle;q=0.5` to a Solid server (Solid Protocol 0.11, 2024) for an RDF document. What should it expect back?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "JSON-LD, because it carries the higher weight"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A type with no `q` has weight 1, so JSON-LD outranks Turtle at 0.5, and a Solid server can serve either."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-web-of-data-5a-d1> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-5a-d2> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-5a-d3> .

<#q-web-of-data-5a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Turtle, because Solid's default format is Turtle"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The server follows the client's stated preferences, and JSON-LD has the implied weight of 1."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-5a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Both formats together in one response"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "One response carries one representation, named by its `Content-Type`."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-5a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An error, because two formats were listed"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Listing several acceptable formats with weights is the normal use of `Accept`."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-5b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Under the Solid Protocol (v0.11, 2024), app A fetches an RDF contact document from a Solid pod with `Accept: text/turtle`; app B fetches the same URL with `Accept: application/ld+json`. What do they receive?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The same triples, written in two different syntaxes"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Content negotiation picks a representation of one resource; Turtle and JSON-LD just write the same data differently."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-web-of-data-5b-d1> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-5b-d2> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-5b-d3> .

<#q-web-of-data-5b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Two separate stored copies, one for each format"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The server keeps one resource and writes it out in whichever format is asked for; there is no second copy."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-5b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "B gets an error, because the document was saved as Turtle"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "However it was stored, a Solid server can return an RDF document as Turtle or JSON-LD."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-5b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "B gets only the triples that were written in JSON-LD"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The data is one set of triples; the server returns all of it in the syntax B asked for."@en .

<#ch-web-of-data-6>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-web-of-data> ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Documents versus things

Some resources can be sent whole in a message: a web page, an image, a
profile document. These are called *information resources*. Alice herself
can't be sent over HTTP. She is a person, and people, places and ideas are not
information resources. As the W3C's Cool URIs note puts it, "one URI can't
stand for both a Web document and a real-world object."

Suppose `https://alice.example/profile/card` named both the document and
Alice. Then these two triples would describe the same thing:

```turtle
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix schema: <https://schema.org/> .

<https://alice.example/profile/card> dcterms:created "2024-01-01" .
<https://alice.example/profile/card> schema:birthDate "1990-07-04" .
```

`dcterms:created` and `schema:birthDate` are abbreviated predicate IRIs from
shared vocabularies (Chapters 3 and 4); read them as "was created on" and "was
born on". A thing that was created in 2024 *and* born in 1990 makes no sense,
and an app could not tell which facts are about the file and which are about
the person. So the document and the person need different identifiers. In a
typical Solid profile, such as one on CSS, the document is `…/profile/card` and
Alice is `…/profile/card#me`.
The next step explains how that `#me` works."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-web-of-data-6a> .

<#q-web-of-data-6a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid, `https://alice.example/profile/card` is Alice's profile document. Why shouldn't that URL also be used as Alice's own identifier?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It already names the profile document, not the person"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A document and the person it describes are different resources, so they need different URIs; otherwise facts about the file and about the person get mixed up."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-web-of-data-6a-d1> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-6a-d2> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-6a-d3> .

<#q-web-of-data-6a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "URLs with no file extension can't be used to name people"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The spelling of a URI says nothing about what it names; the problem is reusing one name for two things."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-6a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server would return HTML instead of RDF"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The server returns the same representation either way; the problem lies in what the data then means."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-6a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only fragment IRIs may name things in RDF"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Any IRI may name anything in RDF; the problem is that this one already names the document."@en .

<#ch-web-of-data-7>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-web-of-data> ;
    schema:position 6 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Hash URIs and the #me convention

The part after `#` is the *fragment*. A client removes it before sending the
request, so the server never sees it. Fetching
`https://bob.example.org/profile#me` sends `GET /profile`; the client then
looks for triples about `#me` inside the returned data. In RDF,
`…/profile#me` is simply a different IRI from `…/profile`, so one names Bob
and the other the document about him. This is the *hash URI* pattern. Inside
the document itself, `<#me>` is short for
`<https://bob.example.org/profile#me>`; Step 3.3 shows how such relative IRIs
are resolved.

The alternative is the *303 URI* pattern. A URI with no fragment, such as
`/id/alice`, answers `303 See Other` and redirects to a document that
describes Alice.

| | Hash URIs | 303 URIs |
|---|---|---|
| Requests | One | Two (redirect, then document) |
| Server | Static files are enough | Needs redirect setup |
| Best for | Small sets that change together | Large datasets; each 303 costs an extra request |

WebID 1.0 allows both. Many Solid servers, such as CSS and NSS, issue
`…/profile/card#me` WebIDs, but not every provider does: Inrupt's PodSpaces
(2026) uses WebIDs with no fragment, like `https://id.inrupt.com/alice`
(Chapter 7). Pod documents usually name their things with fragments of the
document URL."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-web-of-data-7a> ,
                         <#q-web-of-data-7b> .

<#q-web-of-data-7a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "An app dereferences the hash URI `https://bob.example.org/profile#me`. What does Bob's server receive?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A request for `/profile`, without the fragment"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The fragment never reaches the server: the client fetches the document and then looks up the fragment in it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-web-of-data-7a-d1> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-7a-d2> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-7a-d3> .

<#q-web-of-data-7a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A request for `/profile#me`, fragment included"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Clients strip the fragment before the request; it is handled only on the client side."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-7a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A request for `/profile`, then a second request for `#me`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "There is no second request; the client finds `#me` in the document it already has."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-7a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A request that the server answers with 303 See Other"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A 303 redirect belongs to the 303 URI pattern; hash URIs need just one ordinary GET."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-7b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Linked Data, why do hash URIs (such as `…/profile#me`) suit Solid pods well?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "One plain GET returns the document describing the thing"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A hash URI needs no redirect: one request fetches the document, which works with simple file-like storage such as a pod."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-web-of-data-7b-d1> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-7b-d2> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-7b-d3> .

<#q-web-of-data-7b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "They keep the thing's data hidden from the server that stores it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The server stores and serves the whole document; only the fragment in the request is left out."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-7b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "They make each thing a separate file on the server"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Many things can share one document, each named by its own fragment."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-7b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "They are the only identifier pattern that WebID 1.0 allows"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "WebID 1.0 accepts both hash URIs and 303 redirects."@en .

<#ch-web-of-data-8>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-web-of-data> ;
    schema:position 7 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Blank nodes and skolemization

**Blank nodes** are nodes with no global name. Formally, a blank node just
says that *something exists*, without naming it. "Alice lives at an address
whose city is Ghent" can use a blank node for the address.

```turtle
@prefix schema: <https://schema.org/> .

<#me> schema:address _:a .
_:a   schema:addressLocality "Ghent" .
```

The label `_:a` only means something inside this one document. No other
document can point to that address, so it can never be the target of a rule-4
link. When outside links are needed, a system can *skolemize*: replace each
blank node with a fresh IRI. RDF recommends the form
`http://example.com/.well-known/genid/<id>`. In practice, pod data usually
names such things with fragment IRIs like `<#address>` instead. N3 Patch, the
patch format of Solid Protocol 0.11 (Step 6.7), restricts blank nodes, so pod
data tends to avoid them."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-web-of-data-8a> ,
                         <#q-web-of-data-8b> .

<#q-web-of-data-8a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Alice's RDF profile document contains `<#me> schema:address _:a .` Another app wants to say something about that address `_:a`. What is the problem?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A blank node can't be referred to from outside its document"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A blank node label is local to its document, so to be linkable the address needs an IRI, either a fragment like `<#address>` or a skolem IRI."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-web-of-data-8a-d1> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-8a-d2> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-8a-d3> .

<#q-web-of-data-8a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Blank nodes may never appear as the subject of a triple"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Blank nodes may be subjects or objects; `_:a` can be the subject of further triples in its own document."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-8a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The other app must first ask the server to rename it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Servers don't offer this; the label is local to the document by design."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-8a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Blank nodes can hold only literal values, never other nodes"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A blank node is a node like any other and can have any kind of object."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-8b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "An app exports an RDF document containing the blank node `_:a` and wants other pods to be able to point at that node. Which change makes that possible while keeping the same facts?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Give `_:a` an IRI, such as `<#address>`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Giving a blank node a fresh IRI is called skolemization; RDF suggests `/.well-known/genid/` IRIs, and pod data usually uses fragment IRIs instead."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-web-of-data-8b-d1> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-8b-d2> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-8b-d3> .

<#q-web-of-data-8b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Rename the label to something unique, such as `_:alice-address`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A blank node label stays local to its document, however unique it looks."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-8b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Replace `_:a` with the literal `\"Alice's address\"`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A literal can't be a subject, and a value is not a node others can link to."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-8b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Ask the other pods to copy the triples about `_:a`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Each copy makes a new, separate blank node; nothing links back to the original."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "An app fetches Alice's Solid WebID `https://alice.example/profile/card#me` with `Accept: text/turtle`. Which description of what happens is right?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It GETs `/profile/card` as Turtle, then finds triples about `#me`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Dereferencing a hash URI fetches the whole document (here as Turtle, by content negotiation), and the client reads the triples whose subject is `#me`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-web-of-data-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-r01-d3> .

<#q-web-of-data-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It GETs `/profile/card#me`, and the server returns only Alice's triples"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The fragment is never sent, so the server returns the whole document."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server replies 303 and redirects to the profile document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A hash URI needs no redirect; 303 belongs to the 303 URI pattern."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It GETs `/profile/card`, and the server filters the response to Alice's triples"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The server never sees `#me`, so it returns the whole document; the client does the picking."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A note app writes `<#note1> ex:author <https://alice.example/profile/card#me> .` into Alice's Solid pod (`ex:` stands for some vocabulary). Which Linked Data idea does this one triple show best?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A global IRI for Alice, used as a link to her profile"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Reusing Alice's WebID as the object both names her globally and links the note to her profile (Linked Data rules 1, 2 and 4)."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-web-of-data-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-r02-d3> .

<#q-web-of-data-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A blank node standing for an unnamed author"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Both terms are IRIs; nothing in the triple is a blank node."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A copy of Alice's profile stored in the note document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The triple only names Alice; her profile stays in her own document, reached by following the link."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Data that must be open because it uses HTTP URIs"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "HTTP URIs make data linkable, not public; pod data is usually access-controlled."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-r03>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "An app loads Alice's and Bob's RDF profiles into one graph. Both documents mention `<https://bob.example.org/profile#me>`, and both contain a blank node labelled `_:a`. What does the combined graph hold?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "One node for Bob, and two separate `_:a` nodes"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Global IRIs join up across documents, which is how data from several pods combines; blank nodes stay apart, and identical triples collapse because a graph is a set."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-web-of-data-r03-d1> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-r03-d2> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-r03-d3> .

<#q-web-of-data-r03-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "One node for Bob, and one shared `_:a` node"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Blank node labels are local to each document, so the two `_:a` are different nodes even though the labels match."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-r03-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Two nodes for Bob, one from each document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The same IRI names the same thing wherever it appears, so the Bob IRIs join into one node."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-r03-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An error, because the label `_:a` is used twice"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Combining graphs keeps blank nodes from different documents apart, renaming labels where needed; clashing labels are expected."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-r04>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In a Solid pod, a friends-only document `https://alice.example/clubs` describes Alice's running club as `<#club>`. Bob's public profile links to `https://alice.example/clubs#club`. Which statement is right?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Bob's link is valid Linked Data, but only people Alice allows can look the club up"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Naming and linking work the same for private data: following the hash URI sends `GET /clubs`, and access control decides who receives the document."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-web-of-data-r04-d1> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-r04-d2> ,
                          <#q-web-of-data-r04-d3> .

<#q-web-of-data-r04-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The link is broken, because a private document can't be linked to"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Anyone may write the IRI; access control only decides who can fetch the document."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-r04-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Alice's server gets a request for `/clubs#club` and checks access for `#club` alone"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The fragment is never sent; access is checked for the whole `/clubs` document."@en .

<#q-web-of-data-r04-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The club data earns five stars, since a public profile links to it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The stars rate open publishing; data behind access control lacks the open licence of the first star."@en .

# Chapter 3: Writing RDF: Turtle, JSON-LD and friends.
<#ch-rdf-syntaxes>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    dcterms:title "Writing RDF: Turtle, JSON-LD and friends"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Read and write RDF in the formats Solid uses, use JSON-LD as plain JSON, read simple SPARQL and N3 Patch, and know what RDF 1.2 adds."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r01> ,
                              <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r02> ,
                              <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r03> ,
                              <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r04> ,
                              <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r05> ,
                              <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r06> ,
                              <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r07> ,
                              <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r08> .

<#ch-rdf-syntaxes-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-rdf-syntaxes> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## One graph, many syntaxes

Chapter 2 described RDF as a graph of triples. That graph is an abstract model, not a file format: RDF is not "an XML thing", and RDF/XML is just one older way of writing it down. The same graph can be written in many interchangeable syntaxes:

| Syntax | Media type | Holds |
|---|---|---|
| Turtle | `text/turtle` | one graph |
| N-Triples | `application/n-triples` | one graph |
| TriG | `application/trig` | a dataset |
| N-Quads | `application/n-quads` | a dataset |
| JSON-LD | `application/ld+json` | a dataset |
| RDFa | inside HTML | one graph |
| RDF/XML | `application/rdf+xml` | one graph |
| N3 | `text/n3` | a graph, plus formulae and rules |

A dataset is several graphs side by side (Step 3.9).

For Solid, two of these matter most. When a client asks for an RDF document, a Solid server MUST be able to answer with `text/turtle` or `application/ld+json`, whichever the client requests in its `Accept` header. So you can write a document as Turtle and read it back as JSON-LD: the triples are the same, only the text differs."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-rdf-syntaxes-1a> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "An app stores a profile on a Solid server as Turtle. A second app sends `GET` with `Accept: application/ld+json`. What should it receive?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The same triples, written as JSON-LD"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "RDF is a graph, and Turtle and JSON-LD are two ways of writing it. A Solid server must serve RDF documents in either format, chosen by the client's `Accept` header."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-1a-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A 406 Not Acceptable error, because the file was stored as Turtle"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid servers must serve every RDF document as both Turtle and JSON-LD, whatever format was used to write it."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The original Turtle text with a JSON-LD content type"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The server converts the representation; mislabelling Turtle as JSON-LD would break any JSON-LD parser."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A JSON-LD copy that leaves out triples JSON-LD can't express"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "JSON-LD can express any RDF 1.1 graph, so a conversion keeps all the triples."@en .

<#ch-rdf-syntaxes-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-rdf-syntaxes> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Turtle basics

Turtle, the "Terse RDF Triple Language", is the format to master for Solid: servers must serve it, and WebID 1.0 makes it the required format for profiles. A few rules cover most files:

- `@prefix foaf: <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/> .` lets you write `foaf:name` instead of the full IRI. The SPARQL-style `PREFIX foaf: <…>` (no `@`, no final dot) works too.
- `a` is short for `rdf:type`, which says what kind of thing the subject is, such as a person or an agent (Chapter 4 covers these classes).
- Every statement ends with `.`.
- `;` starts a new predicate for the same subject.
- `,` adds another object for the same subject and predicate.

Here is a typical WebID profile:

```turtle
@prefix foaf:  <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/> .
@prefix pim:   <http://www.w3.org/ns/pim/space#> .
@prefix ldp:   <http://www.w3.org/ns/ldp#> .
@prefix solid: <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#> .

<https://alice.example/profile/card#me>
    a foaf:Agent ;
    foaf:name "Alice" ;
    foaf:knows <https://bob.example.org/profile#me>,
               <https://carol.example/#i> ;
    pim:storage <https://alice.example/> ;        # where Alice's pod is
    ldp:inbox <https://alice.example/inbox/> ;    # where to send her messages
    solid:oidcIssuer <https://idp.example/> .     # who logs Alice in
```

Read it aloud as "Alice's WebID is an agent, is named Alice, knows Bob and Carol, …". Every line is a triple about the same subject."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-rdf-syntaxes-2a> ,
                         <#q-rdf-syntaxes-2b> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front """How many RDF triples does this Turtle contain?

```turtle
<#me> a foaf:Person ; foaf:knows <#bob>, <#carol> .
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Three"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`;` keeps the subject and `,` keeps the subject and predicate, so the statement expands to one `rdf:type` triple and two `foaf:knows` triples."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-2a-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Two"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Each object after a `,` is its own triple, repeating the subject and predicate."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "One"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The `.` ends the whole statement, which `;` and `,` can stretch over several triples."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Four"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`a` is just shorthand for `rdf:type`, so `<#me> a foaf:Person` is a single triple."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Turtle, which triple does `<#me> a foaf:Agent .` expand to?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "`<#me> rdf:type foaf:Agent .`" ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`a` expands to `http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type`, so the triple says that `<#me>` is a `foaf:Agent`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-2b-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`<#me> foaf:a foaf:Agent .`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`a` is a Turtle keyword, not a prefixed name; it never picks up a prefix."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`<#me> rdfs:subClassOf foaf:Agent .`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`rdfs:subClassOf` relates two classes; `a` always means `rdf:type`."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`foaf:Agent rdf:type <#me> .`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The subject stays first; `a` doesn't reverse the triple."@en .

<#ch-rdf-syntaxes-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-rdf-syntaxes> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Relative IRIs and the base

Inside angle brackets, Turtle accepts relative IRIs, which a parser resolves against a base IRI. The base is usually the URL the document was fetched from, unless the file sets one with `@base` or `BASE`. Resolution follows RFC 3986, and browsers resolve links the same way for ordinary cases like these.

If the document lives at `https://alice.example/profile/card`:

| Written | Resolves to |
|---|---|
| `<#me>` | `https://alice.example/profile/card#me` |
| `<>` | `https://alice.example/profile/card` (the document itself) |
| `<../inbox/>` | `https://alice.example/inbox/` |

This is why a pod document can say `<#me>` without knowing its own absolute address: the same file works wherever it is served. It also ties in with Chapter 2's hash URIs. The "things" a document describes are usually fragments of its own URL, so `<#task1>` in `https://alice.example/todo/list.ttl` names `https://alice.example/todo/list.ttl#task1`.

A practical consequence: always parse a fetched document with its real URL as the base. A parser given the wrong base, or none, will produce the wrong IRIs, keep them relative, or fail. N3.js 2.13 (2026), for example, keeps `<#me>` as the relative `#me` when it is given no base (Step 3.8 shows why that output is not valid N-Triples)."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-rdf-syntaxes-3a> ,
                         <#q-rdf-syntaxes-3b> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "The Turtle document `https://alice.example/profile/card` contains the relative IRI `<../inbox/>`. Which IRI does it denote?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "`https://alice.example/inbox/`" ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`card` sits in `/profile/`, `..` goes up to `/`, and `inbox/` is appended, giving `https://alice.example/inbox/`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-3a-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`https://alice.example/profile/inbox/`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`..` climbs one level above the folder `/profile/`, so the result is outside it."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`https://alice.example/profile/card/../inbox/`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A parser resolves the relative IRI fully; the `..` does not stay in the stored IRI."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`https://alice.example/profile/card/inbox/`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A relative path replaces the last segment `card`; it is not appended after it."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-3b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "An app fetches the Turtle document `https://bob.example/notes/a.ttl`, which contains `<#n1>`, and parses the text with N3.js 2.13 (2026) without passing a base IRI, so N3.js keeps relative IRIs as written. Later the app looks up `https://bob.example/notes/a.ttl#n1` in its store. Does it find the note?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "No: the stored IRI is not the one the app looks up"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Relative IRIs are resolved against whatever base the parser is given; pass the fetched URL so `<#n1>` becomes `https://bob.example/notes/a.ttl#n1`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-3b-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-3b-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-3b-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-3b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes: the parser fills in the base from the fetch"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A parser only sees the text you hand it; it doesn't know where the text came from."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-3b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes: fragments match regardless of base"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "IRIs are compared as whole strings, so `#n1` and the full IRI differ."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-3b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No: the parser drops triples with relative IRIs"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "N3.js keeps the triple, with the subject `#n1`; the triple is there, under the wrong IRI."@en .

<#ch-rdf-syntaxes-4>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-rdf-syntaxes> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Literals, datatypes and language tags

Chapter 2 introduced literals as data values. In Turtle they look like this:

```turtle
@prefix xsd:    <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix schema: <https://schema.org/> .
@prefix ex:     <http://example.org/> .
<#me> schema:name       "Alice" ;                 # xsd:string
      schema:birthDate  "1990-07-04"^^xsd:date ;  # typed literal
      schema:jobTitle   "Ingénieure"@fr ;         # rdf:langString
      ex:heightInMetres 1.68 ;                    # xsd:decimal
      ex:childCount     2 .                       # xsd:integer
```

(`ex:` stands for an app's own vocabulary; schema.org's `height` and `children` expect a measurement and a person, not bare numbers.)

- A plain quoted string is an `xsd:string`.
- `^^` attaches a datatype, such as `xsd:date` or `xsd:boolean`. The `xsd:` namespace is `http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#` and `rdf:` is `http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#`; copy them exactly, since `http` versus `https` or `#` versus `/` makes a different IRI.
- Bare numbers are shorthands: `2` is `xsd:integer`, `1.68` is `xsd:decimal`, `1.6E3` is `xsd:double`, and `true`/`false` are `xsd:boolean`.
- A language tag (`@fr`) gives the datatype `rdf:langString`, not `xsd:string`.

A literal is its text (the lexical form) plus its datatype, so `"2"` and `2` are different literals, and code that looks for one will not find the other. Literal types are also not JavaScript types: integer and decimal are distinct, and links are IRIs, not strings. `"abc"^^xsd:integer` still parses, but it is an invalid value that tools may reject."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-rdf-syntaxes-4a> ,
                         <#q-rdf-syntaxes-4b> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-4a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In RDF, what is the datatype of the literal `\"Ingénieure\"@fr`?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "`rdf:langString`" ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A literal with a language tag has the datatype `rdf:langString`; a plain string without a tag is an `xsd:string`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-4a-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-4a-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-4a-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-4a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`xsd:string`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Only untagged strings are `xsd:string`; a language tag changes the datatype."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-4a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`xsd:language`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`xsd:language` is a datatype for language codes themselves, such as `\"fr\"`, not for tagged text."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-4a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`xsd:normalizedString`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is a string without line breaks or tabs; a language tag always gives `rdf:langString`."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-4b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "One app writes the Turtle `ex:count \"5\"` to a pod and another searches the pod for `ex:count 5`. Why does the search find nothing?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "`\"5\"` is a string and `5` is an integer, so the literals differ"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A literal is its text (lexical form) plus its datatype. `\"5\"` is an `xsd:string` and `5` is an `xsd:integer`, so they are different values."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-4b-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-4b-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-4b-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-4b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Turtle forbids numbers as objects unless `^^` is used"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Bare numbers are valid Turtle shorthands for integer, decimal or double literals."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-4b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server stores every value as text and loses types"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A Solid server keeps the triples, datatypes included, and serves them back as written."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-4b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Numbers can only be compared in JSON-LD, not Turtle"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Literal identity is defined by RDF, not by the syntax used to write it."@en .

<#ch-rdf-syntaxes-5>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-rdf-syntaxes> ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Blank nodes and collections in Turtle

Chapter 2 explained blank nodes as nodes with no global name. Turtle has two ways to write them:

```turtle
@prefix foaf:   <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/> .
@prefix schema: <https://schema.org/> .

<#me> foaf:based_near [ foaf:name "Ghent" ] .     # anonymous: [ ]

<#me> schema:address _:addr .                       # labelled: _:addr
_:addr schema:addressLocality "Ghent" .
```

The label `_:addr` only matters inside this one document. The same label in another file is a different node.

Ordered lists use parentheses. `( "a" "b" "c" )` is shorthand for a chain of blank nodes linked with `rdf:first` (the item) and `rdf:rest` (the rest of the list), ending in `rdf:nil`; `()` alone is `rdf:nil`. Triples have no order, so this chain is how RDF records order.

Because blank nodes have no global name, they are hard to point at from outside their document. Step 3.10 shows what that means when you patch a document."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-rdf-syntaxes-5a> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-5a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "What does the Turtle collection `( \"a\" \"b\" )` produce in the RDF graph?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A chain of blank nodes linked by `rdf:first` and `rdf:rest`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A Turtle collection expands to blank nodes, each with an `rdf:first` item and an `rdf:rest` link, ending in `rdf:nil`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-5a-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-5a-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-5a-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-5a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "One literal holding a JSON-style array"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "RDF has no array literal; the list is built from ordinary triples."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-5a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Two triples whose order is fixed by the file"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A graph is a set with no order, so order must be encoded with `rdf:rest` links."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-5a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A named graph containing the two strings"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Parentheses build an RDF collection, not a named graph."@en .

<#ch-rdf-syntaxes-6>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-rdf-syntaxes> ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## JSON-LD

JSON-LD is JSON that is also RDF: you can treat a document as plain JSON or process it as triples. Its key words:

- `@context` maps short keys to IRIs;
- `@id` gives a node's IRI;
- `@type` gives a node's class (or a value's datatype);
- `@value` and `@language` spell out literals.

```json
{
  "@context": {
    "foaf": "http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/",
    "xsd": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#",
    "name": "foaf:name",
    "jobTitle": "https://schema.org/jobTitle",
    "height": "http://example.org/heightInMetres",
    "knows": { "@id": "foaf:knows", "@type": "@id" }
  },
  "@id": "https://alice.example/profile/card#me",
  "@type": "foaf:Person",
  "name": "Alice",
  "jobTitle": { "@value": "Ingénieure", "@language": "fr" },
  "height": { "@value": "1.68", "@type": "xsd:decimal" },
  "knows": "https://bob.example.org/profile#me"
}
```

The `"@type": "@id"` on `knows` tells processors that the string is an IRI. Leave it out and `knows` points to a string literal, not to Bob: the link silently breaks and still looks fine as JSON. A value object, like `jobTitle` and `height`, sets a literal's language or datatype. Native JSON numbers map differently: `2` becomes `xsd:integer`, but a bare `1.68` becomes `xsd:double`, not Turtle's `xsd:decimal`.

A context can be a URL that processors fetch. Remote contexts carry security and availability risks, so production apps should cache or pin the contexts they rely on."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-rdf-syntaxes-6a> ,
                         <#q-rdf-syntaxes-6b> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-6a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A JSON-LD context defines `\"knows\": \"foaf:knows\"` without `\"@type\": \"@id\"`, and the data says `\"knows\": \"https://bob.example.org/profile#me\"`. What is the object of the resulting triple?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A string literal that happens to look like a URL"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Without `\"@type\": \"@id\"`, a string value is a literal. Apps following `foaf:knows` links will not find Bob."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-6a-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-6a-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-6a-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-6a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Bob's WebID, because JSON-LD detects URLs automatically"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Processors don't guess; without `\"@type\": \"@id\"` a JSON string becomes a literal."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-6a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing, because the processor rejects the document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The document is valid JSON-LD; the triple is just not the link you meant."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-6a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A blank node standing in for Bob"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A plain string value produces a literal, not a blank node."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-6b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Your app keeps compact JSON-LD documents that point to a remote context at `https://ctx.example/v1.jsonld`. The context's owner edits it so that `name` maps to a different IRI. What happens the next time your app processes those documents?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Their `name` values turn into triples with the new predicate"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A remote context is a live dependency: if its host changes the file, your data means something else, and if the host is down, processing fails. Cache or pin the contexts you rely on."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-6b-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-6b-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-6b-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-6b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The processor notices the change and keeps the old mapping"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Processors apply whatever context they fetch; they don't track versions."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-6b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The processor rejects the documents as tampered with"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "JSON-LD has no built-in integrity check on remote contexts."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-6b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only documents written after the edit change meaning"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Compact JSON stores only the key `name`; its IRI comes from the context each time."@en .

<#ch-rdf-syntaxes-7>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-rdf-syntaxes> ;
    schema:position 6 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Using JSON-LD as plain JSON: compaction and framing

One JSON-LD graph has several shapes. **Expanded** form has no context: every key is a full IRI and every value sits in an array. **Compacted** form applies a context, so keys become your own short terms. CSS 7.2 expands Turtle documents and returns JSON-LD documents as written; other servers may choose other shapes. So compact every response against **your own** context, for example with jsonld.js:

```js
import jsonld from 'jsonld';
const ctx = { name: 'http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/name',
  knows: { '@id': 'http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/knows', '@type': '@id' } };
const res = await fetch(url, { headers: { Accept: 'application/ld+json' } });
const doc = await jsonld.compact(await res.json(), ctx);
```

For a one-subject Turtle document in the pod `https://alice.example/`, CSS 7.2 (2026) returns:

```json
[{ "@id": "https://alice.example/public/me.ttl#me",
   "http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/name": [{ "@value": "Alice" }],
   "http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/knows": [{ "@id": "https://bob.example.org/profile#me" }] }]
```

After `jsonld.compact` (jsonld.js 9.0):

```json
{ "@context": { … },
  "@id": "https://alice.example/public/me.ttl#me",
  "knows": "https://bob.example.org/profile#me",
  "name": "Alice" }
```

Compacting the same input with `knows` left out of the context keeps `"http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/knows": { "@id": "https://bob.example.org/profile#me" }`. Undefined properties stay as full IRIs; keep them when writing back.

A document with several subjects (a profile) compacts to `{ "@graph": [ … ] }`, so `doc.name` is undefined: find your node by its `@id`. Compaction also turns one-element arrays into plain values, so a key may hold a value or an array. For a nested tree (a note with its author inside), use framing: `jsonld.frame(doc, frame)` picks nodes "by example" and nests related ones. On CSS 7.2, framing a profile with `{ "@context": ctx, "@id": webId }` returns the WebID's node, so `doc.name` is set."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-rdf-syntaxes-7a> ,
                         <#q-rdf-syntaxes-7b> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-7a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "On Community Solid Server 7.2 (2026), your app GETs a note stored as Turtle with `Accept: application/ld+json`, parses it with `JSON.parse` and reads `data[0].title`. It gets `undefined`, though the note has a title. Why?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The response is expanded; compact it against your context first"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "CSS 7.2 converts a Turtle document to expanded JSON-LD, where keys are full IRIs such as `http://purl.org/dc/terms/title`. Compacting with your own context gives you the short keys your code expects, whatever shape the server chose."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-7a-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-7a-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-7a-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-7a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "CSS drops some triples when it converts Turtle to JSON-LD"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Conversion keeps every triple; only the shape of the text differs."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-7a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "CSS returns JSON-LD only for documents stored as JSON-LD"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "CSS 7.2 converts Turtle to JSON-LD on request; it is the shape that differs."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-7a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The `title` key is only created by framing, not compaction"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Compaction alone maps the full IRI to your `title` term."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-7b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "After compacting a JSON-LD profile against a context that defines only `name`, your app also sees a key `http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/knows`. What is it, and what should the app do when it writes the profile back?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A real triple your context doesn't name; write it back unchanged"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Compaction only renames what your context defines; everything else stays, under its full IRI. Keep those keys when you write back, or you erase data other apps rely on."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-7b-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-7b-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-7b-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-7b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Leftover noise from compaction; delete it before saving"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It is a triple from the document; dropping it deletes another app's data."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-7b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An error: the context must define every key it meets"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Compaction keeps undefined properties as full IRIs instead of failing."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-7b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Server metadata that the server rebuilds on every write"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It is part of the document's own data, which a full write replaces."@en .

<#ch-rdf-syntaxes-8>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-rdf-syntaxes> ;
    schema:position 7 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## N-Triples

N-Triples is the simplest RDF syntax: one triple per line, written out in full and ending with `.`. It has no prefixes, no `;` or `,` shortcuts and no relative IRIs: every IRI is absolute. It is a subset of Turtle, so any Turtle parser can read it; the reverse is not true. Literals use the double-quoted form with the datatype IRI in full, and none of Turtle's shortcuts: no bare numbers or `true`/`false`, and no `'…'` or `""\"…""\"` strings.

A converter shows the difference. This N3.js 2.13 script parses a short Turtle document, including a bare number, against the document's URL:

```js
import { Parser, Writer } from 'n3';
const turtle = `@prefix foaf: <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/> .
<#me> a foaf:Person ; foaf:name "Alice" ;
  foaf:knows <https://bob.example.org/profile#me> .
<#me> <http://example.org/childCount> 2 .`;
const quads = new Parser({ baseIRI: 'https://alice.example/profile/card' }).parse(turtle);
console.log(new Writer({ format: 'N-Triples' }).quadsToString(quads));
```

It prints:

```text
<https://alice.example/profile/card#me> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/Person> .
<https://alice.example/profile/card#me> <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/name> "Alice" .
<https://alice.example/profile/card#me> <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/knows> <https://bob.example.org/profile#me> .
<https://alice.example/profile/card#me> <http://example.org/childCount> "2"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
```

Without `baseIRI`, the subjects stay `<#me>`, which is not valid N-Triples (Step 3.3).

Because each line stands alone, N-Triples suits diffs, streaming, `sort` and `grep`, and test fixtures. Its dataset counterpart, N-Quads, appears in the next step."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-rdf-syntaxes-8a> ,
                         <#q-rdf-syntaxes-8b> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-8a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Is the line `<#me> foaf:name \"Alice\" .` valid N-Triples?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "No: it needs full absolute IRIs"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "N-Triples drops prefixes, `;`, `,` and relative IRIs. The line must spell out the subject's and the predicate's full IRIs, such as `<http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/name>`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-8a-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-8a-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-8a-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-8a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes: any line of valid Turtle is valid N-Triples"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "N-Triples is a subset of Turtle, not the other way round."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-8a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, if the file first declares `@base`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "N-Triples has no `@base` or `@prefix` directives."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-8a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No: N-Triples can't hold literals like `\"Alice\"`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "N-Triples can hold literals; it only spells them out in full."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-8b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front """N3.js parses this Turtle with the base `https://alice.example/profile/card` and writes it as N-Triples. How is the object `2` written?

```turtle
<#me> <http://example.org/childCount> 2 .
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:back "`\"2\"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer>`" ;
    solid-memo:backNote "N-Triples has no bare numbers, so the Turtle shorthand `2` is spelled out as a typed literal with the full `xsd:integer` IRI."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-8b-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-8b-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-8b-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-8b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`\"2\"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal>`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Turtle's bare `2` is an integer; only a number with a `.`, such as `2.0`, is a decimal."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-8b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`\"2\"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string>`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The bare `2` is a number, so its datatype is `xsd:integer`, not `xsd:string`."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-8b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`\"2\"^^xsd:integer`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "N-Triples has no prefixes, so the datatype IRI is written in full."@en .

<#ch-rdf-syntaxes-9>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-rdf-syntaxes> ;
    schema:position 8 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Datasets and named graphs

An RDF dataset is one default graph plus zero or more named graphs, each a pair of a name (an IRI or blank node) and a graph. TriG extends Turtle with `GRAPH <iri> { … }` blocks, N-Quads adds an optional graph label to each N-Triples line, and JSON-LD can hold datasets too.

```trig
GRAPH <https://alice.example/profile/card> {
  <https://alice.example/profile/card#me> <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/name> "Alice" .
}
```

Datasets fit Solid clients well. Each pod document is one graph at its own URL. An app that loads several documents into one store usually keeps each in its own named graph, so it still knows which document to write a change back to. This is common client practice, not a Solid Protocol rule.

⚠ Named graphs have no formal semantics: the name "is not required to denote the graph", it is only paired with it. Different systems use them for provenance, licensing or one-graph-per-source."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-rdf-syntaxes-9a> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-9a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "An RDF app loads five Solid pod documents into one local store. Why put each document's triples in its own named graph?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "So the app knows which document each triple came from"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Writes go to a specific document, so a client needs to remember where each triple came from. One named graph per document is a common way to do that."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-9a-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-9a-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-9a-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-9a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Because the Solid Protocol requires clients to use TriG"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Servers serve Turtle and JSON-LD; how a client stores data locally is its own choice."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-9a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Because two documents can't share an IRI otherwise"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "IRIs are global, so the same IRI may appear in many graphs without any conflict."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-9a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "So the server can merge the five documents on save"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Each write goes to one document; the server never sees your local store."@en .

<#ch-rdf-syntaxes-10>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-rdf-syntaxes> ;
    schema:position 9 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## N3 and N3 Patch

Notation3 (N3) is a superset of Turtle with formulae (graphs in `{ }`), variables such as `?x`, and rules. It is a Community Group draft (Solid Protocol 0.11 cites its March 2024 version), not a W3C standard, and Solid uses it only for N3 Patch, sent as `text/n3` (applied in Steps 6.7-6.8):

```n3
@prefix solid: <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#> .
@prefix ex:    <http://www.example.org/terms#> .
_:rename a solid:InsertDeletePatch ;
  solid:where   { ?person ex:familyName "Garcia" . } ;
  solid:inserts { ?person ex:givenName "Alex" . } ;
  solid:deletes { ?person ex:givenName "Claudia" . } .
```

`?person` is a variable, as in SPARQL (Step 3.11): the server binds it by matching `where` against the document, and inserts and deletes may only use variables from `where`. `where` must match exactly one way; Step 6.8 shows what happens otherwise.

The patch itself may be a blank node (`_:rename`); the rule below concerns blank nodes inside `solid:inserts` and `solid:deletes`. A blank-node label is local to its document, so a patch can't use one to point at a stored node. Bind the node with a `where` variable or give it a fragment IRI such as `<#address>`.

Solid Protocol 0.11 answers 422 to a malformed patch: two patch resources, an unbound variable, or blank nodes in either formula. CSS 7.2 rejects blank nodes in either formula with 422. ⚠ The 2026 Editor's Draft allows fresh blank nodes in inserts, and NSS 6 accepts them."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-rdf-syntaxes-10a> ,
                         <#q-rdf-syntaxes-10b> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-10a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid pod document contains `<#me> schema:address [ schema:addressLocality \"Ghent\" ] .`. An app sends an N3 Patch whose `solid:deletes` is `{ _:a schema:addressLocality \"Ghent\" . }`. What does a server that follows Solid Protocol 0.11 do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It rejects the patch, because deletes may not contain blank nodes"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Blank-node labels are local to each document, so a patch can't use `_:a` to name a stored node, and the Solid Protocol forbids blank nodes in deletes. Bind the node with a variable in `where` instead, or give it an IRI such as `<#address>`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-10a-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-10a-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-10a-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-10a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It deletes the locality of every node in the document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A blank node in a patch is not a wildcard; use a variable bound in `where`."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-10a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It deletes the triple whose node is labelled `_:a` in the file"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Labels are local to each document, and the stored node has no label to match."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-10a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It treats `_:a` as the variable `?a` and deletes the match"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Blank nodes and variables differ; a variable such as `?a` must also be bound in `solid:where`."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-10b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front """A Solid server receives this N3 Patch. How does it know which node `?person` stands for?

```n3
_:rename a solid:InsertDeletePatch ;
  solid:where   { ?person ex:familyName "Garcia" . } ;
  solid:inserts { ?person ex:givenName "Alex" . } ;
  solid:deletes { ?person ex:givenName "Claudia" . } .
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It matches `where` against the document"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "N3 Patch variables work like SPARQL variables: the server matches the `where` patterns against the document, which must give exactly one binding, and reuses it in inserts and deletes."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-10b-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-10b-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-10b-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-10b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It applies the inserts and deletes to every subject"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A variable takes only the value found by matching `where`, which must match exactly once."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-10b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The client must swap `?person` for a real IRI first"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Variables are part of N3 Patch; the server binds them itself."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-10b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It creates a new node, as for a blank node `_:person`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`?person` is a variable, not a blank node; it names a node that already exists."@en .

<#ch-rdf-syntaxes-11>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-rdf-syntaxes> ;
    schema:position 10 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## SPARQL in one step

**SPARQL** is the W3C query language for RDF. SPARQL 1.1 is the current Recommendation; 1.2 is a Working Draft as of October 2026. A query is a set of **triple patterns** written like Turtle, with **variables** that start with `?`, just like N3 Patch's (Step 3.10). The engine finds every way to fill them so all patterns match:

```sparql
PREFIX ex: <https://vocab.example.org/memo#>
SELECT ?card ?front ?back WHERE {
  ?card a ex:Card ; ex:front ?front .
  OPTIONAL { ?card ex:back ?back }
}
```

`a` and `;` work as in Turtle (Step 3.2). A `SELECT` returns a table: each **row** binds the variables to one match. Data may be missing: a plain pattern drops rows that lack the triple, while `OPTIONAL` keeps them with the variable unbound.

Two more forms appear later. A **property path** follows a link repeatedly: `?a ex:parent+ ?b` matches every ancestor of `?a` (`+` is one or more steps, `*` zero or more); Step 4.9 uses `skos:broader+` this way. **SPARQL Update** changes data. Its simplest forms, `INSERT DATA` and `DELETE DATA`, list exact triples to add and remove, with no variables; other forms add a `WHERE` clause with variables. Step 6.9 uses it:

```sparql
PREFIX ex: <https://vocab.example.org/memo#>
DELETE DATA { <#c1> ex:front "Hi" } ;
INSERT DATA { <#c1> ex:front "Hello" }
```

Step 12.12 shows how to query pod documents."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-rdf-syntaxes-11a> ,
                         <#q-rdf-syntaxes-11b> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-11a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front """What does this SPARQL query return?

```sparql
SELECT ?card ?front ?back WHERE {
  ?card a ex:Card ; ex:front ?front .
  OPTIONAL { ?card ex:back ?back }
}
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:back "One row per match, giving values for `?card`, `?front` and `?back`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A SELECT query returns a table: each row binds the query's variables to values that make every pattern match."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-11a-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-11a-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-11a-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-11a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A new Turtle document holding only the matching cards"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is what a CONSTRUCT query does; SELECT returns rows of variable bindings."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-11a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "True or false, depending on whether any card exists"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is an ASK query; SELECT returns the matches themselves."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-11a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The list of documents in which the cards are stored"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "SELECT returns values bound to the variables, not where they came from."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-11b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front """Why is `ex:back` inside `OPTIONAL` in this SPARQL query?

```sparql
SELECT ?card ?front ?back WHERE {
  ?card a ex:Card ; ex:front ?front .
  OPTIONAL { ?card ex:back ?back }
}
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:back "So cards with no back triple still appear in the results"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A missing triple just means the value isn't known. OPTIONAL keeps rows that lack the value instead of dropping them."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-11b-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-11b-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-11b-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-11b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "So the server skips loading backs to save bandwidth"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "OPTIONAL is evaluated by the query engine and changes no network behaviour."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-11b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "So cards without a back are reported as invalid data"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "OPTIONAL keeps those rows; it does not validate anything."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-11b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "So the query also matches cards from other vocabularies"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Only the `ex:` predicates are matched; OPTIONAL just tolerates a missing value."@en .

<#ch-rdf-syntaxes-12>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-rdf-syntaxes> ;
    schema:position 11 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## RDF 1.2 (what's coming)

RDF 1.2 is the next version of RDF. Its main addition is the triple term: a triple used as the object of another triple, written `<<( s p o )>>` in Turtle 1.2. A triple term names a claim without asserting it, so the data does not say the claim is true. Statements about the claim hang off a resource linked to the triple term with `rdf:reifies`.

Annotation syntax, written `{| … |}` after a triple, asserts the triple and describes it at once:

```turtle
@prefix :        <#> .
@prefix rdf:     <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix foaf:    <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

:bob foaf:knows :alice {| dcterms:date "2024-05-01"^^xsd:date |} .  # asserted, with a date
:claim rdf:reifies <<( :carol foaf:knows :alice )>> .               # only named
```

The first line says Bob knows Alice. The second says nothing about whether Carol does.

⚠ As of October 2026, RDF 1.2 Concepts is a Candidate Recommendation (April 2026) and Turtle 1.2 a Working Draft; RDF 1.1 (2014) is still the Recommendation. The Solid Protocol doesn't require any RDF 1.2 feature, so don't rely on it in pods yet."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-rdf-syntaxes-12a> ,
                         <#q-rdf-syntaxes-12b> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-12a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Turtle 1.2, which line makes a document state that Dana knows Erik?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "`:dana foaf:knows :erik {| dcterms:source :party |} .`" ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Annotation syntax asserts the triple and describes it. A triple term only talks about the claim, and a string or an N3 formula asserts nothing in RDF."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-12a-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-12a-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-12a-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-12a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`:c1 rdf:reifies <<( :dana foaf:knows :erik )>> .`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A triple term only names the claim; it doesn't assert it."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-12a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`:c1 :text \":dana foaf:knows :erik\" .`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That object is just a string literal; no `foaf:knows` triple results."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-12a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`:erik :says { :dana foaf:knows :erik } .`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is an N3 formula, which quotes a graph and isn't Turtle."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-12b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Should a Solid app written in October 2026 store RDF 1.2 triple terms in users' pods?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Not yet: servers and libraries may not parse them"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "In October 2026 RDF 1.2 Concepts was a Candidate Recommendation and Turtle 1.2 a Working Draft, and pod support depends on each server's RDF library. Treat RDF 1.2 as coming, not as something to build on."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-12b-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-12b-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-12b-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-12b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes: the Solid Protocol requires RDF 1.2 support"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The Solid Protocol v0.11 doesn't require any RDF 1.2 features."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-12b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes: Turtle 1.1 parsers simply skip triple terms"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A 1.1 parser rejects the `<<( )>>` syntax as an error."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-12b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No: triple terms can only be written in N3"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Turtle 1.2, TriG 1.2 and other RDF 1.2 syntaxes define triple terms."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A Turtle file and a JSON-LD file describe Alice. When are they \"the same RDF data\"?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "When both parse to the same set of triples"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Syntaxes are just ways of writing a graph. Two documents mean the same when they produce the same triples, which is how a server can turn Turtle into JSON-LD."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r01-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "When both use the same IRI for the subject"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "They can share a subject and still say different things about it."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "When the JSON-LD's keys match the Turtle's prefixed names"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Prefixes and keys are shorthands; only the expanded IRIs and literals count."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "When the server can convert one into the other"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A server can convert any RDF document; that doesn't make two different documents equal."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Turtle `ex:height 1.68` and JSON-LD `\"height\": 1.68` (with `height` mapped to `ex:height`) appear in two documents. Do they give the same RDF triple?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "No: Turtle gives `xsd:decimal` and JSON gives `xsd:double`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A bare Turtle number with a decimal point is `xsd:decimal`, while a JSON number with a fraction becomes `xsd:double`. To agree, write the JSON-LD as a value object with a string value, `{\"@value\": \"1.68\", \"@type\": \"xsd:decimal\"}`; a context type alone still gives the invalid `\"1.68E0\"^^xsd:decimal`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r02-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes: `1.68` is the same number in every syntax"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The datatype is part of the literal, and the two syntaxes pick different ones."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No: JSON-LD turns all numbers into strings"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "JSON numbers become numeric literals, `xsd:integer` or `xsd:double`."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes: both syntaxes store numbers as JavaScript numbers"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "RDF literal types are not JavaScript types; the datatype is an IRI."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r03>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "An RDF app loads two documents into one store, and both contain the blank node label `_:b1`. How should the store treat them?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "As two different nodes, since labels are local to each document"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`_:b1` in one file and `_:b1` in another are different nodes. If something needs to be referred to from outside its document, give it an IRI such as `<#address>`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r03-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r03-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r03-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r03-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "As one node, since the labels are identical"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Blank-node labels are only meaningful inside the document they appear in."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r03-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "As an error that the app must fix by renaming one"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "RDF libraries normally give each parsed document's blank nodes fresh identities, so nothing needs renaming by hand."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r03-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "As the same node only if both documents are on one pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Blank-node scope is the document, not the server or pod."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r04>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid pod document at `https://alice.example/todo/list.ttl` contains `<#task1>`. An app GETs it with `Accept: application/ld+json` and parses the response with the document's URL as base. Which IRI does task1 denote after parsing?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "`https://alice.example/todo/list.ttl#task1`" ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Changing the syntax doesn't change the graph. Whether the server writes the `@id` in full or as a relative `#task1`, parsing it against the document's URL gives the same IRI the Turtle named."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r04-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r04-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r04-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r04-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`#task1`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "JSON-LD resolves a relative `@id` against the base, just as Turtle does."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r04-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`https://alice.example/todo/list.jsonld#task1`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The IRI comes from the document's URL, not from the format the server chose."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r04-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`https://alice.example/todo/#task1`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A fragment doesn't replace the last path segment; it is added to the whole document URL."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r05>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "An N3 Patch's `solid:where` contains `?card ex:front \"Hi\" .`, and a SPARQL `SELECT` contains `?card ex:front ?front .`. What does `?card` do in both?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It is a variable, bound to nodes that make the patterns match"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "N3 Patch borrowed SPARQL's variables: in both, `?card` is filled in by matching the patterns against the data. SELECT returns every match as a row, while an N3 Patch must match exactly once."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r05-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r05-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r05-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r05-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It is a blank node, local to the patch or query"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Blank nodes are written `_:card` or `[ ]`; `?card` is a variable that matches existing nodes."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r05-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It creates a new card each time the request runs"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Variables find nodes already in the data; they don't create any."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r05-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It stands for the document URL the request is sent to"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A variable can bind to any matching node, not to the request's target."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r06>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A server returns JSON-LD as a flat list of nodes: a note, and separately its author, linked by `@id`. Your UI wants one object with the author nested inside the note. Which jsonld.js call gives that shape?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "`jsonld.frame(doc, frame)`" ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Compaction fixes the names of keys; framing fixes the shape of the tree, matching nodes by example and embedding related ones, then compacting with the frame's context."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r06-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r06-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r06-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r06-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`jsonld.compact(doc, ctx)`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Compaction shortens keys and values but keeps the nodes' layout."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r06-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`jsonld.expand(doc)`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Expansion removes the context and gives full IRIs, still as separate nodes."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r06-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`jsonld.flatten(doc)`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Flattening does the opposite: it pulls every node out into one flat list."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r07>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front """A Turtle document at `https://alice.example/profile/card` declares the `foaf:` prefix. Which Turtle line gives the same triple as this N-Triples line?

```text
<https://alice.example/profile/card#me> <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/age> "34"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:back "`<#me> foaf:age 34 .`" ;
    solid-memo:backNote "N-Triples spells everything out; Turtle shortens it with a base, prefixes and bare numbers. A bare `34` is exactly `\"34\"^^xsd:integer`, and `<#me>` resolves to the full IRI against the document's URL."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r07-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r07-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r07-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r07-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`<#me> foaf:age \"34\" .`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A plain quoted `\"34\"` is an `xsd:string`, not an integer."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r07-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`<#me> foaf:age 34.0 .`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`34.0` is an `xsd:decimal`, a different literal from the integer `34`."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r07-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`<#me> foaf:age 3.4E1 .`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A number with an exponent is an `xsd:double`, a different literal from the integer `34`."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r08>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "An RDF app keeps triples from `https://alice.example/profile/card` and `https://alice.example/notes/a.ttl` in one store. The user edits a note. What lets the app know which Solid document to send the change to?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Keeping each document in a named graph named after its URL"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A dataset can hold one named graph per document. Naming each graph after the document's URL tells the app which document to write the change back to."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r08-d1> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r08-d2> ,
                          <#q-rdf-syntaxes-r08-d3> .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r08-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Looking up the subject's IRI in the default graph"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Once merged into one graph, triples no longer say which document they came from."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r08-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Asking the server which document holds the triple"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A Solid server serves documents; it has no lookup for where a triple lives."@en .

<#q-rdf-syntaxes-r08-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Writing the change to every document it loaded"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That would copy the triple into documents it doesn't belong to."@en .

# Chapter 4: Semantics and vocabularies.
<#ch-semantics>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    dcterms:title "Semantics and vocabularies"@en ;
    dcterms:description "What vocabularies give triples, how RDFS and OWL semantics behave in an open world, and how to pick, mint and evolve vocabularies for a Solid app."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-semantics-r01> ,
                              <#q-semantics-r02> ,
                              <#q-semantics-r03> ,
                              <#q-semantics-r04> ,
                              <#q-semantics-r05> .

<#ch-semantics-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-semantics> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Why vocabularies matter

A triple is subject, predicate and object, and its predicates and classes are IRIs. But a triple alone says nothing about what `foaf:name` *means*. A **vocabulary** (also called an ontology or schema) is a published set of IRIs with documented meanings, so two apps that have never met can agree on what `foaf:name` or `vcard:hasEmail` stands for. The RDF 1.1 Primer names FOAF, Dublin Core, schema.org and SKOS as common examples.

In Solid, shared vocabularies are the whole basis of interoperability, and the ecosystem explicitly encourages reusing standard vocabularies. The Solid specs only fix the **infrastructure** terms: where the user's storage is, who handles their login, what a folder contains, and who may read what. The WebID Profile spec explicitly allows triples "using any predicates". So whether an app stores your name as `foaf:name` or `vcard:fn` is not decided by Solid; it is decided by the vocabularies apps choose."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-semantics-1a> .

<#q-semantics-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "The Solid WebID Profile spec doesn't say which predicate holds a person's name. What does that mean for a Solid app developer?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Apps pick a vocabulary, such as `foaf:name` or `vcard:fn`."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Solid fixes infrastructure terms (storage, login, folder contents, access control) and leaves domain data to whatever vocabularies apps choose, so vocabulary choice is an interoperability decision."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-1a-d3> .

<#q-semantics-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Apps must use `foaf:name`, because profiles are FOAF documents."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Profiles often use FOAF, but the spec allows triples using any predicates."@en .

<#q-semantics-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Apps must store the name under both `foaf:name` and `vcard:fn`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Nothing requires both; some apps write several for compatibility, by choice."@en .

<#q-semantics-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The Solid Protocol defines a `solid:name` predicate for this."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The Solid specs standardise only infrastructure terms, not domain data like names."@en .

<#ch-semantics-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-semantics> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## RDFS in one table

RDF Schema (RDFS) is a small vocabulary for describing classes and properties. Its terms live in two namespaces: `rdf:` is `http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#` and `rdfs:` is `http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#`. RDF 1.2 (still a draft as of October 2026) keeps these terms and their meaning, and adds a few new ones, such as `rdf:reifies` and `rdfs:Proposition`.

| Term | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `rdfs:Class` / `rdf:Property` | The class of all classes / of all properties |
| `rdf:type` (`a` in Turtle) | This resource is an instance of that class |
| `rdfs:subClassOf` | Every instance of A is also an instance of B; transitive (A→B and B→C give A→C) |
| `rdfs:subPropertyOf` | Every pair linked by P is also linked by Q |
| `rdfs:domain` / `rdfs:range` | Subjects / objects of triples with this predicate are instances of C |
| `rdfs:label` / `rdfs:comment` | Human-readable name / description |
| `rdfs:isDefinedBy` | The resource that defines this term |

A tiny flashcard vocabulary (prefixes omitted):

```turtle
ex:Card      a rdfs:Class ;
             rdfs:label "Flashcard"@en .
ex:ClozeCard rdfs:subClassOf ex:Card .
ex:front     a rdf:Property ;
             rdfs:domain ex:Card ;
             rdfs:range  xsd:string ;
             rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://vocab.example.org/memo> .
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-semantics-2a> ,
                         <#q-semantics-2b> .

<#q-semantics-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "An RDFS vocabulary says `ex:front rdfs:domain ex:Card`. What does that say about a triple `X ex:front Y`?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "X is an `ex:Card`."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`rdfs:domain` describes the subjects of a property's triples, and `rdfs:range` describes the objects."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-2a-d3> .

<#q-semantics-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Y is an `ex:Card`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That would be `rdfs:range`; domain is about the subject."@en .

<#q-semantics-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`ex:front` is a subclass of `ex:Card`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`ex:front` is a property; domain links it to the class of its subjects, not to a class hierarchy."@en .

<#q-semantics-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`ex:front` can only have string values."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Domain is about X, not Y. Even `ex:front rdfs:range xsd:string` would only let a reasoner infer that Y is a string; it never forbids other values."@en .

<#q-semantics-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A pod contains `<#c7> a ex:ClozeCard .` Given `ex:ClozeCard rdfs:subClassOf ex:Card`, what does RDFS say about `<#c7>`?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It is also an `ex:Card`."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`rdfs:subClassOf` means every instance of the subclass is an instance of the superclass."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-2b-d3> .

<#q-semantics-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It is no longer an `ex:ClozeCard`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Subclassing adds a type; it never removes the original one."@en .

<#q-semantics-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing, because subclass triples are only documentation."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`rdfs:subClassOf` has formal meaning: instances of the subclass are instances of the superclass."@en .

<#q-semantics-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It is an `ex:Card` only if it has an `ex:front`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Subclass membership doesn't depend on which properties the resource has."@en .

<#ch-semantics-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-semantics> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Domain and range infer; they do not validate

RDFS semantics come as **entailment rules**. A **reasoner** is software that applies them to a graph to add the triples they license.

| Rule | If you have | You may infer |
|---|---|---|
| rdfs2 | `P rdfs:domain C . x P y .` | `x a C .` |
| rdfs3 | `P rdfs:range C . x P y .` | `y a C .` |
| rdfs7 | `P rdfs:subPropertyOf Q . x P y .` | `x Q y .` |
| rdfs9 | `A rdfs:subClassOf B . x a A .` | `x a B .` |

Now someone writes `<#alice> ex:front "hi" .`, where `<#alice>` is a person and `ex:front` has the domain `ex:Card`. An RDFS reasoner reports no error: by rdfs2, **Alice is a flashcard**. Domain and range describe; they don't constrain. Checking data takes a shape language such as SHACL.

Entailment is **monotonic**: adding triples only adds conclusions, never retracts them.

No Solid spec requires a server to reason; it serves the triples you wrote. So an app that needs `x a ex:Card` mustn't assume the inferred triple is in the document."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-semantics-3a> ,
                         <#q-semantics-3b> .

<#q-semantics-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Given `ex:front rdfs:domain ex:Card`, an app writes `<#alice> ex:front \"hi\" .` about a person. What does an RDFS reasoner do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It infers that `<#alice>` is an `ex:Card`."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Rule rdfs2 turns a domain declaration into a new type triple for the subject; domain and range are inference, not validation."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-3a-d3> .

<#q-semantics-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It reports a domain violation for `<#alice>`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "RDFS has no notion of violation for domain; it only adds conclusions."@en .

<#q-semantics-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It deletes the triple because Alice is a person."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Reasoners never remove triples; entailment is monotonic."@en .

<#q-semantics-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It ignores the triple because `<#alice>` lacks a type."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "rdfs2 applies to every subject of `ex:front`, typed or not; it simply adds the type."@en .

<#q-semantics-3b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app fetches a pod document and looks for resources typed `ex:Card`. The document only says `<#c7> a ex:ClozeCard`, and the vocabulary says `ex:ClozeCard rdfs:subClassOf ex:Card`. What happens?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The app misses `<#c7>`, since only `ex:ClozeCard` is stated."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Inferred triples exist only if someone computes them, so unless the app applies the subclass rule itself it must look for every class it accepts."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-3b-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-3b-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-3b-d3> .

<#q-semantics-3b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server adds `<#c7> a ex:Card` before sending the document."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid servers aren't required to reason; they serve the triples that were written."@en .

<#q-semantics-3b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The app finds `<#c7>` because `ClozeCard` contains the word `Card`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "IRIs are compared as whole strings; their spelling carries no meaning."@en .

<#q-semantics-3b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The app finds `<#c7>` because Turtle parsers expand subclasses."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Parsers turn text into triples; they don't apply RDFS rules."@en .

<#ch-semantics-4>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-semantics> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## A little OWL

OWL 2 adds richer modelling on top of RDFS. Its namespace is `http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#`, written `owl:`.

- `owl:equivalentClass` / `owl:equivalentProperty`: two classes have the same instances, or two properties link the same pairs.
- `owl:inverseOf`: if A `ex:hasChild` B, then B `ex:hasParent` A.
- `owl:FunctionalProperty`: each subject has at most one value for the property.
- `owl:sameAs`: two IRIs denote the same individual, so what's said of one holds for the other.
- `owl:differentFrom`: two individuals are explicitly distinct.

```turtle
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix ex:  <https://vocab.example.org/family#> .

ex:hasParent owl:inverseOf ex:hasChild .
```

Solid apps rarely run OWL reasoners. You still need to *read* OWL, because FOAF, vCard and PROV-O, which Solid apps reuse every day, are published in it."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-semantics-4a> ,
                         <#q-semantics-4b> .

<#q-semantics-4a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A vocabulary says `ex:hasParent owl:inverseOf ex:hasChild`, and the data says `<#ann> ex:hasChild <#bo>`. What may an OWL reasoner infer?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "`<#bo> ex:hasParent <#ann>`" ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`owl:inverseOf` lets a reasoner derive the reverse relationship, with subject and object swapped."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-4a-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-4a-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-4a-d3> .

<#q-semantics-4a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`<#ann> ex:hasParent <#bo>`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The inverse swaps subject and object, so Bo is the one with a parent."@en .

<#q-semantics-4a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`<#ann> owl:sameAs <#bo>`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Inverse properties say nothing about identity."@en .

<#q-semantics-4a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`<#bo> ex:hasChild <#ann>`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That reverses the data with the same property, making Bo Ann's parent."@en .

<#q-semantics-4b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Why should a Solid developer learn to read OWL even if their app runs no reasoner?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Vocabularies it reuses, like FOAF and vCard, are written in OWL."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "FOAF, vCard and PROV-O are published as OWL, so understanding their axioms tells you what the terms you reuse mean."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-4b-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-4b-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-4b-d3> .

<#q-semantics-4b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Apps must declare an OWL class for every type they write."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "RDF needs no declarations; any IRI can be used as a class."@en .

<#q-semantics-4b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The Solid Protocol requires apps to send OWL with each write."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Writes carry ordinary RDF data; there is no such requirement."@en .

<#q-semantics-4b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "OWL replaces Turtle as the storage format for pods."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "OWL is a vocabulary for modelling, not a serialisation; pods serve Turtle and JSON-LD."@en .

<#ch-semantics-5>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-semantics> ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Open world

A database usually treats a missing fact as false (the **closed-world** assumption). RDF and OWL follow the **open-world** assumption: a missing fact "may simply be missing (but possibly true)". A profile with no `vcard:hasEmail` doesn't mean Alice has no email. It means this document doesn't say.

Solid makes this very concrete. Access control hides some documents, and a server may even answer 404 rather than reveal that a document exists, so data you can't read can look exactly like data that doesn't exist. An app only ever sees the documents it fetched and was allowed to read, never "everything about Alice".

So an app should phrase what it shows carefully: "no email in this profile", not "Alice has no email". When it really needs a value, it can ask the user, or look in other documents the user points it to."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-semantics-5a> .

<#q-semantics-5a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app reads Bob's profile and finds no `vcard:hasEmail`. What can it conclude?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "This document doesn't state an email for Bob."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Under the open-world assumption a missing triple means \"unknown\", and in Solid it may live in a document the app can't read."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-5a-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-5a-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-5a-d3> .

<#q-semantics-5a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Bob has no email address."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Under the open-world assumption a missing triple means unknown, not false."@en .

<#q-semantics-5a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The profile is invalid and should be rejected."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A missing optional triple doesn't make RDF data invalid."@en .

<#q-semantics-5a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Bob's email is in a document the app can't read."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is possible, but nothing in the data tells the app it is true."@en .

<#ch-semantics-6>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-semantics> ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## No unique names

OWL also makes **no unique name assumption**: "OWL does not make the assumption that different names are names for different individuals". That suits the web, where people coin IRIs for the same thing without knowing it. A person may have two WebIDs, or a contact may appear in two address books under different IRIs. If they are the same, someone has to say so with `owl:sameAs`, or the app has to handle it.

This combines with the open world in surprising ways. If `ex:hasHusband` is functional and a subject has two different IRIs as husbands, an OWL reasoner doesn't report an error. It concludes that the two IRIs name the same person. Only if the data also says the two are `owl:differentFrom` does it find a contradiction."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-semantics-6a> .

<#q-semantics-6a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "`ex:hasHusband` is an `owl:FunctionalProperty`. The data says `<#eve> ex:hasHusband <#adam>` and `<#eve> ex:hasHusband <#a2>`. What does an OWL reasoner conclude?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "`<#adam>` and `<#a2>` are the same individual."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "With no unique name assumption, \"at most one value\" plus two IRIs means the IRIs name one individual; it would only be a contradiction if they were stated `owl:differentFrom`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-6a-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-6a-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-6a-d3> .

<#q-semantics-6a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The data is inconsistent and must be rejected."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Without a unique name assumption, two IRIs can denote one person, so there's no clash."@en .

<#q-semantics-6a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The second triple overwrites the first one."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "RDF graphs are sets of triples; reasoning never overwrites data."@en .

<#q-semantics-6a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`<#eve>` has two husbands after all."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A functional property allows at most one value, so the values must be the same individual."@en .

<#ch-semantics-7>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-semantics> ;
    schema:position 6 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Solid's infrastructure vocabularies

A handful of vocabularies make Solid itself work. Each comes back in later chapters:

| Prefix | Namespace | Used for |
|---|---|---|
| `ldp:` | `http://www.w3.org/ns/ldp#` | Containers and `ldp:contains`, and `ldp:inbox` |
| `pim:` | `http://www.w3.org/ns/pim/space#` | `pim:storage` and `pim:preferencesFile` in profiles |
| `solid:` | `http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#` | `solid:oidcIssuer` (login), N3 Patch, type indexes |
| `acl:` | `http://www.w3.org/ns/auth/acl#` | Web Access Control (WAC) rules |
| `stat:` | `http://www.w3.org/ns/posix/stat#` | File-like metadata such as `stat:size` in container listings |
| `as:` | `https://www.w3.org/ns/activitystreams#` | Change notifications; also a general vocabulary of activities and objects |

Only the full IRI matters, not the prefix. On Community Solid Server (CSS) 7.2 (2026), for example, container listings bind the `stat:` namespace to the prefix `posix:` and write `posix:size`.

These vocabularies also reuse each other. A WAC rule that grants public access says `acl:agentClass foaf:Agent`, using FOAF's class of all agents."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-semantics-7a> .

<#q-semantics-7a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Why does a Solid Web Access Control (WAC) rule that makes a document public contain `foaf:Agent`?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The access control vocabulary reuses FOAF's class of all agents."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`acl:agentClass foaf:Agent` grants access to every agent, a small example of one vocabulary reusing another."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-7a-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-7a-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-7a-d3> .

<#q-semantics-7a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only people described with FOAF profiles get access."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`acl:agentClass foaf:Agent` means everyone, whatever vocabulary describes them."@en .

<#q-semantics-7a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "FOAF is the access control language of Solid."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The access control vocabulary is `acl:`; it only borrows one FOAF class."@en .

<#q-semantics-7a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server converts public rules into FOAF profiles."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Nothing is converted; the rule simply names a FOAF class."@en .

<#ch-semantics-8>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-semantics> ;
    schema:position 7 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## General vocabularies you will reuse

Beyond the infrastructure terms, most app data is written with a few general vocabularies:

| Vocabulary | Namespace | Typical terms |
|---|---|---|
| FOAF | `http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/` | `foaf:name`, `foaf:knows` |
| vCard | `http://www.w3.org/2006/vcard/ns#` | `vcard:fn`, `vcard:hasEmail`, `vcard:Group` |
| schema.org | `https://schema.org/` | `schema:Person`, `schema:name` |
| Dublin Core (`dcterms:`) | `http://purl.org/dc/terms/` | `dcterms:title`, `dcterms:created` |
| SKOS | `http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#` | `skos:Concept`, `skos:broader` |
| PROV-O | `http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#` | `prov:Entity`, `prov:wasAttributedTo` |
| DCAT 3 | `http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#` | `dcat:Catalog`, `dcat:Dataset` |

FOAF's last version is 0.99 (2014), but it's embedded in Solid profiles, and vCard is recommended for contacts in Solid. Dublin Core comes from the Dublin Core Metadata Initiative (DCMI). It has an older namespace, `dc:` (`http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/`), whose terms have no formal ranges, and the newer `dcterms:`, which DCMI encourages.

Prefixes can mislead here. On CSS 7.2 (2026), resource metadata binds the prefix `dc:` to `http://purl.org/dc/terms/`, the `dcterms:` namespace. Read the prefix declaration, not the prefix."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-semantics-8a> .

<#q-semantics-8a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app records who made a document and when. Which pair of common RDF vocabularies fits best?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Dublin Core and PROV-O."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Dublin Core covers creators and dates (`dcterms:creator`, `dcterms:created`) and PROV-O covers provenance in depth. Others can do it too, such as schema.org (`schema:creator`, `schema:dateCreated`) and Activity Streams (`as:attributedTo`, `as:published`)."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-8a-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-8a-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-8a-d3> .

<#q-semantics-8a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "SKOS and vCard."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "SKOS organises topics and vCard describes contacts; neither records who made a document or when."@en .

<#q-semantics-8a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "FOAF and SKOS."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "FOAF describes people and SKOS organises topics; neither records when a document was made."@en .

<#q-semantics-8a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "FOAF and vCard."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Both describe people and their contact details; neither records when a document was made."@en .

<#ch-semantics-9>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-semantics> ;
    schema:position 8 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Two traps in popular vocabularies

**Two schemes.** Both `http://schema.org/` and `https://schema.org/` are in use, and schema.org says both are fine. To RDF they are different IRIs, so a reader should accept both. As of October 2026, for example, the Linked Open Vocabularies (LOV) catalogue lists schema.org's namespace as `http://schema.org/`, while the table in the previous step uses `https://schema.org/`. Also, schema.org's expected types are "closer to 'guidelines' than to formal rules"; where a Person is expected you may get a text string.

**SKOS hierarchies.** `skos:broader` links a concept to its direct parent, and it is **not** transitive. SKOS defines `skos:broaderTransitive` as a transitive superproperty of `skos:broader`, but by convention it is "not used to make assertions"; it exists so that a reasoner can infer the full chain. Pods therefore hold only direct links (`skos:broader` / `skos:narrower`). With no reasoner, your app follows `skos:broader` hop by hop to get every ancestor. In SPARQL, the property path `skos:broader+` means one or more hops."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-semantics-9a> ,
                         <#q-semantics-9b> .

<#q-semantics-9a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app looks for `https://schema.org/name`, but another app wrote `http://schema.org/name`. What happens?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "They don't match; read both forms to be safe."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "schema.org accepts both schemes, but to RDF they are different IRIs, so robust readers accept both."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-9a-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-9a-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-9a-d3> .

<#q-semantics-9a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "They match because RDF ignores the scheme."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "IRIs are compared as whole strings, so http and https are different predicates."@en .

<#q-semantics-9a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "They match if both files bind the prefix `schema:`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A prefix is only shorthand; it expands to whichever full IRI it was bound to."@en .

<#q-semantics-9a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The http form is invalid and no app writes it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "schema.org says both forms are fine, and both appear in real data."@en .

<#q-semantics-9b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A pod says `:French skos:broader :Languages` and `:Languages skos:broader :Humanities`. An app without a reasoner must list every topic under `:Humanities`, including `:French`. What should it do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Follow `skos:broader` links repeatedly."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`skos:broader` gives direct parents only; `skos:broaderTransitive` is for inference, so an app without a reasoner walks the `broader` chain itself."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-9b-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-9b-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-9b-d3> .

<#q-semantics-9b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Query `skos:broader` once; it is transitive."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`broader` isn't transitive, so one hop finds only `:Languages`."@en .

<#q-semantics-9b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Query the `skos:broaderTransitive` triples in the pod."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "By convention nobody writes them; they exist only if a reasoner infers them."@en .

<#q-semantics-9b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Query `rdfs:subClassOf` instead."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "SKOS concepts are individuals linked by SKOS properties, not classes."@en .

<#ch-semantics-10>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-semantics> ;
    schema:position 9 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Reuse first, mint last, and how to find terms

The W3C Data on the Web Best Practices say (BP15): "Use terms from shared vocabularies, preferably standardized ones". Solid guidance agrees: mint a custom vocabulary only for a domain "for which nothing else exists".

A practical checklist:
1. A Solid infrastructure term (`ldp:`, `pim:`, `solid:`, `acl:`)? Use it.
2. What do other Solid apps already write? As of October 2026, the Solid Data Modules package read/write code for bookmarks, chats and contacts, with profiles and tasks in progress, so their code shows the community's conventions.
3. A W3C, DCMI or schema.org term? Search Linked Open Vocabularies (LOV), which as of October 2026 indexes over 900 vocabularies and searches their terms, or schema.org's type list. prefix.cc maps a prefix to its namespace, but its certificate expired on 31 December 2025 and was still expired in October 2026; use LOV's vocabulary pages for prefixes instead, and don't click through a browser warning.
4. Only then mint your own, linked with `rdfs:subClassOf` or `rdfs:subPropertyOf`.

In October 2026, a LOV term search for `due date` listed, among its first results:

| Term | IRI | Label |
|---|---|---|
| `ocds:dueDate` | `http://purl.org/onto-ocds/ocds#dueDate` | Due date |
| `lto:P3013` | `https://w3id.org/lto#P3013` | due date |
| `ncal:due` | `http://www.semanticdesktop.org/ontologies/2007/04/02/ncal#due` | due |

Read a candidate's vocabulary before reusing it. Skipping all this costs interoperability: if one contacts app writes `vcard:fn` and another reads only `schema:name`, each sees an empty address book in the same pod."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-semantics-10a> ,
                         <#q-semantics-10b> .

<#q-semantics-10a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app saves bookmarks and needs a term for the bookmarked page's address. No Solid infrastructure term fits. Following reuse-first practice, where do you look next?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "At what other Solid apps already write for bookmarks."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Data other Solid apps already write is what yours must interoperate with, so their conventions (for bookmarks, the Solid Data Modules' code) come before general searches in LOV or schema.org."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-10a-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-10a-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-10a-d3> .

<#q-semantics-10a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "At prefix.cc, which explains what each term means."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "prefix.cc maps prefixes to namespaces; it doesn't explain terms."@en .

<#q-semantics-10a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nowhere; mint `ex:url` and let other apps map it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Minting is the last step, after checking what already exists."@en .

<#q-semantics-10a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "At the Solid Protocol, which defines app data terms."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid only standardises infrastructure terms, not domain data."@en .

<#q-semantics-10b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid flashcard app needs a \"review interval\" property, and no existing vocabulary has one. What's the best practice?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Mint it in your own namespace and document it."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Reuse when a term really fits; when nothing exists, mint a term in a namespace you control, with labels and definitions."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-10b-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-10b-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-10b-d3> .

<#q-semantics-10b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Reuse `foaf:age` because it is also a number."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Reusing a term with a different meaning corrupts the data for every app that knows FOAF."@en .

<#q-semantics-10b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Store it in a plain JSON field beside the RDF."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That hides it from every RDF reader and isn't Linked Data."@en .

<#q-semantics-10b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Add it to the `solid:` namespace yourself."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "You don't control that namespace, and it's for infrastructure terms."@en .

<#ch-semantics-11>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-semantics> ;
    schema:position 10 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Choosing a namespace

The W3C's Best Practice Recipes for Publishing RDF Vocabularies put it this way: "The choice of URI namespace is a fundamental decision you make early in the design of your vocabulary." With a **hash namespace** (`…/vocab#Term`, like SKOS) the whole vocabulary is one document, which suits small vocabularies. With a **slash namespace** (`…/vocab/Term`, like FOAF) each term can be served on its own, which suits large or fast-changing ones. Slash IRIs are usually served with a 303 redirect or content negotiation. Give every term `rdfs:label`, `rdfs:comment` and `rdfs:isDefinedBy`.

Term IRIs end up in every pod that ever used them, so host them on a domain you'll control for decades. As of October 2026 the `shapetrees.org` address of the Shape Trees spec redirects to an unrelated website, and the spec now lives on GitHub. The Solid technical reports index still links the old address, which shows how far a dead domain spreads."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-semantics-11a> ,
                         <#q-semantics-11b> .

<#q-semantics-11a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "You are publishing an RDF vocabulary of 2,000 terms that changes every month. Which namespace style fits?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Slash, so each term can be served on its own."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The W3C recipes for publishing vocabularies recommend hash namespaces for small vocabularies and slash namespaces for large or frequently changing ones."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-11a-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-11a-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-11a-d3> .

<#q-semantics-11a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Hash, so the whole vocabulary is one document."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Every lookup would then fetch all 2,000 terms; hash suits small vocabularies."@en .

<#q-semantics-11a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Hash, because slash IRIs aren't allowed in RDF."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Both are ordinary IRIs; RDF accepts either."@en .

<#q-semantics-11a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Either; you can switch styles later."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Term IRIs end up in data, so the style is fixed once they are used."@en .

<#q-semantics-11b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Why does the domain that hosts an RDF vocabulary matter so much?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Its term IRIs stay in pod data for decades."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Once data uses your IRIs they can't be changed, so a lost domain leaves those terms undocumented, as happened to the Shape Trees spec's old `shapetrees.org` address."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-11b-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-11b-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-11b-d3> .

<#q-semantics-11b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Apps look up the domain's owner before trusting the terms."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Apps match term IRIs as strings; no ownership check happens."@en .

<#q-semantics-11b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Each read of a triple fetches the vocabulary online."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Apps match IRIs as strings and seldom dereference them."@en .

<#q-semantics-11b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only W3C domains may host RDF vocabularies."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Anyone can publish a vocabulary on a domain they control."@en .

<#ch-semantics-12>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-semantics> ;
    schema:position 11 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Evolving a vocabulary

Never delete or redefine a term; mark it `owl:deprecated true` and point to its replacement. The Solid terms vocabulary does exactly this for `solid:inbox`, deprecated in favour of `ldp:inbox`. Fetched as Turtle from `http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms` in October 2026, the term reads (prefixes omitted; in this vocabulary `dc:` is bound to `http://purl.org/dc/terms/`, the `dcterms:` namespace):

```turtle
solid:inbox
    owl:deprecated true ;
    a rdf:Property ;
    dc:issued "2015-11-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dc:modified "2016-02-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dc:modified "2018-10-25"^^xsd:date ;
    owl:equivalentProperty ldp:inbox ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf ldp:inbox ;
    rdfs:comment "Deprecated pointer to a Linked Data Notifications inbox; please use http://www.w3.org/ns/ldp#inbox instead."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#> ;
    rdfs:label "inbox (deprecated)"@en .
```

`owl:equivalentProperty` fits because the meaning didn't change: old `solid:inbox` data still means an inbox. The `rdfs:subPropertyOf` line lets even a plain RDFS reasoner (rule rdfs7) read old `solid:inbox` data as `ldp:inbox`. When the successor means something different, say only "use this one now" with `dcterms:isReplacedBy`.

Putting a version in the namespace (`…/v2#front`) is a trade-off. It makes breaking changes explicit, but it splits the data: `…/v1#front` and `…/v2#front` are different IRIs, so readers of `v1` terms don't see `v2` data. One flashcard app, for example, adds new terms to its `v1` namespace and keeps a new namespace for breaking changes only."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-semantics-12a> ,
                         <#q-semantics-12b> ,
                         <#q-semantics-12c> .

<#q-semantics-12a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A term in your published RDF vocabulary turns out to be badly designed. What should you do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Mark it `owl:deprecated` and point to a replacement."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Term IRIs are baked into data you don't control, so deprecate and point onward instead of deleting or redefining."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-12a-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-12a-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-12a-d3> .

<#q-semantics-12a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Delete it from the vocabulary document."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Existing pods still contain it, and readers lose its meaning."@en .

<#q-semantics-12a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Quietly change its meaning to the better design."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Old data would then be read with a meaning its writers never intended."@en .

<#q-semantics-12a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Rename it and update every pod yourself."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "You can't reach every pod, and other apps still use the old IRI."@en .

<#q-semantics-12b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "You move the RDF term `ex:front` from `…/v1#front` to `…/v2#front`. An older app reads only `v1` terms. What does it see in cards written with `v2`?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "No fronts, because `v2#front` is a different predicate."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A versioned namespace mints new IRIs, so readers of the old ones silently miss the new data."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-12b-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-12b-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-12b-d3> .

<#q-semantics-12b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The same fronts, since only the version changed."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "IRIs are compared as whole strings, so `v1#front` and `v2#front` are different predicates."@en .

<#q-semantics-12b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The fronts, once it follows a redirect to `v2`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Apps match IRIs in the data; they don't follow redirects to rename predicates."@en .

<#q-semantics-12b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An error telling it to upgrade."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Nothing reports the mismatch; the data simply doesn't match its query."@en .

<#q-semantics-12c>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A new Solid app advertises the user's inbox in their profile. Which predicate should it write?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "`ldp:inbox`" ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The Solid terms vocabulary marks `solid:inbox` as deprecated and points to `ldp:inbox`, which the WebID Profile spec uses. When reading, also accept `solid:inbox` from older profiles."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-12c-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-12c-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-12c-d3> .

<#q-semantics-12c-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`solid:inbox`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It is deprecated in the Solid terms vocabulary in favour of `ldp:inbox`."@en .

<#q-semantics-12c-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`pim:storage`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That points to the user's storage, not to a place for messages."@en .

<#q-semantics-12c-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`solid:oidcIssuer`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That names the login provider, not a mailbox."@en .

<#q-semantics-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "An RDFS vocabulary says `ex:front rdfs:domain ex:Card`, and a Solid app needs every card to have a front. A pod holds `<#c1> a ex:Card` and nothing more. What can the app conclude?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Only that this document states no front for `<#c1>`."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "RDFS and OWL describe data in an open world: a missing value is unknown and nothing in them checks it is there, so the app tests for it itself or uses a shape language such as SHACL."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-r01-d3> .

<#q-semantics-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The data is invalid, because the domain declaration requires a front."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Domain only licenses inferences about subjects; it never requires anything."@en .

<#q-semantics-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`<#c1>` has no front, so the card can be deleted."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Open world: a missing triple means unknown, not false."@en .

<#q-semantics-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A reasoner will add the missing front by rule rdfs2."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "rdfs2 infers a type for subjects of `ex:front`; it can't invent values."@en .

<#q-semantics-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "You mint `ex:Deck` and declare `ex:Deck rdfs:subClassOf dcat:Dataset`. How do you make sure Solid apps that only know DCAT find your decks?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Also type each deck as `dcat:Dataset` in the data."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Linking your vocabulary documents the meaning, but readers without reasoners only see triples that are written, so write the reused type too."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-r02-d3> .

<#q-semantics-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing else; the server infers `dcat:Dataset`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid servers aren't required to reason, so the inferred type isn't there."@en .

<#q-semantics-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Use `owl:sameAs` between `ex:Deck` and `dcat:Dataset`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`sameAs` is for individuals, and a deck class isn't identical to all datasets."@en .

<#q-semantics-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Declare `ex:Deck owl:equivalentClass dcat:Dataset` instead."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It would also claim every dataset is a deck, and without a reasoner the `dcat:Dataset` type still isn't in the data."@en .

<#q-semantics-r03>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "One person has two WebIDs, and a Solid contacts app shows them as two contacts. What would let the app merge them?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "An `owl:sameAs` triple between them."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Identity between IRIs must be stated, and since no Solid server reasons for you, the app has to act on `owl:sameAs` itself."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-r03-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-r03-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-r03-d3> .

<#q-semantics-r03-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing; two IRIs always mean two things."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "RDF and OWL make no unique name assumption."@en .

<#q-semantics-r03-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Two contacts having the same `foaf:name`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Equal names don't prove identity; two people can share one."@en .

<#q-semantics-r03-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`owl:differentFrom` between the two WebIDs."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That states they are distinct, the opposite of what you need."@en .

<#q-semantics-r04>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Your published v1 vocabulary's `ex:author` holds an author's name as a string. From now on, apps should link to the author's WebID instead. What do you do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Link WebIDs with `dcterms:creator`; mark `ex:author` `owl:deprecated`, point onward with `dcterms:isReplacedBy`; read both."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A WebID link means something different from a name string, so it needs another property, and `dcterms:isReplacedBy` points onward without claiming equivalence. Keep reading both, since old data stays in pods."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-r04-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-r04-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-r04-d3> .

<#q-semantics-r04-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Change the range of `ex:author` to `foaf:Agent`, so it holds WebIDs from now on, and keep reading only the new form."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Old data would then be read with a meaning its writers never intended."@en .

<#q-semantics-r04-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Declare `ex:author owl:equivalentProperty dcterms:creator`, and write WebIDs in either of the two properties."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Equivalence claims the same meaning, but a WebID link is not a name string."@en .

<#q-semantics-r04-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Write the WebID as a string in `ex:author`, so old readers still work, and keep the vocabulary unchanged."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A string is a literal, not a link, so apps can't follow it."@en .

<#q-semantics-r05>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Carol's contacts were written by a Solid app that uses `schema:name` with `http://schema.org/`. Your app reads only `vcard:fn` and lists no contacts. What is the most likely cause, and the fix?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A vocabulary mismatch; also read the common alternative terms."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Apps that chose different vocabularies see each other's data as missing, so read the common alternatives (here `vcard:fn` and both schemes of `schema:name`), or agree on terms first."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-semantics-r05-d1> ,
                          <#q-semantics-r05-d2> ,
                          <#q-semantics-r05-d3> .

<#q-semantics-r05-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Carol has no contacts; show an empty list."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Open world: missing triples mean unknown, and here the names are in the data under another predicate."@en .

<#q-semantics-r05-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A server filter; ask for schema.org access."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Servers serve the triples that were written; they don't filter by vocabulary."@en .

<#q-semantics-r05-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Missing equivalence; add `owl:equivalentProperty` to Carol's pod."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Your app runs no reasoner, so the triple changes nothing, and you may not control her pod."@en .

# Chapter 5: Pods, storages and containers.
<#ch-pods>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    dcterms:title "Pods, storages and containers"@en ;
    dcterms:description "How the Solid Protocol structures a pod into storages, containers and auxiliary resources, and how apps find their way around one by following `Link` headers instead of guessing URLs."@en ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-pods-r01> ,
                              <#q-pods-r02> ,
                              <#q-pods-r03> ,
                              <#q-pods-r04> ,
                              <#q-pods-r05> .

<#ch-pods-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-pods> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## A reference pod on CSS 7.2

The Solid Protocol says how any pod behaves, but servers differ in the details. So this course shows real exchanges, recorded in October 2026 on Community Solid Server (CSS) 7.2.0, the Solid community's open-source server. It ran at `http://localhost:3000/` with one pod, for an agent called alice:

| What | URL |
|---|---|
| Her pod | `http://localhost:3000/alice/` |
| Her WebID | `http://localhost:3000/alice/profile/card#me` |

Her profile is public, so an anonymous request reads it. On CSS 7.2 (prefixes and some triples left out):

```http
GET /alice/profile/card HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:3000

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: text/turtle
WAC-Allow: user="read",public="read"

<http://localhost:3000/alice/profile/card#me>
    solid:oidcIssuer <http://localhost:3000/>;
    a foaf:Person.
```

A request for protected data must carry a login proof, made anew for every request (Chapter 7). Without one, CSS answers `401 Unauthorized`. Examples marked "as alice" carried her proof.

Two more CSS 7.2 behaviours are worth knowing. Its default configuration keeps every account and pod in memory, so a restart loses them; `-c @css:config/file.json -f data/` stores them in files instead (Step 12.3). And it accepts `http` WebIDs only on hosts ending in `localhost`: a request to `127.0.0.1` is silently treated as anonymous."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-pods-1a> ,
                         <#q-pods-1b> .

<#q-pods-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front """On Community Solid Server 7.2 (2026), an anonymous `HEAD` request for alice's private container `http://localhost:3000/alice/profile/` got this response. What must the client do to read the container?

```http
HTTP/1.1 401 Unauthorized
WWW-Authenticate: Bearer scope="openid webid"
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Send it again with a login proof from an agent allowed to read it"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "401 means \"who are you?\": the resource needs credentials the request didn't carry. Public data, such as her profile document `/alice/profile/card`, needs none."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-1a-d3> .

<#q-pods-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Send it again as `GET`, since a `HEAD` request always needs a login"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The method doesn't decide whether you must log in; the access rules of the data do."@en .

<#q-pods-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Send it again to `/alice/profile`, without the trailing slash"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is a different URL; on CSS 7.2 nothing exists there, and anonymously it also gets 401."@en .

<#q-pods-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Send it again with `Accept: text/turtle` to ask for a public format"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The format asked for doesn't change who may read the resource; only credentials do."@en .

<#q-pods-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Community Solid Server 7.2 (2026) was started with its default configuration, `npx @solid/community-server@7`. After a restart, every account and pod on it is gone. Why?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The default configuration keeps all data in memory only"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Starting CSS with `-c @css:config/file.json -f data/` stores data in files instead, so it survives a restart."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-1b-d3> .

<#q-pods-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "CSS deletes pods that nobody has logged in to for an hour"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "There is no such expiry; the data lived only in the server process."@en .

<#q-pods-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The login credentials expired and now hide every pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Expired credentials don't hide data; the pods themselves no longer exist."@en .

<#q-pods-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A restart resets all access rules, making every pod private"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Access rules lived in memory too, but the pods are gone, not hidden."@en .

<#ch-pods-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-pods> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## The Solid Protocol as a hub spec

The Solid Protocol tells server and app builders how a pod behaves. In its own words, it "connects a set of specifications that, together, provide applications with secure and permissioned access to externally stored data in an interoperable way". Think of it as a hub: it adds few new inventions and mostly ties existing standards together: data formats such as Turtle and JSON-LD (Chapter 3), containers via Linked Data Platform (this chapter), reading and editing over HTTP, including N3 Patch (Chapter 6), login with WebID and Solid-OIDC (Chapter 7), access control with WAC and ACP (Chapters 8-9), notifications (Step 5.11, Chapter 10) and browser rules such as CORS.

As of October 2026 the latest published version is **v0.11.0** (12 May 2024), a Draft Community Group Report. A **v0.12.0 editor's draft** (dated 9 September 2026 when checked in October 2026) exists but is not endorsed. The protocol is "not a W3C Standard nor is it on the W3C Standards Track".

Specs use MUST (required), SHOULD (expected unless there's a good reason) and MAY (optional), so a MAY feature might be missing on a given server."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-pods-2a> ,
                         <#q-pods-2b> .

<#q-pods-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Which description best fits the Solid Protocol?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A hub spec tying existing web standards together for pods"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The Solid Protocol connects HTTP, LDP, RDF syntaxes, WebID, Solid-OIDC, WAC and ACP, and notifications into one coherent set of rules for storages and apps."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-2a-d3> .

<#q-pods-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A new network protocol that replaces HTTP for pods"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid builds on HTTP (RFC 9110 and 9112) rather than replacing it."@en .

<#q-pods-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A finished W3C Recommendation that every server must pass"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "As of 2026 it is a Community Group draft, not on the W3C Standards Track."@en .

<#q-pods-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A JavaScript library that apps install to talk to pods"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It is a specification; libraries such as those in Chapter 12 implement it."@en .

<#q-pods-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "The Solid Protocol marks a feature as MAY. What should an app assume about it?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Some servers may lack it, so check before relying on it"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "MUST is required, SHOULD is expected unless there's a good reason, and MAY is optional, so robust apps detect MAY features (often from headers) before using them."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-2b-d3> .

<#q-pods-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Every server has it, but users may switch it off for their pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "MAY means optional for the server, so it can be missing entirely."@en .

<#q-pods-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It is deprecated and will be removed in the next spec version"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "MAY says nothing about deprecation; it only marks the feature as optional."@en .

<#q-pods-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only apps that the pod owner has approved are allowed to use it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "MAY concerns what servers implement, not which apps may call it."@en .

<#ch-pods-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-pods> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Pod, storage, owner, agent

"Pod" is the everyday word: "Users have personal online data stores called Pods". The specification does not use "pod" normatively. It says **storage**: "a space of URIs that affords agents controlled access to resources". In this course both words mean the same thing.

Two more defined terms:

- **Agent**: a person, social entity or software identified by a URI. A WebID denotes an agent (Chapter 2).
- **Owner**: the person or social entity with the rights and responsibilities of a storage. The owner "implicitly has control over all resources in a storage". It is set when the storage is provisioned and can be changed.

A server MUST provide one or more storages, and different storages on one server use non-overlapping URI space. So a pod is not a server: one server hosts many pods, and one person can have several storages, since a profile can point to several (Step 5.5).

Watch out for one trap. "A URI allocated to a WebID in a Solid storage does not imply that the WebID is the storage owner". Your profile being hosted in a pod doesn't make you its owner."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-pods-3a> ,
                         <#q-pods-3b> .

<#q-pods-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In a Solid pod, Alice's WebID is `https://pods.example/alice/profile/card#me`. What does that tell you about who owns the storage at `https://pods.example/alice/`?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Nothing: the server records ownership, not the profile's URL"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Ownership is set when the storage is provisioned and tracked by the server, which may also advertise it (Step 5.6). A WebID's URL alone proves nothing about ownership."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-3a-d3> .

<#q-pods-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Alice must be the owner, because her profile lives inside it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The spec says explicitly that a WebID's location does not imply ownership."@en .

<#q-pods-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server operator owns it, because the domain is theirs"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Ownership is set when the storage is provisioned, not derived from the domain."@en .

<#q-pods-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing yet: no one owns it until Alice writes a resource there"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Every storage has an owner from provisioning time onward."@en .

<#q-pods-3b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid, which statement about pods and servers is correct?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "One server can host many storages, and a person can have several"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "\"Storage\" is a space of URIs, not a machine. Servers MUST provide one or more, and profiles can point to several."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-3b-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-3b-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-3b-d3> .

<#q-pods-3b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Each server hosts exactly one pod, which belongs to its operator"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Servers MUST provide one or more storages, and usually host many people's."@en .

<#q-pods-3b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Each person has exactly one pod, named in their WebID's domain"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A profile can point to several storages, on any domain."@en .

<#q-pods-3b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A pod is a physical machine that the user runs at home"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A pod is a URI space on a server, which a provider can host."@en .

<#ch-pods-4>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-pods> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## HTTP Link headers

Solid tells apps what a URL is, and where related resources live, mainly with the HTTP **`Link` header** from RFC 8288. A link is "a typed connection between two resources": a context (the URL you requested), a relation type (`rel`), and a target URL. A response can carry several links, as separate `Link` lines or comma-separated in one header. On CSS 7.2, a `HEAD` (a GET without the body) of alice's pod root answered, among other headers:

```http
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Link: <http://www.w3.org/ns/pim/space#Storage>; rel="type"
Link: <http://www.w3.org/ns/ldp#BasicContainer>; rel="type"
Link: <http://localhost:3000/alice/.meta>; rel="describedby"
Link: <http://localhost:3000/alice/.acl>; rel="acl"
Link: <http://localhost:3000/.well-known/solid>; rel="http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#storageDescription"
```

Registered relation types are short names; extension types are full URIs. The rels this chapter uses:

| `rel` | Target | Step |
|---|---|---|
| `type` | What kind of resource this is | 5.5, 5.7 |
| `acl` | Its access-control document | 5.10 |
| `describedby` | Its description resource | 5.10 |
| `…/solid/terms#storageDescription` | The storage description | 5.6 |
| `…/solid/terms#owner` | The owner's WebID (optional) | 5.6 |
| `…/ldp#inbox` | Its inbox | 5.11 |

The rule: **follow links, never guess URLs**. The `.acl` and `.meta` suffixes and the `/.well-known/solid` path are one server's choices; the spec leaves such names to each server and requires them to be advertised in `Link` headers."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-pods-4a> ,
                         <#q-pods-4b> ,
                         <#q-pods-4c> .

<#q-pods-4a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app wants the access-control document for `https://pod.example/notes/a.ttl`. What should it do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Request the resource and follow its `Link` with `rel=\"acl\"`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Servers MUST advertise auxiliary resources such as the ACL with Link headers, and their naming is implementation-specific, so you discover the ACL by following `rel=\"acl\"`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-4a-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-4a-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-4a-d3> .

<#q-pods-4a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Append `.acl` to the URL, since every server names ACLs that way"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The `.acl` suffix is one server's convention; others may name it differently."@en .

<#q-pods-4a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Look for a file called `acl.ttl` in the folder above it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "No such naming rule exists; only the advertised link is reliable."@en .

<#q-pods-4a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Read the owner's WebID profile, which lists all their ACLs"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A profile describes an agent; each resource advertises its own ACL by Link header."@en .

<#q-pods-4b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid server's response carries `Link: <http://www.w3.org/ns/pim/space#Storage>; rel=\"type\"`. What is this link's context?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The URL that was requested, which the header describes"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A link connects a context (the resource you asked for) to a target via a relation type. Here it says \"this URL is of type `pim:Storage`\"."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-4b-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-4b-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-4b-d3> .

<#q-pods-4b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The `pim:Storage` URI, which the header describes"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is the link target; the context is the resource whose response carries the header."@en .

<#q-pods-4b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The relation type `type`, which names what is described"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`type` is the relation; the context is the resource it is about."@en .

<#q-pods-4b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server's origin, since headers describe the whole host"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Links in a response are about the requested resource, not the whole server."@en .

<#q-pods-4c>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Why should a Solid app follow the links and headers a server sends instead of building URLs from a known server's layout?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Layouts vary by server and version; headers say what's true"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The spec leaves names such as the ACL's to each server and requires them to be advertised in `Link` headers. The habit also carries over to Linked Web Storage (LWS), the draft successor to the Solid Protocol (Chapter 17)."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-4c-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-4c-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-4c-d3> .

<#q-pods-4c-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Hard-coded URLs are forbidden by the HTTP specification itself"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "HTTP does not forbid them; the problem is that guessed URLs are often wrong."@en .

<#q-pods-4c-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Headers are faster to parse than any URL string in JavaScript"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Speed is not the reason; correctness across servers and versions is."@en .

<#q-pods-4c-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Servers reject any request whose URL was not copied from a header"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Servers cannot tell where a URL came from; a guessed URL simply may not exist."@en .

<#ch-pods-5>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-pods> ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Finding the storage

An app often holds a URL and needs to know which storage it belongs to. The protocol gives three ways:

1. **Ask the URL itself.** A storage root MUST answer with `Link: <http://www.w3.org/ns/pim/space#Storage>; rel="type"`.
2. **Walk up the path.** From `/a/b/c`, try `/a/b/`, then `/a/`, then `/`, until a response carries that link.
3. **Read RDF.** Look for a `pim:storage` triple. Nothing requires an ordinary document to contain one; in practice this is the WebID profile (Chapter 7), which may list zero or more storages:

```turtle
@prefix pim: <http://www.w3.org/ns/pim/space#> .

<https://alice.example/profile/card#me>
    pim:storage <https://alice.example/> .
```

Mind the capital letter. **`pim:Storage`** is a *class* (what a storage root is). **`pim:storage`** is a *property* linking an agent to their storage. IRIs are case-sensitive.

⚠ As of October 2026, a fresh CSS 7.2 profile has no `pim:storage`, so only the walk-up finds the storage. Recorded on CSS 7.2:

```text
GET  /alice/profile/card            200, no pim:storage triple
HEAD /alice/profile/                401, no rel="type" link (private)
HEAD /alice/profile/  (as alice)    200, ldp:Container types, no pim:Storage
HEAD /alice/                        200, Link: <…/pim/space#Storage>; rel="type"
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-pods-5a> ,
                         <#q-pods-5b> .

<#q-pods-5a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app has `https://pod.example/alice/notes/today.ttl` and no profile. How can it find the storage root?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Walk up the path until a response has a `pim:Storage` type link"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Walking up the path and checking each response for a `pim:Storage` type link is one of the three discovery methods the Solid Protocol defines."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-5a-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-5a-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-5a-d3> .

<#q-pods-5a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Take the URL's origin, since the storage root is always `/`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A server can host many storages under different paths, so `/` may not be the root."@en .

<#q-pods-5a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Strip the last segment once, since files sit directly in the root"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Resources can be nested several levels deep below the root."@en .

<#q-pods-5a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Follow today.ttl's `rel=\"acl\"` link, which points to the root"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The `acl` link leads to the document's access rules, not to its storage."@en .

<#q-pods-5b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid's vocabulary, what is the difference between `pim:Storage` and `pim:storage`?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The first is a storage-root class; the second is a property"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`pim:Storage` is the type a storage root advertises; `pim:storage` is the property a profile uses to point at a storage."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-5b-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-5b-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-5b-d3> .

<#q-pods-5b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "They are two spellings of the same term, and either one works"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "RDF IRIs are case-sensitive, so these are two different terms."@en .

<#q-pods-5b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The first is the newer name; the second is a deprecated alias"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Both are current, with different jobs."@en .

<#q-pods-5b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The first is used in Turtle and the second in JSON-LD"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The syntax doesn't change the term; both appear in both formats."@en .

<#ch-pods-6>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-pods> ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## The storage description

Once you know a storage, you'll want to know what it offers. Every resource in a storage links to the **storage description** with `rel="http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#storageDescription"`, on GET, HEAD and OPTIONS. So one request to any URL in a pod is enough to find it.

⚠ As of October 2026, CSS 7.2 leaves this link out of `401` responses, so the request must be one you're allowed to make: an anonymous request for a private URL finds nothing to follow.

The storage description is an RDF document about the storage, including that it is a `pim:Storage`. It is also where notification services are advertised (Chapter 10). CSS serves it at `/.well-known/solid`, but that's an implementation choice: follow the link.

A server may also say who owns the storage. If it does, the root's response carries `rel="http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#owner"` pointing to the owner's WebID. This link is optional, so apps can't rely on finding it.

On CSS 7.2 the description read, in part:

```turtle
<http://localhost:3000/> a <http://www.w3.org/ns/pim/space#Storage>;
    <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/notifications#subscription>
        <http://localhost:3000/.notifications/WebSocketChannel2023/>,
        <http://localhost:3000/.notifications/WebhookChannel2023/>.
```

⚠ As of October 2026 it is server-wide: it names the server root, not alice's pod, as the `pim:Storage`. That clashes with the non-overlapping rule of Step 5.3, since `/alice/` is a storage too. On CSS 7, trust the type link on the pod root (Step 5.5) to find the root."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-pods-6a> .

<#q-pods-6a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app holding any URL in a pod wants to find which notification services the server offers. Which `Link` header does it follow?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The one with `rel=\"…#storageDescription\"`, on every resource"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Every resource in a storage links to the storage description, an RDF document about the storage that also advertises notification services. ⚠ CSS 7.2 (2026) leaves the link out of `401` responses, so request a URL you may read."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-6a-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-6a-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-6a-d3> .

<#q-pods-6a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The one with `rel=\"type\"`, whose target is `pim:Storage`, on the root"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That link says what the root is, not what the server offers."@en .

<#q-pods-6a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The one with `rel=\"acl\"`, which lists the storage's features"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The ACL holds access rules for one resource, not a feature list."@en .

<#q-pods-6a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The one with `rel=\"…#owner\"`, on every resource"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The owner link is optional, sits on the root, and names a WebID."@en .

<#ch-pods-7>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-pods> ;
    schema:position 6 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Containers and LDP

Solid's containers, folder-like resources that list others, come from **Linked Data Platform (LDP)**, a 2015 W3C Recommendation. LDP splits resources into **RDF sources** (state fully in RDF, such as a Turtle document) and **non-RDF sources** (images, PDFs, plain text). LDP has three container kinds (Basic, Direct, Indirect), but Solid uses only **Basic Containers**.

GET a container and you receive RDF that lists its members with `ldp:contains`. These statements are its **containment triples**. Servers SHOULD add metadata about each member, such as its size, last-modified time and media type, and protect it from edits. Exact triples vary between servers. On CSS 7.2, GET of alice's fresh pod root returned:

```turtle
@prefix dc: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/>.
@prefix ldp: <http://www.w3.org/ns/ldp#>.
@prefix posix: <http://www.w3.org/ns/posix/stat#>.
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>.

<> a <http://www.w3.org/ns/pim/space#Storage>, ldp:Container, ldp:BasicContainer, ldp:Resource;
    dc:modified "2026-10-07T21:33:04.276Z"^^xsd:dateTime.
<profile/> a ldp:Container, ldp:BasicContainer, ldp:Resource;
    dc:modified "2026-10-07T21:33:04.271Z"^^xsd:dateTime.
<README> a ldp:Resource;
    dc:modified "2026-10-07T21:33:04.272Z"^^xsd:dateTime;
    <http://www.w3.org/ns/ma-ont#format> "text/markdown";
    posix:size "980".
<> ldp:contains <profile/>, <README>.
```

Recall that `<>` means "this document". ⚠ As of October 2026, CSS 7.2 binds `dc:` to the Dublin Core *terms* namespace (often written `dcterms:`) and writes `posix:` for the namespace often written `stat:`. Prefix labels are local; check each declaration."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-pods-7a> ,
                         <#q-pods-7b> .

<#q-pods-7a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A user uploads a holiday photo to their Solid pod. In Linked Data Platform (LDP) terms, what is it?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A non-RDF source, which the pod returns byte for byte as stored"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "LDP distinguishes RDF sources from non-RDF sources such as images; Solid servers return non-RDF resources as stored."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-7a-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-7a-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-7a-d3> .

<#q-pods-7a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An RDF source, because everything in a pod is converted to RDF"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Pods store images, PDFs and text as they are; only their listing entry is RDF."@en .

<#q-pods-7a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A Basic Container, because it can hold image metadata inside it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Containers list other resources; a photo is not one."@en .

<#q-pods-7a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A Direct Container, because it has binary members of its own"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid uses only Basic Containers, and a photo is not a container."@en .

<#q-pods-7b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "How does a Solid app find out what is in the container `/notes/`?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "GET `/notes/` and read its `ldp:contains` triples"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A container is an RDF source whose body lists its members with `ldp:contains`, plus server-managed metadata about each one."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-7b-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-7b-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-7b-d3> .

<#q-pods-7b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "GET `/notes/index.ttl`, the file every container keeps"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "No such file is defined; the container itself returns the listing."@en .

<#q-pods-7b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Send a LIST request, the HTTP method for folders"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "HTTP has no LIST method; a container is read with GET."@en .

<#q-pods-7b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Read the `Link` headers of `/notes/`, one per member"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Members are listed in the RDF body, not in headers."@en .

<#ch-pods-8>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-pods> ;
    schema:position 7 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## A container's Last-Modified

`Last-Modified` is the HTTP header that says when a resource last changed. For containers, Solid has a surprise. The spec lets servers base a container's `Last-Modified` on changes to its **containment triples** only, meaning something was added or removed. Its note says editing a member, or anything deeper, does not change it, "to avoid instant propagation of changes all the way to the root container". The note adds that this design may be revisited in future versions.

There is a version detail too. v0.11 does not require servers to send `Last-Modified` at all. The v0.12 editor's draft, as of October 2026, adds that servers MUST send it on GET and HEAD.

Either way, don't use a container's `Last-Modified` to detect changes in a whole tree. Chapter 10 covers the tools for that."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-pods-8a> .

<#q-pods-8a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In a Solid pod, you edit `/notes/shopping.ttl`. What does the Solid Protocol let you conclude about the `Last-Modified` of the container `/notes/`?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It need not change: servers may count only added or removed members"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A container's `Last-Modified` may reflect changes to its containment triples only, so it is not a recursive change detector."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-8a-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-8a-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-8a-d3> .

<#q-pods-8a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It must update, because the spec requires recursive change tracking"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The spec requires no such thing; it lets servers count only containment changes."@en .

<#q-pods-8a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It must update, along with every container up to the root"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The spec's note says its design avoids exactly this propagation to the root."@en .

<#q-pods-8a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It updates, but only after the server's cache has expired"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Caching isn't involved; servers may ignore edits to members entirely."@en .

<#ch-pods-9>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-pods> ;
    schema:position 8 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Slash semantics

In Solid the URL path carries meaning. "Paths ending with a slash denote a container resource", and slashes mark hierarchy. So `https://alice.example/notes/` is a container, while `https://alice.example/notes/shopping.ttl` is a resource inside it.

Two rules follow:

- **No twins.** If two URIs differ only in the trailing slash and one of them is a resource, the other MUST NOT be a different resource. `/notes` and `/notes/` can't both exist as separate things. The server may redirect (301) from one to the other.
- **Containment mirrors the path.** "There is a 1-1 correspondence between containment triples and relative reference within the path name hierarchy." So "it is not possible to create orphan resources". When you PUT `/a/b/c.ttl`, the server creates `/a/` and `/a/b/` if needed (Chapter 6).

On CSS 7.2, as alice, `HEAD /alice/profile` and `HEAD /alice/profile/card/` both got `404 Not Found`: the slash twins of a container and a document don't exist, and CSS answers 404 instead of redirecting. Anonymously both got `401`, since the server checks access before saying whether anything exists.

LWS, the draft successor to the Solid Protocol (Chapter 17), drops this rule: clients "SHOULD NOT assume that URI structure reflects containment"."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-pods-9a> ,
                         <#q-pods-9b> .

<#q-pods-9a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid pod already holds a document at `/notes`. What happens with `/notes/`?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It may redirect to `/notes`, but can never be a second resource"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Slash semantics forbids `/notes` and `/notes/` from being two different resources; the server may 301-redirect between them or, like CSS 7.2 (2026), answer 404."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-9a-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-9a-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-9a-d3> .

<#q-pods-9a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It becomes an independent container you can fill with documents"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Two URIs that differ only in the trailing slash must not name different resources."@en .

<#q-pods-9a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It holds the description resource for `/notes`, by convention"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Metadata resources are found through links (Step 5.10), not by adding a slash."@en .

<#q-pods-9a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It may become a container later, once `/notes` has been emptied"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Emptying a document changes nothing; while `/notes` exists, `/notes/` can't be a different resource."@en .

<#q-pods-9b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Why can't a Solid pod contain an \"orphan\" resource that no container lists?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Containment follows the path, so each resource is listed"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "There is a 1-1 correspondence between containment triples and the path hierarchy, so all resources are discoverable from a container."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-9b-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-9b-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-9b-d3> .

<#q-pods-9b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server adds a containment triple only if the app asks for one"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The server adds it for every new resource; no request is needed."@en .

<#q-pods-9b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Apps must manually add each new resource to its container listing"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The server keeps containment triples itself and apps can't edit them."@en .

<#q-pods-9b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Orphans are allowed, but only the owner is permitted to see them"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The spec says orphan resources cannot be created at all."@en .

<#ch-pods-10>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-pods> ;
    schema:position 9 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Auxiliary resources

Some resources exist only to say something about another resource, its **subject**. The Solid Protocol calls them **auxiliary resources**: they supply "descriptive metadata, authorization conditions, data shape constraints, digital rights or provenance". Two types are defined:

| Type | Link rel | Holds |
|---|---|---|
| Web Access Control (ACL) | `acl` | Who may do what with the subject (Chapter 8) |
| Description resource | `describedby` | Metadata about the subject |

Rules worth remembering:

- Servers MUST advertise them with `Link` headers, and they are RDF documents.
- A subject has **at most one** description resource, which follows the subject's authorization rules. It may link back with `rel="describes"`; ⚠ as of October 2026, CSS 7.2 doesn't send that link.
- When the subject is deleted, its auxiliary resources are deleted too.
- An auxiliary resource may even live on a different origin from its subject.

CSS uses the suffixes `.acl` and `.meta`, only a server convention. On CSS 7.2, alice's public profile advertised:

```http
Link: <http://localhost:3000/alice/profile/card.meta>; rel="describedby"
Link: <http://localhost:3000/alice/profile/card.acl>; rel="acl"
```

The description resource was public too, and described its subject (prefixes as in Step 5.7):

```turtle
<http://localhost:3000/alice/profile/card> <http://www.w3.org/ns/ma-ont#format> "text/turtle";
    a ldp:Resource;
    dc:modified "2026-10-07T21:33:04.266Z"^^xsd:dateTime;
    posix:size "438".
```

Its ACL answered anonymous visitors `401 Unauthorized` (Chapter 8)."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-pods-10a> ,
                         <#q-pods-10b> .

<#q-pods-10a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In a Solid pod, you delete `/notes/shopping.ttl`. What happens to its ACL and description resource?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The server deletes them along with the resource itself"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Deleting a resource MUST also delete its auxiliary resources, so they never outlive their subject."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-10a-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-10a-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-10a-d3> .

<#q-pods-10a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "They stay behind as orphans until the owner removes them"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The spec requires auxiliary resources to be deleted with their subject."@en .

<#q-pods-10a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "They move up to the parent container's auxiliary resources"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Nothing is moved; they are deleted."@en .

<#q-pods-10a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The DELETE fails until the app has deleted them first"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The server removes them itself as part of deleting the subject."@en .

<#q-pods-10b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "An app wants to attach a title and creator to a photo in a Solid pod as RDF, without changing the image file. Where should it write them?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "In its description resource, found via `rel=\"describedby\"`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A description resource is the auxiliary resource for metadata about its subject, and clients find it through the `describedby` link."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-10b-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-10b-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-10b-d3> .

<#q-pods-10b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In the photo's ACL, which holds any RDF about the photo"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The ACL holds authorization rules, not general metadata."@en .

<#q-pods-10b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In a file made by appending `.meta` to the photo's URL"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`.meta` is one server's naming convention; follow `describedby` instead."@en .

<#q-pods-10b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In the container listing, by PUTting extra triples into it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Apps can't change a container's server-managed listing that way."@en .

<#ch-pods-11>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-pods> ;
    schema:position 10 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Inboxes in brief

Sometimes another person or app needs to tell you something: "I commented on your photo" or "here is an invitation". Solid uses **Linked Data Notifications (LDN)**, a 2017 W3C Recommendation, for this. An **inbox** is a container that works like a mailbox.

- **Discovery.** A sender finds a resource's inbox through a `Link` header with `rel="http://www.w3.org/ns/ldp#inbox"` or an `ldp:inbox` triple in its RDF. Profiles usually list one: "zero or one `ldp:inbox`".
- **Sending.** The sender POSTs the notification to the inbox, in JSON-LD unless another RDF syntax was negotiated, and gets `201 Created` or `202 Accepted`.
- **Reading.** A consumer GETs the inbox and finds the messages through `ldp:contains`, like any container listing.

Solid servers MUST implement the receiving side, and Solid clients the sending or consuming side. Chapter 4 noted that `solid:inbox` is deprecated in favour of `ldp:inbox`. ⚠ As of October 2026, CSS 7.2 creates no inbox; Step 9.6 shows how an app can make one.

Don't confuse this with **Solid Notifications** (Chapter 10). An LDN inbox holds messages *between agents* that wait until read. Solid Notifications push live *change events about resources* to subscribed apps."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-pods-11a> ,
                         <#q-pods-11b> .

<#q-pods-11a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid, Bob's app wants to tell Alice that he shared a document with her. What does it do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Find Alice's inbox from her profile and POST a notification to it"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Linked Data Notifications senders discover the inbox via `ldp:inbox` and deliver with POST; Alice's apps later read it like any container."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-11a-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-11a-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-11a-d3> .

<#q-pods-11a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "PUT the message into Alice's profile document as a new triple"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Others usually can't write to a profile; the inbox is the place for incoming messages."@en .

<#q-pods-11a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Subscribe to Alice's pod with a WebSocket channel and send it there"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Notification channels deliver change events from a server, not messages to a person."@en .

<#q-pods-11a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "PATCH a new triple into Alice's inbox container listing"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Container listings are server-managed; new messages are created with POST."@en .

<#q-pods-11b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid, what is the main difference between a Linked Data Notifications (LDN) inbox and Solid Notifications?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Inboxes hold agents' messages; Notifications report resource changes"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "An inbox is a mailbox that agents POST messages to, read later. Solid Notifications deliver live events such as \"this resource was updated\" (Chapter 10)."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-11b-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-11b-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-11b-d3> .

<#q-pods-11b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Inboxes are the new standard; Solid Notifications are the deprecated one"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Both are current; they serve different purposes."@en .

<#q-pods-11b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Inboxes are for apps; Solid Notifications are only for human users"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Both are used by apps; the difference is what kind of message they carry."@en .

<#q-pods-11b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Inboxes use WebSockets; Solid Notifications use plain HTTP POST"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "LDN uses POST to a container; WebSockets are one Notifications channel type."@en .

<#q-pods-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app holds only the URL `https://host.example/u/bob/data/x.ttl`. Which plan finds Bob's storage description without guessing any path?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Request `x.ttl` and follow its `storageDescription` link"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Servers MUST send a `storageDescription` Link header on GET, HEAD and OPTIONS of any resource in a storage, so one request is enough if you may read `x.ttl`. ⚠ On CSS 7.2 (2026) a `401` answer omits the link, and the link leads to a server-wide description (Step 5.6)."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-r01-d3> .

<#q-pods-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Fetch `/.well-known/solid`, which every server uses"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That path is one server's choice; the link header is what servers must send."@en .

<#q-pods-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Walk up to `/` and append `description.ttl` to it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "No spec defines that name; the description is found by its link."@en .

<#q-pods-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Walk up to the storage root and read its `pim:storage` triple"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Nothing requires the root's data to contain `pim:storage`; the description is linked from any resource."@en .

<#q-pods-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Alice keeps her notes in a `notes/` container in her Solid storage. Starting from her WebID only, which plan lists them?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Get `pim:storage` from her profile, GET `notes/` there, read `ldp:contains`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Each step follows data the profile or server publishes. If the profile has no `pim:storage`, as on a fresh CSS 7.2 pod (2026), walk up from the profile's URL to the container with the `pim:Storage` type link instead."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-r02-d3> .

<#q-pods-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "GET `notes/` on her WebID's host and read the files listed there"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A WebID's location says nothing certain about where her storage is."@en .

<#q-pods-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Get `pim:Storage` from her profile, GET `notes/` there, read `ldp:contains`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`pim:Storage` is a class; the profile points to storage with `pim:storage`."@en .

<#q-pods-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Read `notes/.meta`, which lists every member of the container"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`.meta` is a server convention for metadata; members come from `ldp:contains`."@en .

<#q-pods-r03>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Bob uploads a PDF to the container `/shared/` in his Solid pod and wants Alice to know. Which description is right?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The PDF is a non-RDF source listed in `/shared/`; Bob POSTs to Alice's `ldp:inbox`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The server lists the PDF in its container automatically, and Bob tells Alice by POSTing to the inbox her profile advertises with `ldp:inbox`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-r03-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-r03-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-r03-d3> .

<#q-pods-r03-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The PDF becomes an RDF source so `/shared/` can list it; Bob POSTs to Alice's `ldp:inbox`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Pods keep PDFs as non-RDF sources; only the listing entry is RDF."@en .

<#q-pods-r03-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The PDF is listed once Bob adds `ldp:contains`; Bob POSTs to Alice's `ldp:inbox`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The server adds containment triples itself, following the path."@en .

<#q-pods-r03-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The PDF is a non-RDF source listed in `/shared/`; Bob subscribes Alice to its notifications"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Bob can't subscribe for Alice; a message to her inbox is how agents tell each other."@en .

<#q-pods-r04>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app wants to add a caption to `/notes/cat.png` as RDF, and later notice when new photos appear in `/notes/`. Which plan works?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Write it where `describedby` points; re-read `/notes/`'s `ldp:contains`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Metadata about a non-RDF source goes in its description resource. New photos show up as new containment triples, so re-reading the listing finds them (Chapter 10 shows how to be told instead)."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-r04-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-r04-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-r04-d3> .

<#q-pods-r04-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Write it to `cat.png.meta`; poll `/notes/`'s `Last-Modified` for caption edits"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`.meta` is one server's name, and a container's `Last-Modified` tracks added or removed members, not edits to a member's metadata."@en .

<#q-pods-r04-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Write it into `cat.png`'s ACL; re-read `/notes/`'s `ldp:contains`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The ACL holds access rules for the photo, not metadata about it."@en .

<#q-pods-r04-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "PATCH the caption into `/notes/`'s listing; re-read its `ldp:contains`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Containment triples and their metadata are managed by the server, not written by apps."@en .

<#q-pods-r05>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "You PUT `/notes/2026/today.ttl` into a Solid pod where `/notes/` doesn't exist yet. What is now true?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "`/notes/` and `/notes/2026/` exist, each listing the next level"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Containment mirrors the path: the server creates the intermediate containers and their containment triples, so no resource is an orphan."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-pods-r05-d1> ,
                          <#q-pods-r05-d2> ,
                          <#q-pods-r05-d3> .

<#q-pods-r05-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The PUT fails until you create `/notes/` and `/notes/2026/` first"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The server creates missing containers itself, so no orphan can arise."@en .

<#q-pods-r05-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`today.ttl` exists, but no container lists it until you add it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Containment mirrors the path, so `/notes/2026/` lists it at once."@en .

<#q-pods-r05-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`/notes/2026` (no slash) also exists, as a separate document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Slash twins can't be different resources; `/notes/2026/` is the container."@en .

# Chapter 6: Reading and writing resources.
<#ch-read-write>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    dcterms:title "Reading and writing resources"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Every CRUD operation on a Solid pod: reading, content negotiation, `PUT`, `POST`, containers, files, N3 Patch, SPARQL Update and `DELETE`, ending with safe concurrent writes using ETags."@en ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-read-write-r01> ,
                              <#q-read-write-r02> ,
                              <#q-read-write-r03> ,
                              <#q-read-write-r04> ,
                              <#q-read-write-r05> .

<#ch-read-write-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-read-write> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Reading: GET, HEAD, OPTIONS

Every Solid server must support `GET` (fetch the representation), `HEAD` (the same headers, no body) and `OPTIONS` (what can I do here?); an unsupported method gets `405 Method Not Allowed`. Any request can be redirected (`301`, `307`, `308`); `307` and `308` mean "repeat the same request, write included, at the new URL".

Successful responses must carry `Allow`, the methods allowed here. An authorized read must also say which formats each write method in `Allow` accepts, in `Accept-Patch`, `Accept-Post` and `Accept-Put` (a Solid invention). A cheap `HEAD` tells you what you may send before you send it. On CSS 7.2 (October 2026), a HEAD on a Turtle document returned (some headers trimmed):

```http
HEAD /alice/notes/first.ttl HTTP/1.1

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: text/turtle
Allow: OPTIONS, HEAD, GET, PATCH, PUT, DELETE
Accept-Patch: text/n3, application/sparql-update
Accept-Put: */*
ETag: "1791408808150-text/turtle"
WAC-Allow: user="append control read write"
```

There is no `Accept-Post`, because CSS leaves POST out of a document's `Allow`. node-solid-server (NSS) 6.0.0, the older server early pod providers ran, lists POST and sends `Accept-Post`. Servers using Web Access Control (Chapter 8) also send `WAC-Allow`.

⚠ CSS 7.2 answers a bare `OPTIONS` with `204 No Content` and no `Allow`, taking it for a browser's cross-origin check (CORS, Step 8.8), so use HEAD."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-read-write-1a> ,
                         <#q-read-write-1b> .

<#q-read-write-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Before writing to a resource on a Solid server, your app wants to know which PATCH formats the server accepts, without downloading the body. What should it do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Send a HEAD and read the `Accept-Patch` header"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Authorized reads must advertise `Accept-Patch`, `Accept-Post` or `Accept-Put` for each of those write methods listed in `Allow`, so a HEAD gives the write formats without the body. CSS 7.2 answers a bare OPTIONS without them, another reason to use HEAD."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-write-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-write-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-write-1a-d3> .

<#q-read-write-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Send a GET and read the `Content-Type` header"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`Content-Type` describes the body you received, not the formats a PATCH may use."@en .

<#q-read-write-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Send a HEAD and read the `Accept-Post` header"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`Accept-Post` lists formats for POST, not for PATCH."@en .

<#q-read-write-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Send a test PATCH and see whether it gets a `405`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "405 means the method is not supported at all, and trial and error risks changing data."@en .

<#q-read-write-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "On Community Solid Server 7.2 (2026), a HEAD on the Turtle document `/notes/shopping.ttl` returns `Allow: OPTIONS, HEAD, GET, PATCH, PUT, DELETE` with `Accept-Patch` and `Accept-Put`, but no `Accept-Post`. Why?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "POST isn't in this document's `Allow` header"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The `Accept-*` headers mirror the write methods in `Allow`, and POST goes to containers, so CSS 7.2 sends no `Accept-Post` on a document. NSS 6.0.0 lists POST on documents too, and then sends `Accept-Post`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-write-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-write-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-write-1b-d3> .

<#q-read-write-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server does not support POST on any resource at all"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Servers must support POST; it just is not allowed on a plain document."@en .

<#q-read-write-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`Accept-Post` is optional, and servers usually leave it out"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "When POST is listed in `Allow`, `Accept-Post` must be sent too."@en .

<#q-read-write-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`Accept-Put` also covers the formats of POST requests"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Each write method has its own header; `Accept-Put` is only about PUT."@en .

<#ch-read-write-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-read-write> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Content negotiation in practice

Chapter 2 showed that the `Accept` header asks for a format. Solid makes this concrete: when a resource is created as RDF (by PUT, POST or PATCH), the server must serve it as both `text/turtle` and `application/ld+json`, whichever of the two created it. On CSS 7.2 (October 2026), a document written as Turtle was read back as JSON-LD:

```http
PUT /alice/notes/first.ttl HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: text/turtle

<#it> <http://schema.org/text> "Hello, Solid" .

HTTP/1.1 201 Created

GET /alice/notes/first.ttl HTTP/1.1
Accept: application/ld+json

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/ld+json
ETag: "1791408808150-application/ld+json"

[ { "@id": "https://pod.example/alice/notes/first.ttl#it",
    "http://schema.org/text": [ { "@value": "Hello, Solid" } ] } ]
```

The ETag has the Turtle one's number but its own suffix (Step 6.11).

For writers the reverse direction matters most: clients must send `Content-Type` on any PUT, POST or PATCH with a body, and servers must reject such a request without it with `400 Bad Request` (CSS 7.2 says "HTTP request body was passed without a Content-Type header").

Negotiation only converts RDF. A non-RDF file (Step 6.6) comes back as stored, or as an error if the server can't meet your `Accept`. HTTP suggests `406 Not Acceptable`, which NSS 6.0.0 sends. ⚠ CSS 7.2 answers `501 Not Implemented`: "No conversion path could be made from image/png to text/turtle"."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-read-write-2a> ,
                         <#q-read-write-2b> .

<#q-read-write-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "On a Solid pod, one app saves a document by PUTting JSON-LD. A second app that only parses Turtle later GETs it with `Accept: text/turtle`. What does a conforming server do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It returns the same triples serialized as Turtle"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Solid servers must serve any RDF source created by PUT, POST or PATCH as both `text/turtle` and `application/ld+json`, so apps never need to agree on which of the two to write."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-write-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-write-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-write-2a-d3> .

<#q-read-write-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It returns 406 because the document was stored as JSON-LD"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Servers must serve RDF sources in both Turtle and JSON-LD, whatever format created them."@en .

<#q-read-write-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It returns the original JSON-LD text unchanged"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That ignores the client's `Accept` header, which the server must honour for these two formats."@en .

<#q-read-write-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It redirects to a separate `.ttl` copy of the document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "There is one resource; the format is chosen by negotiation, not by a second file."@en .

<#q-read-write-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A PUT with a valid Turtle body fails with `400 Bad Request` on a Solid server. What is the most likely cause?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The request had no `Content-Type` header"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Solid servers must reject a PUT, POST or PATCH that carries a body without a `Content-Type` with 400."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-write-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-write-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-write-2b-d3> .

<#q-read-write-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The request had no `Accept` header"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`Accept` is about the response format and is not required on a write."@en .

<#q-read-write-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The URL did not end in `.ttl`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid does not use file extensions to decide formats; headers do."@en .

<#q-read-write-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The request had no `Link` type header"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A plain document needs no type link; leaving it out does not make the request malformed."@en .

<#ch-read-write-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-read-write> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## PUT: you choose the URL

Use `PUT` when you know the URL you want. HTTP defines it as "make the resource at this URL look like this body": it creates the resource if nothing is there and replaces it entirely if something is. Replacing means the old content is gone: any triple you leave out of the new body is deleted.

A create answers `201 Created`. For a replace HTTP expects `200` or `204`, and NSS 6.0.0 answers `204`. ⚠ CSS 7.2 answers a successful replace, PATCH or DELETE with `205 Reset Content`: on CSS 7.2 (October 2026), sending the PUT of Step 6.2 a second time got `205`, where the first had got `201`. Treat any 2xx as success.

You don't need to create folders first. A server must create any missing intermediate containers from the URL path, with their containment triples, so `PUT /a/b/c.ttl` also creates `/a/` and `/a/b/`. Step 6.2's first PUT created `notes/` this way.

To make a PUT create-only, add `If-None-Match: *`. The request then succeeds only if no representation exists; otherwise it fails with `412 Precondition Failed` instead of overwriting.

```http
PUT /notes/shopping.ttl HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: text/turtle
If-None-Match: *

<#milk> a <http://schema.org/Product> .
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-read-write-3a> ,
                         <#q-read-write-3b> .

<#q-read-write-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Your app PUTs `/recipes/2026/soup.ttl` into a Solid pod where only the root container exists. What happens on a conforming server?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It also creates `/recipes/` and `/recipes/2026/`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Solid servers must create the missing intermediate containers, with their containment triples, from the URL path of a PUT or PATCH."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-write-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-write-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-write-3a-d3> .

<#q-read-write-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server answers 404 because the parent container does not exist"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Servers must create intermediate containers for PUT and PATCH."@en .

<#q-read-write-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server answers 409 because containers must be created first"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Nested creation is not a conflict; the server must create the missing containers."@en .

<#q-read-write-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server stores the document as `/soup.ttl` in the root container"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "With PUT the client chooses the URL; the server does not move it."@en .

<#q-read-write-3b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Two devices might create the same settings document in a Solid pod at the same moment. Which header stops the second PUT from overwriting the first?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "`If-None-Match: *`" ;
    solid-memo:backNote "With `If-None-Match: *` the server refuses a PUT or PATCH with 412 when a current representation exists, so a create cannot clobber existing data."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-write-3b-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-write-3b-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-write-3b-d3> .

<#q-read-write-3b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`Accept: text/turtle`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`Accept` chooses the format of the response; it does not make the server check whether the resource exists."@en .

<#q-read-write-3b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`Content-Type: text/turtle`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It names the body's format and adds no protection against overwriting."@en .

<#q-read-write-3b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`Cache-Control: no-store`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Caching directives do not make the server check whether the resource exists."@en .

<#ch-read-write-4>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-read-write> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## POST: the server chooses the URL

Use `POST` when you don't care about the exact name, such as adding a new message or log entry. You POST to a container, and the server picks the URL: a POST to `/foo/` creates `/foo/{id}`, directly inside `/foo/`. A successful create answers `201 Created`, and the `Location` header holds the new resource's URL. On CSS 7.2 (October 2026):

```http
POST /alice/notes/ HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: text/turtle
Slug: second

<#it> <http://schema.org/text> "Second note" .

HTTP/1.1 201 Created
Location: https://pod.example/alice/notes/second
```

The `Slug` header (defined in AtomPub, RFC 5023, and adopted by LDP as a client hint) lets you suggest a name. It is only a hint, and current Solid specs do not require servers to honour it. A server may use it, change it or ignore it, so always read the URL from `Location`. ⚠ CSS 7.2 happens to keep a Slug exactly and adds no extension, as above; that is no promise for other servers (Step 6.6 shows NSS 6.0.0 adding one).

POST is only meaningful on something that exists: a POST to a URL with no existing representation gets `404 Not Found`."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-read-write-4a> ,
                         <#q-read-write-4b> .

<#q-read-write-4a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Your app POSTs a new note to the Solid container `/notes/` with `Slug: groceries`. How should it learn the new note's URL?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Read the `Location` header of the 201 response"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "With POST the server assigns the URL and reports it in the `Location` header of the `201 Created` response; a Slug never guarantees the name."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-write-4a-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-write-4a-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-write-4a-d3> .

<#q-read-write-4a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Assume it is `/notes/groceries` because of the Slug"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A Slug is only a hint; servers may change or ignore it."@en .

<#q-read-write-4a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "List `/notes/` again and take the newest entry"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is slow, and racy when other clients also add notes."@en .

<#q-read-write-4a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Generate a UUID in the app and use `/notes/{uuid}`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "With POST the server, not the client, names the resource."@en .

<#q-read-write-4b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "On a Solid server, when is POST the better choice than PUT for creating a resource?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "When the app doesn't need to pick the URL"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Clients use PUT (and PATCH) to choose a URL themselves, and POST to have the server assign one."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-write-4b-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-write-4b-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-write-4b-d3> .

<#q-read-write-4b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "When the app needs to replace an existing document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Replacing at a known URL is exactly what PUT is for."@en .

<#q-read-write-4b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "When the parent container does not exist yet"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A POST to a missing container gets 404; PUT is the one that creates intermediate containers."@en .

<#q-read-write-4b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "When the body is RDF rather than an image"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Both methods accept RDF and non-RDF bodies; the difference is who chooses the URL."@en .

<#ch-read-write-5>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-read-write> ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Creating containers

Solid has no "mkdir", but there are three routes.

1. **PUT (or PATCH) something inside it.** Servers must create intermediate containers, so writing `/photos/2026/cat.jpg` also creates `/photos/` and `/photos/2026/`.
2. **PUT to a URL ending in `/`.** The trailing slash names a container (Chapter 5), so the spec covers this too. CSS 7.2 ignores the body. ⚠ CSS 7.2 answers a PUT to an existing container with `409 Conflict` ("Existing containers cannot be updated via PUT."), even with `If-None-Match: *`, where you might expect 412. Send no body, and never use it to "update" a container.
3. **POST with an LDP type link.** Send a POST to the parent with `Link: <http://www.w3.org/ns/ldp#BasicContainer>; rel="type"` and usually a `Slug`. Solid Protocol v0.10 required this; v0.11 dropped the requirement. Many servers still accept it (on CSS 7.2, `Slug: sub` got `201` with a `Location` ending in `/notes/sub/`), but don't rely on it.

Nor can you write a container's membership: containment mirrors the URL paths (Chapter 5), so a PUT or PATCH that changes `ldp:contains` triples gets `409 Conflict`. Never send a listing; create or delete the contents instead."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-read-write-5a> ,
                         <#q-read-write-5b> .

<#q-read-write-5a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Which way of creating a container is NOT guaranteed to work on every server that follows Solid Protocol v0.11?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "POST to the parent with an `ldp:BasicContainer` type link"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Writing inside a container and PUT to a slash URL both rest on spec requirements. POST with a type link was a MUST up to v0.10, and many servers still support it, but v0.11 dropped the requirement."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-write-5a-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-write-5a-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-write-5a-d3> .

<#q-read-write-5a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "PUT a resource whose URL lies inside the new container"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Servers must create intermediate containers from a PUT's path."@en .

<#q-read-write-5a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "PATCH a new resource whose URL lies inside the new container"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Intermediate containers are created for PATCH as well as PUT."@en .

<#q-read-write-5a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "PUT with no body to the new container's URL ending in `/`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Slash semantics makes that URL a container, which servers must support."@en .

<#q-read-write-5b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Your app PUTs each note to a URL it chooses inside the Solid container `/notes/`, which may not exist yet. What is the simplest portable way to make sure the container exists?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Just PUT the note, and the server creates `/notes/`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Servers must create missing intermediate containers for PUT and PATCH, so writing the note is enough. A separate container PUT adds a request, and CSS 7.2 even refuses it with 409 once the container exists."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-write-5b-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-write-5b-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-write-5b-d3> .

<#q-read-write-5b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "PUT `/notes/` itself before every note"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It is unneeded, and on CSS 7.2 a PUT to an existing container gets 409."@en .

<#q-read-write-5b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "POST to the root with an `ldp:BasicContainer` type link first"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid Protocol v0.11 no longer guarantees that route, and it is unneeded."@en .

<#q-read-write-5b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "GET `/notes/` and stop with an error on 404"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A 404 only means it doesn't exist yet; a PUT inside it creates it."@en .

<#ch-read-write-6>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-read-write> ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Files and other non-RDF resources

Images, PDFs and other files are non-RDF resources. You write them like documents, by PUT or POST, with the file's real media type in the required `Content-Type` (Step 6.2). A GET returns the bytes and the type they were stored under.

Metadata goes in the file's description resource, which the server links with `rel="describedby"` (Step 5.10). Its name is the server's choice, so follow the link: CSS 7.2 names it `photo.meta`, and NSS 6.0.0 `photo.png.meta`, because there the file itself became `photo.png`. NSS 6.0.0 also spells the relation `describedBy`; relation names compare case-insensitively, so match them that way. On CSS 7.2 (October 2026), uploading a one-pixel PNG and captioning it with an N3 Patch (Step 6.7) went like this (headers trimmed):

```http
POST /alice/notes/ HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: image/png
Slug: photo

<70 bytes of PNG data>

HTTP/1.1 201 Created
Location: https://pod.example/alice/notes/photo

HEAD /alice/notes/photo HTTP/1.1

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: image/png
Link: <https://pod.example/alice/notes/photo.meta>; rel="describedby"

PATCH /alice/notes/photo.meta HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: text/n3

@prefix solid: <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#>.
@prefix schema: <http://schema.org/>.
_:patch a solid:InsertDeletePatch;
  solid:inserts { <photo> schema:description "A one-pixel test image" . }.

HTTP/1.1 205 Reset Content
```

A GET on `photo.meta` then returned the server-managed triples plus the caption:

```turtle
@prefix dc: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/>.
@prefix ldp: <http://www.w3.org/ns/ldp#>.
@prefix posix: <http://www.w3.org/ns/posix/stat#>.
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>.

<https://pod.example/alice/notes/photo> <http://www.w3.org/ns/ma-ont#format> "image/png";
    a ldp:Resource;
    dc:modified "2026-10-07T21:33:28.927Z"^^xsd:dateTime;
    posix:size "70";
    <http://schema.org/description> "A one-pixel test image".
```

A GET on that description resource lists `Allow: OPTIONS, HEAD, GET, PATCH`, with no PUT; a PUT to it gets `405` ("use PATCH instead"), whose `Allow` also lists `DELETE`.

⚠ URL and type can disagree. CSS 7.2 kept `Slug: photo` as `…/notes/photo`, served as `image/png`. NSS 6.0.0 adds an extension from the type: `photo.png`. Never infer the type from the URL: read `Content-Type`."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-read-write-6a> ,
                         <#q-read-write-6b> .

<#q-read-write-6a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
