<#ch-read-write-7>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-read-write> ;
    schema:position 6 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## The shape of an N3 Patch

`PUT` replaces the whole document; `PATCH` changes part of it. For RDF documents, servers must accept an **N3 Patch** with media type `text/n3` and list it in `Accept-Patch`. It is written in N3 (Step 3.10): Turtle plus `{ }` formulas (groups of triples) and variables such as `?person`, which stand for "whatever node fits here".

The body describes one change, the **patch resource**, typed `solid:InsertDeletePatch`. It has at most one each of `solid:where` (what must be true now), `solid:deletes` (triples to remove) and `solid:inserts` (triples to add):

```n3
@prefix solid: <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#>.
@prefix ex: <http://www.example.org/terms#>.

_:rename a solid:InsertDeletePatch;
  solid:where   { ?person ex:familyName "Garcia". };
  solid:inserts { ?person ex:givenName "Alex". };
  solid:deletes { ?person ex:givenName "Claudia". }.
```

The server finds the node that makes `where` true and uses it for `?person` in `inserts` and `deletes`. A patch that breaks the format rules gets `422`: for example two patch resources, a variable in `inserts` or `deletes` that never appears in `where`, or blank nodes in `inserts` or `deletes` (Step 6.8 has the version details). HTTP now calls 422 "Unprocessable Content"; CSS 7.2 still sends the older text, "Unprocessable Entity"."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-read-write-7a> ,
                         <#q-read-write-7b> .

<#q-read-write-7a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
