      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server answers 409, because there is nothing to match"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A missing `where` counts as empty, which matches exactly once."@en .

<#q-read-write-8b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The document is created empty, and the inserts are ignored"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The inserts are applied to the empty starting graph, so they are stored."@en .

<#ch-read-write-9>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-read-write> ;
    schema:position 8 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## SPARQL Update: the legacy format

Before N3 Patch, Solid servers patched with SPARQL Update, media type `application/sparql-update`. SPARQL is RDF's query language (Step 3.11); its update forms `DELETE DATA` and `INSERT DATA` list exact triples to remove and add. The archived early Solid REST API document used it like this:

```http
PATCH /data/ HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/sparql-update

DELETE DATA { <> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/title> "Basic container" };
INSERT DATA { <> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/title> "My data container" }
```

It is still widely supported (Community Solid Server accepts it, for example), and many existing libraries send it. But Solid Protocol v0.9 to v0.11 never require it; only N3 Patch is required.

Support is also uneven in practice. As of October 2026, multi-server tests recorded the JavaScript Solid Server (JSS) deleting nothing on `DELETE DATA` yet answering `204`, and Solid-Nextcloud deleting every value of the named subject and predicate, not just the one triple. The portable habit is to read `Accept-Patch` first, prefer `text/n3` when it is listed, and fall back to SPARQL Update only if the server advertises nothing else."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-read-write-9a> ,
                         <#q-read-write-9b> .

<#q-read-write-9a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid server's `Accept-Patch` header lists `text/n3, application/sparql-update`. Which format should a portable app use for its patches?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "N3 Patch, because it is the format every server must accept"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Solid servers must accept N3 Patch (`text/n3`) for RDF documents; SPARQL Update is widely supported but not required, so check `Accept-Patch` and prefer N3."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-write-9a-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-write-9a-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-write-9a-d3> .

<#q-read-write-9a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "SPARQL Update, because it is the official Solid patch format"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The Solid Protocol requires N3 Patch; SPARQL Update is a legacy format."@en .

<#q-read-write-9a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Either one, because the specs define them identically"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "They are different languages, and only N3 Patch is in the Solid Protocol."@en .

<#q-read-write-9a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Neither, because a full PUT is always the safer write"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A PUT replaces the whole document and risks erasing other apps' triples."@en .

<#q-read-write-9b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A document in a Solid pod gives a card two `ex:tag` values. Your app sends a SPARQL Update `DELETE DATA` naming one of them. On one server both tags disappear, yet the server answered with success. What should the app learn from this?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "SPARQL Update support varies; prefer N3 Patch where offered"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "As of October 2026, multi-server tests recorded Solid-Nextcloud deleting every value of the named subject and predicate, and JSS deleting nothing while answering 204. SPARQL Update is optional, so read `Accept-Patch` and prefer `text/n3`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-write-9b-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-write-9b-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-write-9b-d3> .

<#q-read-write-9b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`DELETE DATA` is defined to remove every value of the predicate"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It removes only the triples it lists; deleting both is that server's deviation."@en .

<#q-read-write-9b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The app should send `INSERT DATA` before `DELETE DATA`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The order is not the problem; the server applied the delete too broadly."@en .

<#q-read-write-9b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A 2xx answer proves the patch was applied as written"@en ;
