<#ch-read-write-10>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-read-write> ;
    schema:position 9 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## DELETE

Servers must support `DELETE`. Deleting a resource also removes its containment triple from the parent container and deletes its auxiliary resources, such as its ACL and description resource (Chapter 5).

Two deletes are refused:
- A **non-empty container** gets `409 Conflict`. Solid has no recursive delete, so to remove a folder tree you delete depth-first: the files first, then the deepest containers, then their parents.
- The **storage root** (and its ACL) gets `405 Method Not Allowed`, and `DELETE` is left out of its `Allow` header.

```text
DELETE /notes/             -> 409 (still contains shopping.ttl)
DELETE /notes/shopping.ttl -> 204 (205 on CSS 7.2)
DELETE /notes/             -> 204 (205 on CSS 7.2)
DELETE /                   -> 405
```

On CSS 7.2 (October 2026), a DELETE of a `notes/` container that still held three resources got `409 Conflict` with the message "Can only delete empty containers.", and nothing was deleted. Deleting a document, then its emptied container, then that container's emptied parent got `205` each time.

After a successful delete, GET usually returns `404 Not Found` or `410 Gone`; the spec suggests servers should not reuse URLs and may use 410 to signal permanent removal."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-read-write-10a> ,
                         <#q-read-write-10b> .

<#q-read-write-10a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Your app must remove the Solid container `/project/`, which contains `/project/a.ttl` and the container `/project/img/` with one photo inside. Which order of DELETEs works?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The photo, then `/project/img/`, then `a.ttl`, then `/project/`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Only empty containers can be deleted, so a client must remove children depth-first before their containers."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-write-10a-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-write-10a-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-write-10a-d3> .

<#q-read-write-10a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Just `/project/`, which removes its contents along with it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A DELETE of a non-empty container gets 409; Solid has no recursive delete."@en .

<#q-read-write-10a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`/project/`, then `/project/img/`, then the photo, then `a.ttl`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`/project/` is not empty yet, so the first request gets 409."@en .

<#q-read-write-10a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`/project/img/`, then the photo, then `a.ttl`, then `/project/`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`/project/img/` still holds the photo, so deleting it first gets 409."@en .

<#q-read-write-10b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Your cleanup script empties a test Solid pod and then sends `DELETE /` for the storage root. What does a conforming server answer?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "405, with `DELETE` left out of its `Allow`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Solid servers must not allow DELETE on the storage root or its ACL; they answer 405 and leave DELETE out of `Allow`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-write-10b-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-write-10b-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-write-10b-d3> .

<#q-read-write-10b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "204, because the root is now empty"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Emptiness is not enough; the storage root can never be deleted."@en .

<#q-read-write-10b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "409, because the root still holds its ACL"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "409 is for containers with contained resources; the root is refused for a different reason."@en .

<#q-read-write-10b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "403, because only the owner may delete the root"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Even the owner is refused: the protocol forbids deleting the root itself."@en .

<#ch-read-write-11>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-read-write> ;
    schema:position 10 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Lost updates and conditional requests

Pods have no locks. Two apps can read a document, edit it and write it back, and the later write silently erases the earlier one: a *lost update*.

HTTP's answer is conditional requests. A server MAY send an `ETag`, a version tag for the current representation. Echo it in `If-Match` when you write; if the document changed or was deleted since, the server answers `412 Precondition Failed` instead:

```text
# Tabs A and B both read
GET /decks/catalog.ttl                  -> 200, ETag: "v7"
# A writes first
PUT /decks/catalog.ttl  If-Match: "v7"  -> 2xx (now "v8")
# B writes with its stale tag
PUT /decks/catalog.ttl  If-Match: "v7"  -> 412
```

On 412, B re-reads, reapplies its change and retries. For creates, `If-None-Match: *` does the same job (Step 6.3). On CSS 7.2 (October 2026), two identical PATCHes sending `If-Match: "1791408809120-text/turtle"` got `205`, then `412`. Send the tag exactly as received: wrapped in a second pair of quotes it never matches.

Limits ⚠: CSS 7.2's strong ETag includes the media type, so Turtle and JSON-LD differ. NSS 6.0.0 sends no ETag on a GET in the stored format; the weak `W/"…"` ETags on its HEAD and converted responses are hashes of that response's body, not version tags. It ignores `If-Match` and honours `If-None-Match: *`.

A single PATCH is all-or-nothing, but Solid has no transactions, so a change touching two documents can be left half done."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-read-write-11a> ,
                         <#q-read-write-11b> .

<#q-read-write-11a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Your app read a Solid document with `ETag: \"v7\"` and now PUTs an edit with `If-Match: \"v7\"`, but gets `412 Precondition Failed`. What does that tell you?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The document changed or was deleted since your read"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`If-Match` makes the write conditional on the current ETag, and a 412 means the representation changed or is gone, so re-read, merge and retry."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-write-11a-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-write-11a-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-write-11a-d3> .

<#q-read-write-11a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Your app lacks write permission on the document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Missing permission is refused with 401 or 403, not 412."@en .

<#q-read-write-11a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server does not support ETags at all"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A server without ETags would not have sent one; a 412 means the tag no longer matches."@en .

<#q-read-write-11a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The request body is not valid Turtle for this document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A syntax problem is a client error such as 400, unrelated to the precondition."@en .

<#q-read-write-11b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "An app moves a card from deck A's document to deck B's document in a Solid pod, with two PATCHes. Why can this still go wrong even if each PATCH succeeds or fails cleanly?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Each PATCH is all-or-nothing for its own document only"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Solid offers no transactions across documents, so if the first PATCH succeeds and the second fails, the card can end up in both decks or neither."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-write-11b-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-write-11b-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-write-11b-d3> .

<#q-read-write-11b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "PATCH requests are never all-or-nothing, even within one document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Each PATCH applies wholly or not at all to its one document."@en .

<#q-read-write-11b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`If-Match` on both PATCHes would make the pair all-or-nothing"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`If-Match` protects each document separately; the first PATCH can still succeed while the second fails."@en .

<#q-read-write-11b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "ETags are required, and the second PATCH sent no ETag"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "ETags are a MAY in Solid, and they protect one document, not a pair."@en .

<#q-read-write-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Your app wants to change one triple in a shared Solid document without erasing triples other apps added since it last read it. Which write is best?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "An N3 Patch that deletes the expected old value"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A PATCH touches only the named triples, and its `where` and `deletes` make it fail with 409 instead of overwriting a value that changed since you read it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-write-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-write-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-write-r01-d3> .

<#q-read-write-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A PUT of the app's own copy of the full document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "PUT replaces everything, so other apps' newer triples would be lost."@en .

<#q-read-write-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A POST of the new triple to the document's own URL"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "POST creates a new resource in a container; it does not edit a document."@en .

<#q-read-write-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A DELETE of the document followed by a fresh PUT"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That loses other apps' triples and leaves a moment where the data is gone."@en .

<#q-read-write-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "When writing to a Solid pod, which pair of status codes usually means the resource is no longer in the state your app read, so it should re-read before trying again?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "409 from an N3 Patch and 412 from an `If-Match` write"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A patch whose `where` or `deletes` no longer match gets 409, and a stale `If-Match` gets 412; both usually mean \"re-read and retry\". A 409 from a `where` that matches twice needs a sharper `where`, not a retry."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-write-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-write-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-write-r02-d3> .

<#q-read-write-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "400 from a missing `Content-Type` and 422 from a bad patch"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Both mean the request itself is malformed, whatever the server state."@en .

<#q-read-write-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "405 from deleting the root and 404 from a POST to nothing"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "These say the method is never allowed or the target does not exist; retrying will not help."@en .

<#q-read-write-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "301 and 308 from a redirect to the resource's new URL"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A redirect says where to send the request; it says nothing about the resource's state."@en .

<#q-read-write-r03>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A new user's Solid pod has only the root container. Your app must create `/journal/2026/entry-1.ttl` at that exact URL. What is the simplest portable request?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "One PUT to `/journal/2026/entry-1.ttl` with `If-None-Match: *`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "PUT lets you choose the URL, the server creates the missing intermediate containers, and `If-None-Match: *` avoids overwriting an existing entry."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-write-r03-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-write-r03-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-write-r03-d3> .

<#q-read-write-r03-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "One POST to `/journal/2026/` with `Slug: entry-1.ttl`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The container doesn't exist (404), and a Slug would not guarantee the name."@en .

<#q-read-write-r03-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A POST per container with an LDP type link, then a PUT"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid Protocol v0.11 no longer guarantees that route, and it is unnecessary."@en .

<#q-read-write-r03-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "One PATCH to `/journal/` adding an `ldp:contains` triple for the entry"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Containment triples cannot be written directly; the server answers 409."@en .

<#q-read-write-r04>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Your app's PATCHes work on one Solid server but get `415 Unsupported Media Type` on another, which does list PATCH in `Allow`. What should it check?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The `Accept-Patch` header the second server advertises"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "415 means the server does not take the patch's media type, and it lists the ones it does take in `Accept-Patch`. N3 Patch is required, SPARQL Update is optional, and real servers differ, so read the header rather than assume."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-write-r04-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-write-r04-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-write-r04-d3> .

<#q-read-write-r04-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Whether the second server serves JSON-LD"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "All servers must serve JSON-LD for RDF sources, and it doesn't affect patching."@en .

<#q-read-write-r04-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Whether the second server requires `If-Match` on every PATCH"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A server that required a conditional request would answer `428 Precondition Required`, not 415."@en .

<#q-read-write-r04-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Whether the app has write access to the document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Missing permission is refused with 401 or 403, not 415."@en .

<#q-read-write-r05>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Your app uploads a photo to a Solid pod and wants to record who took it, in the most portable way. Which sequence fits?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Upload with its `Content-Type`, then N3 Patch its `describedby` resource"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Files are written with their real media type, and metadata about them goes into the description resource the server advertises with `rel=\"describedby\"` (compared case-insensitively; NSS 6.0.0 sends `describedBy`), which accepts an N3 Patch. The protocol does not promise every server such a link, so check that it is there."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-write-r05-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-write-r05-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-write-r05-d3> .

<#q-read-write-r05-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Upload the bytes, then PUT the creator to the photo's URL with `.meta` appended"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The protocol doesn't fix the description resource's name, and on CSS 7.2 a PUT there gets 405; follow the `describedby` link and PATCH."@en .

<#q-read-write-r05-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Upload the bytes as `text/turtle` with the creator triple added at the end"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That corrupts the image and stores it under the wrong media type."@en .

<#q-read-write-r05-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Upload the bytes without a `Content-Type`, so the server detects the type itself"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A body without `Content-Type` is rejected with 400."@en .

# Chapter 7: Identity and authentication.
<#ch-identity>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 6 ;
    dcterms:title "Identity and authentication"@en ;
    dcterms:description "How Solid logs people in: WebIDs and their profiles, the Solid-OIDC login flow, the trust anchor `solid:oidcIssuer`, and the four defences (PKCE, the `iss` check, DPoP and Client ID Documents) that make one login safe to use at many pods."@en ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-identity-r01> ,
                              <#q-identity-r02> ,
                              <#q-identity-r03> ,
                              <#q-identity-r04> ,
                              <#q-identity-r05> ,
                              <#q-identity-r06> ,
                              <#q-identity-r07> ,
                              <#q-identity-r08> .

<#ch-identity-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-identity> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Why identity is different in Solid

A classic web app keeps its own accounts, or offers "Sign in with X" for a few providers it registered with in advance. An **identity provider** is the server where you log in. After login it hands the app a **token**: a signed note the app can show to other servers as proof of who you are.

Solid wants something different: the user chooses their storage, their identity provider and their apps separately. A pod then has **no existing trust relationship** with the user's identity provider, and apps are often **ephemeral**: nobody registered them with the pod or the provider beforehand. To solve these two problems, Solid needs four things that classic logins don't:

1. a **global user identifier** that anyone can look up (the WebID);
2. a way to **discover which identity provider** may vouch for that identifier (`solid:oidcIssuer`);
3. a **global client identifier**, so an app (the *client*) can say who it is without registering first (a Client ID Document);
4. **tokens that can't be replayed**, that is, reused by whoever receives them, because one token is shown to many pods (DPoP).

This chapter takes them one at a time, then puts them together."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-identity-1a> .

<#q-identity-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid pod receives a request from an app it has never heard of, on behalf of a user whose identity provider it has never heard of. Why can't the pod rely on a pre-registered list?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Users pick providers and apps the pod has never seen"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Users pick providers and apps freely, so a pod meets providers and apps it has never seen before. Solid-OIDC names these two gaps (no prior trust, ephemeral clients) as its design drivers."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-1a-d3> .

<#q-identity-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Pods can only check users stored on the same server"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid users log in with a provider anywhere on the web; the pod's job is to check identities that come from elsewhere."@en .

<#q-identity-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Apps must first copy the data into their own database"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid separates apps from storage; apps read the data from the pod."@en .

<#q-identity-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A list of all providers would be too large to download"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Size isn't the issue; no such list can exist when anyone may run a provider or write an app."@en .

<#ch-identity-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-identity> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## WebID and the profile document

A **WebID** is an HTTP(S) URI that denotes an **agent**: a person, an organisation, a group or a piece of software. Looking it up gives the **WebID profile document**, an RDF document that describes that agent. This is the documents-versus-things split from Chapter 2: in `https://alice.example/profile/card#me`, the part before `#` names the document and `#me` names Alice herself.

```text
https://alice.example/profile/card      ← the profile document
https://alice.example/profile/card#me   ← Alice (her WebID)
```

Profiles are served as Turtle or JSON-LD (Chapter 3); readers ask for either with the `Accept` header (Chapter 2). Use **HTTPS** WebIDs: Solid-OIDC requires tokens to travel only over HTTPS. ⚠ Local test servers are the exception: as of October 2026, CSS 7.2 accepts `http` WebIDs and issuers only when the host name is `localhost` or ends in `.localhost` (so not `127.0.0.1`), which is why the examples in this chapter use `http://localhost:3000/`.

A WebID is just a URL, so **it doesn't have to live in your pod**. Inrupt's PodSpaces issues WebIDs on `id.inrupt.com` while pods live on `storage.inrupt.com`, and CSS can link an account to a WebID hosted elsewhere."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-identity-2a> ,
                         <#q-identity-2b> .

<#q-identity-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "What does the Solid WebID `https://alice.example/profile/card#me` identify?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Alice, the agent the profile describes"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A WebID denotes an agent; dereferencing it gives the profile document that describes that agent."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-2a-d3> .

<#q-identity-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The profile document at that address"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The document is the URL without `#me`; the WebID names the agent it describes."@en .

<#q-identity-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Alice's pod and everything stored in it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A pod is a storage found through `pim:storage`, not the WebID itself."@en .

<#q-identity-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Alice's login account at her identity provider"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The account and its credentials live at the provider; the WebID is a public identifier."@en .

<#q-identity-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app assumes a user's pod is always at the same origin as their WebID. Why is that a bug?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The WebID can be hosted somewhere other than the pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A WebID is only a URL; PodSpaces, for example, uses `id.inrupt.com` for WebIDs and `storage.inrupt.com` for pods. Find the storage by following `pim:storage` or Link headers."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-2b-d3> .

<#q-identity-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Pods are always hosted on the identity provider's origin"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The provider, the WebID host and the storage can all be different servers."@en .

<#q-identity-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The pod is always found by appending `/storage/` to the WebID"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "There is no fixed path, and guessing URLs is exactly what Solid apps must not do."@en .

<#q-identity-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Browsers block apps from reading a WebID's origin"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The origin of any URL is easy to read; the problem is that it tells you nothing about the pod."@en .

<#ch-identity-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-identity> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## What a profile contains

The Solid WebID Profile draft (v1.0.0, a Draft Community Group Report of January 2026) lists what a profile holds:

```turtle
PREFIX solid: <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#>
PREFIX foaf:  <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/>
PREFIX pim:   <http://www.w3.org/ns/pim/space#>
PREFIX ldp:   <http://www.w3.org/ns/ldp#>
PREFIX rdfs:  <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>

<#me> a foaf:Agent ;
    solid:oidcIssuer   <https://idp.example> ;                 # who may log me in
    pim:storage        <https://alice.pod.example/> ;          # my pods (0 or more)
    pim:preferencesFile <https://alice.pod.example/settings/prefs.ttl> ;  # private
    ldp:inbox          <https://alice.pod.example/inbox/> ;    # 0 or 1
    rdfs:seeAlso       <https://alice.pod.example/profile/extended.ttl> ;
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://alice.pod.example/profile/extended.ttl> .
```

`solid:oidcIssuer` names the identity providers allowed to log in as you (Step 7.9). `pim:storage` points to your pods, and `ldp:inbox` to an inbox others can post to. The **preferences file** holds settings, such as the link to the private type index, and is meant to be readable only by the owner's logged-in apps. `rdfs:seeAlso` and `foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf` point to **extended profile documents** holding more data.

The draft makes `pim:storage`, `ldp:inbox` and the extended-profile links optional (it allows zero `pim:storage` triples), and some servers write none. On CSS 7.2 (recorded October 2026), a new account's profile is only this:

```turtle
@prefix foaf: <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/>.
@prefix solid: <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#>.
@prefix pim: <http://www.w3.org/ns/pim/space#>.

<>
    a foaf:PersonalProfileDocument;
    foaf:maker <http://localhost:3000/alice/profile/card#me>;
    foaf:primaryTopic <http://localhost:3000/alice/profile/card#me>.

<http://localhost:3000/alice/profile/card#me>
    solid:oidcIssuer <http://localhost:3000/>;
    a foaf:Person.
```

There is no `pim:storage`, and not even the `pim:preferencesFile` the draft requires, so apps must not count on any of these links: they fall back to walking the Link headers up to the storage root (Chapter 5)."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-identity-3a> .

<#q-identity-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app has a user's WebID and needs to know where to save their notes. Which profile predicate should it follow?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "`pim:storage`" ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`pim:storage` links the agent to their storages (pods); a profile may list zero or more, so apps also need a fallback."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-3a-d3> .

<#q-identity-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`solid:oidcIssuer`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That names the identity provider, which need not store any data."@en .

<#q-identity-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`pim:preferencesFile`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is a private settings document, not the storage root."@en .

<#q-identity-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`rdfs:seeAlso`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That points to more profile data, not to where data should be saved."@en .

<#ch-identity-4>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-identity> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Protected profile data

`solid:oidcIssuer` decides who may log in as you. So the Solid WebID Profile draft calls it a **protected triple**: servers MUST refuse a PUT or PATCH that changes it, with **409 Conflict**. Other profile data can be written where the server allows it.

⚠ As of October 2026, CSS 7.2 does not enforce this: the owner's N3 Patch that swaps `solid:oidcIssuer` succeeds with `205 Reset Content`. Treat the profile as something only the user and apps she trusts should be able to write.

Some servers go further. Inrupt's **Enterprise Solid Server (ESS)** issues WebIDs (like `https://id.inrupt.com/alice`) from a separate WebID service. Apps cannot rely on the Solid Protocol to edit those documents, and ESS restricts which apps may modify them. So Inrupt advises apps to write profile data to the **extended profile**, an ordinary resource in the pod linked from the WebID."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-identity-4a> ,
                         <#q-identity-4b> .

<#q-identity-4a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Why does the Solid WebID Profile draft require servers to refuse a PATCH that changes `solid:oidcIssuer`?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Changing it would change who may log in as that person"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`solid:oidcIssuer` names the providers trusted to vouch for the WebID, so an attacker who could edit it could log in as you. The draft says servers MUST answer such writes with 409, though CSS 7.2 (2026) still accepts them."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-4a-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-4a-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-4a-d3> .

<#q-identity-4a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The triple is generated from the pod's ACL and can't be stored"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It is ordinary profile data; it is protected because of what it controls."@en .

<#q-identity-4a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Profiles must be read-only on every Solid server"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The draft protects only certain triples; other profile data can be written where the server allows it."@en .

<#q-identity-4a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "N3 Patch can't express a change to an IRI object"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "N3 Patch can insert and delete any triple without blank nodes."@en .

<#q-identity-4b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app wants to save a link in the profile of a user whose WebID is hosted by Inrupt's Enterprise Solid Server (ESS). Where should it write?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "To the user's extended profile document in their pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "ESS restricts editing the WebID document, so Inrupt recommends writing to the extended profile, which is a normal pod resource linked from the WebID."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-4b-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-4b-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-4b-d3> .

<#q-identity-4b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Into the WebID document itself with a PUT"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "ESS restricts WebID edits to its own WebID service and to approved apps; ordinary apps can't rely on writing it."@en .

<#q-identity-4b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Into the identity provider's metadata document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That document describes the provider, and apps can't write to it."@en .

<#q-identity-4b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Into a new `.meta` resource next to the WebID"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Description resources aren't part of the profile, and the WebID isn't in the pod anyway."@en .

<#ch-identity-5>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-identity> ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Access tokens and ID tokens

Two older standards do the heavy lifting. **OAuth 2.0** is about *authorization*: getting an **access token** that a client presents to a server to reach a resource. **OpenID Connect (OIDC)** adds *authentication* on top: an **ID token** that says who logged in. The server that logs people in is the **OpenID Provider (OP)**; Inrupt calls it the "Solid Identity Provider".

Both tokens are usually **JWTs**: a JSON object signed by its issuer, so anyone can check it wasn't altered. Its fields are called **claims**. A Solid-OIDC ID token looks like this:

```json
{ "webid": "https://janedoe.com/web#id",
  "iss": "https://idp.example.com",
  "sub": "janedoe",
  "aud": ["https://client.example.com/client_id", "solid"],
  "azp": "https://client.example.com/client_id",
  "iat": 1311280970, "exp": 1311281970 }
```

`iss` is who issued it; `sub` is only the user's local ID at that provider; `webid` is Solid's global identity; `iat`/`exp` are the issue and expiry times. `aud` says who it's for: the app (`azp`) and also `"solid"`, because any Solid server on the web may receive it.

Access tokens for Solid carry `aud` `"solid"` too. On CSS 7.2 (recorded October 2026), an access token issued to a script through client credentials (Step 13.5) had these claims; there was no ID token, so the access token itself carries `webid`. Its `sub` is the client's ID, not the WebID:

```json
{ "webid": "http://localhost:3000/alice/profile/card#me",
  "jti": "JbJ9i9K3kLSM-viA9zClg",
  "sub": "solid-curl_2814e0d3-…",
  "iss": "http://localhost:3000/",
  "aud": "solid",
  "client_id": "solid-curl_2814e0d3-…",
  "cnf": { "jkt": "w6cV4pf5zwfXr7oTQUrK6EcSZQ8a1Sxi6j88VCv5SXw" },
  "iat": 1791408787, "exp": 1791409387 }
```

It expires after 600 s; `cnf.jkt` is explained in Step 7.10."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-identity-5a> ,
                         <#q-identity-5b> .

<#q-identity-5a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect, what is the difference between an ID token and an access token?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "An ID token says who logged in; an access token grants access"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "OAuth 2.0 issues access tokens for authorization; OpenID Connect adds the ID token, which carries claims about the authenticated user."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-5a-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-5a-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-5a-d3> .

<#q-identity-5a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An ID token grants access to the pod; an access token says who you are"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The purposes are the other way round."@en .

<#q-identity-5a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An ID token is unsigned; an access token is a signed JWT"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "In Solid-OIDC the ID token is a signed JWT too; the two tokens differ in purpose, not in being signed."@en .

<#q-identity-5a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An ID token lasts forever; an access token expires"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "ID tokens carry a required `exp` claim and expire too."@en .

<#q-identity-5b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid server receives a Solid-OIDC access token whose `aud` claim names only the app's client ID. Why might it reject it?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Its `aud` lacks `\"solid\"`, so it isn't for pods"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "In Solid a token's audience is the client plus any Solid server, so `aud` must include `\"solid\"`; CSS's verifier checks this on the access token."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-5b-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-5b-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-5b-d3> .

<#q-identity-5b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Its `aud` must hold the pod's own URL instead"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`\"solid\"` is a fixed string that covers every Solid server, so no pod URL is needed."@en .

<#q-identity-5b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Its `aud` tells pods to skip checking the issuer"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Nothing in `aud` switches off the issuer check; it always applies."@en .

<#q-identity-5b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Its `aud` should name the user's WebID instead"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The WebID goes in the `webid` claim; `aud` names who the token is for."@en .

<#ch-identity-6>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-identity> ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## The authorization code flow

Solid uses OIDC's **authorization code flow**:

1. The app redirects the user to the OP.
2. The user logs in there (password, passkey… the app never sees it) and consents.
3. The OP redirects back to the app with a one-time **code**.
4. The app swaps the code for tokens at the OP's **token endpoint**.

The app finds the OP's endpoints and signing keys in the OP's metadata at `/.well-known/openid-configuration`. On CSS 7.2 (recorded October 2026) it includes these keys, and further ones for the `issuer`, the `token_endpoint` and the DPoP signing algorithms the OP accepts:

```json
{"authorization_endpoint":"http://localhost:3000/.oidc/auth",
 "claims_supported":["azp","sub","webid","sid","auth_time","iss"],
 "code_challenge_methods_supported":["S256"]}
```

In step 1 the app sends **scopes**: what the app asks for. `openid` asks for an ID token. Solid adds a `webid` scope for the `webid` claim; an OP that supports Solid-OIDC advertises it in `scopes_supported`. `offline_access` asks for a **refresh token**, which gets new tokens later without the user present (Step 13.7)."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-identity-6a> ,
                         <#q-identity-6b> .

<#q-identity-6a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "In the OpenID Connect authorization code flow, what does the app receive when the user is redirected back from the OP?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A one-time code it exchanges for tokens"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The redirect carries a short-lived code; the app then calls the token endpoint to exchange it for the ID token (and access and refresh tokens)."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-6a-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-6a-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-6a-d3> .

<#q-identity-6a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The user's password, encrypted for the app"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The user authenticates at the OP and the app never sees credentials."@en .

<#q-identity-6a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An access token valid for every pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Tokens come from the token endpoint after the code exchange, not in the redirect."@en .

<#q-identity-6a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The user's WebID profile document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The app fetches the profile itself; the redirect carries the code, not the profile."@en .

<#q-identity-6b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Which scope makes a Solid-OIDC provider put the user's WebID in the ID token?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "`webid`" ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Solid-OIDC adds the `webid` scope, which requests the `webid` claim; a conformant OP lists it in `scopes_supported`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-6b-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-6b-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-6b-d3> .

<#q-identity-6b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`openid`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It asks for an ID token, but the `webid` claim needs the `webid` scope."@en .

<#q-identity-6b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`profile`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A standard OIDC scope for name and picture, not Solid's global identity."@en .

<#q-identity-6b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`offline_access`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It asks for a refresh token, not for any identity claim."@en .

<#ch-identity-7>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-identity> ;
    schema:position 6 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## PKCE: protecting the code

Browser apps are **public clients**: they can't keep a secret. Without extra protection, an attacker who intercepts the redirect's code could exchange it for tokens. **PKCE** (RFC 7636) stops this.

A **hash** is a one-way fingerprint: easy to compute from the input, impossible to reverse. PKCE uses the SHA-256 hash, and BASE64URL turns the result into URL-safe text:

1. Before redirecting, the app makes a random **code verifier** (43-128 characters) and keeps it.
2. It sends `code_challenge = BASE64URL(SHA256(verifier))` with `code_challenge_method=S256`.
3. At the token endpoint it sends the verifier, and the OP recomputes the hash and compares.

The attacker has the code and maybe the challenge, but not the verifier, and the hash can't be reversed to get it. An OP that supports this lists `S256` in `code_challenge_methods_supported` in its metadata (Step 7.6)."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-identity-7a> ,
                         <#q-identity-7b> .

<#q-identity-7a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "In an OAuth login, an attacker intercepts the authorization code on its way to a PKCE-using app. Why can't they redeem it?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "They lack the code verifier that only the app holds"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The token endpoint requires the verifier whose S256 hash matches the challenge sent earlier, and only the app has it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-7a-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-7a-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-7a-d3> .

<#q-identity-7a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The code is encrypted with the user's password"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Codes aren't encrypted with passwords; PKCE works by proving knowledge of the verifier."@en .

<#q-identity-7a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The OP accepts codes only from the user's IP address"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "PKCE has nothing to do with IP addresses."@en .

<#q-identity-7a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The code challenge is secret and never leaves the app"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The challenge is sent in the authorization request; it's the verifier that stays secret."@en .

<#q-identity-7b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In PKCE (RFC 7636), what does the app send with the redirect, and what does it keep for the token request?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It sends the challenge and keeps the verifier"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The app sends `code_challenge` (the S256 hash) up front and reveals the verifier only at the token endpoint, where the OP recomputes the hash."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-7b-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-7b-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-7b-d3> .

<#q-identity-7b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It sends the verifier and keeps the challenge"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The other way round; the verifier must not travel through the browser redirect."@en .

<#q-identity-7b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It sends both and keeps neither for later"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Then an interceptor would hold everything needed to redeem the code."@en .

<#q-identity-7b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It sends a password hash and keeps the code"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The app never handles the user's password; the OP does."@en .

<#ch-identity-8>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-identity> ;
    schema:position 7 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## The `iss` check: stopping mix-up

A second attack, **mix-up**, targets apps that talk to many OPs, as Solid apps do: the attacker tricks the app into sending a code to the wrong provider. Say a user picks an honest provider, but an attacker makes the app believe she chose the attacker's provider, which the app also supports. She logs in at the honest provider, its code comes back, and the app sends that code to the attacker's token endpoint. RFC 9207 adds an `iss` parameter to the redirect back, naming the OP that issued the code:

```http
Location: https://app.example/callback?code=m-OrTP...&iss=https%3A%2F%2Fidp.example
```

In the example, `iss` names the honest provider, not the one the app expected, so the app stops. The Solid-OIDC draft of July 2026 makes this mandatory: the OP must include `iss`, and the app must check it equals the OP it sent the user to, by exact string match, and must not redeem the code otherwise.

⚠ As of October 2026 this rule is in the July 2026 draft but not in the published Solid-OIDC v0.1.0 of March 2022. Inrupt's and solidcommunity.net's OPs already advertise support for `iss`."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-identity-8a> ,
                         <#q-identity-8b> .

<#q-identity-8a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In a Solid-OIDC login, what should an app do when the `iss` in the redirect doesn't match the OP it sent the user to?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Refuse to redeem the code and abort the login"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Under the July 2026 Solid-OIDC draft and RFC 9207, a missing or mismatched `iss` means the code must not be redeemed."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-8a-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-8a-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-8a-d3> .

<#q-identity-8a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Redeem the code at the OP named in `iss`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is exactly what a mix-up attacker wants."@en .

<#q-identity-8a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Ignore `iss`, because PKCE already covers this"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "PKCE stops code interception, not mix-up between providers."@en .

<#q-identity-8a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Redeem it, then check the ID token's issuer later"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The code would already have been sent to the wrong party."@en .

<#q-identity-8b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Why does the OAuth mix-up attack matter more for Solid apps than for an app with one \"Sign in with X\" button?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Solid apps talk to many OPs, which mix-up exploits"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Mix-up tricks an app that works with several providers into sending a code to the wrong one; Solid apps meet whatever OP each user chose."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-8b-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-8b-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-8b-d3> .

<#q-identity-8b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Solid OPs skip PKCE, so codes are unprotected"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid-OIDC assumes the code flow with PKCE."@en .

<#q-identity-8b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Solid apps redeem codes at the pod, not the OP"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Codes are always redeemed at the OP's token endpoint."@en .

<#q-identity-8b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In Solid the `iss` value is the user's WebID"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`iss` names the provider that issued the code, not the user."@en .

<#ch-identity-9>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-identity> ;
    schema:position 8 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Issuer discovery: the trust anchor

Anyone can run an OP and sign a token saying `"webid": "https://alice.example/profile/card#me"`. Solid is safe because **a token is believed only if Alice's own profile lists its issuer** in `solid:oidcIssuer`. A verifier fetches the `webid`'s profile, checks the token's `iss` is listed, then checks the signature.

Apps use the same link to **start** a login. A provider list or a typed issuer URL is fine too: the pod still checks `iss`. Never **guess** the OP from the WebID's domain or assume a default; WebID host, OP and pod are often different servers.

The match is exact, trailing slash included. On CSS 7.2 (recorded October 2026), the two agree character for character:

```text
profile:      solid:oidcIssuer <http://localhost:3000/>
OP metadata:  "issuer":"http://localhost:3000/"
```

⚠ As of October 2026, CSS 7.2, NSS 6 and, by its documented default, ESS 2.7 issue tokens only for WebIDs of their own accounts or linked to one. On CSS 7.2 a linked WebID gets tokens before its profile lists that provider: pods answer 401 until it does, and CSS pods keep doing so for up to two minutes more, as they cache issuer lists. So after login, compare `iss` exactly with the profile's issuers (`https://solidcommunity.net/` has a trailing slash, `https://login.inrupt.com` none) and show a clear error (Step 12.6)."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-identity-9a> ,
                         <#q-identity-9b> .

<#q-identity-9a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid, a malicious OpenID Provider signs a valid-looking token claiming Alice's WebID. What stops a pod from accepting it?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Alice's profile doesn't list that OP as an issuer"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Verifiers fetch the WebID profile and accept the token only if its `iss` appears in a `solid:oidcIssuer` triple there."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-9a-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-9a-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-9a-d3> .

<#q-identity-9a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Every pod keeps a central list of approved OPs"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid deliberately has no central list; trust comes from each user's profile."@en .

<#q-identity-9a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The token's signature can only be made by Alice"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The OP signs the token, not Alice."@en .

<#q-identity-9a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "OPs may only issue tokens for WebIDs on their own domain"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Some OPs restrict themselves this way, but nothing makes a malicious OP do so; only the profile check stops it."@en .

<#q-identity-9b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A user logs into a Solid app by entering the WebID `https://id.inrupt.com/alice`. How should the app find her OpenID Provider?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Read `solid:oidcIssuer` from her WebID profile"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The profile's `solid:oidcIssuer` is the authoritative pointer to the user's OP, and apps must not infer it from the WebID's domain or assume a default."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-9b-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-9b-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-9b-d3> .

<#q-identity-9b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Use the WebID's origin, `https://id.inrupt.com`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The OP may be on a different origin; guessing can send her to the wrong server."@en .

<#q-identity-9b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Ask the pod for its `/.well-known/openid-configuration`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Pods don't have to be OPs, and the app doesn't know the pod yet."@en .

<#q-identity-9b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Use the app's default provider unless she objects"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is guessing; her profile may list a different OP."@en .

<#ch-identity-10>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-identity> ;
    schema:position 9 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## DPoP: tokens bound to a key

An ordinary OAuth token is a **bearer** token: whoever holds it can use it. Solid shows one token to many pods, so a malicious pod could replay it elsewhere. Solid-OIDC therefore requires **DPoP** (RFC 9449), optional in plain OAuth.

The app makes a key pair and keeps the private key; the OP puts a **thumbprint** (a short hash) of its public key in the token's `cnf.jkt` claim. For **every request**, the app signs a **DPoP proof**, a short JWT holding the method (`htm`), the URL (`htu`), the time (`iat`), a unique id (`jti`) and, with a token, its hash (`ath`). The server checks the proof's key against `cnf.jkt`, and its `htm`/`htu` against this request. A pod that saw your token lacks that key.

On CSS 7.2 (recorded October 2026), reading a private note without, then with, a token:

```http
GET /alice/notes/first.ttl HTTP/1.1

HTTP/1.1 401 Unauthorized
WWW-Authenticate: Bearer scope="openid webid"

GET /alice/notes/first.ttl HTTP/1.1
Authorization: DPoP eyJhbGciOiJFUzI…
DPoP: eyJhbGciOiJFUzI1NiIs…

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
```

Each login makes a new key, so a new `jkt`.

⚠ As of October 2026, CSS 7.2 accepts proofs up to about 120 s old (plus 120 s clock tolerance), blocks reused `jti`s and checks `ath` only if present.

DPoP does **not** stop **XSS** (cross-site scripting: an attacker's script in your app), which can sign with the app's key."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-identity-10a> ,
                         <#q-identity-10b> .

<#q-identity-10a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid, a malicious pod records a DPoP-bound token you sent it. Why can't it use the token at your real pod?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It cannot sign proofs without your app's private key"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The token's `cnf.jkt` binds it to the app's key; every request needs a fresh proof signed with the private key, which the malicious pod never had."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-10a-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-10a-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-10a-d3> .

<#q-identity-10a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The token is encrypted so only the OP can read it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The pod can read the token; what it lacks is the key to make proofs."@en .

<#q-identity-10a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Tokens are valid only on the pod that requested them"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "In Solid one token is shown to many pods, which is why DPoP is needed."@en .

<#q-identity-10a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Your real pod blocks requests from other servers' IPs"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The protection is cryptographic, not based on network addresses."@en .

<#q-identity-10b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "An attacker injects a script into a Solid notes app (XSS). The app's tokens are DPoP-bound with a non-exportable key. What can the script do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Make requests to the user's pod as the app"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "RFC 9449 says plainly that DPoP is no defence against XSS: malicious code in the app can have the browser sign with the app's key. Preventing XSS is a separate job."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-10b-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-10b-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-10b-d3> .

<#q-identity-10b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing, because the token is bound to the app's key"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The script runs inside the app, so it can ask the browser to sign with that key."@en .

<#q-identity-10b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only read the token, never use it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It doesn't need to take the key away; it can make fresh proofs in place."@en .

<#q-identity-10b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only reach pods on the app's own origin"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The app talks to pods on any origin, and so can the script."@en .

<#ch-identity-11>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-identity> ;
    schema:position 10 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## How an app identifies itself

The OP must know which app (the **client**) is asking. Solid gives three options:

1. **Client ID Document** (recommended): the `client_id` is an HTTPS URL that serves JSON-LD describing the app.
2. **Dynamic registration**: the app registers itself at the OP at runtime and gets an opaque `client_id`.
3. **Static registration**: a developer registers the app with an OP in advance and gets an ID and secret, typical for scripts (Step 13.5).

A Client ID Document uses the context `https://www.w3.org/ns/solid/oidc-context.jsonld`:

```json
{ "@context": ["https://www.w3.org/ns/solid/oidc-context.jsonld"],
  "client_id": "https://app.example/id",
  "client_name": "My Notes",
  "redirect_uris": ["https://app.example/callback"],
  "scope": "openid webid offline_access",
  "grant_types": ["refresh_token", "authorization_code"],
  "response_types": ["code"] }
```

The OP fetches the Client ID Document and refuses a `redirect_uri` that isn't listed. So the document proves that **tokens for this client ID only go to the redirect URIs its URL's owner chose**: another site can't receive them. It does **not** prove the app behaves well. An IETF draft, the **Client ID Metadata Document (CIMD)**, generalises the idea to all of OAuth. It calls itself "largely inspired by" Solid-OIDC, and also lists IndieAuth and OpenID Federation as earlier specs that used URLs as client IDs."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-identity-11a> ,
                         <#q-identity-11b> .

<#q-identity-11a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid-OIDC, what does a Client ID Document actually guarantee?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Tokens go only to redirect URIs its owner listed"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The OP matches the `redirect_uri` against the document at the client ID URL, so only that URL's controller can receive tokens for it. It says nothing about whether the app is trustworthy."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-11a-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-11a-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-11a-d3> .

<#q-identity-11a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The app has been reviewed and is safe to use"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Nobody vouches for the app's behaviour; the document only fixes where tokens go."@en .

<#q-identity-11a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The app can only read the data the user chooses"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Limiting what apps can access is an authorization problem, handled by access control."@en .

<#q-identity-11a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Its tokens can't be replayed at other pods"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Stopping replay is DPoP's job, not the client ID's."@en .

<#q-identity-11b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid-OIDC, which `client_id` value is valid for a Client ID Document?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "An HTTPS URL that serves the app's JSON-LD document"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The client ID is an HTTPS URL that the OP dereferences to get the client's metadata, including its redirect URIs."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-11b-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-11b-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-11b-d3> .

<#q-identity-11b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The app's display name, such as \"My Notes\""@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A name goes in `client_name`; the ID must be a dereferenceable URL."@en .

<#q-identity-11b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The WebID of the developer who wrote the app"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The client ID identifies the app, not the person who wrote it."@en .

<#q-identity-11b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A random string generated in the browser"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A random string can't be dereferenced; that would need registration."@en .

<#ch-identity-12>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-identity> ;
    schema:position 11 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## The whole login, and what the pod learns

Putting it together, a browser login runs like this:

1. The app learns the OP: from `solid:oidcIssuer` in the user's profile, or from the provider the user picked (Step 7.9).
2. The app fetches the OP's `/.well-known/openid-configuration`.
3. It creates a PKCE verifier and redirects to the OP with `scope=openid webid offline_access`, its `client_id` and the challenge.
4. The OP fetches the Client ID Document and checks the redirect URI; the user logs in and consents.
5. The OP redirects back with a code and `iss`; the app checks `iss`.
6. The app makes a DPoP key pair and redeems the code with the verifier and a DPoP proof.
7. It gets a DPoP-bound ID token (and access and refresh tokens).
8. For each pod request, it sends a token plus a fresh DPoP proof.

The trust anchor and each defence answer a different attack:

| Mechanism | Stops | Step |
|---|---|---|
| PKCE | an intercepted code being redeemed | 7.7 |
| `iss` check | mix-up between providers | 7.8 |
| `solid:oidcIssuer` (trust anchor) | a provider vouching for someone else's WebID | 7.9 |
| DPoP | a pod replaying your token elsewhere | 7.10 |
| Client ID Document | tokens reaching another site | 7.11 |

After verifying a request, the pod knows three things: the **WebID**, the **client** and the **issuer**. Chapters 8-9 use them in access rules; ACP can match all three (Step 9.1)."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-identity-12a> .

<#q-identity-12a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "After a Solid pod verifies a Solid-OIDC request, which facts can its access rules use?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The user's WebID, the client ID and the issuer"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A verified request yields the WebID, the client and the issuer; ACP rules can match all three, while WAC matches only the WebID (plus the browser's Origin)."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-12a-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-12a-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-12a-d3> .

<#q-identity-12a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The user's password and their OP account name"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The pod never sees credentials; the OP handles them."@en .

<#q-identity-12a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only the user's WebID, since apps are anonymous"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The client ID (`azp`/`client_id`) also reaches the pod."@en .

<#q-identity-12a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The user's IP address and browser fingerprint"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid-OIDC carries no such data; it identifies the WebID, client and issuer."@en .

<#ch-identity-13>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-identity> ;
    schema:position 12 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Spec vs practice: UMA

The last item of the login in Step 7.12, sending a token to the pod, hides a gap between spec and servers. Solid-OIDC (v0.1.0 and the July 2026 draft alike) says a pod's authorization server SHOULD use **UMA** (User-Managed Access, an OAuth extension in which the pod points apps to an authorization server that issues tokens for that pod). The app trades its ID token there for a pod-specific access token. Deployed servers like CSS instead accept DPoP-bound access tokens directly: the OP's access token carries a `webid` claim, as in the example of Step 7.5. The checks are the same either way: WebID, issuer listed in the profile, signature, DPoP binding. Libraries hide the difference (Step 12.5)."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-identity-13a> .

<#q-identity-13a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "On CSS 7.2 (2026), a Solid app sends a valid DPoP-bound access token straight to the pod, without any UMA exchange. What happens?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "CSS accepts it and runs the usual checks on it"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Solid-OIDC says pods SHOULD use UMA, but in 2026 deployed servers like CSS verify DPoP-bound access tokens with a `webid` claim directly, running the same checks."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-13a-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-13a-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-13a-d3> .

<#q-identity-13a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "CSS rejects it until the app trades an ID token via UMA"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The spec says SHOULD use UMA, but CSS accepts the OP's access token directly."@en .

<#q-identity-13a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "CSS accepts it but skips the issuer check"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The WebID, issuer, signature and DPoP checks all run on the access token."@en .

<#q-identity-13a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "CSS asks the OP to re-issue it for this pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "No such exchange exists; the pod verifies the token it was given."@en .

<#ch-identity-14>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-identity> ;
    schema:position 13 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Older login methods

Older mechanisms still turn up in tutorials. **WebID-TLS** used browser client certificates instead of an OP. The ecosystem first relied on it; the Solid Protocol now recommends Solid-OIDC and keeps WebID-TLS only as an optional additional method. **WebID-OIDC** was Solid-OIDC's predecessor; its spec repository was archived in 2021. The `solid-auth-client` library calls itself "a legacy browser library" and tells new projects to use `solid-client-authn` instead; its last release was in June 2021. If a tutorial imports `solid-auth-client`, its login code predates DPoP: look for a newer one."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-identity-14a> .

<#q-identity-14a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "You find a tutorial that logs users into Solid with WebID-TLS client certificates. What should you conclude?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It is outdated; Solid now recommends Solid-OIDC"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The Solid Protocol says the ecosystem first relied on WebID-TLS and now recommends Solid-OIDC, keeping WebID-TLS only as an optional additional method."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-14a-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-14a-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-14a-d3> .

<#q-identity-14a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It is the newer method that replaces Solid-OIDC"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "WebID-TLS came first and was superseded, not the other way round."@en .

<#q-identity-14a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It is required for every Solid server to work"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The Protocol recommends Solid-OIDC; WebID-TLS remains only as an optional additional method."@en .

<#q-identity-14a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It is the same as Solid-OIDC with another name"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "WebID-TLS uses client certificates and no OP at all."@en .

<#q-identity-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid login starts from the user's WebID. What is the app's first step, before it knows where to send her to log in?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The app reads `solid:oidcIssuer` from the profile"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The app can't start the login until it knows the OP, so reading `solid:oidcIssuer` comes first; the redirect and `iss` check, the code exchange and DPoP-signed pod requests follow."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-r01-d3> .

<#q-identity-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The app redeems the code at the token endpoint"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A code only exists after the OP redirects back, so the app must know the OP first."@en .

<#q-identity-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The app checks the `iss` in the redirect"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The redirect comes after the user has logged in at the OP."@en .

<#q-identity-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The app sends a DPoP proof to the pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Pod requests come last, once the app holds tokens."@en .

<#q-identity-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid-OIDC, which mechanism lets an app that nobody registered say who it is?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A Client ID Document at an HTTPS URL"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Of Solid's four login needs, the global client identifier is met by the Client ID Document; the WebID is the global user ID, `solid:oidcIssuer` finds the provider, and DPoP stops replay."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-r02-d3> .

<#q-identity-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A WebID in the user's profile"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A WebID identifies the user (or another agent), not the app making the request."@en .

<#q-identity-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A DPoP key pair made by the app"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "DPoP binds tokens to a key so they can't be replayed; it doesn't name the app."@en .

<#q-identity-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A PKCE verifier sent at login"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "PKCE protects the code exchange; it doesn't identify the app."@en .

<#q-identity-r03>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid pod receives a request with a valid DPoP-bound token, from an app whose redirect URI is listed in its Client ID Document. But the token's issuer isn't listed in the user's profile. What happens?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The pod doesn't believe the token's WebID"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Each piece answers a different question; only the profile's `solid:oidcIssuer` decides which providers may vouch for a WebID, so a token from an unlisted issuer is not believed."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-r03-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-r03-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-r03-d3> .

<#q-identity-r03-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The pod accepts it, because the app's ID checks out"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The Client ID Document says where tokens may go; it can't vouch for the user's identity."@en .

<#q-identity-r03-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The pod accepts it, because DPoP proves it genuine"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "DPoP proves the sender holds the key, not that the issuer may speak for this WebID."@en .

<#q-identity-r03-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The pod adds the issuer to the profile and goes on"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Pods never edit a user's trusted issuers; those are protected triples."@en .

<#q-identity-r04>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid, a user's WebID is `https://id.example/alice`, her OpenID Provider is `https://login.example` and her pod is `https://pods.example/alice/`. Is this setup valid?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Yes; all three may be on different hosts"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Solid decouples identity, issuer and storage; the profile links them with `solid:oidcIssuer` and `pim:storage`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-r04-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-r04-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-r04-d3> .

<#q-identity-r04-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No; the OP must be on the WebID's origin"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The profile names the OP, which can be anywhere."@en .

<#q-identity-r04-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No; the WebID must live inside the pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A WebID is just a URL and can be hosted anywhere."@en .

<#q-identity-r04-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only if the pod's server also runs the OP"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Pods and OPs are separate roles that may be on different servers."@en .

<#q-identity-r05>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Which part of a Solid-OIDC login carries the user's global identity?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The token's `webid` claim"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Solid-OIDC adds a `webid` claim for the global identity; `sub` is only the OP's local subject. The WebID is trusted once its profile lists the token's issuer."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-r05-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-r05-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-r05-d3> .

<#q-identity-r05-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The token's `sub` claim"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`sub` is only a local ID at the OP and may differ from the WebID."@en .

<#q-identity-r05-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The login request's `client_id`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That identifies the app, not the user."@en .

<#q-identity-r05-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The redirect's `iss` parameter"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`iss` names the provider, not the user."@en .

<#q-identity-r06>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In a Solid-OIDC login, which attack does DPoP stop?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A pod replaying your token at another pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "DPoP binds the token to the app's key, so a pod that saw it can't make the proofs needed to use it elsewhere. PKCE protects the code, the `iss` check stops mix-up, and the Client ID Document fixes where tokens go."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-r06-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-r06-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-r06-d3> .

<#q-identity-r06-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An attacker redeeming an intercepted code"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is PKCE's job: only the app holds the code verifier."@en .

<#q-identity-r06-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An app sending its code to the wrong provider"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is mix-up, which the `iss` check stops."@en .

<#q-identity-r06-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Tokens being sent to another site's callback"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The Client ID Document's listed redirect URIs stop that."@en .

<#q-identity-r07>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A user picks provider X from a Solid app's list, but her WebID profile lists only provider Y. Login at X succeeds and the token's `webid` is her WebID. What should the app do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Check `iss` against her profile and show a clear error"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Pods won't accept a token from an issuer her profile doesn't list, so checking `iss` against `solid:oidcIssuer` lets the app show a clear error instead of unexplained 401s. The fix is hers: add X to her profile, or log in at Y."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-r07-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-r07-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-r07-d3> .

<#q-identity-r07-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing; a successful login means pods will accept her"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Pods accept the token only if her profile lists its issuer, and X isn't listed."@en .

<#q-identity-r07-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Add X to her profile's `solid:oidcIssuer` for her"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That changes who may log in as her, which only she should decide."@en .

<#q-identity-r07-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Retry the login at X until the pod accepts the token"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Every token from X fails the same issuer check at the pod."@en .

<#q-identity-r08>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Which way of finding a Solid user's OpenID Provider counts as guessing it from her WebID?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Taking the OP from the WebID's domain"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Guessing means deriving the issuer from the WebID's domain or assuming a default. Issuer-first logins are fine, because verifiers accept a token only if the user's profile lists its issuer."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-identity-r08-d1> ,
                          <#q-identity-r08-d2> ,
                          <#q-identity-r08-d3> .

<#q-identity-r08-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Showing a list of providers to pick from"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Legitimate; the pod still checks the token's issuer against the profile."@en .

<#q-identity-r08-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Letting the user type their provider's URL"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Legitimate for the same reason: the profile has the final say."@en .

<#q-identity-r08-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Reading `solid:oidcIssuer` from the profile"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is the authoritative pointer, not a guess."@en .

# Chapter 8: Web Access Control.
<#ch-wac>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 7 ;
    dcterms:title "Web Access Control"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Write, read and debug Web Access Control (WAC) rules in a Solid pod, and know what a client sees when access is checked."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-wac-r01> ,
                              <#q-wac-r02> ,
                              <#q-wac-r03> ,
                              <#q-wac-r04> ,
                              <#q-wac-r05> .

<#ch-wac-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-wac> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Authentication vs authorization; rules are data

Chapter 7 answered "who are you?" (*authentication*): after Solid-OIDC login, each request carries a token naming the user's WebID, the app and the issuer. This chapter answers "what may you do?" (*authorization*). Solid keeps them apart: the WAC spec "does not specify mechanisms for authentication".

The rules are ordinary RDF documents (usually Turtle) stored in the pod next to the data they protect, and read and written with the HTTP requests of Chapter 6.

Solid has two rule languages. **Web Access Control (WAC)** is older, simpler and the most widely supported; this chapter teaches it. **Access Control Policy (ACP)** is newer and more expressive; Inrupt's ESS uses it, and the Community Solid Server (CSS) offers it as an option. Chapter 9 teaches it. Every Solid server must implement WAC, ACP or both, while clients must conform to both, so a portable app has to cope with either.

Neither is a W3C standard. As of 2026, WAC 1.0.0 (2024-05-12) is a Draft Community Group Report, and ACP 0.9.0 (2022) is an Editor's Draft. CSS 7.2 uses WAC by default."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-wac-1a> ,
                         <#q-wac-1b> .

<#q-wac-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A user has logged in to a Solid app with Solid-OIDC. What does the pod server still have to decide before answering their request?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Whether the stored access rules let that identity do this operation"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Authentication establishes who is asking; authorization then checks that identity against the rule documents stored with the data."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-wac-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-wac-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-wac-1a-d3> .

<#q-wac-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Whether the user's identity provider is registered with the pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid pods keep no registry of approved identity providers; ACP can match on the issuer, but only as part of the stored rules."@en .

<#q-wac-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Whether the user's password matches the one stored in the pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Pods never see passwords; the identity provider handles login."@en .

<#q-wac-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Whether the app was installed from the pod's app store"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "There is no app store; apps are unregistered and identify themselves by a client ID."@en .

<#q-wac-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "You are writing a sharing feature for a Solid app that must work on any Solid server. Which access control languages does it need to handle?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Both WAC and ACP, because a server may use either"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Every Solid server must implement WAC, ACP or both, so a portable client has to detect which one it faces and handle it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-wac-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-wac-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-wac-1b-d3> .

<#q-wac-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only WAC, because every server is required to support it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The Solid Protocol allows ACP-only servers, such as Inrupt's ESS."@en .

<#q-wac-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only ACP, because WAC has been deprecated"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "WAC is not deprecated; it is the most widely deployed language."@en .

<#q-wac-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Neither, because sharing is handled by the identity provider"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Access rules live in the pod and are enforced by the pod server, not the identity provider."@en .

<#ch-wac-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-wac> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## WAC: finding the ACL

In WAC the rules for a resource live in an **ACL resource**, one of the auxiliary resources of Chapter 5. A server advertises it with a `Link` header whose `rel` is `acl`.

The spec is blunt: "Clients MUST NOT derive the URI of the ACL resource through string operations on the URI of the resource." Appending `.acl` happens to work on CSS and NSS, but it is only their convention, and the ACL may even be on a different server. Always follow the link.

On CSS 7.2 (recorded in 2026, other headers left out), Alice, logged in, sent a HEAD on her new note and a GET on its ACL. The note links its ACL, but the ACL doesn't exist yet: the note has no rules of its own, and `Allow` shows that one can be created.

```http
HEAD /alice/notes/first.ttl HTTP/1.1
Authorization: DPoP …
DPoP: …

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Link: <http://localhost:3000/alice/notes/first.ttl.acl>; rel="acl"

GET /alice/notes/first.ttl.acl HTTP/1.1
Authorization: DPoP …
DPoP: …

HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found
Allow: PATCH, PUT
```

A server attaches at most one ACL directly to a resource. Inside it is an unordered set of **authorizations** (`acl:Authorization`), any one of which can permit a request. Each says *what* can be accessed, *how* (the modes) and *who* may do it:

```turtle
@prefix acl: <http://www.w3.org/ns/auth/acl#>.

<#bob-reads>
    a acl:Authorization;
    acl:accessTo <todo.ttl>;                          # what
    acl:mode acl:Read;                                # how
    acl:agent <https://bob.example/profile/card#me>.  # who
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-wac-2a> ,
                         <#q-wac-2b> .

<#q-wac-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Your Solid app needs the Web Access Control (WAC) ACL of `https://pod.example/photos/cat.jpg`. How should it find the ACL's URL?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Read the `Link` header with `rel=\"acl\"` from a HEAD or GET on the photo"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "WAC requires clients to discover the ACL through `Link: rel=\"acl\"`; the `.acl` suffix is only some servers' convention."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-wac-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-wac-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-wac-2a-d3> .

<#q-wac-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Append `.acl` to the photo's URL, as Solid servers name ACLs that way"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is only a convention of some servers; clients must not build the URL by string operations."@en .

<#q-wac-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Fetch `/.acl` at the pod root, which holds every rule for the pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Each resource can have its own ACL; the root ACL is only used when nothing nearer exists."@en .

<#q-wac-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Look for an `acl:accessControl` triple in the user's WebID profile"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Profiles don't point to ACLs; the resource itself advertises its ACL in a `Link` header."@en .

<#q-wac-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front """In this Solid Web Access Control (WAC) authorization, which line decides *who* gets access?

```turtle
@prefix acl: <http://www.w3.org/ns/auth/acl#>.

<#team> a acl:Authorization;
    acl:accessTo <plan.ttl>;
    acl:mode acl:Write;
    acl:agent <https://carol.example/profile/card#me>.
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:back "`acl:agent <https://carol.example/profile/card#me>`" ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Every authorization says what (`acl:accessTo`), how (`acl:mode`) and who (`acl:agent` and its relatives); it never names servers or identity providers."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-wac-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-wac-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-wac-2b-d3> .

<#q-wac-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`acl:accessTo <plan.ttl>`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That names what can be accessed, not who."@en .

<#q-wac-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`acl:mode acl:Write`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That names how the resource may be used, not who."@en .

<#q-wac-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`a acl:Authorization`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That only marks the description as an authorization; it names nobody."@en .

<#ch-wac-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-wac> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## The four modes

WAC has four access modes:

| Mode | Lets you |
|---|---|
| `acl:Read` | view the resource (GET) |
| `acl:Write` | create, change or delete it (PUT, POST, PATCH, DELETE) |
| `acl:Append` | add information but not remove it (POST, PATCH) |
| `acl:Control` | read and write the resource's ACL |

Two relationships cause most confusion. First, **Append is a subclass of Write**: if a request needs Append, an agent with Write is allowed too. The reverse is not true; Append never gives Write. Append alone is what you want for a drop box such as an inbox, where people may add messages but not read or remove others' messages.

Second, **Control covers only the ACL**. "Having acl:Control does not imply that the agent has acl:Read or acl:Write access to the resource itself." An owner can even remove their own Write to avoid accidental overwrites, and add it back later because they still have Control.

Finally, if an ACL contains a mode the server doesn't understand, it must carry on as if that mode were not there. Unknown modes "MUST NOT cause increased access"."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-wac-3a> ,
                         <#q-wac-3b> .

<#q-wac-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid's Web Access Control (WAC), Bob has `acl:Write`, and no other mode, on a container and on everything inside it. Can he POST a new item into it?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Yes, because whatever Append allows, Write allows too"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A request needing `acl:Append` is also allowed for agents with `acl:Write`, but not the other way round. Bob's Write covers both the container and the new item."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-wac-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-wac-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-wac-3a-d3> .

<#q-wac-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, because Append must always be granted in its own rule"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Write already includes Append; listing Append separately is unnecessary."@en .

<#q-wac-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, because POST requires Control on the container"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Control is only for the ACL; POST needs Append or Write."@en .

<#q-wac-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only if he also has Read, because writing requires reading"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Neither Write nor Append depends on Read."@en .

<#q-wac-3b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid's Web Access Control (WAC), Carol has `acl:Control` but no other mode on a document. What can she do right now, without changing any rules?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Read and edit its ACL, but not the document"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Control gives access to the ACL only, but because she can edit the ACL, Carol can give herself Read or Write."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-wac-3b-d1> ,
                          <#q-wac-3b-d2> ,
                          <#q-wac-3b-d3> .

<#q-wac-3b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Read and edit the document, since Control includes every mode"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Control does not imply Read or Write on the resource itself."@en .

<#q-wac-3b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Read the document but not change it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Control gives no Read on the resource, only access to its ACL."@en .

<#q-wac-3b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Delete the document, since Control is the owner's mode"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Deleting needs Write; Control only covers the ACL."@en .

<#ch-wac-4>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-wac> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Who and what

**Who** (access subjects):
- `acl:agent <WebID>`: one specific agent.
- `acl:agentClass foaf:Agent`: **everyone**, including requests with no login at all.
- `acl:agentClass acl:AuthenticatedAgent`: anyone who is logged in, with any WebID.
- `acl:agentGroup <group>`: the members of a `vcard:Group`, listed with `vcard:hasMember` in a separate document, which has its own ACL (Step 9.8 puts groups to work).

**What** (access objects): `acl:accessTo <r>` covers resource `r` itself. `acl:default <container/>` is inherited by the container's descendants that have no ACL of their own (Step 8.6).

An authorization applies only if it has all four of these: `a acl:Authorization`, at least one `accessTo` or `default`, at least one `acl:mode`, and at least one `acl:agent`, `acl:agentGroup` or `acl:agentClass` (or the `acl:origin` restriction of Step 9.11). Leave one out and the rule silently does nothing.

```turtle
@prefix acl: <http://www.w3.org/ns/auth/acl#>.

# Logged-in users may drop items in this inbox but not read it
<#inbox> a acl:Authorization;
    acl:agentClass acl:AuthenticatedAgent;
    acl:accessTo <./>;
    acl:mode acl:Append.
```

Access is granted when a matching authorization exists, and denied otherwise (the spec also adds an `Origin` check for browser requests, which servers apply unevenly; see Step 9.11). WAC only grants: it has **no deny rules**, so you can't say "everyone except Mallory". To exclude someone, grant to a narrower set instead."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-wac-4a> ,
                         <#q-wac-4b> ,
                         <#q-wac-4c> .

<#q-wac-4a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid's Web Access Control (WAC), which authorization makes a document readable by people who have not logged in?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "One with `acl:agentClass foaf:Agent`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`foaf:Agent` means every agent, logged in or not; `acl:AuthenticatedAgent` means only logged-in ones."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-wac-4a-d1> ,
                          <#q-wac-4a-d2> ,
                          <#q-wac-4a-d3> .

<#q-wac-4a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "One with `acl:agentClass acl:AuthenticatedAgent`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That class requires a logged-in agent; anonymous requests don't match."@en .

<#q-wac-4a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "One with `acl:agentGroup` pointing to a public group"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Groups contain specific listed members, not anonymous visitors."@en .

<#q-wac-4a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "One with no `acl:agent`, `acl:agentClass` or `acl:agentGroup` line"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "An authorization without any subject never applies."@en .

<#q-wac-4b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front """Alice wrote this Solid Web Access Control (WAC) rule, but Bob still gets 403 when he reads the file. What is wrong?

```turtle
@prefix acl: <http://www.w3.org/ns/auth/acl#>.

<#bob> acl:agent <https://bob.example/profile/card#me>;
    acl:accessTo <report.ttl>;
    acl:mode acl:Read.
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It lacks `a acl:Authorization`, so the rule doesn't apply"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "An authorization applies only when it is typed `acl:Authorization` and has a target, a mode and a subject; a missing type silently disables it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-wac-4b-d1> ,
                          <#q-wac-4b-d2> ,
                          <#q-wac-4b-d3> .

<#q-wac-4b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It lacks `acl:default`, which every rule needs"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`accessTo` alone is enough to target the file itself."@en .

<#q-wac-4b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Its `accessTo` needs a full URL, as ACLs don't allow relative ones"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Relative IRIs are resolved against the ACL's own URL, so `<report.ttl>` is fine."@en .

<#q-wac-4b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Bob must be put in a group, because `acl:agent` is obsolete"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`acl:agent` is a current way to name a single WebID."@en .

<#q-wac-4c>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid's Web Access Control (WAC), Alice wants a folder to be readable by every logged-in user except Mallory. What can she do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Only approximate it: grant Read to a listed group that omits Mallory"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "WAC only grants, so \"everyone except X\" can't be expressed, and nobody can list every logged-in user. The closest is granting to an explicit list or group that leaves X out."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-wac-4c-d1> ,
                          <#q-wac-4c-d2> ,
                          <#q-wac-4c-d3> .

<#q-wac-4c-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Add a rule with `acl:mode acl:Deny` that names Mallory's WebID"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "WAC defines no Deny mode, and unknown modes never change access."@en .

<#q-wac-4c-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Give Mallory a rule with no modes, which overrides her other rules"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "An authorization without a mode does not apply at all."@en .

<#q-wac-4c-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Put Mallory's rule first in the ACL, since the first match wins"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Authorizations are unordered; any matching one grants access."@en .

<#ch-wac-5>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-wac> ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Method-to-mode mapping

Each request of Chapter 6 needs these modes:

| Request | Modes needed |
|---|---|
| GET, HEAD | Read on the resource |
| POST into a container | Append or Write on the new resource (its rights come from the container's `acl:default`) |
| PUT that creates | Write on the new resource; Append or Write on its container |
| PUT that replaces | Write on the resource |
| DELETE | Write on the resource **and** on its container |
| Anything on the ACL | Control |

A new resource has no ACL yet, so its rights come from its container's `acl:default` rules (Step 8.6). Creating and deleting change the container's `ldp:contains` listing, hence the container rights.

⚠ On CSS 7.2 (2026), POST is checked only against the container, so Append on an inbox itself is enough there (Step 9.6). Don't rely on this elsewhere.

N3 Patch is checked part by part:
- a non-empty `where` needs **Read**;
- non-empty `inserts` need **Append**;
- non-empty `deletes` need **Read and Write**.

So an insert-only patch with no `where` needs only Append, which suits logs and inboxes."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-wac-5a> ,
                         <#q-wac-5b> .

<#q-wac-5a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid's Web Access Control (WAC), Bob has Write on `/shared/doc.ttl` but only Read on the `/shared/` container. What happens when he sends DELETE for the document?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It is refused, because DELETE also needs Write on the container"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "WAC requires Write on both the resource and its container to delete, because deleting changes the container's listing."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-wac-5a-d1> ,
                          <#q-wac-5a-d2> ,
                          <#q-wac-5a-d3> .

<#q-wac-5a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It succeeds, because Write on the document is all DELETE needs"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Deleting changes the container's listing, so the container needs Write too."@en .

<#q-wac-5a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It succeeds, because Read on the container lets him see the item"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Read on the container doesn't allow changing its membership."@en .

<#q-wac-5a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It is refused, because DELETE always requires Control"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Control is needed only for the ACL, not for deleting resources."@en .

<#q-wac-5b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "On a Solid pod using Web Access Control (WAC), an app has only Append on a log document. Which N3 Patch can it apply?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "One with only `inserts`, and no `where` or `deletes`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "N3 Patch needs Read for `where`, Append for `inserts` and Read plus Write for `deletes`, so pure inserts work with Append alone."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-wac-5b-d1> ,
                          <#q-wac-5b-d2> ,
                          <#q-wac-5b-d3> .

<#q-wac-5b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "One with a `where` and `inserts`, to add after a check"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A non-empty `where` needs Read, which the app lacks."@en .

<#q-wac-5b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "One with `deletes` only, to remove its own old entries"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Deletes need Read and Write."@en .

<#q-wac-5b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "None, because every PATCH needs Write"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "An insert-only N3 Patch needs only Append."@en .

<#ch-wac-6>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-wac> ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Inheritance: the effective ACL

The server uses a resource's **effective ACL**: its own ACL or, if it has none, the nearest ancestor container's ACL, up to the root, using only its `acl:default` rules.

Inheritance is **replacement, not addition**: once `/photos/cat.jpg` has its own ACL, nothing from `/photos/` or `/` applies, so it must restate the owner's rule, Control included. The spec allows servers to merge (union) instead; CSS 7.2 doesn't.

⚠ WAC doesn't require implicit owner Control. CSS 7.2 (2026) gives it only with a pod-based storage config; by default, an owner left out of an ACL gets 403 even on that ACL.

A container's ACL usually needs both `acl:accessTo <./>` (the container) and `acl:default <./>` (everything below it). The root ACL must exist and grant `acl:Control` to someone. CSS 7.2 writes this one for a new pod (comments removed):

```turtle
@prefix acl: <http://www.w3.org/ns/auth/acl#>.
@prefix foaf: <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/>.

<#public> a acl:Authorization;
    acl:agentClass foaf:Agent;
    acl:accessTo <./>;
    acl:mode acl:Read.

<#owner> a acl:Authorization;
    acl:agent <http://localhost:3000/alice/profile/card#me>;
    acl:accessTo <./>;
    acl:default <./>;
    acl:mode acl:Read, acl:Write, acl:Control.
```

The public rule has no `acl:default`: anyone can read the root listing, but nothing below inherits it; the profile has its own public ACL. On CSS 7.2 (recorded in 2026), Alice made her note `notes/open.ttl` public by PUTting this ACL at its `rel="acl"` URL:

```turtle
@prefix acl: <http://www.w3.org/ns/auth/acl#>.
@prefix foaf: <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/>.

<#owner> a acl:Authorization;
    acl:agent <http://localhost:3000/alice/profile/card#me>;
    acl:accessTo <open.ttl>;
    acl:mode acl:Read, acl:Write, acl:Control.

<#public> a acl:Authorization;
    acl:agentClass foaf:Agent;
    acl:accessTo <open.ttl>;
    acl:mode acl:Read.
```

Anonymous HEADs on the note:

```text
before the ACL                        401 Unauthorized
PUT the ACL (as Alice)                201 Created
after the ACL                         200 OK, WAC-Allow: user="read",public="read"
PUT it again, "<#public>" untyped     205 Reset Content
after the broken ACL                  401 Unauthorized
```

Untyped, the public rule silently did nothing."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-wac-6a> ,
                         <#q-wac-6b> .

<#q-wac-6a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid's Web Access Control (WAC), the ACL of the `/docs/` container gives Bob Read with `acl:default <./>`. Alice then creates an ACL for `/docs/plan.ttl` that only grants herself full access. Can Bob still read `plan.ttl`?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "No, because the file's own ACL replaces the inherited rules"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The effective ACL is the nearest one that exists; once `plan.ttl` has its own ACL, none of the ancestor rules apply."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-wac-6a-d1> ,
                          <#q-wac-6a-d2> ,
                          <#q-wac-6a-d3> .

<#q-wac-6a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, because the container's rules are added to the file's rules"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "In WAC's standard algorithm, inheritance is replacement; the nearest existing ACL is the only one used."@en .

<#q-wac-6a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, because `acl:default` rules always take priority"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Default rules apply only when no nearer ACL exists."@en .

<#q-wac-6a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, because `acl:default` never applies to files, only containers"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Default rules are inherited by all descendants without their own ACL, files included."@en .

<#q-wac-6b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid's Web Access Control (WAC), a container's ACL has one rule giving Bob `acl:Read` with `acl:default <./>` and no `acl:accessTo`. What can Bob read?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The resources inside the container, but not its own listing"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`acl:accessTo <./>` covers the container itself and `acl:default <./>` covers its descendants, which is why container ACLs normally use both."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-wac-6b-d1> ,
                          <#q-wac-6b-d2> ,
                          <#q-wac-6b-d3> .

<#q-wac-6b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The container listing and everything inside it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`default` covers descendants; the container itself needs `accessTo`."@en .

<#q-wac-6b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only the container listing, not the resources inside it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is the reverse of what `default` does."@en .

<#q-wac-6b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing, because `default` only works on the root container"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`acl:default` can appear in any container's ACL."@en .

<#ch-wac-7>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-wac> ;
    schema:position 6 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## What the client sees: status codes and WAC-Allow

When access is refused, the server answers:
- **401**: no valid credentials, and the resource needs some.
- **403**: you are known, but not allowed.
- **404**: "not found". The Solid Protocol lets a server send it instead of 401 "for security reasons", and HTTP lets it replace 403 to hide that a forbidden resource exists.

After a 403 or 404 a client may retry with other credentials. To grey out an Edit button, an app needs its permissions *before* trying, but reading the ACL needs Control. Instead, WAC servers MUST send a `WAC-Allow` header on GET and HEAD: `user` lists the requester's modes and `public` everyone's; clients ignore unknown groups or modes. It is a UI hint; the server still checks every request. ACP defines no such header; without it, an app offers the action and handles a 401 or 403.

On CSS 7.2 (recorded in 2026), a private note answered an anonymous HEAD, then Alice's:

```http
HTTP/1.1 401 Unauthorized
WWW-Authenticate: Bearer scope="openid webid"

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
WAC-Allow: user="append control read write"
```

No `public=` entry: the note isn't public. CSS 7.2 and NSS 6.0.0 send no `WAC-Allow` on errors (CSS 7.2 none on 201 or 205 either), so a refusal carries no hint."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-wac-7a> ,
                         <#q-wac-7b> .

<#q-wac-7a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A logged-in user of a Solid app requests a private file in someone else's pod and gets 404. What can the app conclude?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The file may exist; the server may be hiding it from this user"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A server may answer 404 instead of 401 (Solid Protocol) or 403 (HTTP) so it does not reveal that a protected resource exists."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-wac-7a-d1> ,
                          <#q-wac-7a-d2> ,
                          <#q-wac-7a-d3> .

<#q-wac-7a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The file definitely doesn't exist, because 404 means not found"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Servers may use 404 instead of 401 or 403 to hide existence."@en .

<#q-wac-7a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The user's token has expired and must be refreshed"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "404 can't tell you the token expired; even an expired token's 401 may be hidden as 404."@en .

<#q-wac-7a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The user lacks Read, so the server was required to answer 404"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Lacking Read gives 403, or 404 only if the server chooses to hide existence."@en .

<#q-wac-7b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "How should a Solid app decide whether to show an \"Edit\" button for a document in a pod?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Read the `user` modes in the `WAC-Allow` response header"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`WAC-Allow` tells the client its own and the public's modes on GET and HEAD. ACP doesn't define it, so when it is missing, send the real request and handle 401 or 403."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-wac-7b-d1> ,
                          <#q-wac-7b-d2> ,
                          <#q-wac-7b-d3> .

<#q-wac-7b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Fetch and parse the document's ACL to look for a Write rule"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Reading the ACL needs Control, which most users won't have."@en .

<#q-wac-7b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Send a test PUT and see whether it fails"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A test write could change data; without the header, try the user's real edit and handle 401 or 403."@en .

<#q-wac-7b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Check the user's profile for a list of documents they can edit"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Profiles don't list permissions; the pod does."@en .

<#ch-wac-8>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-wac> ;
    schema:position 7 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## CORS: why pods let every app in

An **origin** is the scheme, host and port of a URL: `https://app.example` and `https://alice.example` are different origins. By default, a browser's *same-origin policy* stops a page's script from reading responses from another origin. A server can relax this with CORS headers: the response's `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` names the origin that may read it.

Solid apps are hosted on one origin and read pods on many others, so Solid servers open CORS completely: they repeat the request's `Origin` in `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`. They don't send `*` ("anyone"), because browsers reject `*` for requests sent with credentials. Servers must also list headers apps need, such as `Link` and `WAC-Allow`, in `Access-Control-Expose-Headers`, or browser apps can't read them.

The Protocol says blocking "MUST NOT happen via CORS". Who may do what is decided by access control and answered with 401, 403 or 404.

On CSS 7.2 (recorded in 2026, other headers left out), an anonymous HEAD on a public profile got:

```http
HEAD /alice/profile/card HTTP/1.1
Origin: https://app.example

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.example
Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true
Access-Control-Expose-Headers: Accept-Patch,Accept-Post,Accept-Put,Allow,Content-Range,ETag,Last-Modified,Link,Location,Updates-Via,WAC-Allow,Www-Authenticate
Vary: Accept,Authorization,Origin
```

Without an `Origin` header, CSS 7.2 answered `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` instead; that is harmless, because no browser sent the request."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-wac-8a> .

<#q-wac-8a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Why does a Solid pod server repeat any request's `Origin` in its `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` response header?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Access is enforced by authorization, so CORS must not block apps"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Solid apps are hosted on many origins, so servers open CORS fully and rely on 401, 403 or 404 from access control instead."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-wac-8a-d1> ,
                          <#q-wac-8a-d2> ,
                          <#q-wac-8a-d3> .

<#q-wac-8a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Repeating the Origin proves which app is making the request"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Origin is not client identification; non-browser clients can send any value."@en .

<#q-wac-8a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It is a temporary fallback until servers support `*`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Browsers reject `*` for requests with credentials, so repeating the origin is the lasting answer."@en .

<#q-wac-8a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "CORS checks the user's WebID against the ACL"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "CORS knows nothing about WebIDs or ACLs."@en .

<#q-wac-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid's Web Access Control (WAC), Bob can read every file in `/team/` through an inherited `acl:default` rule. Alice adds an ACL to one file that grants only Bob Write. What can Bob now do with that file?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Write it but not read it, because the new ACL replaces the inherited Read"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The file's own ACL is now its effective ACL, and it grants only Write; Write covers Append but not Read."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-wac-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-wac-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-wac-r01-d3> .

<#q-wac-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Read and write it, because new rules are added to inherited ones"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "In WAC's standard algorithm, inheritance is replacement, not union."@en .

<#q-wac-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only read it, because inherited rules beat local ones"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Once the file has its own ACL, inherited rules no longer apply."@en .

<#q-wac-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Write and read it, because Write implies Read"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Write does not imply Read; it only implies Append."@en .

<#q-wac-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A GET on a shared notes document in a Solid pod returns `WAC-Allow: user=\"read append\"`. Which action should the app's UI offer?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Adding a note with an insert-only N3 Patch"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "With Read and Append the user can add triples (even with a `where`), but deleting, replacing or changing the ACL needs Write or Control."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-wac-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-wac-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-wac-r02-d3> .

<#q-wac-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Editing an existing note with an N3 Patch that deletes and inserts"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Deletes need Write, which the user lacks."@en .

<#q-wac-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Replacing the document with PUT"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Replacing a resource needs Write."@en .

<#q-wac-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Changing who can see the document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Editing the ACL needs Control."@en .

<#q-wac-r03>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid's Web Access Control (WAC), the ACL of the `/notes/` container has one rule giving Bob `acl:Write` with `acl:default <./>` and no `acl:accessTo`. No file inside has its own ACL. Can Bob delete `/notes/a.ttl`?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "No, because he lacks Write on the `/notes/` container itself"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The `default` rule gives Bob Write on `a.ttl`, but deleting changes the listing of `/notes/`, and Write on the container would need `acl:accessTo <./>`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-wac-r03-d1> ,
                          <#q-wac-r03-d2> ,
                          <#q-wac-r03-d3> .

<#q-wac-r03-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, because the inherited Write on `a.ttl` is all DELETE needs"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "DELETE also needs Write on the container, whose listing changes."@en .

<#q-wac-r03-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, because `acl:default` also covers the container itself"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`default` covers only descendants; the container needs `accessTo`."@en .

<#q-wac-r03-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, because DELETE needs Control on the file"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Control is only for the ACL; DELETE needs Write."@en .

<#q-wac-r04>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Bob's browser app sends a GET for Alice's note on a Solid pod that uses Web Access Control (WAC), and gets 403 with no `WAC-Allow` header. Which conclusion and next step fit?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Bob is logged in but not allowed; tell him he lacks access to the note"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "403 means authenticated but not authorized. On CSS 7.2 (2026), errors carry no `WAC-Allow` header; CORS never blocks Solid apps, and an ACL is found by its link and needs Control to read."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-wac-r04-d1> ,
                          <#q-wac-r04-d2> ,
                          <#q-wac-r04-d3> .

<#q-wac-r04-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The browser's CORS policy blocked it; retry from another origin"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid servers never block through CORS, and a CORS block would hide the status from the app altogether."@en .

<#q-wac-r04-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Bob's token has expired; send him through login again"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A missing or unusable token gives 401 (or a hiding 404); 403 means the server knew who he was."@en .

<#q-wac-r04-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "GET the note's URL plus `.acl` to find which rule is missing"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That builds the ACL URL by string, and reading an ACL needs Control, which a reader rarely has."@en .

<#q-wac-r05>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid's Web Access Control (WAC), Alice shares `/notes/a.ttl` with Bob by giving the file its own ACL. Besides Bob's Read rule, what must that new ACL contain?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A rule restating Alice's own access, Control included"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Once a resource has its own ACL, no inherited rule applies, so the owner must restate her own rule, Control included, or lose access."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-wac-r05-d1> ,
                          <#q-wac-r05-d2> ,
                          <#q-wac-r05-d3> .

<#q-wac-r05-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing else, because her `acl:default` rule in the root ACL still applies"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A resource's own ACL replaces inheritance entirely."@en .

<#q-wac-r05-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A copy of the root's public rule, so the file stays readable"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Sharing with Bob doesn't call for public access, and Alice would still be locked out."@en .

<#q-wac-r05-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An `acl:default` rule for Bob, so his grant reaches the file"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`acl:default` is for a container's descendants; a file needs `acl:accessTo`."@en .

# Chapter 9: Sharing in practice, ACP and app permissions.
<#ch-sharing>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 8 ;
    dcterms:title "Sharing in practice, ACP and app permissions"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Read ACP policies, share resources with people and groups on WAC and ACP pods, and tell them about it through their inbox. Then work across other people's pods and see where app permissions still fall short."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-sharing-r01> ,
                              <#q-sharing-r02> ,
                              <#q-sharing-r03> ,
                              <#q-sharing-r04> ,
                              <#q-sharing-r05> ,
                              <#q-sharing-r06> ,
                              <#q-sharing-r07> .

<#ch-sharing-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-sharing> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## ACP's building blocks

ACP (Access Control Policy) builds rules from four layers:

**Access Control Resource (ACR)** → **Access Control** → **Policy** → **Matcher**

The ACR is linked from the resource with the same `rel="acl"` as WAC. Its `acp:accessControl` applies to the resource itself; its `acp:memberAccessControl` applies to all descendants. An access control lists the policies it applies with `acp:apply`. A **policy** lists modes to `acp:allow` or `acp:deny`, reusing `acl:Read` and the other WAC modes, and names the matchers that must match. A **matcher** tests the request: `acp:agent` (the WebID), `acp:client` (the app's client ID) or `acp:issuer` (the identity provider).

`acp:agent` can also take the special values `acp:PublicAgent` (anyone) and `acp:AuthenticatedAgent` (anyone logged in), the ACP versions of WAC's `foaf:Agent` and `acl:AuthenticatedAgent`.

```turtle
@prefix acl: <http://www.w3.org/ns/auth/acl#>.
@prefix acp: <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/acp#>.

<#noWritesFromAppY> a acp:Policy;
    acp:deny acl:Write;
    # anyOf: the policy applies if any listed matcher matches (Step 9.2)
    acp:anyOf [ a acp:Matcher; acp:client <https://appy.example/id> ].
```

To tell which system you face, follow `rel="acl"` and check the response: an ACR carries `Link: <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/acp#AccessControlResource>; rel="type"`.

⚠ As of October 2026, ACP's latest published version is 0.9.0 (May 2022), an Editor's Draft of the Solid Community Group; its editor's draft was last updated in September 2022. Inrupt's ESS uses it; on the Community Solid Server (CSS) it is an option."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-sharing-1a> ,
                         <#q-sharing-1b> .

<#q-sharing-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid, your app followed a resource's `rel=\"acl\"` link and fetched the rule document. How can it tell that the server uses ACP rather than WAC?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A `rel=\"type\"` link to `acp:AccessControlResource`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "An ACR response must carry a type link to `acp:AccessControlResource`; that is the reliable way to detect ACP."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-1a-d3> .

<#q-sharing-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The document's URL ends in `.acr` instead of `.acl`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "File names are server conventions and must not be relied on."@en .

<#q-sharing-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server answers with `rel=\"acp\"` instead of `rel=\"acl\"`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "ACP uses the same `rel=\"acl\"` link as WAC."@en .

<#q-sharing-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The response is JSON-LD rather than Turtle"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Both kinds of rule document are RDF, in any RDF syntax the server offers."@en .

<#q-sharing-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In a Solid ACP policy, which matcher makes a resource readable by anyone, logged in or not?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "One with `acp:agent acp:PublicAgent`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`acp:PublicAgent` is ACP's \"everyone\", like WAC's `foaf:Agent`; `acp:AuthenticatedAgent` means anyone logged in."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-1b-d3> .

<#q-sharing-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "One with `acp:agent acp:AuthenticatedAgent`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That matches only requests with a logged-in agent."@en .

<#q-sharing-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "One with `acl:agentClass foaf:Agent`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is WAC's way; ACP matchers use `acp:agent acp:PublicAgent`."@en .

<#q-sharing-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "One with no attributes at all"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "An empty matcher never matches anything."@en .

<#ch-sharing-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-sharing> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## How ACP decides

A policy names its matchers in three lists:
- `acp:allOf`: every matcher must match;
- `acp:anyOf`: at least one must match;
- `acp:noneOf`: none may match.

A policy is *satisfied* when all three conditions hold. It must also have at least one `allOf` or `anyOf` matcher, so **a policy with only `noneOf` is never satisfied**. Inside one matcher, several values of the same attribute are alternatives (any one may match), while different attributes must all match. An empty matcher never matches.

A mode is granted only if some satisfied policy allows it and **no satisfied policy denies it**: deny overrides allow. WAC has no deny at all.

Which policies count? The resource's own `acp:accessControl` policies plus the `acp:memberAccessControl` policies of **every ancestor**. Unlike WAC, where the nearest ACL replaces the others, ACP's member policies **accumulate**: a resource's own ACR doesn't switch them off."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-sharing-2a> ,
                         <#q-sharing-2b> ,
                         <#q-sharing-2c> .

<#q-sharing-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "On a Solid ACP server, a satisfied policy allows Read and Write for Bob, and another satisfied policy denies Write for the app he uses. What can Bob do through that app?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Read only, because deny overrides allow"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "An ACP mode is granted only if a satisfied policy allows it and no satisfied policy denies it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-2a-d3> .

<#q-sharing-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Read and Write, because allows are checked first"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "In ACP a satisfied deny always wins over an allow."@en .

<#q-sharing-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing, because a conflict denies every mode"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Only the denied mode is removed; Read is still allowed."@en .

<#q-sharing-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Read and Write, because agent rules beat client rules"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "ACP gives no priority to agent matchers over client matchers."@en .

<#q-sharing-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid ACP policy allows Read with `acp:allOf` naming two matchers: one for Bob's WebID and one for the client ID of app X. When can Bob read?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Only when he uses app X"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`allOf` requires every listed matcher to match; with `anyOf`, either Bob or app X alone would have been enough."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-2b-d3> .

<#q-sharing-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Whenever he is logged in, with any app"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "With `allOf`, the client matcher must match too."@en .

<#q-sharing-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Whenever anyone uses app X"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "With `allOf`, the agent matcher must match too."@en .

<#q-sharing-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Never, since a policy can't test agent and client"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A policy can combine any matchers."@en .

<#q-sharing-2c>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid's ACP, a container's ACR applies a policy to its members with `acp:memberAccessControl`. A file inside has its own ACR with its own policies. Which policies apply to the file?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Both the file's own and the container's member policies"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "In ACP the effective policies are the resource's own plus every ancestor's member access controls."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-2c-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-2c-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-2c-d3> .

<#q-sharing-2c-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only the file's own, since the nearest ACR replaces the others"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is WAC's rule; ACP member policies accumulate."@en .

<#q-sharing-2c-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only the container's, since members can't have their own ACR"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Every resource can have an ACR with its own access controls."@en .

<#q-sharing-2c-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Neither, until the file's ACR copies the container's policies"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Ancestor member policies apply without being copied."@en .

<#ch-sharing-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-sharing> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Sharing a resource with WAC

Alice wants Bob to read one note. On a WAC pod that takes four steps:

1. **Find the ACL.** HEAD the note and follow its `rel="acl"` link (Step 8.2). Never build the URL yourself.
2. **Check for Control.** Only Control lets you edit an ACL, so look for `control` in `WAC-Allow: user="…"` (Step 8.7).
3. **Read the ACL.** A 404 means the note has no ACL of its own and inherits one (Step 8.6).
4. **Write the ACL.** A new ACL replaces inheritance, so first restate the inherited rules you still want, above all the owner's Read, Write and Control. Then add Bob's rule and PUT the whole document as `text/turtle`. If the ACL already exists, an N3 Patch also works.

```turtle
@prefix acl: <http://www.w3.org/ns/auth/acl#>.
<#owner> a acl:Authorization;
  acl:agent <http://localhost:3000/alice/profile/card#me>;
  acl:accessTo <http://localhost:3000/alice/notes/first.ttl>;
  acl:mode acl:Read, acl:Write, acl:Control.
<#bob> a acl:Authorization;
  acl:agent <http://localhost:3000/bob/profile/card#me>;
  acl:accessTo <http://localhost:3000/alice/notes/first.ttl>;
  acl:mode acl:Read.
```

This chapter's recordings ran on a local CSS 7.2 (October 2026) at `localhost:3000`. Sharing went like this:

```text
HEAD  /alice/notes/first.ttl      as Alice → 200
      wac-allow: user="append control read write"
      link: <http://localhost:3000/alice/notes/first.ttl.acl>; rel="acl"
GET   /alice/notes/first.ttl      as Bob   → 403
PUT   /alice/notes/first.ttl.acl  as Alice → 201
GET   /alice/notes/first.ttl      as Bob   → 200, wac-allow: user="read"
PATCH /alice/notes/first.ttl      as Bob   → 403 (Read gives no Append)
```

**Pitfall:** leave out `#owner` and Alice may lock herself out of the note and its ACL. The storage owner "implicitly has control" (Step 5.3), and servers may use that to rescue owners, but you can't count on it. ⚠ CSS 7.2 doesn't: Alice loses the note and its ACL, and even a DELETE of the ACL gets 403."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-sharing-3a> ,
                         <#q-sharing-3b> .

<#q-sharing-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "On a Solid WAC pod, Alice's note inherits its rules from the pod root. Her app PUTs a new ACL for the note that contains only a rule giving Bob Read. What is the result?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Bob can read the note, but Alice's own rules no longer apply to it"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The note's new ACL is now its effective ACL, so the root's owner rule no longer applies. Always restate the owner's Read, Write and Control."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-3a-d3> .

<#q-sharing-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Bob can read it, and Alice keeps full access through the root ACL"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A resource's own ACL replaces all inherited rules."@en .

<#q-sharing-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing changes until Bob's rule also has `acl:default`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`acl:accessTo` covers the note; `acl:default` is for a container's descendants."@en .

<#q-sharing-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Bob can read it, and Alice keeps access because she created it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "WAC has no creator rule; only the effective ACL counts."@en .

<#q-sharing-3b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Before offering a \"Share\" button, how can an app check that the user may change a document's ACL on a Solid WAC pod?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Look for `control` in the `user` part of its `WAC-Allow` header"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`WAC-Allow` lists the requester's modes on GET and HEAD, and only `control` allows editing the ACL."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-3b-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-3b-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-3b-d3> .

<#q-sharing-3b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Look for `write` in its `WAC-Allow` header, since Write covers the ACL"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Write applies to the document; editing its ACL needs Control."@en .

<#q-sharing-3b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "GET the pod root's ACL and search it for the user's WebID"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The effective ACL may be elsewhere, and reading an ACL needs Control."@en .

<#q-sharing-3b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Check whether the document's triples mention the user's WebID"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Data triples grant nothing; access comes from the ACL."@en .

<#ch-sharing-4>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-sharing> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Sharing a resource with ACP

On an ACP pod the same `rel="acl"` link leads to an Access Control Resource, which you recognise by its `acp:AccessControlResource` type link (Step 9.1). To share, add an access control with a policy for the friend, and list it in the ACR under `acp:accessControl` (this resource only) or `acp:memberAccessControl` (everything inside a container). The edited ACR of a note can look like this (on CSS 7.2 a new note has no ACR until the first share creates one; the owner's access comes from the root's member policy):

```turtle
@prefix acl: <http://www.w3.org/ns/auth/acl#>.
@prefix acp: <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/acp#>.

<> a acp:AccessControlResource;
  acp:resource <https://alice.example/notes/note.ttl>;
  acp:accessControl <#ownerControl>, <#bobReads>.   # added: <#bobReads>

# <#ownerControl> ...: if the ACR already had it, keep it unchanged

<#bobReads> a acp:AccessControl;
  acp:apply [ a acp:Policy;
    acp:allow acl:Read;
    acp:anyOf [ a acp:Matcher;
      acp:agent <https://bob.example/profile/card#me> ] ].
```

How this differs from WAC:
- **You only add.** Ancestors' member policies keep applying (Step 9.2), so you need not restate the owner's access. If you rewrite the whole ACR, keep the access controls already in it.
- **Control means control of the ACR.** In CSS 7.2's ACP template (2026), the owner's policy allows `acl:Control`, and that is what lets the owner change the ACR. The ACP spec does not say which HTTP requests edit an ACR.
- **No `WAC-Allow` to check first.** ACP does not define the header (Step 8.7). Try the change and handle a 403.

Step 9.5 shows an API meant to write both kinds of rule."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-sharing-4a> ,
                         <#q-sharing-4b> .

<#q-sharing-4a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "You share one file with Bob on a Solid ACP pod. Which step from the WAC sharing workflow can you skip?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Restating the owner's inherited access"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "In ACP, ancestors' member policies keep applying, so a new policy only adds access. In WAC, a new ACL replaces everything inherited."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-4a-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-4a-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-4a-d3> .

<#q-sharing-4a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Finding the rule document through `rel=\"acl\"`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "ACP uses the same link to find the ACR."@en .

<#q-sharing-4a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Naming Bob's WebID in the new rule"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The matcher's `acp:agent` is what identifies Bob."@en .

<#q-sharing-4a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Having control over the rule document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "You still need control to change the ACR."@en .

<#q-sharing-4b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "On a Solid ACP pod, where does your app attach Bob's new access control so that it covers one file and nothing else?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "In the file's ACR, under `acp:accessControl`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`acp:accessControl` applies to the resource itself, and `acp:memberAccessControl` to a container's descendants."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-4b-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-4b-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-4b-d3> .

<#q-sharing-4b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In the file's ACR, under `acp:memberAccessControl`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That applies to members, and only containers have them."@en .

<#q-sharing-4b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In the file itself, as an `acp:agent` triple"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Rules live in the ACR, not in the data."@en .

<#q-sharing-4b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In Bob's profile, as an `acp:apply` triple"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Only the pod's own ACRs grant access to its resources."@en .

<#ch-sharing-5>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-sharing> ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## One API for both: universal access

Clients must cope with both WAC and ACP, and hand-writing both kinds of rule is error-prone. Inrupt's `@inrupt/solid-client` (Step 12.10) has a **universal access API** that is meant to work on both. Inrupt recommends it where it fits:

```js
import { universalAccess } from "@inrupt/solid-client";

// fetch: the logged-in fetch from your auth library
await universalAccess.setAgentAccess(noteUrl, bobWebId,
  { read: true, append: false, write: false }, { fetch });
const bobs = await universalAccess.getAgentAccess(noteUrl, bobWebId, { fetch });
```

`setPublicAccess(url, access, { fetch })` does the same for everyone. Access is described by an object `{ read, append, write }`. A `null` result means the access could not be read, or cannot be expressed this way.

⚠ In a test in October 2026, `setAgentAccess` worked on CSS 7.2 with WAC. On CSS 7.2 with ACP, for a resource with no ACR yet, it returned `null` and shared nothing. Always check the result.

Its limits:
- It reports only access **set directly** on that resource for that agent (or the public). Inherited or group access is not shown, so Bob may be able to do more than `getAgentAccess` says.
- It acts on one resource, not on its children.
- Anything beyond the shared model, such as ACP deny rules or client matchers, needs the WAC- or ACP-specific APIs."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-sharing-5a> ,
                         <#q-sharing-5b> .

<#q-sharing-5a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Inrupt's universal access API (`getAgentAccess`) reports `read: false` for Bob on a file in a Solid pod, yet Bob can read it. What is the likely reason?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "His Read comes from a container's inherited rules"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The universal access API reports only access set directly on that resource, not inherited or indirect access."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-5a-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-5a-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-5a-d3> .

<#q-sharing-5a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The API reads only ACP rules, and this pod uses WAC"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It is meant to read both WAC and ACP rules, and it does read WAC rules."@en .

<#q-sharing-5a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The pod remembers an earlier grant that was removed"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The pod checks the current rules on every request."@en .

<#q-sharing-5a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Bob reads through an app that the API ignores"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The API reports agent access; the app does not change that."@en .

<#q-sharing-5b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Which Solid sharing task needs a mechanism-specific API instead of Inrupt's universal access API?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Denying Write to one app on an ACP pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The universal model covers read, append and write for agents and the public. ACP deny rules and client matchers need the ACP-specific API."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-5b-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-5b-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-5b-d3> .

<#q-sharing-5b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Letting a friend read one document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is exactly what `setAgentAccess` does."@en .

<#q-sharing-5b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Making a single document public"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`setPublicAccess` does this."@en .

<#q-sharing-5b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Checking what Bob was granted on a file"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`getAgentAccess` does this, for direct grants."@en .

<#ch-sharing-6>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-sharing> ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Telling the recipient: a message to their inbox

Bob doesn't know about the share. Tell him through his inbox (Step 5.11): read `ldp:inbox` from his profile and POST a message. In Activity Streams 2.0, an `Announce` calls the `target`'s attention to the `object`; `Offer` and `Invite` suit proposals.

```json
{"@context": "https://www.w3.org/ns/activitystreams",
 "type": "Announce",
 "actor": "http://localhost:3000/alice/profile/card#me",
 "object": "http://localhost:3000/alice/notes/first.ttl",
 "target": "http://localhost:3000/bob/profile/card#me",
 "summary": "alice shared a note with you"}
```

**Bob's side: creating an inbox.** A CSS 7 profile has no `ldp:inbox`, and only Bob's own app can add one: PUT the container, PUT its ACL, then add `ldp:inbox` to his profile with an insert-only N3 Patch. A typical inbox ACL:

```turtle
@prefix acl: <http://www.w3.org/ns/auth/acl#>.
@prefix foaf: <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/>.
<#owner> a acl:Authorization;
  acl:agent <http://localhost:3000/bob/profile/card#me>;
  acl:accessTo <http://localhost:3000/bob/inbox/>;
  acl:default <http://localhost:3000/bob/inbox/>;
  acl:mode acl:Read, acl:Write, acl:Control.
<#public> a acl:Authorization;
  acl:agentClass foaf:Agent;
  acl:accessTo <http://localhost:3000/bob/inbox/>;
  acl:mode acl:Append.
```

On CSS 7.2 (October 2026):

```text
POST /bob/inbox/            as Alice → 201
     location: http://localhost:3000/bob/inbox/58f88a45-…
GET  /bob/inbox/58f88a45-…  as Alice → 403
GET  /bob/inbox/58f88a45-…  as Bob   → 200, the Announce
```

The public rule has no `acl:default`, so each message inherits only Bob's rule. node-solid-server 6 gives every new account this same inbox ACL.

⚠ WAC 1.0 also wants Append or Write on the new resource (Step 8.5). CSS 7.2 checks only the container for POST, so this ACL works there. A strict server would need `acl:default` on the public rule; senders then get Append, but not Read, on every message."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-sharing-6a> ,
                         <#q-sharing-6b> .

<#q-sharing-6a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front """A Solid inbox has this WAC ACL. Alice POSTs an `Announce` to the inbox and gets 201 with a `Location`; her GET on that URL then returns 403. What does this tell her?
```turtle
@prefix acl: <http://www.w3.org/ns/auth/acl#>.
@prefix foaf: <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/>.
<#owner> a acl:Authorization;
  acl:agent <https://bob.example/profile/card#me>;
  acl:accessTo <./>; acl:default <./>;
  acl:mode acl:Read, acl:Write, acl:Control.
<#public> a acl:Authorization;
  acl:agentClass foaf:Agent;
  acl:accessTo <./>;
  acl:mode acl:Append.
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It arrived, but senders can't read messages back"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Only Bob's rule has `acl:default`, so each new message inherits it alone: senders can deliver but can't read anything back, their own messages included."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-6a-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-6a-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-6a-d3> .

<#q-sharing-6a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It was stored, then rejected by Bob's spam filter"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "201 means it was created; the 403 is about reading."@en .

<#q-sharing-6a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Her token expired between the two requests"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "An unusable token gives 401, not 403."@en .

<#q-sharing-6a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It will exist only once Bob has accepted it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "LDN has no acceptance step; the POST stores the message."@en .

<#q-sharing-6b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Alice's Solid app wants to notify Bob of a share, but Bob's WebID profile lists no `ldp:inbox`. What should the app do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Not send, and suggest Alice tell Bob another way"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Senders find the inbox through `ldp:inbox`. Creating and linking one needs write access to Bob's pod and profile, so only Bob's own app can do it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-6b-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-6b-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-6b-d3> .

<#q-sharing-6b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "POST to `inbox/` at the root of Bob's pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That guesses a path; an inbox must be discovered."@en .

<#q-sharing-6b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Create an inbox in Bob's pod on his behalf"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Only Bob can create it and link it from his profile."@en .

<#q-sharing-6b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "PATCH the message into Bob's profile document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Others can't write his profile, and it isn't a mailbox."@en .

<#ch-sharing-7>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-sharing> ;
    schema:position 6 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Processing an inbox

Bob's app reads his inbox like any container: GET it, follow `ldp:contains` to each message and GET that. Once it has acted on a message, a common pattern is to DELETE it (Step 6.10) or move it to an archive container, so the inbox holds only new mail.

Anyone with Append can post, so every message is an **unverified claim**. The `actor` field is text the sender wrote, so Alice's WebID in it proves nothing. LDN advises receivers to verify senders and filter unwanted notifications.

General advice (not a spec rule):
- **Check against the source.** For "Alice shared a note", fetch the `object` with Bob's own login. A 200 proves the share; the message does not.
- **Treat text as data.** In Activity Streams, `summary` may contain HTML. Insert it with `textContent`, never `innerHTML`, and don't follow `javascript:` links (Step 13.1).
- **Limit spam.** Show messages from unknown senders separately or not at all, and cap how many you process at a time. Or give Append only to people you trust (Step 9.8 shows how to name a group).

Bob's app processing Alice's message on CSS 7.2 (October 2026):

```text
GET    /bob/inbox/               → 200, ldp:contains <58f88a45-…>
GET    /bob/inbox/58f88a45-…     → 200, Announce, object …/alice/notes/first.ttl
GET    /alice/notes/first.ttl    → 200, wac-allow: user="read"   (the proof)
DELETE /bob/inbox/58f88a45-…     → 205 Reset Content
GET    /bob/inbox/               → 200, no ldp:contains left
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-sharing-7a> ,
                         <#q-sharing-7b> .

<#q-sharing-7a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A message in Bob's Solid inbox says Alice shared `https://alice.example/plan.ttl` with him. How should his app check the claim?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "GET that URL with Bob's login and see whether it succeeds"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A message is only a claim. The pod holding the object decides whether Bob really has access, so ask it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-7a-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-7a-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-7a-d3> .

<#q-sharing-7a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Check that the message's `actor` is Alice's WebID"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Any sender can put any WebID in `actor`."@en .

<#q-sharing-7a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Trust it, since only Alice may post to Bob's inbox"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Inboxes usually let anyone append."@en .

<#q-sharing-7a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Look for the shared file in Alice's public profile"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Profiles don't list shares; the pod's answer to Bob is what counts."@en .

<#q-sharing-7b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Bob's Solid app lists the `summary` of each Activity Streams message in his inbox. How should it display the summary?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "As plain text, for example with `textContent`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Inbox content comes from strangers, so it is untrusted input. Render it as text, never as markup."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-7b-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-7b-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-7b-d3> .

<#q-sharing-7b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "With `innerHTML`, because summaries are HTML"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Anyone can post, so this lets strangers inject script."@en .

<#q-sharing-7b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "As HTML after stripping `<script>` tags with a regex"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Attributes such as `onerror` and `javascript:` links still run code; filtering by pattern is unreliable."@en .

<#q-sharing-7b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "As HTML, but only when the actor is a WebID Bob trusts"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Anyone can put any WebID in `actor`, and even a real friend's message may carry hostile markup."@en .

<#ch-sharing-8>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-sharing> ;
    schema:position 7 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Groups: sharing with many people at once

Sharing with a team one WebID at a time means editing many ACLs whenever the team changes. WAC's `acl:agentGroup` (Step 8.4) points to a group instead: a `vcard:Group` whose members are listed with `vcard:hasMember` in an ordinary RDF document:

```turtle
# https://alice.example/groups/friends.ttl
@prefix vcard: <http://www.w3.org/2006/vcard/ns#>.
<#group> a vcard:Group;
  vcard:hasMember <https://bob.example/profile/card#me>,
                  <https://carol.example/profile/card#me>.
```
```turtle
# in a container's ACL
@prefix acl: <http://www.w3.org/ns/auth/acl#>.
<#friends-read> a acl:Authorization;
  acl:agentGroup <https://alice.example/groups/friends.ttl#group>;
  acl:accessTo <./>; acl:default <./>;
  acl:mode acl:Read.
```

To give Dave Read on everything that uses the group, add him to `friends.ttl`; no ACL changes. The flip side: **whoever can write the group document decides who gets that access**, so keep write access to it tight with its own ACL.

⚠ CSS 7.2 fetches the group document anonymously, so it must be publicly readable. In a test in October 2026, a note shared with the group above went like this for Bob:

| Group document readable by | Bob's GET of the note |
|---|---|
| Alice only | 403 |
| Alice, Bob and Carol | 403 |
| Everyone | 200, `wac-allow: user="read"` |

The privacy cost: anyone can read who is in the group.

ACP matchers have no group attribute; they test agent, client or issuer (Step 9.1). One matcher can list several `acp:agent` values, any of which may match, and Inrupt's ESS 2.0 dropped group matching by URL. So on ACP, keep the WebIDs in the policy itself."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-sharing-8a> ,
                         <#q-sharing-8b> ,
                         <#q-sharing-8c> .

<#q-sharing-8a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid WAC, Alice's ACL grants Read to `acl:agentGroup <…/friends.ttl#group>`. Carol may write `friends.ttl` but has no Control on the ACL. What can Carol do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Give anyone Read by adding them to the group"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Group membership is data in the group document, so write access to it amounts to control over who the group's authorizations cover."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-8a-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-8a-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-8a-d3> .

<#q-sharing-8a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing extra, since she can't edit the ACL"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Editing the group changes whom the ACL grants access to."@en .

<#q-sharing-8a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Change which modes the group's members get"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Modes are in the ACL, which needs Control."@en .

<#q-sharing-8a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Add members, but only once Alice approves them"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "WAC has no approval step for group changes."@en .

<#q-sharing-8b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "On a Solid ACP pod, how do you let three friends read one document?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A policy whose matcher lists their three WebIDs as `acp:agent`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Several values of one matcher attribute are alternatives, so one matcher listing three WebIDs matches any of them."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-8b-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-8b-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-8b-d3> .

<#q-sharing-8b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A policy whose matcher uses `acl:agentGroup` and a vCard group"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is a WAC subject; ACP matchers have no group attribute."@en .

<#q-sharing-8b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A policy whose `acp:allOf` names three single-agent matchers"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`allOf` needs all to match at once, but a request has one agent."@en .

<#q-sharing-8b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A policy whose matcher uses `acp:agent acp:AuthenticatedAgent`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That lets anyone who is logged in read the document."@en .

<#q-sharing-8c>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front """On CSS 7.2 (2026), Alice's note grants Read to `acl:agentGroup <groups/friends.ttl#group>`, and the group lists Bob. The group document's own ACL is below. Bob still gets 403 on the note. To keep using `acl:agentGroup`, what must Alice change?
```turtle
@prefix acl: <http://www.w3.org/ns/auth/acl#>.
<#owner> a acl:Authorization;
  acl:agent <https://alice.example/profile/card#me>;
  acl:accessTo <friends.ttl>;
  acl:mode acl:Read, acl:Write, acl:Control.
<#members> a acl:Authorization;
  acl:agent <https://bob.example/profile/card#me>,
            <https://carol.example/profile/card#me>;
  acl:accessTo <friends.ttl>;
  acl:mode acl:Read.
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Make the group document publicly readable"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "⚠ CSS 7.2 fetches the group document anonymously, so a group document only members can read grants nothing. `acl:agentGroup` works there only when that document is public, and then anyone can read who is in the group."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-8c-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-8c-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-8c-d3> .

<#q-sharing-8c-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Give the group's members Write on the group document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Members need no access to it; only the server reads it, and Write would let them change the group."@en .

<#q-sharing-8c-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Have each member read the group document once"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "CSS reads it itself, anonymously; members' own reads play no part."@en .

<#q-sharing-8c-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Move the group document next to the note"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Any URL works; the group document's location is not the problem."@en .

<#ch-sharing-9>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-sharing> ;
    schema:position 8 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Friends and data across pods

"Solid" began as **So**cial **Li**nked **D**ata. A profile can list the people its owner knows with `foaf:knows`, which FOAF describes as "indicating some level of reciprocated interaction":

```turtle
@prefix foaf: <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/>.
<#me> foaf:knows <https://bob.example/profile/card#me>,
                 <https://carol.example/profile/card#me>.
```

It is a claim that grants nothing. To show friends' data, an app follows each WebID to its profile and then to the data. It reads `pim:storage` for the pod (Step 5.5) and, where there is one, the public type index, which lists which container holds which kind of data (Step 11.4). Many profiles have neither: fall back or skip.

One logged-in `fetch` works everywhere. The token says who the user is, and every pod checks it against its own rules. Each friend's pod returns only what that friend shared with this user or with the public. For everything else it answers 403 or 404 (Step 8.7); show that as "not shared", not as an error. A 401 means the pod didn't accept the login, such as an expired token (Chapter 7). Some pods use ACP; some are slow or offline.

On CSS 7.2 (October 2026), Bob's requests to Alice's pod:

```text
GET /alice/notes/            → 403   (before and after Alice adds foaf:knows Bob)
GET /alice/notes/first.ttl   → 200   (the note she shared with him)
```

On CSS 7.2 (2026) a new profile lacks `pim:storage`, so apps walk up to the `pim:Storage` root (Step 5.5)."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-sharing-9a> ,
                         <#q-sharing-9b> .

<#q-sharing-9a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid, Alice's WebID profile lists Bob with `foaf:knows`. What does that let Alice's app do on Bob's pod?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Nothing extra; it only tells the app where to look"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`foaf:knows` is a statement in Alice's profile. Access to Bob's data is decided only by Bob's pod."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-9a-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-9a-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-9a-d3> .

<#q-sharing-9a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Read Bob's private data, since he is Alice's friend"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`foaf:knows` grants nothing; Bob's pod applies its own rules."@en .

<#q-sharing-9a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Post to Bob's inbox even without an Append rule"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Inbox access depends on Bob's ACL, not Alice's profile."@en .

<#q-sharing-9a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Append to any of Bob's containers"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Only Bob's ACLs can grant Append; Alice's profile can't."@en .

<#q-sharing-9b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app reads five friends' pods with the user's logged-in fetch. Two return 403. What should it do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Show those two as not shared with this user"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Each pod decides for itself what this user may see, so seeing some friends' data and not others' is normal."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-9b-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-9b-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-9b-d3> .

<#q-sharing-9b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Log the user in again separately for those two pods"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The same login works for every pod; 403 means \"not allowed\"."@en .

<#q-sharing-9b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Retry anonymously, in case the data is public"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Public data is already readable with the user's login."@en .

<#q-sharing-9b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Stop and report that the friends list is broken"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Partial access is normal across pods."@en .

<#ch-sharing-10>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-sharing> ;
    schema:position 9 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## One document, several writers

A shared shopping list lives in Alice's pod, but Bob and Carol should edit it too. Alice's ACL lists them, or a group (Step 9.8), with the modes they need:

```turtle
# alongside the #owner rule from Step 9.3
@prefix acl: <http://www.w3.org/ns/auth/acl#>.
<#editors> a acl:Authorization;
  acl:agent <https://bob.example/profile/card#me>,
            <https://carol.example/profile/card#me>;
  acl:accessTo <list.ttl>;
  acl:mode acl:Read, acl:Append.
```

Grant the least that works. An N3 Patch with only `inserts` needs just Append, while `deletes` need Read and Write (Step 8.5). People who only add items can have Append, and then they can't erase anyone's items. On CSS 7.2 (October 2026), with this ACL:

```text
PATCH list.ttl  inserts "Milk"   as Bob → 205 Reset Content
PATCH list.ttl  deletes "Bread"  as Bob → 403 Forbidden
PUT   list.ttl  "Only mine"      as Bob → 403 Forbidden
GET   list.ttl                   as Bob → 200, wac-allow: user="append read"
                                          "Bread", "Milk"
```

Several writers make lost updates likely (Step 6.11). Patch only the triples you change rather than PUTting the whole document. Insert-only patches can't lose others' changes, so `If-Match` matters most for patches with `deletes` and for PUT: send the ETag you read, and on 412 re-read, reapply and retry. ⚠ node-solid-server 6.0.0 ignores `If-Match`, so a stale PUT silently overwrites. A stale patch is caught only when its `deletes` no longer match (409).

An **invitation** combines this chapter: change the access (Steps 9.3-9.5), then post an `Invite` or `Announce` naming the list as its `object` to each friend's inbox (Step 9.6)."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-sharing-10a> ,
                         <#q-sharing-10b> .

<#q-sharing-10a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Bob should be able to add items to Alice's shared list in her Solid pod, but not remove anyone's. Which WAC modes does he need?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Read and Append"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Insert-only N3 Patches need only Append, so Read plus Append lets Bob see and add but not delete."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-10a-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-10a-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-10a-d3> .

<#q-sharing-10a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Read and Write"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Write also lets him delete or change others' items."@en .

<#q-sharing-10a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Append and Control"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Control lets him edit the ACL and give himself Write."@en .

<#q-sharing-10a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Write only"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Write allows deleting, and without Read he can't see the list."@en .

<#q-sharing-10b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "On a Solid server that honours `If-Match`, such as CSS 7.2 (2026), Bob and Carol both rename the same item on a shared list. Each sends a PATCH that deletes the old name and inserts a new one, with `If-Match` and the same ETag. Carol's request arrives second. What happens?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "She gets 412; her app re-reads and retries her change"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Bob's patch changed the ETag, so Carol's `If-Match` no longer matches and the server answers 412 Precondition Failed. ⚠ node-solid-server 6.0.0 ignores `If-Match`, so you can't rely on this check there."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-10b-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-10b-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-10b-d3> .

<#q-sharing-10b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Her patch overwrites Bob's change, and both get 205"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`If-Match` makes the server refuse a write based on a stale version."@en .

<#q-sharing-10b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "She gets 409; her app should ask Bob to undo his change"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A failed precondition is 412, and the fix is to retry on fresh data."@en .

<#q-sharing-10b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server merges both patches and sends her the new ETag"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The server compares tags; it does not merge."@en .

<#ch-sharing-11>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-sharing> ;
    schema:position 10 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Apps vs users: the unsolved part

Plain WAC grants access to *people*. An app you log into acts with your WebID, so it gets **all** of your access, not just what it needs. Fixes tried so far:

- **`acl:origin`** limits a rule to a web origin, compared with the `Origin` header browsers send (Step 8.8). The spec warns this "is not intended as client identification": other clients can send any value.
- **`acl:trustedApp`** (old NSS only) listed trusted apps by origin in the user's public profile, pod-wide, revealing which apps a person uses. It is not in the WAC spec or the ACL ontology.
- **ACP client matchers** check the authenticated client ID from the login (`client_id` in the access token, `azp` in the ID token; Step 7.5): much stronger, but only on ACP servers.
- **Access requests and grants** (Inrupt ESS) give another agent scoped, expiring, revocable access, recorded as Verifiable Credentials: an Inrupt feature, not part of the Solid Protocol.
- **SAI** (a CG draft, covered in Step 11.8) adds an Authorization Agent that grants apps access by data type; adoption is narrow.

The universal access API (Step 9.5) can't help: it never names an app."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-sharing-11a> ,
                         <#q-sharing-11b> ,
                         <#q-sharing-11c> .

<#q-sharing-11a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Alice logs into a new to-do app on a Solid WAC pod with no origin or client rules. What can the app access?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Everything Alice herself can access"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Plain WAC grants access to agents, and an app acting for a user carries that user's WebID, so it inherits all of the user's access."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-11a-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-11a-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-11a-d3> .

<#q-sharing-11a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only the to-do data it created itself"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Plain WAC does not limit apps to their own data."@en .

<#q-sharing-11a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only resources Alice approved for this app at login"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Plain WAC has no per-app consent step."@en .

<#q-sharing-11a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing, until Alice adds the app to her ACLs"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The app acts as Alice, so her own authorizations already apply."@en .

<#q-sharing-11b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Why is WAC's `acl:origin` a weak way to restrict which app may access data in a Solid pod?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Any non-browser client can send any `Origin` value"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Browsers set `Origin`, but the WAC spec says it is not client identification, and other clients can send any value."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-11b-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-11b-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-11b-d3> .

<#q-sharing-11b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It only works for public resources, not private ones"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It can be combined with any agent rule; its problem is trust in the header."@en .

<#q-sharing-11b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It was removed from the WAC spec in 2024"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It is still in WAC 1.0.0."@en .

<#q-sharing-11b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It stores the list of allowed apps in the public profile"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That was the problem with `acl:trustedApp`, not `acl:origin`."@en .

<#q-sharing-11c>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid, which mechanism restricts a specific app by its authenticated client ID and is defined in an open Solid spec?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "An ACP policy with an `acp:client` matcher"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "ACP matchers can test the client ID from the Solid-OIDC login, which is far stronger than the Origin header; it works only on servers that use ACP."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-11c-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-11c-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-11c-d3> .

<#q-sharing-11c-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An Inrupt Access Grant naming the app"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Access grants are a vendor feature of ESS, not part of an open Solid spec."@en .

<#q-sharing-11c-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A WAC authorization with `acl:origin`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It checks the browser's `Origin` header, which is not an authenticated client ID."@en .

<#q-sharing-11c-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An `acl:trustedApp` entry in the user's profile"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It was a legacy NSS feature listed in the public profile, and it is not in the WAC spec."@en .

<#q-sharing-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid access control, which feature does ACP have that WAC lacks?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Deny rules that override allows"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "WAC is purely additive, while ACP has `acp:deny`, which overrides any allow."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-r01-d3> .

<#q-sharing-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Separate rule documents linked with `rel=\"acl\"`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Both use rule documents linked with `rel=\"acl\"`."@en .

<#q-sharing-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A mode for appending without writing"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Both use the same modes, including `acl:Append`."@en .

<#q-sharing-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Rules for anonymous public access"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "WAC has `foaf:Agent`; ACP has `acp:PublicAgent`."@en .

<#q-sharing-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Which statement best describes the state of app permissions in Solid as of October 2026?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Only partial solutions exist, none on every server"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`acl:origin`, trusted apps, ACP client matchers, access grants and SAI each cover part of the problem, and none works everywhere; LWS is working on a standard answer."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-r02-d3> .

<#q-sharing-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "ACP client matchers are now supported by most Solid servers"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "WAC is still the more widely deployed language, and it has no client matchers."@en .

<#q-sharing-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The Solid Protocol's 2024 version added a standard app grant"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The Protocol defines no app-permission mechanism; LWS is working on one."@en .

<#q-sharing-r02-d3>
