<#q-sharing-r05>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Alice gives Bob Read on a note in two Solid pods, and neither change restates her own access. On her ACP pod she adds an access control for Bob to the note's ACR. On her WAC pod she PUTs a new ACL for the note that holds only Bob's rule. Where does she lose access to the note?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Only on the WAC pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A WAC ACL replaces everything the note inherited, so it must restate the owner's rule. ACP member policies accumulate, so a new access control only adds."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-r05-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-r05-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-r05-d3> .

<#q-sharing-r05-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only on the ACP pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "ACP ancestors' member policies keep applying, so adding a policy removes nothing."@en .

<#q-sharing-r05-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "On both pods"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "In ACP a new access control only adds; the WAC pod is the one where inheritance is replaced."@en .

<#q-sharing-r05-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "On neither pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Implicit owner control isn't guaranteed, and CSS 7.2 (2026) does lock the owner out."@en .

<#q-sharing-r06>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Alice's Solid app shares a note with Bob on a WAC pod and then tells him about it. Which sequence is right?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Follow `rel=\"acl\"`, check `control` in `WAC-Allow`, write the ACL, find Bob's `ldp:inbox`, POST an `Announce`; Bob's app then GETs the note with his login"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Discover the ACL and check Control, then grant; discover the inbox and announce. The recipient verifies by asking the pod, not by trusting the message."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-r06-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-r06-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-r06-d3> .

<#q-sharing-r06-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Find Bob's `ldp:inbox`, POST an `Announce`, then follow `rel=\"acl\"`, check `control` in `WAC-Allow` and write the ACL; Bob's app then GETs the note"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The message would announce a share that doesn't exist yet; grant first, then tell."@en .

<#q-sharing-r06-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Follow `rel=\"acl\"`, check `control` in `WAC-Allow`, write the ACL, POST an `Announce`; Bob's app trusts it because its `actor` is Alice's WebID"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The sender writes `actor`; Bob's app must check by fetching the note."@en .

<#q-sharing-r06-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Add `.acl` to the note's URL, write the ACL, POST an `Announce` to `inbox/` at the root of Bob's pod; Bob's app then GETs the note with his login"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Both URLs must be discovered: the ACL through `rel=\"acl\"`, the inbox through `ldp:inbox`."@en .

<#q-sharing-r07>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Alice shares a shopping list in her Solid pod with her book club through WAC's `acl:agentGroup`. Members should add items but never remove anyone's. Which setup fits?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Read and Append for the group, and members' apps send insert-only N3 Patches"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The group says who and the modes say what. Insert-only N3 Patches need only Append, so Read plus Append lets members see and add items but not delete them."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-sharing-r07-d1> ,
                          <#q-sharing-r07-d2> ,
                          <#q-sharing-r07-d3> .

<#q-sharing-r07-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Read and Write for the group, and members' apps send insert-only N3 Patches"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Write still lets any member delete items with another patch; the modes, not the app, must set the limit."@en .

<#q-sharing-r07-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Append only for the group, and members' apps PUT the whole list"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Replacing an existing document needs Write, and without Read members can't see the list."@en .

<#q-sharing-r07-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Read and Control for the group, so members manage their own entries"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Control lets members edit the ACL and give themselves Write."@en .

# Chapter 10: Notifications and live updates.
<#ch-notifications>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 9 ;
    dcterms:title "Notifications and live updates"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Keep an app in sync with changing pod data using the Solid Notifications Protocol: discover a subscription service, open a channel, read its messages, and refetch what changed."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-notifications-r01> ,
                              <#q-notifications-r02> ,
                              <#q-notifications-r03> ,
                              <#q-notifications-r04> ,
                              <#q-notifications-r05> .

<#ch-notifications-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-notifications> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Two kinds of notification

Solid uses the word "notification" for two different things. In Steps 5.11 and 9.6–9.7 you met **Linked Data Notifications (LDN)**, a W3C Recommendation from 2017: an inbox, found through `ldp:inbox`, that other agents POST messages to, like a mailbox. A Solid server must act as an LDN receiver. Those messages are *between agents*: "Bob shared a photo with you".

The **Solid Notifications Protocol** is about something else: *change events about resources*. Your app says "tell me when `/notes/shopping.ttl` changes", and the server tells it when that happens. This is what keeps an open app up to date when another app, or another device, edits the same data. Often the events arrive over a **WebSocket**, a connection the browser keeps open so the server can push messages to it.

| | LDN | Solid Notifications |
|---|---|---|
| Carries | messages an agent chose to send | change events about a resource |
| Found through | `ldp:inbox` | the resource's description links |
| Typical use | "I shared this with you" | "`shopping.ttl` was updated" |

Older servers offered an unauthenticated WebSocket API, `solid-0.1`, so anyone could learn when a private resource changed. The Solid Protocol now requires the Solid Notifications Protocol instead."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-notifications-1a> ,
                         <#q-notifications-1b> .

<#q-notifications-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app shows a shared to-do list stored in a pod and should redraw it when a teammate's app edits the list. Which mechanism fits?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Open a Solid Notifications channel on the list resource"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Solid Notifications are change events about a resource you subscribed to; LDN inboxes are mailboxes for messages between agents."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-1a-d3> .

<#q-notifications-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Read new items from the user's LDN inbox as they arrive"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The inbox holds messages other agents chose to send, not automatic change events about a resource."@en .

<#q-notifications-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Have the teammate's app POST to your app's inbox after each edit"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That couples the two apps and misses edits from any other app; the server should report changes."@en .

<#q-notifications-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Re-read the WebID profile until its `ldp:inbox` changes"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That predicate only says where the inbox is; it says nothing about changes to the list."@en .

<#q-notifications-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Why did Solid replace the old `solid-0.1` WebSocket API for live updates with the Solid Notifications Protocol?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Anyone could subscribe, with no authentication"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The legacy API let any client subscribe without proving who it was, so anyone could learn when a private resource changed."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-1b-d3> .

<#q-notifications-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "WebSockets are not supported by modern browsers"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Browsers support WebSockets, and the new protocol's main channel type still uses them."@en .

<#q-notifications-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It could only watch containers, not documents"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It accepted any resource URL; the problem was who could subscribe."@en .

<#q-notifications-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It sent the full new content, which was too large"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It sent only the URL of what changed."@en .

<#ch-notifications-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-notifications> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Discovery: finding a subscription service

You never guess where to subscribe. Discovery starts from the **topic resource**, the resource you want to hear about. From its `Link` headers (Step 5.4) follow `rel="describedby"` to its description resource, or `rel="http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#storageDescription"` to the storage description. Either must be available as JSON-LD. Follow the link rather than hard-coding a path.

The description lists **subscription services**, each with a `channelType` and the optional `feature`s it supports, and may list ready-made `channel`s. The terms live in the `notify:` namespace, `http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/notifications#`. The spec's examples show compact JSON-LD (Step 3.7):

```json
"subscription": [{
  "id": "https://websocket.example/subscription",
  "channelType": "WebSocketChannel2023",
  "feature": ["endAt", "rate", "state"]
}]
```

On CSS 7.2 (October 2026), a profile document's headers included:

```http
Link: <http://localhost:3000/alice/profile/card.meta>; rel="describedby"
Link: <http://localhost:3000/.notifications/StreamingHTTPChannel2023/http%3A%2F%2Flocalhost%3A3000%2Falice%2Fprofile%2Fcard>; rel="http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#updatesViaStreamingHttp2023"
Link: <http://localhost:3000/.well-known/solid>; rel="http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#storageDescription"
```

The storage description, `/.well-known/solid` (Step 5.6), came back *expanded*, every key a full IRI:

```json
{ "@id": "http://localhost:3000/.notifications/WebSocketChannel2023/",
  "http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/notifications#channelType": [
    { "@id": "http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/notifications#WebSocketChannel2023" } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/notifications#feature": [
    { "@id": "…#accept" }, { "@id": "…#endAt" }, { "@id": "…#rate" },
    { "@id": "…#startAt" }, { "@id": "…#state" } ] }
```

It listed a second service, `…/.notifications/WebhookChannel2023/`, with the same five features.

⚠ On CSS 7.2 only the storage description lists subscription services. The `describedby` resource (`card.meta`, Step 5.10) lists none, so an app should try both links."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-notifications-2a> ,
                         <#q-notifications-2b> .

<#q-notifications-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app wants live updates for `https://alice.example/notes/a.ttl` via the Solid Notifications Protocol. What should it do first?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Follow the topic's `describedby` or storage description link"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Discovery starts at the topic and follows its links to a JSON-LD description that lists subscription services. On CSS 7.2 (2026) only the storage description lists them, so try both."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-2a-d3> .

<#q-notifications-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "POST a subscription to `https://alice.example/notes/a.ttl` itself"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The topic resource is what you watch, not the service that creates channels."@en .

<#q-notifications-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Open a WebSocket to `wss://alice.example/` and send `sub`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is the legacy unauthenticated API, not the current protocol."@en .

<#q-notifications-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Look up the subscription URL in Alice's WebID profile"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Subscription services are advertised in description resources, not the profile."@en .

<#q-notifications-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid Notifications, a description resource lists a subscription service with `\"channelType\": \"WebhookChannel2023\"` and `\"feature\": [\"rate\"]`. What does that tell your app?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It can create webhook channels there and may ask for a `rate`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A subscription service states which channel type it creates and which optional features a client may set when subscribing."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-2b-d3> .

<#q-notifications-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It must set a `rate` in every request it sends there"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Features are optional; the list only says what a client may ask for."@en .

<#q-notifications-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server sends webhooks to every app at the given rate"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Nothing is sent until a client subscribes and asks for a channel."@en .

<#q-notifications-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It must use WebSockets, because `rate` needs a live connection"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`rate` is a shared feature that is not tied to one channel type."@en .

<#ch-notifications-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-notifications> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Subscribing: request and permissions

To subscribe, POST a JSON-LD **channel request** to the subscription service. For a WebSocket channel only `type` and `topic` are required:

```http
POST /subscription
Authorization: DPoP <token>
DPoP: <proof>
Content-Type: application/ld+json

{
  "@context": ["https://www.w3.org/ns/solid/notifications-context/v1"],
  "type": "WebSocketChannel2023",
  "topic": "https://alice.example/notes/a.ttl"
}
```

Use the protocol's context, as here (Step 10.8 explains why). It lets you write the short `WebSocketChannel2023`; the full IRI `http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/notifications#WebSocketChannel2023` works too. CSS 7.2 accepts both forms of this request. The response describes the new channel, including how to receive from it (Step 10.5).

The protocol leaves authentication and authorization to implementations, but the main servers use what you learned in Chapters 7 and 8: CSS needs a Solid-OIDC request and **Read** permission on the topic, and Inrupt's ESS 2.x WebSocket service also needed Read. ESS 3.0 removed that service in May 2026 (Step 10.8). You can only watch what you could read, because even the timing of changes to a private resource reveals information."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-notifications-3a> .

<#q-notifications-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "On the Community Solid Server (CSS 7.2, 2026), Bob can read Alice's `/photos/` container but not her `/diary.ttl`. Which Solid Notifications subscriptions will succeed for Bob?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "One on `/photos/` only, because subscribing needs Read on the topic"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "CSS requires an authenticated subscription request and Read permission on the topic resource, as ESS 2.x's WebSocket service did before it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-3a-d3> .

<#q-notifications-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Both, because subscribing reveals no content, only events"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Change events about a private resource still leak information, so CSS checks Read."@en .

<#q-notifications-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Neither, because only the pod owner may subscribe to changes"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Any authenticated agent with Read on the topic can subscribe."@en .

<#q-notifications-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Both, because CSS checks the subscription service's ACL instead"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "CSS checks Read on the topic resource itself, not on the service."@en .

<#ch-notifications-4>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-notifications> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Channel features and errors

A channel request may add the **features** the service listed:

- `startAt` and `endAt`: when the channel starts and ends, as date-times such as `"2026-10-08T12:00:00Z"`.
- `state`: the last state of the topic you know, such as its ETag.
- `rate`: the minimum time between two notifications, written as an ISO 8601 duration: `PT10S` is ten seconds, `PT1M` is one minute.
- `accept`: the media type you want messages in.

CSS 7.2 advertises all five. A request the server cannot process gets **422 Unprocessable Content** (its older name is Unprocessable Entity, which CSS uses, as in Step 6.7). The protocol's example is a JSON-LD context the server does not know; on CSS a `type` it cannot read gets 422 too. So a 422 points at your request body, not at your login or permissions. A request whose media type has a `profile` parameter the server does not support gets **415 Unsupported Media Type** instead.

On CSS 7.2 (October 2026), a request with the singular context `https://www.w3.org/ns/solid/notification/v1` and the short type `WebSocketChannel2023` (Step 10.8) got:

```http
HTTP/1.1 422 Unprocessable Entity
content-type: application/json

{"name":"UnprocessableEntityHttpError","message":"Unable to process notification channel: Missing expected value <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/notifications#WebSocketChannel2023> - http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type","statusCode":422,"errorCode":"H422","details":{}}
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-notifications-4a> ,
                         <#q-notifications-4b> .

<#q-notifications-4a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In a Solid Notifications channel request, what does the feature `\"rate\": \"PT1M\"` ask for?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "At most one notification per minute on the channel"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`rate` is an ISO 8601 duration giving the minimum time that must pass between notifications sent to the receiver."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-4a-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-4a-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-4a-d3> .

<#q-notifications-4a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The channel closes after one minute"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The channel's end is set with `endAt`, not `rate`."@en .

<#q-notifications-4a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server polls the resource once a minute"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`rate` limits how often notifications are sent, not how changes are detected."@en .

<#q-notifications-4a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Every notification is delayed by exactly one minute"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It is a minimum gap between notifications, not a fixed delay."@en .

<#q-notifications-4b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app's notification channel request (a JSON-LD POST to a subscription service) gets `422 Unprocessable Entity`. Where should it look first?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "At the request body's `@context` and `type`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A 422 means the server could not process what was sent: check the `@context` (use the protocol's own), the `type` and any feature values."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-4b-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-4b-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-4b-d3> .

<#q-notifications-4b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "At its WebID's Read permission on the topic"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A missing permission is an authorization error (401 or 403), not an unprocessable request."@en .

<#q-notifications-4b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "At the server's load, and retry in a minute"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Retrying the same body won't help; the server could not process its content."@en .

<#q-notifications-4b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "At the `Content-Type`, and switch to `text/turtle`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Subscription services expect `application/ld+json`; the body's content is the problem."@en .

<#ch-notifications-5>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-notifications> ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## WebSocket channels

A channel either lets your app **receive from** the server or has the server **send to** your app.

**WebSocketChannel2023** is receive-from: the response holds a `receiveFrom` URL, and your app connects to it. On CSS 7.2 (October 2026), a request like the one in Step 10.3, with a profile document as topic, got this answer, with `type` expanded to the full IRI and `endAt` two weeks ahead by default:

```http
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
content-type: application/ld+json

{"@context":["https://www.w3.org/ns/solid/notification/v1"],
 "id":"http://localhost:3000/.notifications/WebSocketChannel2023/3ee7eea4-…",
 "type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/notifications#WebSocketChannel2023",
 "topic":"http://localhost:3000/alice/profile/card",
 "receiveFrom":"ws://localhost:3000/.notifications/WebSocketChannel2023/3ee7eea4-…",
 "endAt":"2026-10-21T21:33:13.394Z"}
```

```js
const ws = new WebSocket(channel.receiveFrom);
ws.onmessage = (event) => {
  const message = JSON.parse(event.data);
  console.log(message.type, message.object); // Step 10.7: what to do next
};
```

Call `new WebSocket(receiveFrom)` with one argument. The draft suggests passing the channel type's IRI as a second argument, but browsers reject that with a `SyntaxError`, and CSS doesn't need it.

`receiveFrom` is a **capability URL**: whoever knows it can listen, so keep it secret. Closing the socket stops delivery, but on CSS the channel remains until it expires or you DELETE its `id`. On CSS 7.2, after the socket was closed:

```text
DELETE <channel id>          → 205 Reset Content
DELETE <channel id> again    → 404 Not Found
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-notifications-5a> .

<#q-notifications-5a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Why should a Solid app treat a WebSocketChannel2023 notification channel's `receiveFrom` URL as a secret?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Whoever knows the URL can listen to that channel"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The subscription response can contain a capability URL that protects the channel, so possessing the URL is what grants access."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-5a-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-5a-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-5a-d3> .

<#q-notifications-5a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It holds the user's access token, so it works on any pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The URL only stands for this one channel; access tokens are DPoP-bound and go in headers."@en .

<#q-notifications-5a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Whoever knows it can edit the topic resource"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It only gives access to the notifications, not to writing the resource."@en .

<#q-notifications-5a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It reveals the user's identity provider"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It points at the notification sender, not the identity provider."@en .

<#ch-notifications-6>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-notifications> ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Webhook channels, and the other types

**WebhookChannel2023** is send-to, for backends: the request adds an https `sendTo` URL, and the server POSTs each notification there, authenticating itself. It suits a server that has a public HTTPS endpoint but can't keep a socket open; a browser tab has no such endpoint. Unsubscribe explicitly (on CSS, DELETE the `id`); Step 13.8 covers receiving those POSTs safely.

| | Receive-from | Send-to |
|---|---|---|
| Example type | WebSocketChannel2023 | WebhookChannel2023 |
| Address | `receiveFrom`, in the response | `sendTo`, in the request |
| Suits | browser apps | backends with an HTTPS endpoint |

Other channel types are registered. As of October 2026 the protocol and every channel type are still drafts:

| Spec | Status, October 2026 |
|---|---|
| Solid Notifications Protocol | Draft CG Report 0.3.0, 2024-05-12 |
| WebSocketChannel2023 | Editor's Draft, 2022-12-27 |
| WebhookChannel2023 | Draft CG Report v0.1, 2026-08-04 |
| StreamingHTTPChannel2023 | Draft CG Report v0.1, 2026-08-04 |

Each row gives the status in the spec's own heading; the W3C TR index (April 2026) still labels all three channel types Editor's Drafts.

⚠ You have seen one of them already. Every CSS 7.2 resource advertises, in the `Link` headers of a GET or HEAD, a link with `rel="http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#updatesViaStreamingHttp2023"` (Step 10.2). It points to a ready-made **StreamingHTTPChannel2023** channel for that resource: no subscription, just a request that needs the same permission as reading the resource, whose response stays open and streams notifications as Turtle. This course doesn't use it."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-notifications-6a> ,
                         <#q-notifications-6b> .

<#q-notifications-6a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "You are building a browser-only Solid app with no backend and want live updates. Which Solid Notifications channel type suits it?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "WebSocketChannel2023, since the app connects out to `receiveFrom`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Receive-from channels like WebSocketChannel2023 let a client open the connection itself; send-to channels need a reachable endpoint."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-6a-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-6a-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-6a-d3> .

<#q-notifications-6a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "WebhookChannel2023, since the server pushes straight to the browser"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A webhook needs an HTTPS endpoint the server can POST to, which a browser tab does not provide."@en .

<#q-notifications-6a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The user's LDN inbox, since every pod can have one"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "An inbox holds messages that agents chose to send, not change events about a resource."@en .

<#q-notifications-6a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Any type, since the server picks the channel for you"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The client chooses a subscription service whose channel type it can handle."@en .

<#q-notifications-6b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "On CSS 7.2 (2026), a backend subscribed to a Solid resource with a WebhookChannel2023 channel and no longer needs updates. What should it do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Send DELETE to the channel's `id` to unsubscribe"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A webhook channel must be unsubscribed explicitly; CSS does this with DELETE on the channel's `id` from the subscription response, and other servers may differ."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-6b-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-6b-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-6b-d3> .

<#q-notifications-6b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Stop answering the webhook POSTs it receives"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The server keeps the channel and keeps sending until it is removed or expires."@en .

<#q-notifications-6b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Wait for the channel's `endAt` to pass"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Until then the server keeps POSTing to the endpoint; on CSS 7.2 that is two weeks by default."@en .

<#q-notifications-6b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Remove its own Read permission on the topic"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is a roundabout side effect, not how a channel is ended."@en .

<#ch-notifications-7>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-notifications> ;
    schema:position 6 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Notification messages

A notification is an **Activity Streams 2.0** activity in JSON-LD (Chapter 4's `as:`). It must have an `id`, a `type`, an `object` (what changed) and a `published` time; `target` and `state` are optional. CSS sends `Create`, `Update` and `Delete` about a resource, and `Add` or `Remove` with a container as `target`. A message says **what changed, not the new content**: refetch, or skip that when `state` matches the ETag you hold.

On CSS 7.2 (October 2026), with a WebSocket channel on a note, `/alice/notes/scratch.ttl`, a PATCH to that note got `205 Reset Content` and the socket received:

```json
{ "@context": ["https://www.w3.org/ns/activitystreams",
               "https://www.w3.org/ns/solid/notification/v1"],
  "id": "urn:1791391461222:http://localhost:3000/alice/notes/scratch.ttl",
  "type": "Update",
  "object": "http://localhost:3000/alice/notes/scratch.ttl",
  "state": "\\"1791391461219-text/turtle\\"",
  "published": "2026-10-07T16:44:21.222Z" }
```

A Turtle HEAD then returned `ETag: "1791391461219-text/turtle"`, the same value. Deleting the note produced a `Delete` message with no `state`.

So on CSS, `state` is the new ETag, quotes included, of the *Turtle* representation: compare it with the ETag from a Turtle GET or HEAD, not a JSON-LD one (Step 6.11). CSS answers a PUT or PATCH without an ETag, so read the resource back to learn yours.

Treat a message as a hint to refetch. Check that `object` is a resource you subscribed to, and never render message fields without escaping (Step 13.1)."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-notifications-7a> ,
                         <#q-notifications-7b> ,
                         <#q-notifications-7c> .

<#q-notifications-7a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app receives the notification `{\"type\": \"Update\", \"object\": \".../notes/a.ttl\", ...}` on its channel. What should it do next?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "GET `a.ttl` again and redraw from the fresh data"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Solid notifications report that `object` changed, not its new content, so the client fetches the resource to get its current state."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-7a-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-7a-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-7a-d3> .

<#q-notifications-7a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Apply the triples carried in the message to its copy"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A notification carries no triples; it only says the resource changed."@en .

<#q-notifications-7a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Fetch `/notes/` to learn which triples changed"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The container lists members; it doesn't describe edits inside a document."@en .

<#q-notifications-7a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Resubscribe, because an `Update` ends the channel"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A channel stays open across many notifications until it ends or expires."@en .

<#q-notifications-7b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "On CSS 7.2 (2026), a Solid Notifications channel watching the container `/photos/` receives `\"type\": \"Add\"` with `\"target\": \".../photos/\"`. What happened?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A new member was added to the `/photos/` container"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "CSS uses `Add` and `Remove` for changes to a container's contents, with the container as `target` and the member as `object`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-7b-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-7b-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-7b-d3> .

<#q-notifications-7b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Someone PATCHed new triples into the `/photos/` listing"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Container membership can't be PATCHed directly; it changes when members are created."@en .

<#q-notifications-7b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The `/photos/` container itself was just created"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Creating a resource is reported with `Create` about that resource."@en .

<#q-notifications-7b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Your channel gained `/photos/` as a second topic"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Activity types describe changes to resources, not to channels."@en .

<#q-notifications-7c>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "On CSS 7.2 (2026), an app saves `a.ttl`, then reads it back as Turtle and gets ETag `\"1791391461219-text/turtle\"`. A Solid notification for `a.ttl` then arrives whose `state` is `\"1791391461219-text/turtle\"`. What can the app conclude?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Its copy is current, so it can skip the refetch"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "On CSS a notification's `state` is the ETag of the Turtle representation, so a match means the cached version is the latest; here the event is the app's own save."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-7c-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-7c-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-7c-d3> .

<#q-notifications-7c-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Another app overwrote its save, so it must refetch and merge"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A matching state means the current version is the one it read."@en .

<#q-notifications-7c-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It must refetch, because `state` is a random message id"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The message id is `id`; on CSS `state` is the resource's ETag."@en .

<#q-notifications-7c-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server is asking it to send its ETag back"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`state` is information from the server, not a request."@en .

<#ch-notifications-8>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-notifications> ;
    schema:position 7 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Servers differ

The protocol and its channel types are drafts (Step 10.6), and servers differ:

- Inrupt ESS 2.x offered a WebSocket service for "an earlier version" of the protocol, with the subprotocol `solid-0.2`. ESS 3.0 (27 May 2026) removed it for its own Notification Delivery Service.
- Older servers such as Node Solid Server (NSS) offer live updates only through the legacy, unauthenticated `solid-0.1` WebSocket API (Step 10.1), found via an `Updates-Via` header.
- ⚠ Context URLs differ. On 2026-10-07 the protocol's context, `https://www.w3.org/ns/solid/notifications-context/v1`, returned 200, while the singular `https://www.w3.org/ns/solid/notification/v1`, used by the channel drafts and CSS's docs, returned 404.

CSS 7.2 accepts the singular context too, but only with the full type IRI, as in its docs. Its recorded answers to a channel request:

| `@context` | short `type` | full type IRI |
|---|---|---|
| `…/notifications-context/v1` | 200 | 200 |
| `…/notification/v1` | 422 | 200 |

Its responses and messages carry the singular URL anyway. Read them as plain JSON (Step 3.7): a JSON-LD processor would try to load that URL and fail.

So keep a fallback. When no supported channel is found, keep working by polling: reread what you display now and then (Step 10.9 makes that cheap)."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-notifications-8a> ,
                         <#q-notifications-8b> .

<#q-notifications-8a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app uses Solid Notifications on CSS, but another server advertises no subscription service and only sends an `Updates-Via` header. What is a sensible response?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Fall back to polling: reread what the app shows now and then"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Polling works on every server; `Updates-Via` marks the legacy `solid-0.1` API, usable only for data whose change times aren't sensitive, since anyone can listen."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-8a-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-8a-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-8a-d3> .

<#q-notifications-8a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Refuse to run, since the server isn't a Solid server"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Older deployed servers still offer only the legacy API."@en .

<#q-notifications-8a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "POST a WebSocketChannel2023 request to the `Updates-Via` URL"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That URL is a legacy WebSocket endpoint, not a subscription service."@en .

<#q-notifications-8a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Retry discovery every few seconds until a service appears"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A server does not add a subscription service on request; it is advertised or it is not."@en .

<#q-notifications-8b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "CSS 7.2 (2026) answers a Solid Notifications channel request with a body whose `@context` is `https://www.w3.org/ns/solid/notification/v1`, a URL that returned 404 in October 2026. How should the app read the reply?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "As plain JSON, reading `receiveFrom` and the other keys by name"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The reply's keys are fixed, so plain JSON is enough; a JSON-LD processor would fetch the broken context and fail."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-8b-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-8b-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-8b-d3> .

<#q-notifications-8b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Expand it with a JSON-LD processor before reading any keys"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The processor would try to load the context URL, get a 404 and fail."@en .

<#q-notifications-8b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Resubscribe with that context, so the reply comes back valid"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The reply carries the singular URL whatever the request used."@en .

<#q-notifications-8b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Treat the reply as an error, since its context cannot load"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The channel was created; only the context URL is broken."@en .

<#ch-notifications-9>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-notifications> ;
    schema:position 8 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## A hint, not a log

Treat notifications as a **hint, not a log**. Sockets drop, laptops sleep, and channels end, and nothing replays the events you missed. When you reconnect or resubscribe, refetch what you display.

ETags (Step 6.11) keep this cheap. Send the ETag you hold in `If-None-Match`; if nothing changed, the server answers **304 Not Modified** with no body. This is new: Step 6.3 used `If-None-Match: *` on a write.

| Header | Sent on | Means | Otherwise |
|---|---|---|---|
| `If-None-Match: *` | a write | only if nothing exists yet | 412 |
| `If-None-Match: "<etag>"` | a GET | only if it changed since this version | 304 |

On CSS 7.2 (October 2026), a profile document's ETag was `"1791391528575-text/turtle"`, and a GET sending it back got:

```http
GET /alice/profile/card
If-None-Match: "1791391528575-text/turtle"

HTTP/1.1 304 Not Modified
ETag: "1791391528575-text/turtle"
```

On CSS you can also resubscribe with `state` set to your cached ETag; if the resource changed meanwhile, a notification is sent at once."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-notifications-9a> ,
                         <#q-notifications-9b> .

<#q-notifications-9a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app keeps a list current with a WebSocket notification channel. The user's laptop sleeps for an hour and the socket drops. What should the app do when it wakes?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Re-establish its channel and refetch the resources it shows"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Events sent while the app was away are not replayed, so it reconnects or resubscribes (perhaps with `state`) and refetches, using ETags to avoid needless downloads."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-9a-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-9a-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-9a-d3> .

<#q-notifications-9a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Wait for the server to replay the missed notifications"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Nothing in the protocol replays events for a closed connection."@en .

<#q-notifications-9a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Reconnect and wait for the next notification to redraw"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Changes made during the gap produced no message it received."@en .

<#q-notifications-9a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Show the cached data, since delivery is guaranteed"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Notifications are best-effort hints, so the cache may be stale."@en .

<#q-notifications-9b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app holds `a.ttl` with ETag `\"abc-text/turtle\"` and wants to check it is current without downloading it again. Which request fits?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "GET with `If-None-Match: \"abc-text/turtle\"`; 304 means it is"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A GET with the held ETag in `If-None-Match` returns 304 Not Modified with no body when nothing changed, and the new representation otherwise."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-9b-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-9b-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-9b-d3> .

<#q-notifications-9b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "GET with `If-None-Match: *`; 304 means it is"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`*` matches any existing version, so it can't tell whether yours is current."@en .

<#q-notifications-9b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "PUT the copy with `If-Match: \"abc-text/turtle\"`; 200 means it is"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That writes data just to check it; a check should only read."@en .

<#q-notifications-9b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "HEAD with `If-Match: \"abc-text/turtle\"`; 412 means it is"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "412 from `If-Match` means the ETag no longer matches, the opposite; the reading check is `If-None-Match`."@en .

<#q-notifications-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In the Solid Notifications Protocol, what is the right order of the live-update flow for a resource?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Discover the service, subscribe, use the channel, then refetch on a new state"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Follow the topic's links to a subscription service, POST a channel request, use the returned channel, and refetch when a notification reports a state you don't hold."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-r01-d3> .

<#q-notifications-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Subscribe at the resource, read its inbox, then apply the diffs"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "You subscribe at a discovered service, and messages carry no diffs."@en .

<#q-notifications-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Connect to `receiveFrom`, discover the service, then subscribe"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "You only get `receiveFrom` from the subscription response."@en .

<#q-notifications-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Discover the service, subscribe, then read new content from messages"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Messages say what changed; the content must be fetched."@en .

<#q-notifications-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid, Alice shares a deck with Bob and wants Bob's app to learn about it. Alice's open app should also update when Bob edits the deck. Which mechanisms fit?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "An LDN message to Bob's inbox; a notification channel on the deck for Alice's app"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "An LDN inbox is a mailbox other agents POST to, good for \"I shared this with you\"; the Solid Notifications Protocol reports changes to resources you already know about."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-r02-d3> .

<#q-notifications-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A notification channel for Bob's app; an LDN message to Alice's inbox after each edit"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "This swaps them: Bob's app can't subscribe to a deck it doesn't know about, and the server reports edits without Bob sending anything."@en .

<#q-notifications-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A notification channel on the deck for both Bob's app and Alice's app"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Bob's app doesn't know the deck's URL yet, so it has nothing to subscribe to; someone has to tell it."@en .

<#q-notifications-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "LDN messages to both inboxes, one per share and one per edit"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Bob's app would have to send a message after every edit, and edits from any other app would go unreported."@en .

<#q-notifications-r03>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Why do current Solid servers such as CSS check a notification subscriber's identity and Read permission, when the legacy `solid-0.1` API did not?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Knowing when private data changes is itself sensitive"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Even \"this changed at 3am\" can reveal private information, so servers authenticate subscribers, check Read on the topic, and expose no more than needed."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-r03-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-r03-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-r03-d3> .

<#q-notifications-r03-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Notifications now include the resource's full content"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Messages still say which resource changed, not its content."@en .

<#q-notifications-r03-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Webhooks need a WebID to know where to POST"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The webhook destination is the `sendTo` URL in the request."@en .

<#q-notifications-r03-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "WebSockets cannot be opened without a DPoP token"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The WebSocket connects with a capability URL; the checks happen at subscription."@en .

<#q-notifications-r04>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid server lists subscription services for both WebSocketChannel2023 and WebhookChannel2023. An app has a browser UI and a backend that emails the user as soon as a shared deck changes. What is a sensible setup?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "WebSocket for the browser UI, webhook for the backend"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Receive-from WebSocket channels suit clients that stay connected; send-to webhook channels suit backends with an HTTPS endpoint that may not hold a connection."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-r04-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-r04-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-r04-d3> .

<#q-notifications-r04-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Webhook for both, since webhooks need no subscription"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Webhooks also need an authenticated subscription, and a browser has no HTTPS endpoint."@en .

<#q-notifications-r04-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "WebSocket for both, since webhooks are legacy"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Webhooks are a current registered channel type, suited to backends that can't keep a socket open."@en .

<#q-notifications-r04-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "One shared channel, since a topic allows only one"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Nothing limits a topic to one channel; each client subscribes on its own."@en .

<#q-notifications-r05>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A user is editing a note in a Solid app when a Solid Notifications `Update` message for that note arrives. What should the app do before saving?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Refetch, merge, then save with `If-Match` on the new ETag"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A notification warns that the copy is stale; the lost-update rule still applies, so refetch, merge, and write conditionally with `If-Match`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-notifications-r05-d1> ,
                          <#q-notifications-r05-d2> ,
                          <#q-notifications-r05-d3> .

<#q-notifications-r05-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Save immediately, since the notification proves the latest ETag"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The notification only says the note changed; saving over it would lose the other edit."@en .

<#q-notifications-r05-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Refetch, replace the editor's text with the server's, then save"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That throws away the user's unsaved edit; merge the two instead."@en .

<#q-notifications-r05-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "PATCH without `If-Match`, since PATCH is atomic"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Atomic means applied all at once, not that it checks for someone else's newer change."@en .

# Chapter 11: Data-oriented design and interoperability.
<#ch-interop>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 10 ;
    dcterms:title "Data-oriented design and interoperability"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Design an app's data so that other apps, and future versions of the same app, can find it, understand it and safely change it."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-interop-r01> ,
                              <#q-interop-r02> ,
                              <#q-interop-r03> ,
                              <#q-interop-r04> ,
                              <#q-interop-r05> .

<#ch-interop-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-interop> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Thinking data-first

In a classic web app, the company owns the database and its data model is private. In Solid the user owns the storage, and apps become *views* over that data, the way several image editors can open the same photo file. The promised benefit is no lock-in: when you replace the app, the data stays.

That changes how you design:

| Classic app | Solid app |
|---|---|
| The data model is private | The data model is shared with other apps and should describe itself (the data itself can still be private) |
| Schema changes are internal | Schema changes affect every app that reads the data |
| Unknown columns don't exist | Unknown triples exist and must be preserved |

The last row matters every time you save. Another app may have added triples to "your" subject, so a write must change only what your app means to change.

Noel De Martin calls the goal *interoperable serendipity*: many apps that work together "by pure serendipity", without their developers ever having coordinated."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-interop-1a> ,
                         <#q-interop-1b> .

<#q-interop-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "In a Solid pod, your notes app loads a note that has a triple with a predicate your app doesn't know. The user edits the title and saves. What should the app write?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The new title, leaving the unknown triple in place."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "In a pod, many apps may describe the same subject. Change only the triples you mean to change, so other apps' data survives your writes."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-interop-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-interop-1a-d3> .

<#q-interop-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The note without the unknown triple, since the app doesn't use it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Another app wrote that triple and would lose its data."@en .

<#q-interop-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing, because the note doesn't match the app's schema."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Extra triples are normal in an open world and are no reason to refuse."@en .

<#q-interop-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The note, with the unknown triple moved to a separate document."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The other app looks for its triple on the original subject and would no longer find it."@en .

<#q-interop-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid app design, what does Noel De Martin's term \"interoperable serendipity\" describe?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Apps working on the same data without their developers coordinating."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The term names the goal of many independent apps that work together because they follow shared conventions: where data is, what it means and what shape it has."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-interop-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-interop-1b-d3> .

<#q-interop-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Apps finding users' pods at random through a public search engine."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It is about apps sharing data, not about finding pods."@en .

<#q-interop-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Servers translating between vocabularies automatically."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid servers don't translate vocabularies; apps must agree on them."@en .

<#q-interop-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Pods copying data between each other in the background."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It describes apps reading the same pod, not pods syncing."@en .

<#ch-interop-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-interop> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Three things apps must agree on

Ruben Verborgh warns that in today's Solid, "data and apps are not really separated yet", because each app still picks its own document layout. For app B to use app A's data, they must agree on three things:

1. **Where** the data is (discovery): a type index, a newer SAI registry, or a hard-coded path.
2. **What it means** (vocabulary): the classes and predicates, such as `vcard:AddressBook`.
3. **What shape it has** (contract): required properties, how many values, which datatypes, written as a SHACL or ShEx shape.

RDF alone gives only a common data model. In Step 4.10, vCard contacts and schema.org contacts were both valid RDF, yet neither app saw the other's. As Leigh Dodds puts it, "Solid has no built in understanding of any specific schemas or formats".

The Solid Protocol prescribes no app layout, which is why type indexes exist; even popular tutorials hard-code a folder such as `my-solid-app/` for simplicity.

This chapter's worked example is **Solid Memo**, an open-source flashcard app whose published data policy shows one app's practice (Chapter 15 walks through its code). Its rules are good habits, not spec requirements."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-interop-2a> ,
                         <#q-interop-2b> .

<#q-interop-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Solid apps A and B both store address books as `vcard:AddressBook` in Turtle in the same pod, yet B never shows A's address books. Which layer has most likely failed?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Discovery: B doesn't know where A put them."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Sharing a vocabulary doesn't help if B can't find the documents. Discovery is a separate agreement, usually made through a type index."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-interop-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-interop-2a-d3> .

<#q-interop-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Vocabulary: B expects schema.org names for each contact."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The class matches, and a mismatch would hide fields, not whole books."@en .

<#q-interop-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Syntax: B needs JSON-LD instead of Turtle."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A Solid server serves the same RDF as Turtle or JSON-LD on request."@en .

<#q-interop-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Access: B needs Control on the address book."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Control is about editing the ACL, and reading needs only Read."@en .

<#q-interop-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Why isn't \"we store everything as RDF\" enough for two Solid apps to share data?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "They must also agree on location, terms and shape."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "RDF is the common model, but interoperability needs discovery, a shared vocabulary and a shared shape on top of it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-interop-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-interop-2b-d3> .

<#q-interop-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Each Solid server stores RDF in its own incompatible dialect."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Servers exchange standard Turtle and JSON-LD."@en .

<#q-interop-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "RDF data can't be read by browser JavaScript."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "JavaScript RDF libraries run in the browser."@en .

<#q-interop-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The Protocol fixes one folder per kind of data."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The Protocol prescribes no app layout at all."@en .

<#ch-interop-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-interop> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## The profile as the entry point

An app usually starts with only a WebID. Chapter 7 covered what a profile contains; here is how to walk it:

1. **The WebID document.** Look for `pim:storage`, `solid:publicTypeIndex` and `pim:preferencesFile`.
2. **Extended profiles,** linked with `rdfs:seeAlso` or `foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf`. The same links may live there instead.
3. **The preferences file.** It is private, so fetch it signed in. It may link the private type index.
4. **The storage.** Failing `pim:storage`, walk up the URL path from a resource known to be in the pod (such as the extended profile) until a response carries the `pim:Storage` type link (Step 5.5).

A fresh CSS 7.2 profile has no `pim:storage`. Walking up from `/alice/profile/card`, an anonymous `HEAD /alice/profile/` gets `401` with no type links, so keep going; the pod root answers (abridged):

```http
HEAD /alice/ HTTP/1.1

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Link: <http://www.w3.org/ns/pim/space#Storage>; rel="type"
Link: <http://www.w3.org/ns/ldp#Container>; rel="type"
…
```

Real profiles differ. On Inrupt ESS 2.x the WebID document isn't in the pod, apps can't rely on writing it, and by default it holds little more than `a foaf:Agent` and `solid:oidcIssuer`. Profile data belongs in the extended profile.

So read *every* profile document before deciding a link is missing. To add a link, write it to the WebID document if writable, otherwise to the first writable extended profile."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-interop-3a> ,
                         <#q-interop-3b> .

<#q-interop-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A user's Solid WebID document has `foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf` pointing to an extended profile, but no `solid:publicTypeIndex`. What should your app do next?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Check the extended profile before deciding the link is missing."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Profile links can be split across the WebID document and extended profiles, so check all of them first."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-interop-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-interop-3a-d3> .

<#q-interop-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Create a new public type index and link it from the WebID document."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The index may exist elsewhere, and the WebID document may be read-only."@en .

<#q-interop-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Try `/settings/publicTypeIndex.ttl` on the WebID's domain."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Guessing URLs is unreliable, and the WebID may not even be hosted in the pod."@en .

<#q-interop-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Ask the user's OIDC issuer where the index is."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The issuer handles login and doesn't describe where data lives."@en .

<#q-interop-3b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "None of a user's Solid profile documents contains `pim:storage`. How can an app still find the storage root?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Walk up the path until a response has the `pim:Storage` type link."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A Solid server marks a storage root with `Link: <http://www.w3.org/ns/pim/space#Storage>; rel=\"type\"`, so checking each level of the path above a resource in the pod finds the root without guessing."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-3b-d1> ,
                          <#q-interop-3b-d2> ,
                          <#q-interop-3b-d3> .

<#q-interop-3b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Assume the root of the WebID's origin is the storage."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The WebID may be hosted somewhere other than the pod."@en .

<#q-interop-3b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Fetch `/.well-known/solid` on the WebID's host and use the storage it names."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "On CSS 7.2 (2026) that description is server-wide and names the server root, not the pod, and the WebID may live elsewhere."@en .

<#q-interop-3b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Read the storage URL from the ID token's claims."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid-OIDC tokens identify the user, not their storage."@en .

<#ch-interop-4>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-interop> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Type indexes: finding data

A type index maps RDF classes to locations. It is an Editor's Draft (v1.0.0, March 2023), not a W3C standard, but it is the discovery mechanism apps actually use.

There are two indexes:

- The **public** index is linked from the WebID profile by `solid:publicTypeIndex`, is typed `solid:ListedDocument`, and should be publicly readable.
- The **private** index is linked from the preferences file by `solid:privateTypeIndex`, is typed `solid:UnlistedDocument`, and should be readable only by the owner. The preferences file is private too, so only the owner's apps can even follow the link.

Each index holds registrations:

```turtle
<#r1> a solid:TypeRegistration ;
    solid:forClass schema:Recipe ;
    solid:instanceContainer </recipes/> .
```

`solid:instance` points at one resource; `solid:instanceContainer` points at a container the client must list. Discovery is: profile → index → registration for your class → the resource, or the container's `ldp:contains`.

A registration names a class, not a shape: it tells you where `schema:Recipe` data lives, not which properties each recipe has."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-interop-4a> ,
                         <#q-interop-4b> .

<#q-interop-4a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A registration in a Solid type index says `solid:instanceContainer </recipes/>`. How does an app get the recipes?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "List the container and fetch the documents it contains."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`instanceContainer` means \"data of this class lives in documents inside this container\", so the client reads `ldp:contains` and then each document."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-4a-d1> ,
                          <#q-interop-4a-d2> ,
                          <#q-interop-4a-d3> .

<#q-interop-4a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "GET `/recipes/` and expect every recipe triple in that reply."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A container listing gives membership, not the contents of each document."@en .

<#q-interop-4a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Treat `/recipes/` itself as the single recipe document."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is what `solid:instance` means, not `instanceContainer`."@en .

<#q-interop-4a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Look up a separate registration for each recipe."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "One registration covers the whole container."@en .

<#q-interop-4b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Where does the Solid Type Indexes spec (Editor's Draft, 2023) put the links to a user's public and private type indexes?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Public: the profile; private: the preferences file."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The profile links the public index with `solid:publicTypeIndex`; the private one is reached through `pim:preferencesFile`, then `solid:privateTypeIndex`, with an authenticated request. Some real profiles also carry `solid:privateTypeIndex` directly, which is why apps read every profile document."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-4b-d1> ,
                          <#q-interop-4b-d2> ,
                          <#q-interop-4b-d3> .

<#q-interop-4b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Public: the profile; private: the profile too."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The spec keeps the private link out of the public profile, in the private preferences file."@en .

<#q-interop-4b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Public: the preferences file; private: the preferences file too."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The public index must be findable by anyone, and the preferences file is private."@en .

<#q-interop-4b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Public: the storage description; private: the preferences file."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The storage description at `/.well-known/solid` describes the storage, not one user's indexes."@en .

<#ch-interop-5>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-interop> ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Registering, and creating a missing index

Registering is app behaviour, not protocol: ask the user where to store data, then register it. Use the private index unless others must discover the data: a public registration reveals *that* the user keeps a kind of data, and where, even when the data is protected.

Other apps edit the same index, so never PUT an existing one: an insert-only PATCH keeps their registrations and needs no `If-Match`. A patch that also deletes needs `If-Match` (Step 6.11).

Pick a fragment that won't clash, such as `<#reg-notes-9f3c1a>`: an insert into an existing subject merges with it, so two apps using `<#notes>` would corrupt each other's registration. Not a blank node: CSS 7.2 rejects those in inserts with `422` (Step 6.8).

A fresh CSS 7.2 pod has no preferences file or index. Ask the user first (Step 15.4), then:

1. PUT `settings/privateTypeIndex.ttl` and `settings/prefs.ttl`, which links it, with `If-None-Match: *` (Step 6.3). Both inherit the root's owner-only rule (Step 8.6).
2. Link `settings/prefs.ttl` from the profile with an insert-only N3 Patch.
3. Insert the registration; make no public index.

On CSS 7.2 the insert answers `205 Reset Content` (adapted from a recorded run that used `<#notes>` in a fresh pod):

```http
PATCH /alice/settings/privateTypeIndex.ttl HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: text/n3

@prefix solid: <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#>.
@prefix schema: <http://schema.org/>.
_:patch a solid:InsertDeletePatch;
  solid:inserts { <#reg-notes-9f3c1a> a solid:TypeRegistration;
    solid:forClass schema:NoteDigitalDocument;
    solid:instanceContainer </alice/notes/> . }.

HTTP/1.1 205 Reset Content
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-interop-5a> ,
                         <#q-interop-5b> ,
                         <#q-interop-5c> .

