<#ch-interop-6>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-interop> ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Shapes as contracts: SHACL

A pod is open: the server has no built-in understanding of your app's schema, so any app with write access can store any triples. Chapter 4 showed that `rdfs:range` infers rather than rejects. To say "a card has exactly one front, and it is a string", you need a **shape**.

SHACL is a W3C Recommendation (2017). A *node shape* describes a *focus node* (the subject being checked), and a *property shape* (identified by `sh:path`) constrains one property. A *target* such as `sh:targetClass` says which nodes are focus nodes:

```turtle
ex:CardShape a sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:targetClass ex:Card ;
  sh:property [ sh:path ex:front ; sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ;
                sh:datatype xsd:string ] ;
  sh:property [ sh:path ex:back ; sh:minCount 1 ;
                sh:datatype xsd:string ] .
```

A validator checks a data graph against the shapes and returns a report. Here every `ex:Card` in the data is checked. `sh:conforms` is true exactly when the report has no results. A validator can also be handed a focus node directly, and then the shape's targets are ignored.

Shapes are **open by default**: properties a shape doesn't mention are allowed. Only `sh:closed true` forbids them. On shared data, keep shapes open, or every triple another app adds becomes a violation.

As of October 2026, SHACL 1.2 Core is a Working Draft (September 2026), so don't depend on its new features yet."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-interop-6a> ,
                         <#q-interop-6b> .

<#q-interop-6a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A SHACL shape targets `ex:Card` and requires exactly one `ex:front` with `sh:datatype xsd:string`. A card in the data graph has `ex:front 42` (an integer). What does validation produce?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A validation result, so `sh:conforms` is false."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The target makes the card a focus node, and SHACL lists every mismatch between it and the shape. One mismatch makes `sh:conforms` false."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-6a-d1> ,
                          <#q-interop-6a-d2> ,
                          <#q-interop-6a-d3> .

<#q-interop-6a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An inferred triple saying `42` is a string."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is RDFS inference; SHACL reports violations."@en .

<#q-interop-6a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
