    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The literal silently converted to `\"42\"`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Validation reports problems and never changes the data."@en .

<#q-interop-6b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Another Solid app adds `dcterms:created` to one of your cards. Your SHACL `CardShape` doesn't mention that property. Does the card still conform?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Yes, because shapes allow extra properties unless closed."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "SHACL shapes are open by default, which is what lets several apps add to the same subject without breaking each other's validation."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-6b-d1> ,
                          <#q-interop-6b-d2> ,
                          <#q-interop-6b-d3> .

<#q-interop-6b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, because every property must be declared in the shape."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That only holds for shapes with `sh:closed true`."@en .

<#q-interop-6b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, because `sh:targetClass ex:Card` covers every property of a card."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Targeting chooses which nodes to check, not which properties are allowed."@en .

<#q-interop-6b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only if the other app also publishes a shape."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Validation uses your shape and the data, nothing else."@en .

<#ch-interop-7>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-interop> ;
    schema:position 6 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## ShEx and shapes-to-code

ShEx (Shape Expressions) is the second shape language. ShEx 2.1 is a Final Community Group Report (2019), not a W3C Recommendation, and IEEE P3330 is working on a standard for it. Its compact syntax, ShExC, reads like a schema:

```shex
ex:CardShape {
  ex:front xsd:string ;
  ex:back  xsd:string +
}
```

Here each listed property must appear exactly once by default, and `+` means one or more. Like SHACL, ShEx allows properties a shape doesn't mention unless the shape is marked `CLOSED`. Instead of targets in the schema, ShEx uses a **ShapeMap**: you say which node to check against which shape when you run the validation.

Shapes can also generate code. LDO, a TypeScript library for Solid, compiles ShEx into TypeScript types (the LDO guide runs `build:ldo`), so app code reads `person.name` rather than building triples by hand. Solid Memo generates its record types from SHACL. Either way, one published contract drives both validation and your types. Types only check your own code at compile time, though; data from a pod arrives at run time, written by any app, so still validate what you read."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-interop-7a> ,
                         <#q-interop-7b> .

<#q-interop-7a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In ShEx (Shape Expressions), how do you say which node should be checked against which shape?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "With a ShapeMap supplied when you validate."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A ShapeMap pairs nodes with shape labels for one validation run, and the result is pass or fail per node."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-7a-d1> ,
