<#ch-interop-9>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-interop> ;
    schema:position 8 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Pod layout: granularity

Verborgh's "contacts conundrum" shows why no single folder layout suits every app: an address book wants one document per contact, while a birthday app wants one birthday list for family and another for work. So choose document boundaries with care. A document is the unit of fetching and of access control: you read it whole, and you share it whole.

Three rules follow, shown with a flashcard app such as Solid Memo:

- **Group what is read together.** The Protocol has no batch requests or queries, so every document costs one GET. Solid Memo keeps one deck in one document, so opening a deck is one request.
- **Separate fast from slow data.** Card text rarely changes; review state changes every session. Keeping them in separate documents means studying never rewrites the cards. `<cards.ttl#c1>` and `<reviews.ttl#c1>` are different IRIs: the app pairs them by their shared fragment, and the deck links both documents (`sm:cardsDocument`, `sm:reviewsDocument`) so other apps can find the pair.
- **Split growing logs by period.** An append-only history grows forever. One history document per month keeps each write small, and a year of statistics is still only twelve GETs."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-interop-9a> .

<#q-interop-9a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A flashcard app on Solid stores card text, which rarely changes, and review state, which changes every session. Which pod layout fits best?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Separate card and review documents, linked from the deck."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Splitting fast- from slow-changing data keeps frequent writes small and leaves the stable data untouched."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-9a-d1> ,
                          <#q-interop-9a-d2> ,
                          <#q-interop-9a-d3> .

<#q-interop-9a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "One document per card, holding both its text and its reviews."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Each study session rewrites cards and needs one GET per card."@en .

<#q-interop-9a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Everything in one document for the whole pod."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Every review rewrites everything and conflicts with any other edit."@en .

<#q-interop-9a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "One review-log document per card, next to the cards."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Each session then writes many documents."@en .

<#ch-interop-10>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-interop> ;
    schema:position 9 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Pod layout: discoverability

Other apps, and your app on a new device, must find and recognise your data. Solid Memo follows four rules:

- **A configurable, registered root.** Use a default container such as `<storage>myapp/`, let the user change it, and register it in a type index.
- **A self-describing container.** Put a small document in the container (Solid Memo calls it `myapp/meta.ttl`), saying what it holds. If the registration is lost, an app given the container's URL still recognises the data. It is an ordinary resource your app writes, not the server's `.meta` from Chapter 5.
- **Links over names.** Link documents to each other by URL rather than relying on file names, so files can be renamed or moved.
- **No access rules you don't need.** On WAC, a resource without an ACL inherits its ancestors' rules (Chapter 8); an extra ACL replaces them, so a faulty one can lock the owner out. On ACP, ancestors' member policies keep applying (Step 9.2), so an extra policy adds to them, and since a deny overrides any allow, a careless one can expose data or lock people out.

SAI likewise prefers a "relatively flat hierarchy"."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-interop-10a> .

<#q-interop-10a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Your Solid app creates a new container for its data in the user's pod. Should it also write access rules (an ACL or ACR policies) for it?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Only if it needs access different from the parent's."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Inheritance already protects the new container. On WAC an extra ACL replaces the inherited rules and a mistake can lock the owner out; on ACP an extra policy adds allows or denies on top, and a mistake can expose data or block access."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-10a-d1> ,
                          <#q-interop-10a-d2> ,
                          <#q-interop-10a-d3> .

<#q-interop-10a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Always, because containers without their own rules are public."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "They inherit their ancestors' rules (WAC) or member policies (ACP)."@en .

<#q-interop-10a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Always, because the server rejects writes without one."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Servers don't require an ACL for each resource."@en .

<#q-interop-10a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Never, because apps aren't allowed to write ACLs."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Apps with Control may write ACLs; they just shouldn't need to."@en .

<#ch-interop-11>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-interop> ;
    schema:position 10 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Evolving a vocabulary

Step 4.12 covered the vocabulary side: never delete or redefine a term, but deprecate it and name its successor. This step is the app side: what your code does when it meets a deprecated term.

Solid Memo's decks once stated their study direction with `sm:direction`, a string such as `"front-to-back"`. Version 1.6 of its vocabulary deprecated it for `sm:studyDirection`, whose value is a concept from `sm:StudyDirections`, such as `sm:frontToBack`. The values differ, so `dcterms:isReplacedBy` fits: it says only *use this one now*. `owl:equivalentProperty` would claim that old data reads unchanged as the new term, which is false here.

An app that meets the old term should:

- **Read both.** Prefer the new term; otherwise read the old one and translate its value. Old pods hold it, and older installs still write it.
- **Write only the new term**, when the user saves that subject.
- **Never strip it on read.** Whether a save may drop the old term depends on whether older readers still need it, which is a format question (Step 11.12). Solid Memo's deck format 3 drops `sm:direction`, and its version bump warns older readers."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-interop-11a> ,
                         <#q-interop-11b> .

<#q-interop-11a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Solid Memo's vocabulary deprecated `sm:direction` (a string) for `sm:studyDirection` (a concept). An app reads a deck that has `sm:direction \"front-to-back\"` but no `sm:studyDirection`. What should it do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Fall back to `sm:direction`; write `sm:studyDirection` on save."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A deprecated term lives on in old data. Read it as a fallback, translate its value, and write the new term only when the user saves that subject."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-11a-d1> ,
                          <#q-interop-11a-d2> ,
                          <#q-interop-11a-d3> .

<#q-interop-11a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Skip the deck until it is upgraded to `sm:studyDirection`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Old data must stay readable; that is why the term was deprecated, not deleted."@en .

<#q-interop-11a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Read `sm:direction`, and delete it from the pod right away."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Reading should never change the pod, and older apps may still rely on the old term."@en .

<#q-interop-11a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Copy the string unchanged into `sm:studyDirection` and save the deck."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The new term takes a concept such as `sm:frontToBack`, not the old string."@en .

<#q-interop-11b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In RDF, `ex:nextReview` means exactly what the deprecated `ex:dueDate` meant. Which triple lets a reasoner read old `ex:dueDate` data as `ex:nextReview` data?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "`ex:dueDate owl:equivalentProperty ex:nextReview`" ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`owl:equivalentProperty` is a claim about meaning, as with `solid:inbox` and `ldp:inbox`. `dcterms:isReplacedBy` only says which term to use now, so it also fits a successor that means something slightly different."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-11b-d1> ,
                          <#q-interop-11b-d2> ,
                          <#q-interop-11b-d3> .

<#q-interop-11b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`ex:dueDate dcterms:isReplacedBy ex:nextReview`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It says to use the new term, not that both mean the same."@en .

<#q-interop-11b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`ex:dueDate owl:deprecated true`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It marks the old term as obsolete but names no successor."@en .

<#q-interop-11b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`ex:dueDate owl:priorVersion ex:nextReview`" ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`owl:priorVersion` links versions of an ontology, not two terms."@en .

<#ch-interop-12>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-interop> ;
    schema:position 11 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Format versions and migrations

The vocabulary says what terms mean; a **format version** says how they are put together. In Solid Memo each subject carries one. Raise it only when an older reader would *misread* the new data. Optional terms an old reader can ignore need no bump. (Solid Memo also picks which shape to validate a subject with by its type and format version.)

- **Reading:** never refuse old data. Migrate it in memory through a chain of steps, one per version. Data *newer* than your app should pass through: read what you understand, never downgrade its version, and keep its unknown triples when you write.
- **Writing:** save in the current format, editing subjects in place so foreign triples survive. Saving one edited subject this way is fine: the user asked for that change, and it touches only that subject.

Rewriting the whole pod is different, and is the **user's** decision. Copy the data, upgrade and validate the copy, check the original didn't change meanwhile (compare its ETag, Step 6.11), and switch the type index registration last as the single commit point. The original stays in the pod as a backup."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-interop-12a> ,
                         <#q-interop-12b> .

<#q-interop-12a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid flashcard app writes deck format 3 and opens a pod holding format-1 decks. What should it do by default?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Migrate them in memory and save the new format only on edit."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "In-memory migrations keep the app working without touching the pod. Saving one edited deck changes only what the user chose, while a bulk rewrite changes every deck without asking, so it is the user's call."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-12a-d1> ,
                          <#q-interop-12a-d2> ,
                          <#q-interop-12a-d3> .

<#q-interop-12a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Refuse to open them until the user upgrades them."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Apps should never refuse older data."@en .

<#q-interop-12a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Rewrite every deck in the pod to the current format on startup."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Other apps or older installs may still read format 1, and a bulk rewrite is the user's call."@en .

<#q-interop-12a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Open them read-only so they can never be edited."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Edits are fine; they are saved in the current format."@en .

<#q-interop-12b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "When should the format version of a Solid app's data go up?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "When an older reader would misread the new data."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The version exists to warn readers. Raise it only when ignoring the change would make an old reader get the data wrong."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-12b-d1> ,
                          <#q-interop-12b-d2> ,
                          <#q-interop-12b-d3> .

<#q-interop-12b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "With every release of the app that writes the data."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Most releases don't change what the data means to readers."@en .

<#q-interop-12b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Whenever an optional property is added."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Optional terms an old reader can safely ignore need no bump."@en .

<#q-interop-12b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only when the vocabulary namespace changes."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A format can change in ways that break readers without a new namespace."@en .

<#q-interop-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A user moves from Solid recipe app A to recipe app B; the recipes are in the user's pod. What must B do to show A's recipes?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Find the recipes through the user's type index, then validate them."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The three agreements work together: the profile and type index give the location, the class gives the meaning, and a shape checks the structure."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-interop-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-interop-r01-d3> .

<#q-interop-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Ask app A's developers for an export, then import it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The data is already in the user's pod; B reads it there."@en .

<#q-interop-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Fetch every document in the pod, then keep the ones that look like recipes."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That ignores discovery and is far too slow on a real pod."@en .

<#q-interop-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Use the folder name app A hard-coded, then read it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Hard-coded paths break as soon as an app or user picks another location."@en .

<#q-interop-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid, what does a type index registration tell another app, and what does a shape (SHACL or ShEx) tell it?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The registration says where data is; the shape says how it is structured."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Type indexes map classes to locations and say nothing about shape, which is why apps also need SHACL or ShEx contracts."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-interop-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-interop-r02-d3> .

<#q-interop-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The registration gives access; the shape says where the data is."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Registrations don't grant access, and shapes don't locate data."@en .

<#q-interop-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The registration says what the class means; the shape says where it lives."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Meaning comes from the vocabulary, location from the index."@en .

<#q-interop-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The registration says which shape data follows; the shape says what the class means."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Registrations name a class, not a shape, and meaning comes from the vocabulary."@en .

<#q-interop-r03>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid flashcard app makes two changes to decks: it adds an optional property for tags, and it replaces `sm:direction` with `sm:studyDirection`, dropping the old term from saved decks. Older readers assume front-to-back when a deck has no `sm:direction`. Which change needs a format-version bump?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Only the second: older readers would misread the direction."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Deprecation tells developers which term to use; the format version tells readers of the data whether they would get it wrong. An old reader that finds no `sm:direction` assumes front-to-back and studies a bidirectional deck one way only."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-r03-d1> ,
                          <#q-interop-r03-d2> ,
                          <#q-interop-r03-d3> .

<#q-interop-r03-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only the first: it adds a term that older readers don't know."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "An optional term an old reader can ignore needs no bump."@en .

<#q-interop-r03-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Both: each of them changes the vocabulary the decks use."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Adding a term doesn't change how old readers read the data."@en .

<#q-interop-r03-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Neither: deprecating `sm:direction` already warns older readers."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Deprecation is in the vocabulary; an old app reading the deck just sees the term missing."@en .

<#q-interop-r04>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A user-requested format upgrade of a Solid app's data copies the data, upgrades the copy and switches the type index registration last. Why last?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Until the switch, every app still finds the original data."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Switching the registration is a single commit point: if anything fails before it, apps keep using the untouched original."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-r04-d1> ,
                          <#q-interop-r04-d2> ,
                          <#q-interop-r04-d3> .

<#q-interop-r04-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Type indexes can only be written after containers."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Nothing in the spec fixes that order."@en .

<#q-interop-r04-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The switch deletes the original data automatically."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Switching a registration only changes a link; the original stays as a backup."@en .

<#q-interop-r04-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "So the copy can be validated before any other app writes to it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Validation does come before the switch, but the order exists so that one step decides which data apps use."@en .

<#q-interop-r05>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid app design, why do open shapes and preserving foreign triples on write belong together?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Both let several apps add to the same data safely."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "An open shape accepts another app's extra triples, and preserving them on write keeps them in the pod, so shared data stays usable by everyone."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-interop-r05-d1> ,
                          <#q-interop-r05-d2> ,
                          <#q-interop-r05-d3> .

<#q-interop-r05-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Both make reads faster by skipping unknown properties."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "They are about coexistence, not speed."@en .

<#q-interop-r05-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Both are required by the Solid Protocol on every write."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The Protocol doesn't require shapes, or that clients keep other apps' triples."@en .

<#q-interop-r05-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Both stop other apps from writing to your subjects."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "They do the opposite: they welcome other apps' additions."@en .

# Chapter 12: Building Solid apps: servers, libraries, workflow.
<#ch-building-apps>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 11 ;
    dcterms:title "Building Solid apps: servers, libraries, workflow"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Choose a pod server and provider to develop against, log users in properly from the browser, pick the right libraries, and debug and test a Solid app. Version numbers are as of October 2026 and will drift."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-building-apps-r01> ,
                              <#q-building-apps-r02> ,
                              <#q-building-apps-r03> ,
                              <#q-building-apps-r04> ,
                              <#q-building-apps-r05> ,
                              <#q-building-apps-r06> ,
                              <#q-building-apps-r07> .

<#ch-building-apps-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-building-apps> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Pod servers

A pod server implements the Solid Protocol, and the Protocol requires it to support WAC, ACP or both. These are the servers you'll meet (October 2026):

| Server | Notes |
|---|---|
| **Community Solid Server (CSS)** | TypeScript, MIT, v7.2.0 (v8 only as alpha). WAC or ACP; memory, file or SPARQL storage |
| **Node Solid Server (NSS)** | The original server, published on npm as `solid-server`. Still maintained: v6.0.0 shipped in January 2026. WAC, bundles the data browser |
| **Pivot** | A thin wrapper around CSS that runs solidcommunity.net. WAC, quotas |
| **Inrupt ESS** | Enterprise Solid Server, commercial (v3.3). ACP plus Access Grants |
| **Solid-Nextcloud** | A plugin that turns Nextcloud into a pod server and identity provider |
| **JavaScript Solid Server (JSS)** | New in 2026, AGPL, "an early MVP and not production-ready" |

CSS is built on **Components.js**, a framework that assembles a program from separate parts listed in a configuration file (this is called dependency injection). Authentication, authorization and storage are modules wired together by JSON configuration files, so you can swap them by changing the config rather than the code. That makes CSS the usual choice for local development and research."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-building-apps-1a> ,
                         <#q-building-apps-1b> .

<#q-building-apps-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "How can one release of the Community Solid Server (CSS) run with WAC on one machine and ACP on another?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Its Components.js config wires in different modules"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "CSS is assembled through Components.js dependency injection, so authorization, authentication and storage are chosen in JSON config."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-1a-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Separate builds of CSS are published for each variant"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It is one package; the configuration file selects the modules."@en .

<#q-building-apps-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Each client picks the mechanism with a request header"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The server operator picks the mechanism in configuration, not the client."@en .

<#q-building-apps-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It copies the setting from the user's identity provider"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Access control is a pod-server setting, unrelated to the identity provider."@en .

<#q-building-apps-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "As of 2026, your Solid app's sharing feature works on a Pivot pod (solidcommunity.net). A user whose pod is on Inrupt's Enterprise Solid Server (ESS) tries it. What should you expect to handle?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "ACP access rules instead of WAC ACL documents"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "As of 2026, Pivot uses WAC and ESS uses ACP, so a portable app must handle both access-control systems."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-1b-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "SPARQL queries instead of HTTP document reads"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "ESS is still a document-based Solid server; neither server needs SPARQL for reads."@en .

<#q-building-apps-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Sharing through `acl:trustedApp` entries in the profile"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That was a legacy NSS feature, not part of WAC and not used by ESS."@en .

<#q-building-apps-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A different patch format, since ESS rejects N3 Patch"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Every conforming Solid server must accept N3 Patch; what differs for sharing is the access-control language."@en .

<#ch-building-apps-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-building-apps> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Pod providers

Most users won't run a server. They sign up with a **pod provider**. solidproject.org lists hosted providers such as solidcommunity.net (run by the ODI), solidweb.org, Data Pod by iGrant.io, use.id, TrinPod and Inrupt's **PodSpaces**. PodSpaces is a **Developer Preview**: "Do not use for production or for storing sensitive/personal data".

Providers don't all lay things out the same way. On PodSpaces a WebID looks like `https://id.inrupt.com/alice`, its issuer is `https://login.inrupt.com`, and the pod lives at `https://storage.inrupt.com/{id}`. The **WebID host, the storage host and the identity provider can all differ**. For example, a WebID on `alice.datapod.igrant.io` names `datapod.igrant.io` as its issuer.

So your app must never derive one from another. Read `solid:oidcIssuer` and `pim:storage` from the WebID profile, and fall back to a `Link`-header walk-up for the storage: on CSS 7.2 a fresh profile has no `pim:storage` at all. Test with accounts from more than one provider, because each one's URLs and access control (ACP on PodSpaces, WAC on solidcommunity.net) differ."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-building-apps-2a> ,
                         <#q-building-apps-2b> .

<#q-building-apps-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A user types the WebID `https://id.inrupt.com/alice` into your Solid app's login form. How should the app find her identity provider (issuer) and her pod storage?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Read them from the triples in her WebID profile"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The profile states `solid:oidcIssuer` and `pim:storage`. Guessing them from the WebID's domain fails whenever the hosts differ, as on PodSpaces."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-2a-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Derive both from the domain name in her WebID"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The issuer and storage can be on different hosts from the WebID, as on PodSpaces."@en .

<#q-building-apps-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Ask the provider's own REST API for the pod address"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid discovery is done through the profile and `Link` headers, not provider-specific APIs."@en .

<#q-building-apps-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Map her WebID's domain to a storage URL with a built-in table"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A hard-coded table breaks for any provider or self-hosted server it does not know."@en .

<#q-building-apps-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "As of 2026, which of these is a suitable use of an Inrupt PodSpaces account?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Trying out your app against an ACP server"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "PodSpaces is a developer preview that uses ACP: a good place to test against ACP, but not for real or sensitive data."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-2b-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Storing a clinic's patient records in production"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "PodSpaces is a developer preview, not meant for production or sensitive data."@en .

<#q-building-apps-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Running your WAC-specific ACL tests on it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "PodSpaces uses ACP, so WAC ACL documents do not apply there."@en .

<#q-building-apps-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Hosting your app's launch data for its first real users"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A developer preview is not for production."@en .

<#ch-building-apps-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-building-apps> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## The local development loop

Started with no options, CSS keeps everything in memory. Two more start options matter once you build an app:

```sh
# file-backed: data survives restarts in ./data/
npx @solid/community-server@7 -c @css:config/file.json -f data/
# create known accounts and pods at start-up, e.g. in CI
npx @solid/community-server@7 --seedConfig seed.json
```

A seed file is a JSON list of accounts. Each entry needs `email` and `password`; `pods` is optional:

```json
[{ "email": "alice@example.com", "password": "alice123",
   "pods": [{ "name": "alice" }] }]
```

The seeded WebID is `http://localhost:3000/alice/profile/card#me`, with issuer `http://localhost:3000/`. Use `localhost`, never `127.0.0.1`: CSS 7.2 accepts `http` WebIDs only on hosts ending in `localhost`.

On CSS 7.2, starting a file-backed, seeded server twice logged:

```text
first start:
  [SeededAccountInitializer] info: Creating account for alice@example.com
  [SeededAccountInitializer] info: Creating pod with name alice
  [SeededAccountInitializer] info: Initialized 1 accounts and 1 pods.
second start, same command:
  [SeededAccountInitializer] info: Creating account for alice@example.com
  [SeededAccountInitializer] warn: Error while initializing seeded account: There already is a login for this e-mail address.
  [SeededAccountInitializer] info: Initialized 0 accounts and 0 pods.
```

An account `bob`, created between the two starts, still answered `200 OK`. In memory, the restart lost bob (`404 Not Found`) and seeding made alice again with a fresh, empty pod, so alice's `200 OK` alone proves nothing.

**Docker pitfall.** In a container, CSS's base URL must also resolve *inside* the container: CSS fetches the WebID profile to check the issuer, and if that fails, tokens are silently ignored. Keep the inner and outer ports equal.

To inspect what your app wrote, open the pod in **Penny** or the **SolidOS data browser**."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-building-apps-3a> ,
                         <#q-building-apps-3b> .

<#q-building-apps-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "On Community Solid Server 7.2 (2026), you restart a file-backed server with the same `--seedConfig` file. The log says `Initialized 0 accounts and 0 pods.` What does that mean?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The accounts already exist on disk, so seeding skipped them"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Seeding tries to create each listed account at every start. With `file.json` the first run's accounts are still on disk, so CSS logs a warning for each and creates nothing."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-3a-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The seed file is invalid, so the server ignored it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "An invalid seed file stops the server with an error."@en .

<#q-building-apps-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The restart wiped the pods, and seeding failed to recreate them"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "File-backed data survives restarts; the accounts were still there."@en .

<#q-building-apps-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Seeding only runs on the very first start of a server"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It runs on every start; it simply cannot create an account that exists."@en .

<#q-building-apps-3b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Why start a Community Solid Server for your app's tests with `--seedConfig`?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "So every run begins with the same known accounts and pods"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A seed file creates the listed accounts and pods at start-up, so tests always start from a known state."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-3b-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-3b-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-3b-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-3b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "So the server copies data in from a production pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Seeding creates accounts and pods from a JSON file; it imports no remote data."@en .

<#q-building-apps-3b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "So authentication is switched off during the tests"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Seeded accounts still log in normally; seeding does not disable auth."@en .

<#q-building-apps-3b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "So the server writes all its data to disk instead of memory"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Storage is chosen by the config (`file.json`), not by seeding."@en .

<#ch-building-apps-4>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-building-apps> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## A debugging toolkit

Most Solid bugs show up in HTTP headers. Read them with `curl -i` or in the browser's Network panel. On CSS 7.2 an anonymous GET of a fresh pod root answered (excerpt):

```http
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Access-Control-Expose-Headers: Accept-Patch,Accept-Post,Accept-Put,Allow,Content-Range,ETag,Last-Modified,Link,Location,Updates-Via,WAC-Allow,Www-Authenticate
WAC-Allow: user="read",public="read"
Accept-Post: */*
Link: <http://www.w3.org/ns/pim/space#Storage>; rel="type"
Link: <http://www.w3.org/ns/ldp#Container>; rel="type"
Link: <http://localhost:3000/alice/.meta>; rel="describedby"
Link: <http://localhost:3000/alice/.acl>; rel="acl"
ETag: "1791410637631-text/turtle"
```

The same GET with the owner's token got `user="append control read write"`. Also look for `Accept-Patch`, `ETag` (for `If-Match`), the `describedby` link to the resource's metadata, and `Location` after a POST.

Then tell the failures apart:

- **401 or 403** is a response your code can read: "who are you?" versus "I know you, and no".
- **A CORS failure** gives your code no response, only an error; the reason is in the console. Firefox also reports DNS errors and refused connections as "CORS request did not succeed". A header visible in the Network panel but `null` from `res.headers.get()` is missing from `Access-Control-Expose-Headers`.
- **Silently anonymous:** a token was sent, yet the answer is 401 or public data. Read the server log. On CSS 7.2, a pod at `http://127.0.0.1:3001/` answered its owner with `WAC-Allow: user="read"` and logged:

```text
[DPoPWebIdExtractor] warn: Error verifying WebID via DPoP-bound access token: The URI claim could not be verified as secure.
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-building-apps-4a> ,
                         <#q-building-apps-4b> .

<#q-building-apps-4a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app's request to a pod on another origin gives your code no status code: `fetch()` rejects with `TypeError: NetworkError`. The same request from `curl` gets an answer. What most likely happened?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A CORS failure: the browser hid the response"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "For security, browsers hide the details of a CORS failure from JavaScript; only the console says what went wrong. 401, 403 and 500 are ordinary responses with a readable status and headers."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-4a-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-4a-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-4a-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-4a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A 401: the request carried no access token"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A 401 is a normal response with a status your code can read."@en .

<#q-building-apps-4a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A 403: the user lacks Read on the resource"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A 403 is also a readable response; here your code got none."@en .

<#q-building-apps-4a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A 500: the pod crashed while answering"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A 500 still reaches your code as a response with a status."@en .

<#q-building-apps-4b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "On Community Solid Server 7.2 (2026), a user logged in through a pod at `http://127.0.0.1:3000/`, and every private request gets 401 although your app sends its access token. Where do you find out why?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "In the Community Solid Server's log"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "CSS 7.2 accepts `http` WebIDs only on hosts ending in `localhost`; with `127.0.0.1` it logs a warning and answers as if no token was sent. Use `localhost`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-4b-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-4b-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-4b-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-4b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In the body of the 401 response your app received"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "CSS treats the request as anonymous; the reason appears only in its log."@en .

<#q-building-apps-4b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In the `WAC-Allow` header of the 401 response"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "CSS 7.2 sends no `WAC-Allow` on errors, and it would not explain a rejected token."@en .

<#q-building-apps-4b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In the browser console's list of CORS errors"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The 401 reached your code; nothing was blocked by CORS."@en .

<#ch-building-apps-5>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-building-apps> ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Logging in from the browser

`@inrupt/solid-client-authn-browser` (v5) runs the Solid-OIDC login flow for you:

```js
import { login, handleIncomingRedirect, getDefaultSession }
  from "@inrupt/solid-client-authn-browser";

const session = getDefaultSession();
await handleIncomingRedirect();             // on every page load
if (!session.info.isLoggedIn) {
  await login({
    oidcIssuer: issuer,                     // where to log in: see "Two ways in"
    redirectUrl: new URL("/", location.href).toString(),
    clientName: "My application",
    // clientId: "https://myapp.example/id"  // a Client ID Document
  });
}
// from here on, use session.fetch for every pod request
```

`login()` navigates away to the identity provider. The user signs in there and is sent back to your `redirectUrl` with a one-time code in the URL. The login completes only when `handleIncomingRedirect()` runs on that page and exchanges the code for tokens. After that, `session.fetch` sends authenticated requests.

So the redirect URL must load your app, with this same script. In a single-page app the simplest choice is the app's own start page, as here; a `/callback` route your host doesn't serve never runs `handleIncomingRedirect()`.

If you pass a `clientId` URL, the identity provider reads your Client ID Document. Without one, the library registers the app dynamically, and `clientName` is just the name shown on the consent screen."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-building-apps-5a> ,
                         <#q-building-apps-5b> .

<#q-building-apps-5a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "With `@inrupt/solid-client-authn-browser`, a user logs in at the identity provider and is sent back to your app, but `session.info.isLoggedIn` is still `false`. The page never calls `handleIncomingRedirect()`. What went wrong?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Nobody exchanged the returned code for tokens"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The redirect brings back a code. `handleIncomingRedirect()` exchanges it for tokens and marks the session as logged in, so call it on every page load."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-5a-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-5a-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-5a-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-5a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The identity provider rejected the redirect URL"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A rejected redirect URL stops the flow at the provider; here the user did come back."@en .

<#q-building-apps-5a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The app must call `login()` again after returning"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Calling `login()` again just sends the user back to the provider."@en .

<#q-building-apps-5a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The pod has not yet granted the app access"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Logging in involves only the app and the identity provider; the pod is not asked."@en .

<#q-building-apps-5b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In `@inrupt/solid-client-authn-browser`, you set `clientId: \"https://myapp.example/id\"` in `login()`. What changes compared with only setting `clientName`?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The provider fetches your Client ID Document"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "With a `clientId` URL the identity provider dereferences that JSON-LD document and checks the `redirect_uri` against it. With only `clientName`, the library registers the app dynamically."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-5b-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-5b-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-5b-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-5b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The app gets its own WebID separate from the user"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A client ID identifies the app to the identity provider; it is not a WebID, and the app still acts for the user."@en .

<#q-building-apps-5b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The pod automatically trusts everything the app does"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A Client ID Document proves who controls the redirect URIs, not that the app behaves well."@en .

<#q-building-apps-5b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The user no longer has to consent to the app"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The consent screen is still shown; the ID only tells the provider which app is asking."@en .

<#ch-building-apps-6>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-building-apps> ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Two ways in: coding the login form

A pod accepts a token only if the user's profile lists its issuer, so both entry points are safe, and a good login form offers both.

**WebID first.** Fetch the profile and collect every `solid:oidcIssuer`:

```js
import { getSolidDataset, getThing, getUrlAll } from "@inrupt/solid-client";
const OIDC_ISSUER = "http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#oidcIssuer";

async function issuersOf(webId) {      // profiles are public: plain fetch
  const profile = await getSolidDataset(webId, { fetch });
  const me = getThing(profile, webId);  // null if the WebID isn't in it
  return me ? getUrlAll(me, OIDC_ISSUER) : [];
}
```

`getSolidDataset` reads one RDF document and `getThing` picks out one subject (see "Reading and writing with @inrupt/solid-client"). Then:

- **No issuer:** say the profile names no provider; don't fall back to a default.
- **One issuer:** pass it to `login()` exactly as returned. Don't add or strip a trailing slash: issuer strings are compared exactly.
- **Several issuers:** let the user choose; each is equally trusted.

**Issuer first.** Show a few provider buttons **plus a free-text field** for any other issuer URL, and pass the choice as `oidcIssuer`. Without the field, users of unlisted and self-hosted providers are locked out.

Either way, take the WebID from `session.info.webId` after login and continue discovery from its profile. If the user picked a provider their profile doesn't list, login works but pods treat them as anonymous, so check the profile's issuers and explain."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-building-apps-6a> ,
                         <#q-building-apps-6b> .

<#q-building-apps-6a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Your Solid app's login page lists three identity providers and logs the user in with the one she picks. Is that \"guessing the issuer\"?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "No: she chose it, and the pod checks it against her profile"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Starting from a provider list is fine. The token is accepted only if the user's own profile lists that issuer, so a wrong choice just fails."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-6a-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-6a-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-6a-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-6a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes: a Solid login must always start from the WebID"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Issuer-first login is legitimate; only deriving or assuming the issuer is guessing."@en .

<#q-building-apps-6a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, but only if the Solid project has approved the list"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "There is no approved list; safety comes from the pod's check against the profile."@en .

<#q-building-apps-6a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, unless the app reads her WebID profile before the redirect"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The app learns her WebID after login; the pod's check is what makes it safe."@en .

<#q-building-apps-6b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In a Solid app's WebID-first login, a user types her WebID and her profile lists two `solid:oidcIssuer` values. What should your code do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Ask her which of the two to log in with"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Every issuer the profile lists can log her in, and only she knows which account she uses, so let her choose."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-6b-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-6b-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-6b-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-6b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Use the first one, since the profile lists it first"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "RDF triples have no order; \"first\" is just whatever the parser returned."@en .

<#q-building-apps-6b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Refuse to log in until the profile lists only one"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Several issuers are allowed; the pod trusts each of them."@en .

<#q-building-apps-6b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Pick the one on the same host as her WebID"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is guessing the issuer from the domain, which breaks whenever the hosts differ."@en .

<#ch-building-apps-7>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-building-apps> ;
    schema:position 6 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Sessions: restore, refresh and expiry

The browser library keeps tokens **only in memory**, so a page reload logs the user out; tokens kept in storage could be stolen by injected scripts. Pass `restorePreviousSession: true` to `handleIncomingRedirect()`: it does a silent redirect to the provider and brings the user back logged in if the provider session is alive.

While the page stays open, tokens renew themselves. The library asks for `offline_access`, so the provider also issues a **refresh token**, and shortly before the access token expires the library silently gets a new one. On CSS 7.2 a browser login's access token lasted 3600 s and came with a refresh token, because "Remember this client" was left ticked on CSS's consent page; untick it and no refresh token is issued, so the session ends after an hour.

`session.info.expirationDate` says when the current token ends. If a refresh fails, or no refresh token was issued, the library fires `sessionExpired` and logs out locally. From then on `session.fetch` sends **anonymous** requests, so private resources answer 401. Listen for the event:

```js
import { EVENTS } from "@inrupt/solid-client-authn-browser";
session.events.on(EVENTS.SESSION_EXPIRED, () => showLoginPrompt());
```

Never retry a 401 in a loop: try one silent restore, then ask the user to log in. After a reload, `restorePreviousSession` does it. After `sessionExpired`, authn-browser 5.0.1 has already forgotten the session, so your app makes the silent attempt itself: call `login({ oidcIssuer, redirectUrl, prompt: "none" })` (`prompt` is passed through although 5.0.1's typings omit it)."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-building-apps-7a> ,
                         <#q-building-apps-7b> .

<#q-building-apps-7a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A user reloads your Solid app's page and appears logged out. The app uses `@inrupt/solid-client-authn-browser`. What is the reason, and the intended fix (v5, 2026)?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Tokens are memory-only; use `restorePreviousSession`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The browser library keeps tokens in memory for security. `restorePreviousSession: true` silently re-authenticates if the identity provider still has a session."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-7a-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-7a-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-7a-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-7a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The DPoP key expired; ask the pod for a new key"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "DPoP keys are made by the client and the pod issues none; the tokens were simply lost from memory."@en .

<#q-building-apps-7a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The pod revoked access; reapply the ACL rules"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Nothing was revoked; the in-memory tokens disappeared with the page."@en .

<#q-building-apps-7a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "localStorage was cleared; store tokens yourself"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The library avoids persistent storage on purpose, because injected scripts could steal tokens kept there."@en .

<#q-building-apps-7b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In a Solid app using `@inrupt/solid-client-authn-browser` (v5, 2026), a token refresh failed and the app received `sessionExpired`. Saving a note now returns 401, although the code uses `session.fetch`. What should the app do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Try one silent restore, then ask the user to log in"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Once a refresh fails, the library logs out locally and `session.fetch` goes anonymous, so retries cannot succeed. Try one silent restore, then send the user to log in."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-7b-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-7b-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-7b-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-7b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Reapply the ACL rules, since the user's access was revoked"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Missing permission gives 403 to a logged-in user; 401 means no valid token arrived."@en .

<#q-building-apps-7b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Retry the save until the server accepts it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The request is now anonymous, so every retry gets 401 again."@en .

<#q-building-apps-7b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Wait for the library's next automatic refresh"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "After `sessionExpired` the library has logged out locally; nothing more is refreshed."@en .

<#ch-building-apps-8>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-building-apps> ;
    schema:position 7 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Logout, and surviving the login redirect

Logout has two levels:

```js
await session.logout({ logoutType: "app" }); // forget the session here
await session.logout({ logoutType: "idp", postLogoutUrl }); // also at the provider
```

After app logout, the provider session lives on, so the next login needs no password. Provider logout (OpenID "RP-initiated logout") sends the browser to the provider's `end_session_endpoint`, which ends that session and returns to `postLogoutUrl`; it must be listed in the Client ID Document's `post_logout_redirect_uris`.

⚠ On CSS 7.2 the next login still skipped the password after provider logout, because CSS's account page keeps its own `css-account` cookie until the user logs out there too.

`login()` and a silent restore both leave the page, and memory goes with it. So save what the user would miss, the current route and unsaved edits (never tokens), in `sessionStorage` first, and put it back on return. This is an app pattern: the library itself only hands back the previous URL, in its `sessionRestore` event.

```js
function logIn(issuer) {
  sessionStorage.setItem("pending",
    JSON.stringify({ route: location.hash, draft: editor.value }));
  return login({ oidcIssuer: issuer, redirectUrl: new URL("/", location.href).toString() });
}
// on every page load:
await handleIncomingRedirect({ restorePreviousSession: true });
const pending = JSON.parse(sessionStorage.getItem("pending") ?? "null");
if (pending) {
  sessionStorage.removeItem("pending");
  location.hash = pending.route; editor.value = pending.draft;
}
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-building-apps-8a> ,
                         <#q-building-apps-8b> .

<#q-building-apps-8a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
