    solid-memo:distractorText "The user lacks Read access to the document"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That would give 403 to an authenticated request; 401 means no valid credentials reached the server."@en .

<#q-building-apps-10b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The user has to log in again before every request"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "One login serves many requests through the session's fetch."@en .

<#ch-building-apps-11>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-building-apps> ;
    schema:position 10 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Other libraries and how to choose

Most JavaScript RDF libraries implement **RDF/JS**, shared interfaces for terms, `Quad` and `DatasetCore`, so quads from N3.js, rdf-ext or Comunica can pass between them. The ones this course uses, as of October 2026:

| Need | Good fit |
|---|---|
| Browser login | `@inrupt/solid-client-authn-browser` 5.0 |
| CRUD on Things | `@inrupt/solid-client` 4.0 |
| Types from ShEx shapes, React hooks | **LDO** (`@ldo/connected-solid`, 1.0.0-alpha) |
| Parsing, writing, an in-memory store | **N3.js** |
| SPARQL over pod documents | **Comunica** 5.x |

Others you'll meet: **Soukai** (models that save themselves, with offline sync) and **LDkit** (a typed schema that generates SPARQL).

Install LDO's Solid support as `@ldo/connected-solid`; `@ldo/solid` stopped at 1.0.0-alpha.1 (March 2025). LDO is used in the official "first Solid app" guide, and its CLI turns a ShEx shape into TypeScript types. If you build on SolidOS, use **rdflib.js**, the oldest Solid library and the base of SolidOS."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-building-apps-11a> ,
                         <#q-building-apps-11b> .

<#q-building-apps-11a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "You are starting a React Solid app and want TypeScript types generated from the ShEx shapes in your data contract. Which library fits best (2026)?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "LDO, which generates types from ShEx shapes"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "LDO's CLI generates TypeScript from ShEx shapes, and it offers React hooks for loading data. Install it through `@ldo/connected-solid`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-11a-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-11a-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-11a-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-11a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "N3.js, which turns Turtle into typed classes"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "N3.js parses and writes RDF into quads; it generates no types from shapes."@en .

<#q-building-apps-11a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "rdflib.js, which builds types from its store"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "rdflib.js is a quad store and fetcher; it does not generate types from shapes."@en .

<#q-building-apps-11a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Comunica, which infers types from SPARQL queries"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Comunica is a query engine; it does not generate TypeScript types."@en .

<#q-building-apps-11b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Why does it matter that the JavaScript libraries N3.js, rdf-ext and Comunica all implement RDF/JS?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Their quads and terms can be passed between them"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "RDF/JS defines common interfaces such as `Quad` and `DatasetCore`, so data parsed by one library can be stored or queried by another."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-11b-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-11b-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-11b-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-11b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "They all reach pods through the same SPARQL endpoint"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Pods have no SPARQL endpoint; RDF/JS is about in-code data interfaces."@en .

<#q-building-apps-11b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "They all share one common login session automatically"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "RDF/JS defines data structures, not authentication."@en .

<#q-building-apps-11b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "They can all validate data against SHACL shapes"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "RDF/JS covers terms, quads and datasets, not validation."@en .

<#ch-building-apps-12>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-building-apps> ;
    schema:position 11 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Querying pods client-side

**A pod is not a SPARQL endpoint.** The Solid Protocol serves documents over HTTP and requires no query service. To query, the client fetches documents and runs SPARQL over them itself.

**Comunica** is the reference client-side engine. `@comunica/query-sparql` takes document URLs as `sources`, and you pass the authenticated `fetch` to reach private data:

```js
import { QueryEngine } from "@comunica/query-sparql";
const engine = new QueryEngine();
const query = "SELECT ?name WHERE { ?card <http://schema.org/name> ?name }";
const stream = await engine.queryBindings(query,
  { sources: [deckUrl], fetch: session.fetch });
const rows = await stream.toArray();
rows.forEach((b) => console.log(b.get("name").value));
```

If you don't know in advance which documents hold the data, `@comunica/query-sparql-link-traversal-solid` follows links *during* the query. From a WebID it can follow `pim:storage` and then `ldp:contains` through the containers, or follow the type indexes to just the registered documents. Using the type index takes far fewer requests than walking every container. Either way, traversal finds only documents that some link leads to. It is experimental, with answers "in the order of seconds for non-complex queries"."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-building-apps-12a> ,
                         <#q-building-apps-12b> .

<#q-building-apps-12a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "How do you run a SPARQL query over data in a user's Solid pod?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Fetch the documents and run the query on the client"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A pod serves documents, so an engine such as Comunica fetches them with the authenticated `fetch` and evaluates SPARQL locally."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-12a-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-12a-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-12a-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-12a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "POST the query to the pod's `/sparql` endpoint over HTTP"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The Solid Protocol defines no query endpoint; any such feature is server-specific."@en .

<#q-building-apps-12a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Put the query into an N3 Patch `where` clause"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "N3 Patch changes one document; it does not return query results."@en .

<#q-building-apps-12a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Ask the identity provider to run it on the pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The identity provider issues tokens and does not query pod data."@en .

<#q-building-apps-12b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A link-traversal SPARQL query (Comunica) must find a user's notes in a large Solid pod. Which links should it follow to need the fewest requests?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The type index registrations for the notes class"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A type registration points straight at the container or documents holding a class, so traversal reads far fewer documents than a walk through every container."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-12b-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-12b-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-12b-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-12b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`ldp:contains` from the storage root through every container"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That finds the notes too, but only after reading every container."@en .

<#q-building-apps-12b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The `acl` links of every resource in the pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "ACL documents hold access rules, not pointers to the data."@en .

<#q-building-apps-12b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The issuer named in the user's WebID profile"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The identity provider holds no pod data to traverse."@en .

<#ch-building-apps-13>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-building-apps> ;
    schema:position 12 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Testing for interoperability

Test at four levels.

**Your logic, without a pod.** Put pod access behind a **port**, an interface your own code defines and calls, such as `loadDeck(url)`, and unit-test against an in-memory fake that implements it. Solid Memo keeps its domain and use cases free of Solid code this way.

**The real login.** Start a local server with known accounts, seeded or created through CSS's account API, and let a browser-automation tool fill in the provider's login form. As of 2026, Solid Memo runs such journeys in CI with Playwright against CSS 7, creating a fresh account, pod and WebID for each.

**The server.** The **Conformance Test Harness** (CTH) runs the community's spec tests with two users, alice and bob, and reports in **EARL**, a W3C RDF vocabulary for test results. It tells you how well a *server* follows the specs, not whether your app works.

**Your app on many servers.** Servers differ in PATCH handling, paths and concurrent writes. Run your end-to-end suite against several in Docker (CSS, NSS, Solid-Nextcloud, JSS), and keep a per-server list of expected failures so a known deviation doesn't hide a new regression."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-building-apps-13a> ,
                         <#q-building-apps-13b> .

<#q-building-apps-13a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "What does the Solid Conformance Test Harness (CTH) tell you?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "How well a pod server follows the Solid specs"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "CTH runs the specification tests (with users alice and bob) against a server and reports the results in EARL."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-13a-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-13a-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-13a-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-13a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Whether your app's user interface works in browsers"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "CTH tests servers over HTTP; it does not exercise app UIs."@en .

<#q-building-apps-13a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Whether your RDF data conforms to your SHACL shapes"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is data validation; CTH checks server behaviour against the specs."@en .

<#q-building-apps-13a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "How quickly a server answers under heavy load"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "CTH checks conformance, not performance."@en .

<#q-building-apps-13b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Your Solid app's end-to-end tests all pass against the Community Solid Server (CSS). What can you conclude?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It works on CSS; run the suite on other servers too"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "\"Works on my pod\" is not \"works on Solid\". Run the same suite against several servers and track expected failures per server."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-13b-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-13b-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-13b-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-13b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It works on every Solid server, since all follow one spec"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Servers deviate in practice, so passing on one doesn't prove it works on the others."@en .

<#q-building-apps-13b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It needs no more testing once CSS has passed CTH"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "CTH tests the server's conformance, not your app's behaviour on other servers."@en .

<#q-building-apps-13b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It is proven correct for ACP pods as well as WAC"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A CSS instance runs one configuration, usually WAC; ACP servers need their own runs."@en .

<#ch-building-apps-14>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-building-apps> ;
    schema:position 13 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Learning from real apps

Other apps' code and data show how the conventions work in practice.

- **Media Kraken** tracks your movies: search them, collect them, mark them as watched. It is an offline-first web app built with Vue and Soukai.
- **Umai** manages recipes local-first: you can use it in the browser before connecting a pod, and it syncs later. It stores recipes with schema.org's `Recipe` vocabulary, and accepts both `http://` and `https://schema.org` IRIs because other data uses either.
- **SolidOS** shows any RDF it meets through **panes**: viewers and editors chosen by the type of the data, such as profile, contacts, chat or folder.

Media Kraken and Umai are open source (GPLv3). In October 2026, Umai v0.1.13 on a CSS 7.2 pod linked `solid:privateTypeIndex` straight from the public profile (no preferences file) and wrote:

```turtle
# settings/privateTypeIndex
<#8a902a3a-7dd2-458a-9173-880d38d1519e> a <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#TypeRegistration>;
    <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#forClass> <https://schema.org/Recipe>;
    <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#instanceContainer> <http://localhost:4030/alice/cookbook/>.
# cookbook/pancakes
<#it> a <https://schema.org/Recipe>;
    <https://schema.org/name> "Pancakes".
```

Both documents answered `401` without a token: the link to the index is public, but the index and recipes stay private, so another app reads them only while the user (alice) is logged in."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-building-apps-14a> ,
                         <#q-building-apps-14b> .

<#q-building-apps-14a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "You want your Solid app to show the recipes a user saved with Umai, another Solid app. What must your app agree on with Umai?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Where the data is, its vocabulary and shape"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Apps interoperate by agreeing on discovery, vocabulary and shape, not on code."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-14a-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-14a-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-14a-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-14a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An API key issued by Umai's developer"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The data is in the user's pod; Umai runs no API in between."@en .

<#q-building-apps-14a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The same RDF library as Umai, Soukai, for reading"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Any RDF library reads the same triples; the library is not part of the contract."@en .

<#q-building-apps-14a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A login session shared with Umai"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Each app logs the user in itself; the pod checks each one's token."@en .

<#q-building-apps-14b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "What decides which SolidOS pane displays a resource in the Solid data browser?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The type of the data in the resource"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Panes are viewers and editors chosen by the kind of data, which is why one generic tool can show many apps' data."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-14b-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-14b-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-14b-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-14b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The app that originally wrote the resource"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "SolidOS does not track the writing app; it looks at the data."@en .

<#q-building-apps-14b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The file extension in the resource's URL"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Panes are chosen by the data's type, not by its URL."@en .

<#q-building-apps-14b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A setting in the pod server's configuration"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The data browser picks panes itself, on any server."@en .

<#q-building-apps-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Your Solid app works on a local Community Solid Server, but fails for an Inrupt PodSpaces user as soon as she logs in. Which two differences should you check first?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Issuer and storage on other hosts, and ACP instead of WAC"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A local CSS often puts WebID, issuer and storage on one host and uses WAC. PodSpaces puts WebIDs on `id.inrupt.com`, the issuer on `login.inrupt.com` and pods on `storage.inrupt.com`, and uses ACP."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-r01-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "PodSpaces requires a Client ID Document, and N3 Patch"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Dynamic registration works there too, and every Solid server takes N3 Patch."@en .

<#q-building-apps-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "PodSpaces has no identity provider, and it allows no writes"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "PodSpaces has an issuer and writable storage; they are just on different hosts."@en .

<#q-building-apps-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Your CSS ran over HTTP, and PodSpaces forbids DPoP"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "DPoP is part of Solid-OIDC on every server; the differences are hosts and access control."@en .

<#q-building-apps-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A CI job for a Solid app must start a fresh pod server and run end-to-end tests that log in through a real browser, the way users do. Which setup fits?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A fresh local CSS with known accounts, and browser automation"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Seeding, or creating accounts through the account API, gives known accounts on every run, and browser automation drives the provider's real login page. Fakes belong to unit tests behind a port."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-r02-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A shared PodSpaces account that someone resets by hand each run"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A developer-preview account shared across runs gives neither a fresh state nor automation."@en .

<#q-building-apps-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A file-backed CSS whose data folder is never cleared"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Leftover data from earlier runs means tests don't start from a known state."@en .

<#q-building-apps-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A fake pod in memory, so that no login is needed at all"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A fake suits unit tests, but it cannot test the real login."@en .

<#q-building-apps-r03>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Another app stores a user's notes in a second Solid pod on a different server. Her WebID profile lists only her first pod under `pim:storage`, and no type index entry points to the second. Why does a link-traversal query starting from her WebID miss the notes?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "No link from anything the query reads leads to the second pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Link traversal discovers data by following links such as `pim:storage`, type indexes and `ldp:contains`. Data that no link reaches is invisible to it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-r03-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-r03-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-r03-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-r03-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Link traversal only reads documents that are public"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "With an authenticated fetch it can read private documents too; the problem is that no link leads there."@en .

<#q-building-apps-r03-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Comunica refuses to query vocabularies that it does not recognise"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Comunica queries any triples; it needs links to find the documents in the first place."@en .

<#q-building-apps-r03-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Traversal never follows `ldp:contains`, only type indexes"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It can follow `ldp:contains`, but only inside storages it was led to, and nothing leads to the second pod."@en .

<#q-building-apps-r04>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app's user says saves fail after a while. The Network panel shows her PUTs answered with 401, and they carry no `Authorization` header. Which two fixes apply?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Pass `session.fetch` everywhere; handle `sessionExpired`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A request without `Authorization` is anonymous: either a call used the global `fetch`, or the session ended and `session.fetch` went anonymous. On `sessionExpired`, try one silent restore, then ask her to log in."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-r04-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-r04-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-r04-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-r04-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Grant her Write in the ACL; retry each failed PUT"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "No token reached the pod, so it can't tell who she is; permissions are not the problem yet."@en .

<#q-building-apps-r04-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Fix the pod's CORS headers; send the token in the request body"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The 401 was readable, so CORS let it through, and tokens go in headers."@en .

<#q-building-apps-r04-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Store tokens in localStorage; refresh them by hand"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Stored tokens can be stolen, and the library already refreshes them."@en .

<#q-building-apps-r05>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "The server hosting your Solid app's Client ID Document is down for an hour, and your users' identity provider keeps no cached copy of it. What happens?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "New logins to your app fail until the document is back"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Providers dereference the client ID during login. ⚠ Community Solid Server 7.2 (2026) can fetch it again (it keeps no copy), which normally includes silent restores and token refreshes, so the document must stay reachable over HTTPS."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-r05-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-r05-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-r05-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-r05-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Every logged-in user is cut off at once"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Requests with a still-valid access token keep working; on a provider that keeps no copy, such as CSS 7.2, refreshes and restores can fail meanwhile too."@en .

<#q-building-apps-r05-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Providers switch the app to dynamic registration"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The provider needs your document to check the redirect URI; it does not swap methods."@en .

<#q-building-apps-r05-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Users log in, but see only public data meanwhile"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Without the document the provider cannot complete the login at all."@en .

<#q-building-apps-r06>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In the browser's Network panel, a cross-origin response from a Solid pod shows `WAC-Allow: user=\"read\"`, but your code's `res.headers.get(\"WAC-Allow\")` returns `null`. Why?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The server's CORS headers don't expose it to scripts"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Cross-origin scripts see only safelisted headers and those listed in `Access-Control-Expose-Headers`. Community Solid Server 7.2 (2026) lists `WAC-Allow`; a server that doesn't hides it from your code."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-r06-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-r06-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-r06-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-r06-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`WAC-Allow` is sent only on HEAD requests, never on GET"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "WAC servers send it on GET and on HEAD."@en .

<#q-building-apps-r06-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The token was ignored, so the response was anonymous"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The header is there in the panel; the question is whether scripts may read it."@en .

<#q-building-apps-r06-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Header names are case-sensitive, so the lookup missed it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`headers.get()` ignores case; the header was hidden from scripts."@en .

<#q-building-apps-r07>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front """A Solid app's WebID-first login reads the profile with `@inrupt/solid-client` (v4) like this, and `getThing` throws a `ValidThingUrlExpectedError`. What is wrong?

```js
const profile = await getSolidDataset(webId, { fetch });
const me = getThing(profile, "#me");
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:back "`getThing` needs the full WebID, not the fragment alone"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Things are looked up by their full IRI, here the WebID itself: `getThing(profile, webId)`. `getThing` throws for a string that is not a URL, and returns `null` for a valid IRI that isn't in the document."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-building-apps-r07-d1> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-r07-d2> ,
                          <#q-building-apps-r07-d3> .

<#q-building-apps-r07-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The profile is private, so `fetch` returned nothing"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Profiles are public, and the error is about the Thing's URL, not the read."@en .

<#q-building-apps-r07-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`getSolidDataset` cannot read a URL that has a fragment in it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It reads the document the WebID points into; the fragment only names a subject."@en .

<#q-building-apps-r07-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The dataset must be saved before Things can be read"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Reading needs no save; datasets are only saved to write."@en .

# Chapter 13: Security, backends and going live.
<#ch-security>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 12 ;
    dcterms:title "Security, backends and going live"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Keep a Solid app and its users safe, run code that acts without a browser, and publish the app."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-security-r01> ,
                              <#q-security-r02> ,
                              <#q-security-r03> ,
                              <#q-security-r04> ,
                              <#q-security-r05> ,
                              <#q-security-r06> ,
                              <#q-security-r07> .

<#ch-security-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-security> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Pod data is untrusted input

Everything your app reads from a pod was written by someone: the user, any app the user authorised, or, in an inbox, anyone with Append. Treat it like form input from a stranger.

A literal can hold `<img src=x onerror="…">`. Insert it with `textContent`, or let your framework escape it; never use `innerHTML` or escape hatches such as React's `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`. Links are worse: `javascript:…` is a syntactically valid IRI, so an RDF parser accepts it, and clicking it runs script in your app. Allow only `http:` and `https:`:

```js
function linkFromRdf(iri, label) {
  const a = document.createElement("a");
  a.textContent = label;                 // text, never HTML
  let url;
  try { url = new URL(iri); } catch { return a; }
  if (url.protocol === "https:" || url.protocol === "http:") a.href = url.href;
  return a;
}
```

The pod doesn't filter markup. On CSS 7.2 (October 2026), this PUT, made as alice, got `201 Created`, and a later GET returned the literal exactly as written:

```http
PUT /alice/notes/xss.ttl HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:3000
Authorization: DPoP <alice's access token>
DPoP: <proof>
Content-Type: text/turtle

<#it> <http://schema.org/name> "<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>" .
```

```js
const title = '<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>';  // the literal read back
document.body.textContent = title;  // shows the characters <img src=x onerror=alert(1)>
document.body.innerHTML = title;    // runs alert(1): the data has become script
```

Two more habits. Don't automatically fetch, embed or `<iframe>` URLs found in data; let the user choose to follow them. And bound sizes: cap how many bytes you download and how many triples you render, because nothing stops a writer from making a resource huge."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-security-1a> ,
                         <#q-security-1b> .

<#q-security-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A note's title in a Solid pod is `<img src=x onerror=\"steal()\">`. Your web app shows it with `el.innerHTML = title`. What happens?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "`steal()` runs inside your app, with the user's session"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`innerHTML` turns data into markup, so an event handler in a literal runs as your app. Set text with `textContent` or framework escaping."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-security-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-security-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-security-1a-d3> .

<#q-security-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing, since the pod rejects literals containing markup"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A literal can be any string; the pod stores it as written."@en .

<#q-security-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing, since DPoP-bound tokens can't be used by scripts"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "DPoP doesn't stop script running in your own page from using the tokens."@en .

<#q-security-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The browser blocks it, as the data came from another origin"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Once inserted into your page, the markup runs in your origin."@en .

<#q-security-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In a Solid app, a contact's `foaf:homepage` read from a pod is `javascript:steal()`. How should the app render it?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "As a link only if its scheme is `http` or `https`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Validate the scheme of every URL taken from data against an allow-list (`http`, `https`) before putting it in `href` or `src`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-security-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-security-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-security-1b-d3> .

<#q-security-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "As a link, since the Turtle containing it parsed without errors"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Valid RDF says nothing about whether a link is safe to click."@en .

<#q-security-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "As a link, after removing the word `javascript` from the IRI"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A block-list misses other dangerous forms; allow-list the safe schemes."@en .

<#q-security-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "As a link, if the contact's profile was served over HTTPS"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "HTTPS protects the transfer, not the content someone wrote."@en .

<#ch-security-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-security> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Content Security Policy

Escaping (Step 13.1) is the first defence against injected markup. DPoP is none: script in your page can use its tokens, even ones kept in memory. A **Content Security Policy** (CSP) is a second defence: a response header telling the browser where scripts may come from and what the page may load and connect to. It must be one line:

```http
Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; img-src 'self' https: data:; connect-src 'self' https: wss:
```

`script-src 'self'` refuses inline scripts and scripts from other hosts, so injected markup can't easily load code. `default-src 'self'` covers every directive you leave out, so without `img-src` pod images would be blocked. `connect-src` is the trade-off: each user's pod and issuer can be on any host, so it usually allows any `https:` origin. CSP limits what script runs, not where data goes; don't rely on it alone.

**Sending it.** Static hosts set headers in their configuration, such as a `_headers` file on Netlify or Cloudflare Pages. A `<meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy">` tag needs no server but lacks some features. A local development pod at `http://localhost:3000` matches neither `https:` nor `wss:`, so in development add `http://localhost:3000` to `img-src`, and it and `ws://localhost:3000` to `connect-src`, or send no CSP. `default-src 'self'` also blocks inline styles, so keep them in a stylesheet."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-security-2a> ,
                         <#q-security-2b> .

<#q-security-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Why does a typical Solid app's Content Security Policy allow `connect-src https:` rather than a short list of hosts?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Each user's pod and issuer can be on any host"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A Solid app can't know its users' pod and issuer hosts ahead of time, so `connect-src` stays broad; `script-src` is where CSP helps most."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-security-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-security-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-security-2a-d3> .

<#q-security-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "CSP can't list hosts in `connect-src`, only schemes"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Host sources are allowed; the problem is not knowing the hosts in advance."@en .

<#q-security-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Pods refuse requests from pages with a strict CSP"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "CSP is enforced by the browser on your page; the pod never sees it."@en .

<#q-security-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It stops injected script from sending data to other hosts"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Allowing every `https:` origin is exactly what lets it send data anywhere."@en .

<#q-security-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front """A Solid app sends this Content Security Policy while you develop it against a local pod at `http://localhost:3000`. Logging in and loading notes now fail, and the browser console reports blocked requests. Why?

```http
Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; img-src 'self' https: data:; connect-src 'self' https: wss:
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:back "`connect-src` allows `https:` and `wss:`, which don't match `http://localhost:3000`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A scheme source matches only that scheme, so a local `http` and `ws` pod must be listed for development, or the CSP left off locally. The browser console names the directive behind each blocked request."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-security-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-security-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-security-2b-d3> .

<#q-security-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`script-src 'self'` blocks the login library loaded from your bundle"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Your bundled library is served from your own origin, which `'self'` allows."@en .

<#q-security-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`base-uri 'none'` stops the app from resolving the pod's relative URLs"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`base-uri` only limits the page's `<base>` element; it doesn't touch `fetch` or RDF parsing."@en .

<#q-security-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`object-src 'none'` blocks the pod's Turtle and JSON-LD responses"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`object-src` covers plugins such as `<object>`, not `fetch`."@en .

<#ch-security-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-security> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Phishing: look-alike issuers and WebIDs

A login can start from data an attacker wrote. Say your app accepts a pre-filled issuer. He sends a link such as `https://notes.example/login?issuer=https://solidcommunity.net.login-check.example/`, or asks the user to log in with a WebID he controls, whose profile names that issuer (WebID-first login). Your app redirects to a perfect copy of the real login page, and a user who types her password there has given it away.

Solid's trust check protects pods, not passwords. A pod believes a token only if the WebID's own profile lists its issuer, so a fake issuer can log in only as WebIDs that list it: the attacker's own. The danger is to the user's account at the real provider.

What your app can do:

- Before redirecting, show the issuer's host in full and stress its **registrable domain**, the part someone actually registered: "You'll sign in at solidcommunity.net.**login-check.example**". A familiar name at the start proves nothing.
- In issuer-first login, offer known providers to pick from.
- In sharing screens, show the **full WebID** next to the name. A profile's `foaf:name` and photo are claims anyone can copy; WAC warns servers not to trust them for access decisions."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-security-3a> ,
                         <#q-security-3b> .

<#q-security-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "An attacker runs a look-alike Solid-OIDC issuer at `solidcommunity.net.login-check.example` and sends links to your app with that issuer pre-filled. Some users log in through it, among them Alice, whose WebID profile lists `solidcommunity.net`. What can that issuer achieve?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Collect passwords typed into its copied login page"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The profile check stops a fake issuer from speaking for other WebIDs, but a look-alike login page can still steal what users type. Showing the issuer's registrable domain helps users notice."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-security-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-security-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-security-3a-d3> .

<#q-security-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Issue its own tokens that Alice's pod accepts as Alice"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Alice's profile doesn't list that issuer, so her pod rejects its tokens."@en .

<#q-security-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Be trusted for Alice's WebID without her profile changing"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Pods trust an issuer for a WebID only if that WebID's profile lists it."@en .

<#q-security-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Read every pod that trusts tokens from any issuer"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Pods trust an issuer only for WebIDs whose profiles list it."@en .

<#q-security-3b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app's share dialog finds a WebID profile whose `foaf:name` is \"Alice Smith\". What should it show the user?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The name together with the full WebID"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Names and photos are self-asserted. The WebID is what access rules use, so the user should see exactly which one they are granting."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-security-3b-d1> ,
                          <#q-security-3b-d2> ,
                          <#q-security-3b-d3> .

<#q-security-3b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The name alone, since the pod verified the profile"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Pods don't verify what profiles claim; anyone can write that name."@en .

<#q-security-3b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The name alone, since it was read over HTTPS"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "HTTPS proves which host sent it, not who the person is."@en .

<#q-security-3b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The photo alone, since it's harder to copy"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A photo is just a URL in the profile and is as easy to copy as a name."@en .

<#ch-security-4>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-security> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## SSRF: when your server fetches URLs for users

A browser app fetches from the user's machine. A **backend** fetches from inside your network, where it can reach what the public can't: a cloud metadata service at `http://169.254.169.254/`, an admin port on `localhost`, a database. **Server-side request forgery** (SSRF) is making your server fetch such an address for an attacker.

Solid backends fetch user-supplied URLs all the time: WebIDs, issuers, `pim:storage`, inboxes, webhook `sendTo` URLs, Client ID Documents. Guard every such fetch, following OWASP's SSRF guidance (the IETF Client ID Metadata Document draft, July 2026, requires the address and size checks):

- Allow only `https`; the sketch refuses even a local `http` pod.
- Resolve the host, refuse private, loopback and link-local addresses, and connect to the address you checked, so DNS can't change the answer in between.
- Don't follow redirects automatically; check each hop, or refuse them.
- Set a timeout and a maximum response size.

Node's `fetch` can't pin the connection itself, but accepts an undici `Agent` whose `lookup` checks every address before connecting. URLs with an IP address skip the lookup, so check those first. This sketch uses `undici` and `ipaddr.js`:

```js
import { Agent } from "undici";
import { lookup } from "node:dns";
import ipaddr from "ipaddr.js";

const isPublic = (ip) => ipaddr.process(ip).range() === "unicast";
const agent = new Agent({ connect: { lookup(host, opts, cb) {
  lookup(host, { ...opts, all: true }, (err, addrs) => {
    if (err) return cb(err);
    if (!addrs.every((a) => isPublic(a.address))) return cb(new Error(`blocked: ${host}`));
    opts.all ? cb(null, addrs) : cb(null, addrs[0].address, addrs[0].family);
  });
} } });

async function safeFetch(url) {
  const u = new URL(url);
  if (u.protocol !== "https:") throw new Error("https only");
  const host = u.hostname.replace(/^\\[|\\]$/g, "");
  if (ipaddr.isValid(host) && !isPublic(host)) throw new Error("blocked"); // IP literals skip lookup
  return fetch(u, { dispatcher: agent, redirect: "manual", signal: AbortSignal.timeout(5000) });
}
```

With `redirect: "manual"`, pass a 3xx response's `Location` through `safeFetch` again, or refuse it."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-security-4a> ,
                         <#q-security-4b> .

