<#ch-security-5>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-security> ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Scripts that act as you: client credentials

Scripts, bots and CI jobs can't use a browser. They log in with **client credentials**: an id and secret pair that `@inrupt/solid-client-authn-node` exchanges for tokens. The tokens then carry the WebID of the user who created the credentials. On CSS 7.2 (October 2026), this code logged in and read a private container:

```js
import { Session } from "@inrupt/solid-client-authn-node";
const session = new Session();
await session.login({
  clientId: process.env.SOLID_CLIENT_ID,          // never in the source code
  clientSecret: process.env.SOLID_CLIENT_SECRET,
  oidcIssuer: process.env.SOLID_OIDC_ISSUER,      // e.g. "http://localhost:3000/" for a local CSS
});
const res = await session.fetch("http://localhost:3000/alice/notes/"); // a private container
console.log(res.status, await res.text());
```

Where do the id and secret come from? On CSS v7 the account owner creates them, in the account web page or through the JSON account API at `/.account/`, after logging in to that account with its email and password: a POST of `{"name": …, "webId": …}` to the account's client-credentials URL returns an `id` and a `secret`. Only WebIDs linked to the account can be chosen, and a DELETE on the credentials' URL revokes them. Seeding a CSS account creates no credentials, so a CI job makes them through the API after the server starts.

⚠ On CSS 7.2 (October 2026) a client-credentials token lasts 600 seconds and comes with no refresh token, so a script simply requests a new one with the same id and secret. A token recorded there, decoded:

```json
{
  "webid": "http://localhost:3000/alice/profile/card#me",
  "iss": "http://localhost:3000/",
  "aud": "solid",
  "iat": 1791408795,
  "exp": 1791409395
}
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-security-5a> ,
                         <#q-security-5b> .

