<#ch-security-6>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-security> ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Bots and services with their own WebID

Start from **least privilege**: give every agent only the access its job needs. Your app shares exactly the resource and modes the user chose, and never widens ACLs, grants Control or makes data public by default. Code that runs alone is no different.

Client credentials make a script act **as you**, with all your access. That suits your own backups, not a service many people use, such as a backup service, a feed aggregator or an AI assistant.

Such a service should be **an agent of its own**: its own WebID, usually with an account and pod on some server, and client credentials created for that WebID. Users grant that WebID access like anyone else's, with an `acl:agent` authorization or an ACP matcher, and only the modes it needs. The user can then see, narrow and revoke the service's access without touching her own; its tokens carry its WebID, not hers, so a pod can tell its requests from hers; and she never hands over her own credentials.

On CSS 7.2 (October 2026), Alice gave a service account, backupbot, Read on one resource:

```turtle
@prefix acl: <http://www.w3.org/ns/auth/acl#>.

<#owner> a acl:Authorization;
  acl:agent <http://localhost:3000/alice/profile/card#me>;
  acl:accessTo <http://localhost:3000/alice/backup/a.ttl>;
  acl:mode acl:Read, acl:Write, acl:Control.

<#backupbot> a acl:Authorization;
  acl:agent <http://localhost:3000/backupbot/profile/card#me>;
  acl:accessTo <http://localhost:3000/alice/backup/a.ttl>;
  acl:mode acl:Read.
```

backupbot's own requests then got:

```text
GET /alice/backup/a.ttl    200 OK          wac-allow: user="read"
GET /alice/private/b.ttl   403 Forbidden
its token's webid          http://localhost:3000/backupbot/profile/card#me
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-security-6a> ,
                         <#q-security-6b> .

