# Chapter 14: Read a minimal Solid app.
<#ch-read-an-app>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 13 ;
    dcterms:title "Read a minimal Solid app"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Read, one piece at a time, the code of Solid Notes, a small notes app that uses every core piece of Solid from Chapters 5-13, with what it did on Community Solid Server 7.2. It ends with keeping the login healthy, serving the app the way it would go live, and checking who logged in."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-read-an-app-r01> ,
                              <#q-read-an-app-r02> ,
                              <#q-read-an-app-r03> ,
                              <#q-read-an-app-r04> ,
                              <#q-read-an-app-r05> ,
                              <#q-read-an-app-r06> ,
                              <#q-read-an-app-r07> ,
                              <#q-read-an-app-r08> ,
                              <#q-read-an-app-r09> .

<#ch-read-an-app-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-read-an-app> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## What the app does, and its one design rule

**Solid Notes** is a small notes app written for this course. **alice** owns the notes; **bob** is her friend. The app logs alice in, finds her storage, registers where notes live, creates and edits notes, uploads a picture, shares a note with bob, posts to his inbox, follows changes live and keeps its login healthy. Every piece comes from Chapters 5-13. Its code is a few small ES modules, a browser page and a test suite that runs in Node.

The key design choice: **every function that talks to the pod takes a `fetch` parameter**. In the browser it is `session.fetch`, which adds the DPoP-bound token (Step 7.10); in the tests, a client-credentials login (Step 13.5). So the same pod logic is tested in Node and shipped to the browser.

```js
// src/notes.js
export async function createNote(container, text, fetch, slug = 'note') {
  // …
}
```

The worked examples in this chapter were recorded in 2026 on Community Solid Server (CSS) 7.2 in its default configuration (WAC, data in memory), unless they say otherwise. Each test run made fresh accounts, so names such as `alice40742` differ between examples."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-read-an-app-1a> .

<#q-read-an-app-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In the Solid Notes example app, why does every function that talks to the pod take a `fetch` argument instead of calling the global `fetch`?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "So the same code runs with the browser's login or the tests' login"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The authenticated fetch is the only thing that differs between the browser (`session.fetch`) and the tests (a client-credentials login), so passing it in keeps the pod logic identical in both."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-an-app-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-an-app-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-an-app-1a-d3> .

<#q-read-an-app-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Because the global `fetch` cannot send PATCH requests to a Solid pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The global fetch sends any method; it just carries no Solid login."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "So the app can switch between Turtle and JSON-LD at runtime"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Content negotiation is done with the Accept header, not by swapping fetch."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Because Node has no global `fetch`, so the tests need a polyfill"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Node 22 has a global fetch; the parameter is about who is logged in."@en .

<#ch-read-an-app-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-read-an-app> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Logging in: a provider or a WebID

The login form offers both entry points from Step 12.6. **Issuer-first**: the user picks a provider from a list or types any provider's URL; without that field, users of unlisted providers are locked out. **WebID-first**: the user types a WebID and the app reads the profile's `solid:oidcIssuer`. One issuer: log in there. Several: the app lists *those* issuers and lets the user pick. None: pick or type one. Both are safe because the pod checks the token's issuer against the profile (Step 7.9). Never *guess* the issuer from the WebID's domain.

```js
// src/main.js (WebID-first)
  const issuers = await issuersOf($('webid').value); // the profile's solid:oidcIssuer values
  if (issuers.length === 1) return startLogin(issuers[0], { pending: pendingNow() });
  if (issuers.length === 0) return status('Your profile names no provider: pick or type one above.');
  $('provider').replaceChildren(...issuers.map((issuer) => new Option(issuer, issuer)));
```

`startLogin` calls `login` from `@inrupt/solid-client-authn-browser` 5.0.1; back from the provider, `handleIncomingRedirect` finishes the login. Steps 14.14-14.18 handle the rest of the session. A test can't click through a login page, so the Node tests log in with client credentials made through the CSS account API (Step 13.5). On CSS 7.2 a browser test logged in all three ways: WebID-first, a listed provider and a typed provider URL."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-read-an-app-2a> ,
                         <#q-read-an-app-2b> .

<#q-read-an-app-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In the Solid Notes example app, alice's WebID profile lists two `solid:oidcIssuer` values. What does the app do when she logs in with her WebID?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Lists those two issuers and lets her pick one to log in with"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Every issuer the profile lists is trusted, so the user chooses; the app offers exactly those, not its own provider list."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-an-app-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-an-app-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-an-app-2a-d3> .

<#q-read-an-app-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Picks the first issuer, since profile order shows preference"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "RDF triples have no order, so \"first\" means nothing."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Uses the issuer whose host matches her WebID's host"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid-OIDC takes the issuers from the profile's `solid:oidcIssuer`; the WebID's domain says nothing about them."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Refuses, because a WebID may trust only one issuer"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A profile may list several; the pod accepts a token from any of them."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "The Node tests of the Solid Notes example app run against Community Solid Server 7. How do they log alice in without a browser?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "With client credentials made through the CSS account API"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The test setup creates an account, a pod and client credentials; `Session.login({ clientId, clientSecret, oidcIssuer })` then gives a `session.fetch` that adds a DPoP-bound access token."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-an-app-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-an-app-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-an-app-2b-d3> .

<#q-read-an-app-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "By sending alice's email and password along with every request"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Pods never see passwords; only the identity provider does."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "By reusing the session cookie from the last manual browser login"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The tests start fresh; that cookie belongs to the identity provider's pages."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "By making the notes container public while the tests run"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The tests log in for real, so access control is exercised too."@en .

<#ch-read-an-app-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-read-an-app> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Finding the storage

Once alice is logged in, the app needs the root of her storage. Step 5.5 gave the rule: use `pim:storage` from the profile if it is there; otherwise walk up from the profile document, one container at a time, until a response carries `Link: <http://www.w3.org/ns/pim/space#Storage>; rel="type"`. A fresh CSS 7.2 profile has no `pim:storage`, so there the walk-up is what works.

```js
// src/notes.js
export async function findStorage(webId, fetch) {
  const { quads } = await readTurtle(webId, fetch);
  const [storage] = objects(quads, webId, `${ns.pim}storage`);
  if (storage) return storage;
  for (let url = new URL('./', webId).href; ; url = new URL('../', url).href) {
    const res = await fetch(url, { method: 'HEAD' });
    if (linksOf(res, 'type', url).includes(`${ns.pim}Storage`)) return url;
    if (new URL(url).pathname === '/') throw new Error(`no storage above ${webId}`);
  }
}
```

`readTurtle` GETs the document with `Accept: text/turtle` and parses it with the `n3` library. `linksOf` reads one relation from the `Link` header, ignoring the relation's case, and resolves it against the URL that was fetched, because some servers send relative targets.

On CSS 7.2, for the WebID `http://localhost:3000/alice40742/profile/card#me`, the walk-up started at `…/alice40742/profile/` (no Storage link) and stopped at `http://localhost:3000/alice40742/`."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-read-an-app-3a> .

<#q-read-an-app-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "On Community Solid Server 7.2 (2026), a fresh WebID profile has no `pim:storage`. How does the Solid Notes example app's `findStorage` find the user's storage?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It HEADs each parent container until one has a `pim:Storage` type link"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Without `pim:storage`, the app walks up from the profile document until a container's Link header says `rel=\"type\"` `pim:Storage`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-an-app-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-an-app-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-an-app-3a-d3> .

<#q-read-an-app-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It reads `/.well-known/solid` at the server root and takes its storage"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "On CSS 7.2's default configuration that names the server root, not alice's pod."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It takes the first path segment of the WebID as the pod's name"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "URL patterns differ between servers; only the type link is reliable."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It asks the identity provider which pod belongs to the WebID"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The identity provider issues tokens; it doesn't know where the data is."@en .

<#ch-read-an-app-4>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-read-an-app> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Finding or creating the type registration

Following Steps 11.3-11.5, `findRegistration` only reads: the profile, the preferences file if the profile links one, and every public and private type index linked from either. The first registration of `schema:NoteDigitalDocument` with a `solid:instanceContainer` wins, even if another app wrote it.

```js
// src/notes.js (findRegistration)
  for (const index of [...indexes(`${ns.solid}publicTypeIndex`), ...indexes(`${ns.solid}privateTypeIndex`)]) {
    const { quads } = await readTurtle(index, fetch);
    for (const q of quads.filter((t) => t.predicate.value === `${ns.solid}forClass` && t.object.value === NOTE)) {
      const [container] = objects(quads, q.subject.value, `${ns.solid}instanceContainer`);
      if (container) return { ...found, container, registration: q.subject.value };
    }
  }
```

A fresh CSS 7.2 pod has neither a type index nor a preferences file. Creating them changes the user's settings, so the app asks first: "Your pod has no private type index. Create one in settings/?" If she agrees, `createRegistration` PUTs `settings/privateTypeIndex.ttl` and `settings/prefs.ttl` with `If-None-Match: *` and links the preferences file from the profile with an insert-only N3 Patch. Both inherit the root's owner-only rule, so the private index is private. An existing private index is only added to. If she says no, notes go unregistered in `notes/`.

The registration is another insert-only patch. It needs an IRI, because CSS 7.2 answers 422 to blank nodes in `solid:inserts` (Step 6.8), and a fragment no other app is likely to pick: an insert into an existing subject would merge with it.

```js
// src/notes.js (createRegistration)
  const index = privateIndex ?? await createPrivateIndex(webId, storage, prefs, fetch);
  const registration = `${index}#reg-notes-${crypto.randomUUID().slice(0, 8)}`;
  await patch(index, n3Patch({
    inserts: `<${registration}> a solid:TypeRegistration; solid:forClass schema:NoteDigitalDocument; solid:instanceContainer <${container}>.`,
  }), fetch);
```

On CSS 7.2 the private index then held:

```turtle
<> a <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#TypeIndex>, <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#UnlistedDocument>.
<#reg-notes-24f500a3> a <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#TypeRegistration>;
    <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#forClass> <http://schema.org/NoteDigitalDocument>;
    <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#instanceContainer> <../notes/>.
```

In another pod, beside another app's `<#notes>` registering `schema:Recipe`, the new `<#reg-notes-…>` left `<#notes>` as it was."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-read-an-app-4a> ,
                         <#q-read-an-app-4b> ,
                         <#q-read-an-app-4c> .

<#q-read-an-app-4a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "When writing to Community Solid Server 7.2 (2026), why does the Solid Notes example app give its type registration a fragment IRI (`<#reg-notes-…>`) instead of a blank node?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "CSS 7.2 answers 422 to blank nodes in `solid:inserts`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Following the Solid Protocol 0.11 rule, CSS 7.2 answers 422 to a blank node in inserts or deletes, so new nodes get IRIs; the fragment resolves against the type index's URL."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-an-app-4a-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-an-app-4a-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-an-app-4a-d3> .

