<#q-read-an-app-4c-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "CSS refuses the patch, because `<#notes>` already exists there"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "An insert-only patch may add to any subject; the server doesn't check meaning."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-4c-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It PUTs a new index that holds only its own registration"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That would delete the other app's registration; the app only inserts."@en .

<#ch-read-an-app-5>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-read-an-app> ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Creating a container and a document

With the container URL from the registration, the app makes sure the container exists, then adds a note. A container is created with PUT on a URL ending in `/`. A second PUT on an existing container gets 409 on CSS (Step 6.5), so the app checks with HEAD first. A note is created with POST to the container: the server picks the URL, the `Slug` header is only a hint, and the app must read `Location` (Step 6.4).

```js
// src/notes.js
export async function createNotesContainer(container, fetch) {
  const head = await fetch(container, { method: 'HEAD' });
  if (head.ok) return container;
  if (head.status !== 404) throw new HttpError('HEAD', container, head);
  ok('PUT', container, await fetch(container, { method: 'PUT', headers: { 'content-type': 'text/turtle' } }));
  return container;
}

export async function createNote(container, text, fetch, slug = 'note') {
  const res = ok('POST', container, await fetch(container, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'content-type': 'text/turtle', slug },
    body: `<#it> a <${NOTE}>; <${ns.schema}text> ${literal(text)}.`,
  }));
  return new URL(res.headers.get('location'), container).href;
}
```

`ok` throws unless the status is 2xx. `literal` is `JSON.stringify(String(text))`: JSON's escapes (`\\"`, `\\\\`, `\\n`, `\\uXXXX`) are also valid Turtle, so text the user types can't break out of the string.

With `Slug: first`, CSS 7.2 answered `Location: http://localhost:3000/alice40742/notes/first`; node-solid-server 6.0.0 answered `…/notes/first.ttl`.

Deleting works the same way as editing (Step 6.11): send the ETag you read in `If-Match`. On CSS 7.2 a DELETE with a stale ETag got 412, the one with the fresh ETag got 205, and a GET afterwards got 404."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-read-an-app-5a> ,
                         <#q-read-an-app-5b> .

<#q-read-an-app-5a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app POSTs a note to a container with `Slug: first`. Why does it read `Location` instead of building the URL from the container and the slug?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The slug is only a hint; the server picks the final URL"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "POST lets the server choose the URL; CSS 7.2 kept the slug exactly and node-solid-server 6 added `.ttl`, so the app always uses `Location`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-an-app-5a-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-an-app-5a-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-an-app-5a-d3> .

<#q-read-an-app-5a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`Location` also tells the app the note's ETag for later edits"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Location carries only the URL; the ETag comes from a GET or HEAD."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-5a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`Location` is needed only on NSS; CSS always keeps the slug"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "CSS kept it here, but no server promises to."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-5a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A slug must be percent-encoded, so the stored name always differs"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A plain slug like `first` is used as is when the server accepts it."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-5b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front """On Community Solid Server 7.2 (2026), a Solid app deletes a note with the function below (`ok` throws unless the status is 2xx). Another user edited the note after `etag` was read. What happens?

```js
export async function deleteNote(url, fetch, etag) {
  const headers = etag ? { 'if-match': etag } : {};
  return ok('DELETE', url, await fetch(url, { method: 'DELETE', headers }));
}
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The server answers 412 and `ok` throws; the note stays"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "In HTTP, `If-Match` makes the DELETE conditional on the version that was read; on CSS 7.2 the stale ETag got 412 and the fresh one deleted the note with 205. A server that ignores `If-Match` (node-solid-server 6 did on PATCH) gives no such guard."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-an-app-5b-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-an-app-5b-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-an-app-5b-d3> .

<#q-read-an-app-5b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The DELETE succeeds, since servers ignore `If-Match` on DELETE"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Conditional headers apply to every method; CSS 7.2 refused it with 412."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-5b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server answers 409 and `ok` throws; the note stays"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A failed precondition is 412; 409 is for a conflicting request body."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-5b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The note is deleted, then the server answers 412 as a warning"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The precondition is checked before anything changes."@en .

<#ch-read-an-app-6>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-read-an-app> ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Reading and rendering safely

`readNotes` GETs the container and follows each `ldp:contains`. It skips anything whose `Content-Type` isn't `text/turtle` (such as pictures), parses each note with the `n3` library, and keeps a document only if it has a subject typed `schema:NoteDigitalDocument`. Parsing Turtle with an RDF library gives the same triples from any server; CSS's JSON-LD comes out expanded, in a shape a plain-JSON reader would have to special-case.

What it reads is **untrusted input** (Step 13.1): any app that can write to the pod could have put HTML or a `javascript:` URL in a note. `renderNote` therefore sets text with `textContent` and only turns `http:`/`https:` values into links.

```js
// src/render.js
export function renderNote(note, document) {
  const li = document.createElement('li');
  const text = document.createElement('span');
  text.textContent = note.text; // never innerHTML: "<img onerror=…>" stays text
  li.append(text);
  if (note.link && /^https?:$/.test(safeProtocol(note.link))) {
    const a = document.createElement('a');
    a.href = note.link;
    a.rel = 'noopener noreferrer';
    a.textContent = new URL(note.link).host;
    li.append(' ', a);
  }
  return li;
}

const safeProtocol = (url) => {
  try { return new URL(url).protocol; } catch { return ''; } // javascript:, data:, junk
};
```

A test wrote a hostile note (`schema:text "<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>"`, `schema:url "javascript:alert(1)"`) and a safe one, and rendered both:

```text
<li><span>&lt;img src=x onerror=alert(1)&gt;</span></li>
<li><span>docs</span> <a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://solidproject.org/">solidproject.org</a></li>
```

No `<img>` element and no link for the hostile note. The values `data:text/html,…`, `not a url`, `JavaScript:alert(1)` and `ftp://example.org/x` gave no link either."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-read-an-app-6a> ,
                         <#q-read-an-app-6b> ,
                         <#q-read-an-app-6c> ,
                         <#q-read-an-app-6d> .

<#q-read-an-app-6a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A note in a Solid pod has the text `<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>`. Which way of showing it in a web app keeps it harmless?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Setting `textContent`, which shows the string without parsing markup"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "`textContent` never parses markup, so the string is shown as text; pod data is untrusted input whoever wrote it."@en ;
