<#q-read-an-app-9b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "In WAC, a note's ACL already exists when its owner shares it with a second friend. How does the Solid Notes example app add the friend?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "With an insert-only N3 Patch adding one authorization"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "An insert-only patch keeps the existing rules, including the owner's Control and the first friend's Read."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-an-app-9b-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-an-app-9b-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-an-app-9b-d3> .

<#q-read-an-app-9b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "With a PUT of a new ACL that lists only the second friend"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That would drop the owner and the first friend from the ACL."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-9b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "With a POST to the ACL, the way messages go to an inbox"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "ACLs are documents edited with PUT or PATCH, not containers."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-9b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "By adding the friend to the pod root's ACL instead"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The root's rules no longer apply once the note has its own ACL."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-9c>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "To unshare a note in WAC, a Solid app sends an N3 Patch whose `solid:deletes` lists the friend's authorization triples. Why does it build those triples from the ACL it has just read?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Deletes must match stored triples exactly, or the patch fails with 409"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "An N3 Patch whose deletes aren't all in the document is refused with 409, so the app copies the triples it read; on CSS 7.2 bob's GET went back to 403 and alice kept 200."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-an-app-9c-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-an-app-9c-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-an-app-9c-d3> .

<#q-read-an-app-9c-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Deletes may name only subjects, so it needs the rule's fragment"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "N3 Patch deletes whole triples, not subjects."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-9c-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Each GET of an ACL renames its rules, so old names are stale"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Servers keep the fragment IRIs that were written."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-9c-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server ignores deletes unless they come with `If-Match`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "If-Match is optional; the exact match of deletes is the check."@en .

<#ch-read-an-app-10>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-read-an-app> ;
    schema:position 9 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Telling the friend: an inbox message

Bob can now read the note, but he doesn't know it exists. `notifyFriend` reads his profile, finds `ldp:inbox` and POSTs an Activity Streams `Announce` there (Step 9.6). The server names the message and returns `Location`.

```js
// src/share.js
  const [inbox] = objects(quads, friend, `${ns.ldp}inbox`);
  if (!inbox) throw new Error(`${friend} has no ldp:inbox`);
  const res = ok('POST', inbox, await fetch(inbox, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'content-type': 'application/ld+json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ '@context': 'https://www.w3.org/ns/activitystreams',
      type: 'Announce', actor, object, target: friend, summary: 'A note was shared with you' }),
  }));
```

CSS 7.2 creates no inbox, so the test setup makes one in bob's pod, with an ACL that gives the public Append and bob Read, Write and Control (with `acl:default`, so it covers the messages too). Anyone can drop a message in; only bob can read them: alice's GET on her own message got 403, bob's 200.

On bob's side, a message is only a claim (Step 9.7). An app can mark it `verified` when its `object` lies in the actor's storage **and** bob's own fetch gets it. On CSS 7.2 a message about a never-shared note and one pointing into a third pod failed that check, but a forged message about alice's public profile passed. To tidy the inbox, copy a message into an archive with `If-None-Match: *`, and only then DELETE it."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-read-an-app-10a> ,
                         <#q-read-an-app-10b> ,
                         <#q-read-an-app-10c> .

<#q-read-an-app-10a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In a Solid pod, bob's inbox gives the public Append and only bob Read. Alice POSTs an `Announce` there and then GETs the message's URL. Why does she get 403?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The inbox gives the public Append only, not Read"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The inbox's ACL lets anyone append (post) but only bob read, so senders can deliver messages without seeing each other's."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-read-an-app-10a-d1> ,
                          <#q-read-an-app-10a-d2> ,
                          <#q-read-an-app-10a-d3> .

<#q-read-an-app-10a-d1>
