    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Carol gets 412: the server makes concurrent patches send `If-Match`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The patches send no If-Match, so there is no precondition to fail."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-r09-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The later patch replaces the list, so one item is lost"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A patch changes only the triples it names, and each item has its own IRI."@en .

<#q-read-an-app-r09-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Both get 403, because changing a document needs Write"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Insert-only patches need only Append; on CSS 7.2 bob's add got 205."@en .

# Chapter 15: Worked example: walking through a real Solid app.
<#ch-app-walkthrough>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 14 ;
    dcterms:title "Worked example: walking through a real Solid app"@en ;
    dcterms:description "See how a production app, Solid Memo, turns the course's rules into code: login, discovery, conditional writes, its vocabulary contract, migrations and testing across servers."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-app-walkthrough-r01> ,
                              <#q-app-walkthrough-r02> ,
                              <#q-app-walkthrough-r03> ,
                              <#q-app-walkthrough-r04> ,
                              <#q-app-walkthrough-r05> .

<#ch-app-walkthrough-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-app-walkthrough> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## What the app does and how it is built

Solid Memo is a flashcard app: you study decks of cards, and a spaced-repetition
algorithm decides when each card comes back. This chapter describes its code as
of October 2026. All its data lives in the user's pod, in one container called
an *instance*. The browser app is built with Preact, `@inrupt/solid-client` and
the Inrupt browser authn library; it validates with `rdf-validate-shacl`, and
uses N3.js only in build tooling and tests.

The code is hexagonal ("ports and adapters"), in npm packages with their own
import rules. The main layers:

- **domain**: pure types and functions (scheduling, migrations), no network calls;
- **application**: one *use case* per user action, and the *ports* it needs,
  as narrow interfaces;
- **solid**: adapters that implement the ports with the Inrupt libraries;
- **vocab**: the data contract, TypeScript *generated* from the Turtle
  vocabulary and SHACL shapes, which the website also publishes for other
  apps, so code and published contract cannot drift apart.

```ts
export interface DeckRepository {      // a port, in the application package
  listDecks(instanceUrl: string): Promise<Deck[]>;
  createDeck(instanceUrl: string, title: LangText): Promise<Deck>;
  // …
}
```

A check fails the build when a package imports across these lines, so only
the adapters know the data lives in a pod. The rest of this chapter shows
where each rule from Chapters 6-12 ends up."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-app-walkthrough-1a> ,
                         <#q-app-walkthrough-1b> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Why does Solid Memo, a Solid flashcard app, generate its TypeScript types from its Turtle vocabulary and SHACL shapes, rather than writing the types by hand?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Other apps read the published RDF, so it must be the single source of truth."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Other apps see only the RDF and shapes in the pod and on the website, so the app derives its code from them; then the code can never quietly disagree with the published contract."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-app-walkthrough-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-1a-d3> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "TypeScript cannot describe RDF data unless a generator writes its types."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Hand-written types work fine; the reason is keeping code and published contract from drifting apart."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Solid servers check each write against the app's types, so they must match."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Servers never see the app's types; they store whatever RDF they are sent."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Generated code is smaller, so the app loads faster in the browser."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Size and speed have nothing to do with it; the point is one source of truth for the contract."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid Memo's hexagonal (\"ports and adapters\") design, where does the code that calls `@inrupt/solid-client` belong?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "In the Solid adapters that implement the application's ports."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Adapters implement the ports the application defines, so the pod library sits at the edge and can be swapped or mocked without touching the domain."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-app-walkthrough-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-1b-d3> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In the domain package, next to the scheduling algorithm."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The domain is kept pure, free of any library or network call, so it can be tested on its own."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In the vocab package, next to the generated constants."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The vocab package is the data contract, not the code that talks to pods."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In the UI components, because they show the decks and cards."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The UI calls only use cases, one per user action; it never calls a pod library directly."@en .

<#ch-app-walkthrough-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-app-walkthrough> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Login

Solid Memo is **WebID-first**. The user types a WebID, and
`SolidSessionGateway`, the adapter behind the `SessionGateway` port, fetches
the profile without logging in and follows `solid:oidcIssuer` (Step 7.9),
never the WebID's origin:

```ts
const issuers = getIriAll(profile, SOLID_OIDC_ISSUER);
const issuer = issuers.find(isSecureUrl);  // https only: the browser goes there next
```

The profile is outside the app's control, so the issuer is checked first:
`isSecureUrl` accepts only an `https:` URL with no user name or password. A
profile that lists only a plain-http issuer gets an error, not a redirect.

Step 12.6's other way in, **issuer-first**, is offered too: a user who does
not want to type a WebID picks a suggested provider, the same flow starts at
that issuer, and the WebID arrives with the session. Both are safe for one
reason: a pod accepts a token only from an issuer that the WebID's own
profile lists.

The Inrupt library then runs the authorization code flow with PKCE. The app
passes only a `clientName`, no `clientId`, so the library registers the client
dynamically. That needs nothing hosted, but gives up what a Client ID Document
(Steps 7.11 and 12.9) guarantees: a published list of the only redirect URIs
that may receive the app's tokens."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-app-walkthrough-2a> ,
                         <#q-app-walkthrough-2b> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "When a user logs in with a WebID, Solid Memo (2026) filters the issuers in the WebID profile with `isSecureUrl` before redirecting. What does that prevent?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Redirecting the login to a plain-http issuer."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Over plain http the login could be read or altered on the network, so the app follows only an `https:` issuer URL without embedded credentials and reports an error otherwise."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-app-walkthrough-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-2a-d3> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Logging in with a WebID whose profile lists more than one issuer."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Several issuers are allowed; the app takes the first `https:` one."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Logging in through an issuer that the WebID's profile does not list."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The issuers come from the profile in the first place; the filter only drops insecure ones."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Getting a token that the pod will later refuse to accept."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Whether a pod accepts a token depends on the profile's issuers, not on this check."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A user skips Solid Memo's WebID field and picks a Solid identity provider from its suggested list. Why can't a rogue provider on that path log in as someone else's WebID?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Pods accept its tokens only if that WebID's profile lists it."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Whichever way login starts, the token names a WebID and an issuer, and the pod checks that the WebID's profile lists that issuer as its `solid:oidcIssuer`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-app-walkthrough-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-2b-d3> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Solid Memo checks every provider against a central Solid registry."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "There is no central registry; trust comes from the user's own profile."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The app rejects any issuer on a different host from the WebID."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Issuers often sit on other hosts; the app never compares origins."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The provider must also host the pod that the user's data is in."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Identity and storage are separate; any issuer the profile lists may log the user in."@en .

<#ch-app-walkthrough-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-app-walkthrough> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## The discovery chain

Finding the data is the chain from Steps 11.3-11.4, in two adapter files:

1. **Storage** (`solidStorageGateway.ts`): `pim:storage` from the profile;
   else `candidateStorageUrls` walks up from the WebID document and
   `hasStorageLink` looks for `Link: <…pim/space#Storage>; rel="type"`; else
   the user types the URL.
2. **Type indexes** (`typeIndex.ts`): links are read from the WebID document,
   from every extended profile (`rdfs:seeAlso`, `foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf`) and,
   for the private index, from `pim:preferencesFile`. The first link found
   for each index is used. Inrupt PodSpaces, for one, keeps a read-only WebID
   document and a writable profile in the pod.
3. **Registration**: `solid:forClass sm:Instance`, accepting both
   `solid:instanceContainer` and `solid:instance`.

Every one of these reads, the WebID document's too, goes through the app's
authenticated `fetch`: a plain `fetch` would get a 401 on a private extended
profile or preferences file, and the link in it would look missing."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-app-walkthrough-3a> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Why does Solid Memo (2026) look for type-index links in the WebID document, the extended profiles and the preferences file, instead of only one of them?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Real profiles put these links in different documents, so one place could miss them."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "In practice the links appear in the WebID document, an extended profile or the preferences file, so a link counts as missing only after all of them are read."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-app-walkthrough-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-3a-d3> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The three documents each hold a copy of the index, so the copies must be merged."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "They hold links to the index documents, not copies of the index."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Each document may name a different index, so the app must check that they agree."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The app takes the first link it finds for each index; the search is about finding a link at all."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Reading three documents lets the app avoid asking for any permissions."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Where a link is found has nothing to do with what the app may read."@en .

<#ch-app-walkthrough-4>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-app-walkthrough> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## When the type index is missing

On Community Solid Server pods a missing type index is **normal**, so Step
11.5's advice (offer to create one) runs on most first logins. Solid Memo asks
the user and defaults to a **private** index under `<storage>settings/`, for
the privacy reason Step 11.5 gives; registering publicly is the other choice.

Real pods force two more choices:

- **Where the link goes.** Solid Memo writes `solid:privateTypeIndex` (or
  `solid:publicTypeIndex`) straight onto the WebID: into the WebID document
  if it accepts the write, else into the first extended profile that does. On
  PodSpaces the WebID document is read-only. It never creates a preferences
  file. That differs from the pattern of Steps 11.4-11.5: it is simpler and
  works on pods with no preferences file, but profile documents are normally
  public, so the private index's *location* becomes public. The index and the
  data stay private.
- **No fallback.** If registration fails, creating the instance fails loudly.
  A registration kept in browser storage would "work" on this device, but
  another device or app could never find the data."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-app-walkthrough-4a> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-4a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A user logs in to Solid Memo (2026) with a fresh Community Solid Server pod that has no type index. What does Solid Memo do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Asks the user, then creates and links a private index by default."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Discovery only works if the registration is in the pod, so the app creates the missing index with the user's consent (private unless they choose public) and links it from a writable profile document."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-app-walkthrough-4a-d1> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-4a-d2> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-4a-d3> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-4a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Keeps its registration in the browser's local storage until an index exists."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "There is no local fallback; another device would never find the data."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-4a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Reports the pod as broken, because every Solid pod must have an index."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A missing index is normal on these pods, not an error."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-4a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Stores the data at a fixed path, since a registration is optional."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Without a registration, other apps and devices cannot discover the data."@en .

<#ch-app-walkthrough-5>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-app-walkthrough> ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## A deliberate interop bridge

Solid Memo itself reads only its `sm:Instance` registrations. Inside the
instance it finds the catalogue at a fixed name, `catalog.ttl`, part of its
own layout. But an app that has never heard of Solid Memo would not look for
`sm:Instance`. So each instance also registers a standard class: its
catalogue is a `dcat:Catalog`, and that registration's `solid:instance` names
the catalogue itself. Solid Memo adds, switches and removes this registration
but never reads it: it is there for other apps.

```turtle
<#sm-inst-9f3c1a> a solid:TypeRegistration ;
    solid:forClass sm:Instance ;
    solid:instanceContainer <https://pod.example/solid-memo/main/> .

<#sm-cat-4b7d2e> a solid:TypeRegistration ;
    solid:forClass dcat:Catalog ;
    solid:instance <https://pod.example/solid-memo/main/catalog.ttl#catalog> .
```

DCAT is a W3C Recommendation for describing catalogues of datasets. In the
catalogue each deck is both an `sm:Deck` and a `dcat:Dataset`, and its cards
document is linked twice: by `sm:cardsDocument`, which Solid Memo follows, and
by `dcat:distribution` to a node whose `dcat:accessURL` DCAT apps follow. Any
DCAT-aware app can list the user's decks, with titles, descriptions and
licences, knowing nothing about spaced repetition."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-app-walkthrough-5a> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-5a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Why does Solid Memo (2026) register `dcat:Catalog` in the user's type index next to its own `sm:Instance`?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "So apps that know DCAT but not Solid Memo can find the decks."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A shared, standard class is a meeting point: apps that will never know `sm:` can still discover and read the decks as DCAT datasets."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-app-walkthrough-5a-d1> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-5a-d2> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-5a-d3> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-5a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "So the catalogue gets its own class, since a class allows one registration."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A class may have many registrations; the second class is about which apps recognise it."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-5a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "So the catalogue becomes readable by everyone who finds it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A type registration says where data is; it grants no access."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-5a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "So the app meets the Solid Protocol's rule that apps publish a catalogue."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The Protocol prescribes no app data at all; this is Solid Memo's own choice."@en .

<#ch-app-walkthrough-6>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-app-walkthrough> ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## The pod layout and the digest

The instance applies the layout rules of Steps 11.9-11.10:

```text
<storage>solid-memo/<name>/
├── meta.ttl               sm:Instance: describes the container itself
├── preferences.ttl        sm:Preferences
├── catalog.ttl            dcat:Catalog + one sm:Deck/dcat:Dataset per deck
├── decks/<id>.ttl         cards
├── reviews/<id>.ttl       review state
├── history/<YYYY-MM>.ttl  answer log
└── digest.ttl             derived cache, keyed by ETags
```

What is new is the **digest**: facts per document (such as how many cards are
due), each stamped with the ETag it was learned from. The ports return a
`Since<T>`, for example `readCardsSince(deck, version)`:

```ts
type Since<T> = { unchanged: true }
              | { unchanged: false; value: T; version: string | null };
```

The adapter asks with `If-None-Match: <stamp>`. A `304` becomes
`unchanged: true`, and the fact still holds; a `200` brings the document, and
the fact is recomputed from it and the digest repaired. So a stale digest is
never wrong, only slow. `version` is `null` when the pod sends no ETag: then
nothing can be stamped, and the next visit reads the document again (Step
15.13)."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-app-walkthrough-6a> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-6a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Solid Memo's digest cache says a deck's cards document was valid at ETag `\"a1\"`. Since then the user edited the deck on another device. What happens on the next visit?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The pod answers 200, not 304, so the app rereads the deck and repairs the digest."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The app asks for the document with `If-None-Match: \"a1\"`; a 200 instead of a 304 means it changed, so it is read and checked in full."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-app-walkthrough-6a-d1> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-6a-d2> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-6a-d3> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-6a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The app trusts the digest and shows outdated counts until the digest expires."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Every fact is used only after the pod confirms the stamped version is still current."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-6a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The pod server updates `digest.ttl` itself, because it saw that the deck changed."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The server knows nothing of the digest; the app repairs it."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-6a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The app deletes the deck, because its digest entry no longer matches the pod."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A stale digest only means more reading; the data itself is never touched."@en .

<#ch-app-walkthrough-7>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-app-walkthrough> ;
    schema:position 6 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Conditional delta writes

Every write goes through `saveDataset` in `datasets.ts`, which applies Step
6.11's read, modify, save:

- an **edit** of a document read earlier is a PATCH of the delta, with
  `If-Match: <the ETag it was read with>`;
- a **creation** sends `If-None-Match: *`;
- a **412** becomes a `PreconditionFailedError` naming the document.

```http
PATCH /solid-memo/main/decks/d1.ttl HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/sparql-update
If-Match: "v7"

DELETE DATA { <#card-1> sm:back "Hund" . };
INSERT DATA { <#card-1> sm:back "der Hund" . };
```

(The real body spells every IRI out in full.) The notes app of Step 14.7 sends
the same kind of delta as **N3 Patch**, the one format servers MUST accept (Step
6.7). Solid Memo sends **SPARQL Update** (Step 6.9), which most servers accept
but none must.

The type index of Step 15.4 is saved the same way. Other apps' registrations
are not in the delta, so they are kept; and if the index changed since it was
read, the save gets a 412 instead of an edit based on a stale read. That is
stricter than Step 11.5 requires of an insert-only patch."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-app-walkthrough-7a> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-7a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Why does Solid Memo save an edit to a pod document as a PATCH of the delta rather than a PUT of the document rebuilt from its own deck model?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Triples added by other apps are not in the delta, so they stay."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Rebuilding a document from the app's own model would silently delete what other apps wrote, while sending only the change keeps their data. Solid Memo's PUT for very large edits is different: it starts from the document as read."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-app-walkthrough-7a-d1> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-7a-d2> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-7a-d3> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-7a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Solid servers do not allow PUT on documents that already exist."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "PUT may replace an existing document; it would just drop foreign triples."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-7a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A PATCH needs no access rights, while a PUT needs Write access."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Both need rights: an insert-only patch needs Append; one that deletes needs Read and Write."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-7a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A PATCH is atomic across documents, so a failed save leaves nothing half-written."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A PATCH is atomic for one document only; there are no multi-document transactions."@en .

<#ch-app-walkthrough-8>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-app-walkthrough> ;
    schema:position 7 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## What to do on a 412

A 412 means nothing was written. What happens next depends on the data:

- **A user's edit** is not retried. Reapplied to the new version, it could
  undo a change the user never saw, so the app says the data changed
  elsewhere and the user reloads.
- **Data the app can merge mechanically** (the digest, deck groups) is
  reread, changed again and saved, up to a few attempts.

The notes app of Step 14.7 does retry a user's edit, but only through a check:
it refetches and hands the fresh text to the edit's change function, which
reapplies the edit if the text is still what the user saw and otherwise asks
her. A note is one string, so that comparison is easy. Solid Memo cannot know
whether a card edit clashes with an unseen change, so it shows the user the
new version first.

One write needs no precondition at all. Each answer is added to
`history/<YYYY-MM>.ttl` with an insert-only PATCH, without reading the
document and without `If-Match`: each answer is a new subject, so no read is
needed."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-app-walkthrough-8a> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-8a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In one Solid Memo session (2026), a write to the digest cache and a user's card edit both get `412 Precondition Failed`. What does Solid Memo do with each?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Rereads and retries the digest; asks the user to reload for the edit."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The app retries only what it can merge without judgement, derived or structural data such as the digest and deck groups; a user's edit on top of an unseen change needs the user."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-app-walkthrough-8a-d1> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-8a-d2> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-8a-d3> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-8a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Rereads and retries both, since a 412 means nothing was written."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Reapplying a user's edit could overwrite a change the user has never seen."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-8a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Resends both without `If-Match`, so that the writes go through."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That overwrites the other change: the lost update the header exists to prevent."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-8a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Asks the user to reload for both, since a 412 always needs a person."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The digest is derived data, so the app can merge it mechanically and retry."@en .

<#ch-app-walkthrough-9>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-app-walkthrough> ;
    schema:position 8 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## The vocabulary contract in code

Solid Memo mints its terms under `https://solid-memo.com/ns/vocab/v1.ttl#`,
reuses DCAT, Dublin Core, FOAF, PROV and SKOS where they fit, and evolves the
vocabulary by the rules of Steps 4.12 and 11.11. What matters here is how
those rules reach the code.

`npm run generate` turns `ns/vocab/v1.ttl` into `vocab.generated.ts`, and the
check fails if that file differs from what the Turtle would generate. Each
term's `rdfs:comment` becomes its doc comment, and `owl:deprecated` becomes a
`@deprecated` tag, so the editor strikes through old terms:

```ts
/** How the deck is studied: "front-to-back", … @deprecated Use studyDirection. */
direction: `${SM_NS}direction`,
/** How the deck is studied: a concept of solid-memo:StudyDirections. … */
studyDirection: `${SM_NS}studyDirection`,
```

The new property's values are SKOS concepts such as `sm:frontToBack`, not
strings, and the generated record type allows only the three concept IRIs. A
typo becomes a compile error instead of bad data in someone's pod."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-app-walkthrough-9a> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-9a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Solid Memo's Turtle vocabulary marks `sm:direction` as `owl:deprecated`. How does a Solid Memo developer find out before using it in new code?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The generated constant carries a `@deprecated` tag the editor flags."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The generator copies `owl:deprecated` into a `@deprecated` doc tag, so the vocabulary's own deprecation shows up as a warning in the editor."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-app-walkthrough-9a-d1> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-9a-d2> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-9a-d3> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-9a-d1>
