<#q-app-walkthrough-13b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It loses other apps' triples, because a PUT replaces the whole document."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It replaces the document with the one it read plus the change, so their triples are in it."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-13b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It keeps everything: if the deck changed since the read, the server answers 412."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Nothing is checked: the PUT has no `If-Match`, and the server ignores one anyway."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Which path matches how Solid Memo, a Solid flashcard app, in 2026 gets from a user's WebID to a deck's cards?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "WebID profile → type index (`sm:Instance`) → instance container → `catalog.ttl` → cards document."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The profile links the type index, whose `sm:Instance` registration names the instance container, where `catalog.ttl` sits at a fixed name and names each deck's cards document. A DCAT-only app takes the `dcat:Catalog` registration and `dcat:accessURL` instead."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-app-walkthrough-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-r01-d3> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "WebID profile → storage root → fixed folder `solid-memo/main/` → `decks/` → the deck's file."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The instance is found through the type index, never by guessing its folder; only inside it does the app rely on its own layout."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "WebID profile → identity provider (`solid:oidcIssuer`) → the provider's list of the user's decks."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The identity provider only logs the user in; it knows nothing about the pod's data."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "WebID profile → storage root → `.acl` → the root's access rules → the cards they name."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "ACLs say who may access what; they are not used to find data."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In 2026, Solid Memo adds each answer to a monthly history log in the pod with an insert-only PATCH, without reading the log first and without `If-Match`. Why is that safe when its other edits need `If-Match`?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Each answer adds a new subject, so it overwrites nothing."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A lost update happens when a write replaces or deletes something someone else changed; an insert of a new subject touches nothing existing."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-app-walkthrough-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-r02-d3> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "History documents are private, so no other app can write to them."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Privacy is not the reason; the safety comes from only adding a fresh subject."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server locks the log document while the PATCH is applied."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid has no locks; the insert is safe because it removes nothing."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The app rewrites the whole month each time, so nothing is lost."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The opposite: it sends only the new answer, never the whole document."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-r03>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "After a 412 on a user's edit, a Solid notes app whose notes are one string refetches the note and retries instead of asking the user to reload. What condition makes that retry safe?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It reapplies the edit only if the fresh text is what the user saw, else asks her."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A reapply is safe only when the edit cannot clash with what changed; a note is one string, so that is easy to check, while Solid Memo cannot tell whether a card edit clashes with an unseen change."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-app-walkthrough-r03-d1> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-r03-d2> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-r03-d3> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-r03-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It sends an N3 Patch, which can safely be retried, unlike a SPARQL Update."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Both are deltas; the body format says nothing about whether reapplying is safe."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-r03-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It runs on CSS, which allows a retry, unlike node-solid-server."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A 412 means the same on every server, and node-solid-server ignores `If-Match` anyway."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-r03-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Its notes are smaller than 64 KiB, and only small documents may be retried."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "In Solid Memo the 64 KiB limit decides between PATCH and PUT, not whether to retry."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-r04>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A DCAT-only app wants to list Alice's Solid Memo deck titles. Solid Memo created her private type index and linked it from her WebID profile. What does the app read, and what must Alice have granted it?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Profile, type index, then `catalog.ttl`, with Read on the private ones."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The `dcat:Catalog` registration in the index leads to the catalogue, where each deck is a `dcat:Dataset` with its title; had the index link been in a preferences file, the app would need Read on that too."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-app-walkthrough-r04-d1> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-r04-d2> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-r04-d3> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-r04-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Her profile, then every deck's cards document, with Write on each one."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Titles live only in the catalogue, and listing needs only Read."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-r04-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only the instance's `meta.ttl`, because it is public by default."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`meta.ttl` describes the instance, not the decks, and nothing is public by default."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-r04-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Solid Memo's `sm:` vocabulary, then the decks, with no grants needed."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Titles are `dcterms:title` on `dcat:Dataset`s, so no `sm:` knowledge is needed, and private data needs Read."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-r05>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Another app saved the URL of Alice's Solid Memo instance container last month. Since then she ran Solid Memo's bulk upgrade, which copies the instance to a new container. How should that app find the instance now?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Look it up again through the type index registration."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The type index is the one place that says where the current instance lives, so a stored URL is only a cache to re-check."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-app-walkthrough-r05-d1> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-r05-d2> ,
                          <#q-app-walkthrough-r05-d3> .

<#q-app-walkthrough-r05-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Follow the HTTP redirect from the old container to the new one."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "No redirect is set up; the old container stays in place as a backup."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-r05-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "List the storage root and pick the newest `solid-memo` container."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That guesses from names and dates; only the registration says which copy is current."@en .

<#q-app-walkthrough-r05-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Keep using the saved URL, because container URLs never change."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The upgrade moves the instance to a new container and keeps the old one as a backup."@en .

# Chapter 16: Difficulties and open problems.
<#ch-difficulties>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 15 ;
    dcterms:title "Difficulties and open problems"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Judge Solid honestly: which problems come from its design, which from immaturity, and how app builders cope."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-difficulties-r01> ,
                              <#q-difficulties-r02> ,
                              <#q-difficulties-r03> ,
                              <#q-difficulties-r04> ,
                              <#q-difficulties-r05> .

<#ch-difficulties-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-difficulties> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Spec maturity

The Solid specs are published by the W3C Solid Community Group (CG). A Community Group can publish reports without review by W3C members, and those reports carry no W3C endorsement. Only a Working Group, such as the Linked Web Storage (LWS) Working Group, produces Recommendations, with wide review and two interoperable implementations per feature.

As of October 2026, no Solid spec is a Recommendation:

| Spec | Latest published version | Newer draft |
|---|---|---|
| Solid Protocol | 0.11.0 (May 2024), CG report | Editor's draft 0.12.0 (September 2026) |
| Solid-OIDC | 0.1.0 (March 2022) | 2026 draft: adds RFC 9207 `iss` check, client credentials |
| Access Control Policy | 0.9.0 (May 2022), Editor's Draft | Editor's draft (September 2022) |
| SAI | 0.1.0, still in the CG's index | v0.2 (September 2026) |
| Type Indexes | Editor's Draft 1.0.0 (March 2023) | — |

Versions and drafts drift apart; servers may implement either. Before relying on a rule, check which version your target servers implement. A draft's date is usually just when it was last built, not when its rules changed.

**Design or immaturity?** Immaturity: drafts can still change and become Recommendations."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-difficulties-1a> ,
                         <#q-difficulties-1b> .

<#q-difficulties-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "What does it mean that the Solid Protocol (version 0.11, 2024) is a W3C Community Group report?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It was published without W3C member review and carries no W3C endorsement"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Community Groups can publish freely, but only a Working Group produces Recommendations with wide review and an implementation requirement."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-1a-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It is a W3C Recommendation that is waiting for its final version number"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Recommendations come only from Working Groups; a CG report is not on that track at all."@en .

<#q-difficulties-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It is an IETF RFC that the W3C has adopted as one of its standards"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The Solid specs are W3C Community Group documents, not IETF RFCs."@en .

<#q-difficulties-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It is a stable standard that only Inrupt's servers are required to follow"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It is neither stable nor tied to one vendor; any server may implement it."@en .

<#q-difficulties-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A rule appears in the 2026 Solid-OIDC draft but not in the published version 0.1.0 (2022). What should you check before your Solid app depends on it?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Whether your target servers implement that draft or 0.1.0"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Drafts move ahead of the published versions, and servers may implement either. A draft's date is usually just when it was last built."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-1b-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Whether the W3C has approved the draft as a Recommendation"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "No Solid spec is on the Recommendation track, so this check tells you nothing."@en .

<#q-difficulties-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Whether the draft's GitHub repository is still active"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "An active repository says nothing about which version servers implement."@en .

<#q-difficulties-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Whether the rule also appears in the WAC specification"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "WAC covers authorization, not login, so it would not repeat Solid-OIDC rules."@en .

<#ch-difficulties-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-difficulties> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Fragmentation: two of everything

Several areas have two competing answers:

| Problem | Option A | Option B |
|---|---|---|
| Authorization | WAC | ACP |
| Data discovery | Type Indexes | SAI + Shape Trees |
| Patch format | N3 Patch (required by Protocol 0.11) | SPARQL Update (not in Protocol 0.11) |
| Live updates | Solid Notifications channels | older `solid-0.1` / `solid-0.2` WebSockets |

Solid Protocol 0.11 says "Servers MUST conform to either or both" WAC and ACP, so a portable client handles both. ESS (Inrupt's Enterprise Solid Server) uses ACP, and Inrupt does not support running it with WAC in production. SAI lacks server support, so developers use Type Indexes. Protocol 0.11 omits SPARQL Update, yet most servers still accept it. Node Solid Server 6.0.0 (2026) still offers `solid-0.1` WebSockets; ESS 3.0 dropped `solid-0.2` in May 2026.

Detect the option from server headers: the `Accept-Patch` header for the patch format; for ACP, a `rel="type"` link to `acp:AccessControlResource` on the resource that `rel="acl"` points to.

Vendor extensions add more: Inrupt's Access Grants need ESS's Access Grant Service.

**Design or immaturity?** Mostly immaturity: competing drafts and old servers. The WAC-or-ACP choice is in the Protocol itself."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-difficulties-2a> ,
                         <#q-difficulties-2b> .

<#q-difficulties-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Under the Solid Protocol (0.11, 2024), why must a portable Solid client understand both WAC and ACP?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A conforming server may implement only one of them"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Because servers may choose either language, a client that speaks only one cannot manage sharing on some conforming servers."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-2a-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Every server uses WAC for containers and ACP for documents"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "No such split exists; a server picks WAC, ACP or both for everything."@en .

<#q-difficulties-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "ACP is just a newer syntax for writing the same WAC files"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "ACP is a different model, with matchers and deny rules, in a different document."@en .

<#q-difficulties-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The Solid Protocol forbids a server from implementing both"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The Protocol says \"either or both\", so a server may support both."@en .

<#q-difficulties-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app shares data using Inrupt's Access Grants. Where will that sharing feature work?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Only on servers that run an Access Grant Service, such as ESS"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Vendor extensions need the vendor's service; a feature that relies on them is not portable Solid."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-2b-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "On every server, because Access Grants are part of the Solid Protocol"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Access Grants are an Inrupt extension, not a requirement of the Protocol."@en .

<#q-difficulties-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "On every server that supports WAC, which grants are built on"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Grants are verifiable credentials checked by ESS's own services, not WAC rules."@en .

<#q-difficulties-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only on servers that support SAI's Authorization Agent"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "SAI is a separate draft with its own grant model, not Inrupt's service."@en .

<#ch-difficulties-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-difficulties> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Servers disagree

Optional rules and servers of different ages give one request different results. Tests in October 2026 recorded:

- JSS 0.0.220: a SPARQL Update insert drops a literal's language tag, leaving a document that no longer parses; `DELETE DATA` deletes nothing yet answers `204`; Turtle ACLs get `415`; concurrent PATCHes get `409`.
- Solid-Nextcloud 0.11 (Nextcloud 32): `DELETE DATA` removes all values of that subject's predicate, not just the named triple; every document's `rel="acl"` link names the folder's ACL; `HEAD` answers `204` for a missing resource; a PATCH gets through a Read-only ACL; concurrent PATCHes get `500`.

So "works on my pod" is not "works on Solid": test on several servers and read results back.

Hosts add limits. solidcommunity.net has run Pivot, built on CSS, since January 2025, using CSS's pod quota: 70 MB per pod by default. On CSS 7.2 (2026), a PUT or PATCH past the quota got `413` "Quota exceeded" and kept nothing. Pivot reuses this code, so expect the same. The LWS Working Draft (October 2026) uses `507 Insufficient Storage` instead. Treat any non-2xx write as failed and tell the user.

**Design or immaturity?** Immaturity: optional rules and uneven implementations, not a limit of the model."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-difficulties-3a> ,
                         <#q-difficulties-3b> ,
                         <#q-difficulties-3c> .

<#q-difficulties-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app passes every test against Community Solid Server (CSS). What can its developer conclude?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It works on that server version, and other servers may still break it"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Servers differ in real, recorded ways, so only testing on several of them tells you how portable an app is."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-3a-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It works on every Solid server, because CSS is the reference implementation"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Recorded deviations show other servers answer the same requests differently."@en .

<#q-difficulties-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It conforms to the Solid Protocol, because CSS rejects any invalid request"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Passing on one server shows nothing about requests other servers handle differently."@en .

<#q-difficulties-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It works everywhere, because Solid servers must pass a conformance test suite"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "No such requirement exists, and recorded servers fail requests that CSS accepts."@en .

<#q-difficulties-3b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid server answers `204` to an app's SPARQL Update `DELETE DATA` request. What is the safest assumption?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The request was accepted, so re-read the document if you must be sure"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A success code tells you what the server claims; on servers with known deviations, reading the result back is the only proof."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-3b-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-3b-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-3b-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-3b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The triples are certainly gone, because `204` means the change was applied"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "One recorded server (JSS 0.0.220, 2026) answers `204` while deleting nothing."@en .

<#q-difficulties-3b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server rejected the patch, because a delete should return `200`"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`204 No Content` is a normal success code for a write."@en .

<#q-difficulties-3b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server deleted the whole document, because `204` means no content is left"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`204` only means the response has no body, not that the resource is empty."@en .

<#q-difficulties-3c>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "On solidcommunity.net (Pivot, built on CSS 7.2, 2026), a Solid app's PUT gets `413` because the pod's quota is exceeded. What should the app do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Treat the save as failed and tell the user the pod is full"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "On CSS-based hosts such as Pivot, a `413` whose body says \"Quota exceeded\" means nothing was stored; the user must free space first. A `413` can also mean one request was too large, so read the body."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-3c-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-3c-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-3c-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-3c-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Retry the same PUT until the server accepts it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A refused write keeps nothing, and it fails again until the user frees some space."@en .

<#q-difficulties-3c-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Assume part of the file was saved and write only the rest"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "On CSS 7.2, none of a write refused for quota is kept."@en .

<#q-difficulties-3c-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Switch to a PATCH request to get around the size limit"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "On CSS 7.2 the quota counts the whole pod, so a PATCH past it also gets `413`."@en .

<#ch-difficulties-4>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-difficulties> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Querying and performance

The Solid Protocol defines reading, writing, containers, auth and notifications. It defines no query. A container listing tells you where resources are, not what is in them, so reading N items costs one listing plus N reads. This is called the **N+1** pattern:

```http
GET /notes/            -> ldp:contains <n1.ttl>, <n2.ttl>, …
GET /notes/n1.ttl
GET /notes/n2.ttl
```

Solid Protocol 0.11 defines no paging either, so a client cannot ask for a page; expect the whole listing in one response. The LWS drafts add paging.

App builders cope with design patterns, not spec features: put many items in one document, keep a summary document, and cache with ETags (send `If-None-Match`; a `304` means your copy is current). Bigger documents mean more write conflicts. Research such as link traversal querying answers simple queries "in the order of seconds" and is not shipped server behaviour.

**Design or immaturity?** Design: the protocol serves documents and has no query or paging."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-difficulties-4a> ,
                         <#q-difficulties-4b> .

<#q-difficulties-4a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app lists a container of 200 notes and needs the title of each. Why is this slow?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The listing gives only URLs, so the app makes 200 more requests"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Containment says where resources are, not what they hold, which creates the N+1 fetch pattern."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-4a-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-4a-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-4a-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-4a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The server runs a SPARQL query for each note before replying"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid servers have no query in the protocol; they simply serve documents."@en .

<#q-difficulties-4a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The listing includes every note's full content, which is large"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`ldp:contains` lists members, not their contents."@en .

<#q-difficulties-4a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Each note must be validated against a SHACL shape on the server"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Servers do not validate shapes; validation, if any, is the app's job."@en .

<#q-difficulties-4b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Which is a common way for a Solid app to cut the number of requests it makes to a pod?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Keep a summary document that the app updates itself"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Summary documents and larger documents cut the number of requests; ETag caching cuts what each request downloads."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-4b-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-4b-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-4b-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-4b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Send a SPARQL query to the pod's built-in query endpoint"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The Solid Protocol defines no query endpoint for pods."@en .

<#q-difficulties-4b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Ask the server to inline document contents into container listings"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid listings carry membership and some metadata, never full contents."@en .

<#q-difficulties-4b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Use link traversal querying, which answers in milliseconds"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Research reports seconds even for simple queries, and it still fetches documents."@en .

<#ch-difficulties-5>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-difficulties> ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Consistency

The Solid Protocol defines no cross-document transactions or locks, so two apps, or two tabs, can edit the same document at once.

Protection is opt-in on both sides. Servers only MAY send a strong `ETag` "to encourage clients to opt-in to using the `If-Match` header". A client that never sends `If-Match` can silently overwrite someone else's change. NSS 6.0.0 (tested 2026) sends no ETag for a document returned as stored, and ignores `If-Match`.

A PATCH is atomic per document: HTTP requires the server to "apply the entire set of changes atomically". Anything spanning documents is not. On CSS 7.2 (October 2026), an app tagged one item in two documents. The second PATCH sent `If-Match: "stale"`, standing in for an ETag read before another app changed `first.ttl`:

```text
PATCH /alice/notes/deck-b.ttl                    -> 205 Reset Content
PATCH /alice/notes/first.ttl  If-Match: "stale"  -> 412 Precondition Failed
GET   /alice/notes/deck-b.ttl                    -> 200 OK
```

```turtle
<#it> <http://schema.org/text> "Deck B";
    <http://schema.org/keywords> "archived".
```

Nothing rolled back the first write: `deck-b.ttl` is tagged and `first.ttl` is not. A move (insert here, delete there) fails the same way, leaving the item in both places. The app must notice and repair it: retry, undo the first write, or order writes so a half-done state is harmless (data first, index last).

**Design or immaturity?** Design: no cross-document transactions. The optional ETags are a softer, spec-level choice."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-difficulties-5a> ,
                         <#q-difficulties-5b> .

<#q-difficulties-5a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app writes to two documents in a pod, and the second write fails. What does the pod do about the first write?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Nothing; the first write stays and the app must repair the state"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "PATCH is atomic only per document, so a multi-document operation can be left half done and the app must handle it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-5a-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-5a-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-5a-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-5a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It rolls back the first write, because PATCHes run in a transaction"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid has no transactions across documents; each PATCH stands alone."@en .

<#q-difficulties-5a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It retries the second write until that one succeeds"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Servers do not retry client writes; that is the client's decision."@en .

<#q-difficulties-5a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It locks both documents until the app sends a commit request"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid defines no locks and no commit operation."@en .

<#q-difficulties-5b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Why can't a Solid app count on lost-update protection on every Solid server?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Servers needn't send ETags, and clients needn't send `If-Match`"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Conditional writes need the server to send ETags (optional in Solid Protocol 0.11) and the client to send `If-Match`. Not all servers enforce it: NSS 6.0.0 (tested 2026) ignores `If-Match`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-5b-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-5b-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-5b-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-5b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "HTTP has no way to make a write depend on the current version"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`If-Match` and `412` do exactly that, when both sides use them."@en .

<#q-difficulties-5b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "PATCH bodies cannot carry an `If-Match` header with them"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`If-Match` is a request header, not part of the body, and works with PATCH."@en .

<#q-difficulties-5b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "ETags are only defined for containers, not for documents"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "ETags apply to any resource a server chooses to version."@en .

<#ch-difficulties-6>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-difficulties> ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Identity and onboarding UX

A Solid app must never guess the user's issuer: it reads `solid:oidcIssuer` from the profile or lets the user pick a provider. The cost: before login, the user must know, and often type, a WebID or a provider. Most people have never needed to.

Issuers carry a lot of trust. The 2026 Solid-OIDC draft adds an "Issuer Trust" section: an identity provider can impersonate you, an outage at it locks you out, and a big shared provider is a single point that many users depend on. Listing several issuers adds redundancy, but each provider you trust is one more place an attacker can break in.

People struggle too. A 2024 study by Theys et al. (10 participants, a job application scenario) found the problems came from users' mental models, from fears and doubts eased by brand trust, and from "a challenge of explainability rather than usability". The lesson for app builders: explain what a pod is and what the app will do with it, not only where to click.

**Design or immaturity?** Mostly design: users choose their own provider, so someone has to explain the choice."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-difficulties-6a> ,
                         <#q-difficulties-6b> .

<#q-difficulties-6a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Testers move through a Solid app's login screen easily but drop out at \"Enter your WebID\". What does Theys et al.'s 2024 study of Solid users suggest changing?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Explain what a pod is and what the app will do with it"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Theys et al. found \"a challenge of explainability rather than usability\", so the fix is explaining pods, not polishing screens."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-6a-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-6a-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-6a-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-6a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Add a faster issuer lookup so the step takes less time"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The testers were not slowed down; the study found understanding, not speed, was the problem."@en .

<#q-difficulties-6a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Remove the WebID field and guess the provider from the email"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "An email domain says nothing about the identity provider; offer a provider list or ask for the WebID."@en .

<#q-difficulties-6a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Hide the consent screen so there are fewer clicks to make"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Fewer clicks do not help people who do not understand what they are agreeing to."@en .

<#q-difficulties-6b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Why is a large shared identity provider a risk in Solid?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It can impersonate its users, and its outage locks them all out"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The 2026 Solid-OIDC draft's Issuer Trust section names impersonation, outages and concentration as the risks of shared providers."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-6b-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-6b-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-6b-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-6b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It can read every pod's data without any access rule allowing it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It can act only as its own users, with their access; other people's data stays closed to it."@en .

<#q-difficulties-6b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It forces users to store their pods with the same company"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The issuer, the WebID host and the storage can all be different."@en .

<#q-difficulties-6b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It makes DPoP proofs invalid for servers on other domains"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "DPoP proofs are bound to the request URL and key, not the provider's domain."@en .

<#ch-difficulties-7>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-difficulties> ;
    schema:position 6 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Privacy pitfalls

Privacy in a pod depends on more than its access rules.

- **The public type index leaks categories.** The Type Indexes draft flags "the visibility of different data types in the Public Type Index". The data may be private, but the registration is not:

  ```turtle
  @prefix solid: <http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#> .

  # In a PUBLIC type index
  <#reg1> a solid:TypeRegistration ;
    solid:forClass <https://example.org/health#MedicationLog> ;
    solid:instanceContainer </private/meds/> .
  ```

  Register in the private index unless others must find the data.
- **Paths and metadata leak.** A path like `/private/divorce-2026/` tells a story, and listings can carry each member's `stat:size` and `dcterms:modified`.
- **Access is per whole resource.** You cannot share one triple, and Leigh Dodds warns of users "accidentally sharing sensitive information because they don't realise the extent of what they're sharing". Split documents along sharing lines.
- **Apps usually inherit the user's access.** WAC's only app limit, `acl:origin`, relies on the browser's `Origin` header, is easy to forge outside a browser and is often unused. So an app usually acts with all of the user's access.

**Design or immaturity?** Design: public indexes, readable paths and whole-document access follow from how pods are built. Weak app limits are partly immaturity: ACP client matchers exist, but not everywhere."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-difficulties-7a> ,
                         <#q-difficulties-7b> .

<#q-difficulties-7a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid user's medication log is in a private container, but registered in the public type index. What can a stranger learn?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "That the user keeps that kind of data, and where"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The public type index reveals which kinds of data exist and where, even when the data itself is private."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-7a-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-7a-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-7a-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-7a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing, because the container's ACL hides its registration"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The registration lives in the public index, which the container's ACL does not protect."@en .

<#q-difficulties-7a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The full contents of the log, because the index copies them"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A type index holds registrations only, never the data itself."@en .

<#q-difficulties-7a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only the user's WebID, because indexes hold no class names"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Each registration names a class with `solid:forClass`."@en .

<#q-difficulties-7b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In a Solid pod, Bob may read the container `/alice/shared/` but none of the documents in it. What can its listing still tell him?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Each member's name, and often its size and last change"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Read on a container shows its members' URLs and, where the server adds them, their `stat:size` and `dcterms:modified`, so names and activity leak even when the contents stay closed."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-7b-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-7b-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-7b-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-7b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing, because he may not read any of the members"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The listing is a resource of its own; Read on the container is enough to see it."@en .

<#q-difficulties-7b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The full contents of every member, inlined into it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A listing carries membership and some metadata, never the contents."@en .

<#q-difficulties-7b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only how many members there are, without their names"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Each `ldp:contains` triple names a member's URL."@en .

<#ch-difficulties-8>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-difficulties> ;
    schema:position 7 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Law and governance

Under the GDPR, a controller decides the purposes and means of processing personal data; a processor only acts on its behalf. Solid splits the classic single company into a pod provider, app developers and the user. So who is the controller?

Pandit (2023), a GDPR researcher, concludes that users who run their own pod server are controllers for storing their data; on a hosted pod they do not control, the provider is. For apps, choosing and revoking access "cannot be sufficient" to make users the controllers, because "the determination of purpose would typically be performed by the apps". The specs also give apps no way to declare who legally operates them. So Solid does not make an app GDPR-compliant; the Flanders deployment reached compliance through governance bodies, not technology alone.

Solid's own governance is young: the Open Data Institute (ODI) has been its steward only since October 2024, before which the community had no legal body. Inrupt, co-founded by Tim Berners-Lee, is an influential vendor.

**Design or immaturity?** Both: the split of roles is by design, while the missing legal metadata and young governance are immaturity."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-difficulties-8a> ,
                         <#q-difficulties-8b> .

<#q-difficulties-8a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "An app stores users' fitness data in their Solid pods and decides what that data is used for. What is its GDPR role, according to Pandit (2023)?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It is still likely a controller, because it decides the purposes"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Pandit argues purpose is usually decided by the app, so the app likely remains a controller; Solid does not make it compliant by itself."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-8a-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-8a-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-8a-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-8a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It has no role, because the data never leaves the user's pod"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The app still processes personal data, and deciding purposes is what makes a controller."@en .

<#q-difficulties-8a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It is only a processor, because the user granted it access"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Pandit says granting and revoking access is not enough to make the user the controller."@en .

<#q-difficulties-8a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It is the data subject, because it acts on the user's behalf"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The data subject is the person the data is about, never the app."@en .

<#q-difficulties-8b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Inrupt has large weight in the Solid ecosystem, and its products add their own features. What does that mean for a Solid app builder?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Check whether a feature is in a CG spec or only in Inrupt's products"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The ODI stewards Solid and the W3C Solid Community Group publishes the specs; Inrupt is one influential vendor, so features like Access Grants are not portable Solid."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-8b-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-8b-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-8b-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-8b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Follow Inrupt's docs, since Inrupt approves every Solid spec version"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The specs belong to the W3C Solid Community Group; Inrupt is one participant."@en .

<#q-difficulties-8b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Avoid Inrupt's libraries, since they only work with Inrupt's servers"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Many of its libraries work with any server; only its extensions need ESS."@en .

<#q-difficulties-8b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Ignore the difference, since the ODI certifies every Inrupt feature"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The ODI is Solid's steward, not a certifier of vendor features."@en .

<#ch-difficulties-9>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-difficulties> ;
    schema:position 8 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Portability and adoption

Solid promises you can take your data elsewhere. In practice, the PDS (personal data store) Migrator project found that downloading data "is a far cry from actually moving it to somewhere else and have everything continue to work as before". Moving breaks things because documents contain absolute URLs, ACLs name the old URIs, and the WebID often lives on the same pod, so moving the pod moves your identity.

App builders can help. Prefer relative IRIs inside a pod's documents, so a document still makes sense at a new address. Never assume the WebID's host is the storage host.

Adoption raises harder questions. Platforms earn money by holding data, and pods remove that, so who pays for pods and why would large platforms adopt them? This is widely argued but has no authoritative answer. The deployments that exist are top-down: governments and enterprises such as Flanders' Athumi, with governance bodies.

**Design or immaturity?** Portability breaks partly by design (absolute URLs, WebID on the pod). Adoption is an open question about business models, not about the specs."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-difficulties-9a> ,
                         <#q-difficulties-9b> .

<#q-difficulties-9a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A user copies every file from Solid pod A to pod B. Why might their apps break?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Absolute URLs and ACL rules still point at pod A"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Data, ACLs and often the WebID reference the old location, so a copy does not \"continue to work as before\"."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-9a-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-9a-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-9a-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-9a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Pod B cannot store RDF that another server created"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "RDF documents are portable; the problem is the URLs inside them."@en .

<#q-difficulties-9a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Copying files deletes their type registrations on pod A"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Copying does not change the source pod at all."@en .

<#q-difficulties-9a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Pod B stores the copies as non-RDF, so apps cannot parse them"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A proper copy keeps each file's content type; the URLs are the issue."@en .

<#q-difficulties-9b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Which pattern describes Solid adoption as of 2026?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Top-down deployments by governments and enterprises"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Real deployments such as Flanders' Athumi run with governance bodies behind them; consumer adoption is still an open question."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-9b-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-9b-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-9b-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-9b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Mass sign-ups by consumers choosing their own pod provider"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Practitioners such as Leigh Dodds (2024) still doubt what Solid solves for users; live deployments come from institutions."@en .

<#q-difficulties-9b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Large social platforms moving their user data into pods"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "No major platform has done so, and business models are an open question."@en .

<#q-difficulties-9b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Research projects only, with no live deployments"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Flanders' Athumi, for example, is a live government deployment."@en .

<#q-difficulties-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Which of these Solid problems comes from Solid's design rather than from the specs' draft status?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Reading many small documents takes one request each"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The N+1 fetch pattern comes from the document-centric design with no query, not from the specs' draft status."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-r01-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Solid-OIDC 0.1.0 lacks the RFC 9207 `iss` check"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A 2026 draft already adds it; that is draft status, not design."@en .

<#q-difficulties-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The Solid specs carry no W3C endorsement"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Endorsement is exactly what a Recommendation would add."@en .

<#q-difficulties-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Servers answer the same PATCH in different ways"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Those are uneven implementations of optional rules, not a limit of the model."@en .

<#q-difficulties-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app keeps a summary document in the pod to avoid N+1 fetches. Which new risk does that add?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The summary and the data can disagree if one write fails"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Writes that span documents are not atomic, so a summary can fall out of step with the data and the app must repair it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-r02-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "None, because the server keeps the summary in sync automatically"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Servers do not know your summary vocabulary; only your app updates it."@en .

<#q-difficulties-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Reading the summary still costs N requests, as the server expands it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "It is one ordinary document, fetched with one request."@en .

<#q-difficulties-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "None, because PATCHes to the summary and an item are atomic together"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "PATCH is atomic only per document, not across two."@en .

<#q-difficulties-r03>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app must run on CSS, ESS and Solid-Nextcloud. Which approach copes best with Solid's fragmentation and server differences?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Detect features from headers and test on each server"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Portable apps discover capabilities from headers, handle WAC and ACP, and test on every target server."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-r03-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-r03-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-r03-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-r03-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Target the newest editor's drafts, which every server follows"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Servers implement different versions; drafts run ahead of most of them."@en .

<#q-difficulties-r03-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Use one vendor's extensions everywhere to hide the differences"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Extensions such as Access Grants only work where the vendor's service runs."@en .

<#q-difficulties-r03-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Rely on status codes alone, since all servers return the same"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Recorded deviations include servers giving success codes for failed or wrong writes."@en .

<#q-difficulties-r04>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid health app registers its data in the public type index and stores it under `/health/hiv-clinic/`. Which risk does Solid's access control not prevent?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Strangers learning what data exists from the index entry and path"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Access rules protect contents, but public index entries and descriptive paths reveal data categories on their own."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-r04-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-r04-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-r04-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-r04-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Strangers reading the records inside the private container"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The ACL still protects the documents' contents."@en .

<#q-difficulties-r04-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The app being unable to find its own data on the next login"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A public registration is easy to find; that is the problem."@en .

<#q-difficulties-r04-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The pod refusing to store health data without a GDPR consent record"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Pods do not check legal grounds; that is the app's and controller's job."@en .

<#q-difficulties-r05>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid app lists a container with 50,000 members on a server that follows Solid Protocol 0.11 (2024). What does it get back?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The whole listing in one response, with no paging"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Solid 0.11 has no query and no paging, so a client cannot ask for part of a big container, and each member still needs its own GET."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-difficulties-r05-d1> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-r05-d2> ,
                          <#q-difficulties-r05-d3> .

<#q-difficulties-r05-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The first page, with a `next` link to fetch the rest of the members"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Paging links come from the LWS drafts, not Solid 0.11."@en .

<#q-difficulties-r05-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An error, until the app asks for a smaller range of members"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid 0.11 defines no range or limit for container listings."@en .

<#q-difficulties-r05-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Each member's full contents, inlined into the container listing"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A listing carries membership and some metadata, never the contents."@en .

# Chapter 17: The road ahead: Linked Web Storage.
<#ch-lws>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 16 ;
    dcterms:title "The road ahead: Linked Web Storage"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Where Solid is heading: the W3C **Linked Web Storage** (LWS) Working Drafts as of October 2026, what they change, and how to write Solid code today that will carry over."@en ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-lws-r01> ,
                              <#q-lws-r02> ,
                              <#q-lws-r03> ,
                              <#q-lws-r04> ,
                              <#q-lws-r05> .

<#ch-lws-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-lws> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## The LWS Working Group

No Solid specification is a W3C standard. In September 2024 the W3C membership approved a charter for a **Working Group** to put Solid's ideas on the standards track. It is called **Linked Web Storage (LWS)**, not "Solid". Its mission is to "enable the development of web applications where data storage, entity authentication, access control, and application provider are all loosely coupled".

The group does not start from scratch: the **Solid Protocol v0.11.0** is one of its two input documents, next to the Fedora API 1.0, and the draft "draws heavily" from it, though LWS changes terms and mechanisms. It must produce the **Linked Web Storage Protocol 1.0**, plus a test suite, an implementation report and a threat model.

Two charter rules matter to app builders:

- **Two implementations per feature.** A spec can only leave Candidate Recommendation with "at least two independent interoperable implementations of every feature". That targets the "works on my pod" problem.
- **Scope limits.** New identity mechanisms and new data formats are out of scope. LWS reuses existing web standards rather than inventing a new stack.

This chapter describes Working Drafts as of October 2026. Names, media types and rules may change in any later draft."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-lws-1a> ,
                         <#q-lws-1b> .

<#q-lws-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "You know the Solid Protocol 0.11 well. How much of it will help you read the W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) drafts?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Most of it: LWS starts from Solid 0.11 and reworks parts."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "LWS takes Solid Protocol v0.11.0 as an input document, draws heavily from it, and turns its ideas into a standards-track protocol, with its own design changes."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-1a-d3> .

<#q-lws-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "None of it: LWS is a rival project with separate roots."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The charter names Solid Protocol v0.11.0 as an input document, and the draft \"draws heavily\" from it."@en .

<#q-lws-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "All of it: LWS republishes Solid 0.11 unchanged as a standard."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "LWS changes terms and mechanisms; it is not a renamed copy of Solid 0.11."@en .

<#q-lws-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Little of it: LWS swaps RDF for a brand-new data format."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "New data formats are explicitly out of the group's scope."@en .

<#q-lws-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "The W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) charter requires \"two independent interoperable implementations of every feature\". Why should that matter to a Solid app developer?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Features must work alike on separate servers."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Requiring two independent, interoperable implementations, checked by test suites, is aimed directly at the server inconsistency that troubles Solid today."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-1b-d3> .

<#q-lws-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It forces every user to keep two pods as a backup."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The rule is about server implementations of the spec, not about how many pods a user has."@en .

<#q-lws-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It lets each vendor define a feature in its own way."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The opposite: the implementations must be interoperable, verified by open test suites."@en .

<#q-lws-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It means apps must ship two versions of client code."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The rule applies to servers passing the spec's tests, so one client should work against both."@en .

<#ch-lws-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-lws> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Timeline and status

Standards take time, and LWS has slipped. The charter planned a First Public Working Draft (FPWD) for March 2025 and a Recommendation for September 2026. The real FPWD of LWS Protocol 1.0 came on **31 March 2026**. Working Drafts have followed every few weeks; as of October 2026 the latest is dated **5 October 2026**, the 14th Working Draft since the FPWD.

The charter, first set to end on 8 September 2026, was extended to **8 January 2027** while a new charter is prepared. W3C members had not yet voted on it in October 2026.

Every LWS document is a **Working Draft**. Its status section even says "This is an unofficial proposal", and a draft may be "updated, replaced, or obsoleted". So:

- Servers and libraries you use today implement the Solid Protocol 0.11 stack.
- LWS tells you the *direction*, not the exact syntax you will ship against."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-lws-2a> ,
                         <#q-lws-2b> .

<#q-lws-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "It is October 2026. A colleague wants to rewrite your Solid app to use only the W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) Protocol. What is the best advice?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Keep building on Solid 0.11 and track the LWS drafts."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "LWS is a moving Working Draft, while today's servers and libraries implement the Solid Protocol 0.11 stack, so you build on that and watch LWS."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-2a-d3> .

<#q-lws-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Switch now, because LWS is a W3C Recommendation."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "LWS Protocol 1.0 is still a Working Draft, not a Recommendation."@en .

<#q-lws-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Wait, because LWS will be a Recommendation by late 2026."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The September 2026 target has slipped: LWS is still a Working Draft and its charter was extended."@en .

<#q-lws-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Switch now, because drafts are frozen once published."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A Working Draft can be updated, replaced or obsoleted, and LWS has changed many times."@en .

<#q-lws-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "In 2026 the W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) Working Group's charter, which had planned a Recommendation for September 2026, was extended while a new one is prepared. What does that tell you when you plan a Solid app?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The schedule slipped, so expect more draft changes before a standard."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The extension shows the work needs more time, so the drafts will keep changing while you build on the Solid Protocol 0.11 stack."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-2b-d3> .

<#q-lws-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "LWS is final, because a charter is only extended once the spec is done."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "An extension gives the group more time; LWS Protocol 1.0 is still a Working Draft."@en .

<#q-lws-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "LWS is being abandoned, because the group is about to close."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The group was extended, not closed, and still publishes Working Drafts."@en .

<#q-lws-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Write no Solid code until the members vote on the new charter."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Today's servers and libraries implement Solid 0.11, whatever the vote decides."@en .

<#ch-lws-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-lws> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Storage and containers in LWS

LWS says **storage** where Solid says pod: a **storage root** holding **containers** and **data resources**. Its terms live under `https://www.w3.org/ns/lws#`.

The biggest change: **URL structure is not containment**. "Clients SHOULD NOT assume that URI structure reflects containment". A resource names its parent with a `rel="up"` Link header, and every GET and HEAD response also points to its storage:

```http
Link: </alice/notes/>; rel="up"
Link: <https://storage.example/>; rel="https://www.w3.org/ns/lws#storage"
```

A container lists its children in `items`, as JSON-LD (`application/lws+json`); abridged from the draft:

```json
{ "@context": "https://www.w3.org/ns/lws/v1",
  "id": "/alice/notes/", "type": "Container", "totalItems": 1,
  "items": [{ "type": "DataResource", "id": "/alice/notes/todo.json",
    "format": "application/json", "size": 2048,
    "modified": "2025-11-24T13:00:00Z" }] }
```

Each item MUST have an `id` and a `type`, a data resource MUST have a `format`, and `size` and `modified` are SHOULD. Solid 0.11 listings SHOULD carry similar metadata as RDF (`stat:size`, `dcterms:modified`); LWS gives it a fixed JSON shape. What is new is **paging**: servers SHOULD split a large listing with `first`/`next`/`prev`/`last` links.

The Solid **storage description** becomes a W3C **Controlled Identifier (CID)** document (`application/lws+cid`) listing a `StorageRoot` service. A CID document describes an identifier: the keys that may sign for it and the services it uses, like a generalised WebID profile."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-lws-3a> ,
                         <#q-lws-3b> .

<#q-lws-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In the W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) drafts (October 2026), how should a client find the parent container of `https://storage.example/a/b/c`?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Read the response's `rel=\"up\"` Link header."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Containment in LWS is expressed by links, `rel=\"up\"` on the child and `items` in the container, not by the shape of the URL."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-3a-d3> .

<#q-lws-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Strip the last path segment from the URL."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "LWS says clients should not assume URI structure reflects containment."@en .

<#q-lws-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Look up the URL in the storage description."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The storage description advertises the storage root and services, not each resource's parent."@en .

<#q-lws-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Read the `ldp:contains` triples of `/a/b/`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "LWS listings use `items` in `application/lws+json`, and guessing `/a/b/` is itself the URL-structure assumption."@en .

<#q-lws-3b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "What does a container listing in the W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) drafts (October 2026) add that a Solid Protocol 0.11 listing lacks?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Paging links (`first`, `next`) for large containers."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Solid listings already SHOULD carry per-item metadata as RDF; LWS gives it a fixed JSON shape and adds paging. It is still not query: each item needs its own GET."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-3b-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-3b-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-3b-d3> .

<#q-lws-3b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The full contents of every contained resource."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A listing still only describes the items; you GET each one to read it."@en .

<#q-lws-3b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A query endpoint for searching inside the listed items."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "LWS defines no query mechanism; the metadata only helps you choose what to fetch."@en .

<#q-lws-3b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The access rules that apply to each listed item."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A listing carries no access rules; access goes through tokens and grants."@en .

<#ch-lws-4>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-lws> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Metadata as linksets

In the Solid Protocol 0.11 you find a resource's **description resource** through `rel="describedby"` and edit it as RDF. LWS replaces it with a **linkset** (RFC 9264): a JSON document that lists typed links about one resource, served as `application/linkset+json`. A response points to it with a Link header:

```http
Link: </alice/notes/shoppinglist.txt.meta>; rel="linkset"; type="application/linkset+json"
```

The linkset itself looks like this (from the draft's example):

```json
{ "linkset": [ { "anchor": "/alice/personalinfo.json",
    "describedby": [ { "href": "/schemas/personal-info.json" } ] } ] }
```

The draft's examples put linksets at `.meta` URLs, but nothing guarantees that shape: the only promise is the `rel="linkset"` link. Writing to a linkset (a "metadata update") changes the links, not the resource itself. Servers MUST accept JSON Patch for that and advertise it in `Accept-Patch` (Step 17.5 explains JSON Patch)."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-lws-4a> ,
                         <#q-lws-4b> .

<#q-lws-4a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "One W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) server (drafts, October 2026) answers with `Link: <…/todo.json.meta>; rel=\"linkset\"`. How should your app find a resource's linkset on a different LWS server?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Read the `rel=\"linkset\"` Link header that server sends."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A linkset URL is discovered, not built: follow `rel=\"linkset\"` on each response, whatever shape the URL has."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-4a-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-4a-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-4a-d3> .

<#q-lws-4a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Append `.meta`, since every LWS server uses that suffix."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The draft's examples use `.meta`, but LWS only guarantees the link relation, not the URL shape."@en .

<#q-lws-4a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Follow `rel=\"describedby\"` and parse the Turtle it returns."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is Solid 0.11's description resource; LWS metadata is a JSON linkset."@en .

<#q-lws-4a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Look up each resource's linkset in the storage description."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The storage description lists the storage root and services, not per-resource metadata."@en .

<#q-lws-4b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "What is a linkset in the W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) drafts (October 2026)?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A JSON document of typed links about one resource."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A linkset (RFC 9264) collects the links about a resource, such as `describedby`, in one JSON document found through `rel=\"linkset\"`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-4b-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-4b-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-4b-d3> .

<#q-lws-4b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A Turtle document of triples that describes the resource."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is Solid 0.11's description resource; a linkset is `application/linkset+json`."@en .

<#q-lws-4b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A list of every resource stored inside one container."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is the container listing and its `items`."@en .

<#q-lws-4b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An access policy saying who may read the resource."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "LWS defines no ACL files; access goes through tokens and grants."@en .

<#ch-lws-5>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-lws> ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Patch formats

Solid 0.11 requires N3 Patch. An LWS server "MUST minimally support JSON Patch (`application/json-patch+json`)" (RFC 6902). A JSON Patch is a list of operations (`add`, `remove`, `replace` and others) on a JSON document. Each `path` is a JSON Pointer into it: `/done` means the `done` key at the top level. So it fits JSON resources and linksets, not Turtle:

```http
PATCH /alice/notes/todo.json HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json-patch+json
If-Match: "v7"

[{ "op": "replace", "path": "/done", "value": true }]
```

Other formats are optional. The draft's example storage description lists SPARQL Update as a patch capability for Turtle, and clients SHOULD NOT assume a patch format the server does not advertise. Where no advertised format fits, a PUT of the whole new content still works; the draft's own PUT example sends `If-Match`, and clients SHOULD use conditional requests."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-lws-5a> ,
                         <#q-lws-5b> .

<#q-lws-5a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "You want to update `/alice/notes/todo.json` on a W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) server (drafts, October 2026). Its `Accept-Patch` lists only `application/json-patch+json`, and your code supports N3 Patch and JSON Patch. What should it send?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A JSON Patch, the format the server advertises."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Pick the patch format from what the server advertises; under LWS, JSON Patch is the one every server must support, and it fits a JSON resource."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-5a-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-5a-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-5a-d3> .

<#q-lws-5a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An N3 Patch, since every server must accept it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "N3 Patch is a Solid 0.11 requirement; LWS only requires JSON Patch."@en .

<#q-lws-5a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A SPARQL Update, since most Solid servers accept it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "LWS servers need not accept it, and this one does not advertise it."@en .

<#q-lws-5a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A DELETE, then a POST of the new version."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That loses the resource's URL; a supported PATCH, or a conditional PUT, is the right tool."@en .

<#q-lws-5b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "For the Turtle document `/alice/notes/list.ttl`, a W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) server (drafts, October 2026) advertises only JSON Patch. How should your app change one triple in it?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "PUT the whole edited document with `If-Match`."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "JSON Patch fits JSON, not Turtle. A conditional PUT of the document as read plus the edit keeps other apps' triples and cannot overwrite a change you have not seen."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-5b-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-5b-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-5b-d3> .

<#q-lws-5b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Send a JSON Patch whose `path` names the triple."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A JSON Pointer addresses keys in a JSON document; Turtle has none."@en .

<#q-lws-5b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Send an N3 Patch, which every LWS server accepts."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "LWS requires only JSON Patch; N3 Patch works only where advertised."@en .

<#q-lws-5b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Convert it to JSON-LD, then send a JSON Patch."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That changes the format every other app reads; a conditional PUT keeps the document Turtle."@en .

<#ch-lws-6>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-lws> ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## ETags, recursive delete and quotas

**ETags.** Solid 0.11 says servers MAY send an `ETag`. LWS says servers MUST send one on GET and HEAD responses. Clients SHOULD use conditional requests such as `If-Match` for writes, and a request whose precondition fails gets `412 Precondition Failed`. Rules still move: from the FPWD (March 2026) to 21 August 2026 the drafts answered `428 Precondition Required` to an unconditional PUT on a resource with ETags and to linkset writes without `If-Match`; the September 2026 drafts dropped both rules.

**Recursive delete.** Solid answers `409` when you delete a non-empty container, so you delete children first. LWS keeps that `409` by default, but a server MAY support recursive delete, which a client requests with the `Depth: infinity` header from WebDAV (RFC 4918):

```http
DELETE /alice/notes/ HTTP/1.1
Depth: infinity
```

**Quotas.** On CSS 7.2 a write past a pod quota gets `413` and stores nothing; the LWS draft's operations overview names `507 Insufficient Storage` for quota exceeded. Treat both alike."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-lws-6a> ,
                         <#q-lws-6b> .

<#q-lws-6a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Your Solid app always sends `If-Match` with the last `ETag` it saw. Why does this habit fit the W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) drafts (October 2026) better than the Solid Protocol 0.11?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "LWS servers must send an `ETag` on every read."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "In Solid 0.11 an `ETag` is a MAY, so `If-Match` only helps on servers that send one; LWS makes the `ETag` mandatory, so `If-Match` protects against lost updates on every LWS server."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-6a-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-6a-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-6a-d3> .

<#q-lws-6a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "LWS rejects any write sent without `If-Match`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Sending `If-Match` is a SHOULD; the earlier drafts' 428 rule for unconditional PUTs was dropped in September 2026."@en .

<#q-lws-6a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Solid 0.11 servers reject `If-Match` headers."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Solid encourages conditional requests; it just doesn't require servers to send ETags."@en .

<#q-lws-6a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "LWS uses ETags to carry the access token."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "ETags identify a version of a resource; they have nothing to do with authorization."@en .

<#q-lws-6b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Your app must delete `/alice/old-project/`, a container that still holds files, on a W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) server (drafts, October 2026). What should the client do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Try `Depth: infinity`; if refused, delete children first."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "LWS keeps Solid's `409` for non-empty containers unless the client asks for recursion with `Depth: infinity`, which servers MAY support; a portable client falls back to deleting the children first."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-6b-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-6b-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-6b-d3> .

<#q-lws-6b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Send a plain `DELETE`; LWS then removes the contents too."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Without `Depth: infinity` the server must refuse a non-empty container with `409`."@en .

<#q-lws-6b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Send `Depth: infinity`; every LWS server must support it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Recursive delete is a MAY, so a client must cope with a server that refuses."@en .

<#q-lws-6b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Send `Depth: 1`; repeat it to delete one level at a time."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "LWS defines recursive delete only through `Depth: infinity`."@en .

<#ch-lws-7>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-lws> ;
    schema:position 6 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Authentication suites

Solid 0.11 has one login method: Solid-OIDC with a WebID. LWS builds identity on the **CID document**: your identifier dereferences to a document that lists your keys and the services you trust. Authentication is split into **suites**, each its own draft:

- **OpenID Connect.** The user's identifier goes in the standard **`sub`** claim, not a `webid` claim. It must resolve to a CID document, and the issuer is trusted when that document has a `service` of type `https://www.w3.org/ns/lws#OpenIdProvider` whose endpoint equals the token's `iss`. This replaces `solid:oidcIssuer`, but the idea is the same: dereference the identifier to learn whom to trust.
- **SAML 2.0**, the XML-based single sign-on standard common in enterprises. Like OpenID Connect, it needs an identity provider.
- **Self-signed identity** with a CID. A bot or server signs its own JWT with `sub` = `iss` = `client_id` = its own URI, and its CID document publishes the key. **No identity provider is needed**.

The suites move too: they were first published as drafts on 23 April 2026, a `did:key` suite among them, which was discontinued on 29 September 2026. Whatever the suite, the credential is not sent to the storage directly; it is exchanged for an access token (Step 17.8)."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-lws-7a> ,
                         <#q-lws-7b> .

<#q-lws-7a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In the OpenID Connect suite of the W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) drafts (October 2026), where does the user's identifier appear in the ID token?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "In the `sub` claim."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "LWS puts the subject identifier in `sub` and verifies the issuer through a `service` entry in the identifier's CID document."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-7a-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-7a-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-7a-d3> .

<#q-lws-7a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In a custom `webid` claim."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is Solid-OIDC; the LWS suite says the ID token must use `sub`."@en .

<#q-lws-7a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In the `aud` claim."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`aud` names the intended audiences, such as the client, not the user."@en .

<#q-lws-7a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In the `iss` claim."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "`iss` identifies the OpenID provider that issued the token."@en .

<#q-lws-7b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A nightly backup bot has its own identifier and no account at any identity provider. Which authentication suite of the W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) drafts (October 2026) lets it authenticate?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The self-signed suite with its own CID."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "With self-signed identity, the agent signs its own JWT and publishes its key in its CID document, so no identity provider is involved."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-7b-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-7b-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-7b-d3> .

<#q-lws-7b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The OpenID Connect suite via the owner's provider."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "This suite needs an identity provider to issue the ID token, and the bot has none."@en .

<#q-lws-7b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The SAML 2.0 suite via a company login."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "SAML also needs an identity provider, and the bot has none."@en .

<#q-lws-7b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No suite, because bots need no authentication."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The storage still needs to know who is asking before granting access."@en .

<#ch-lws-8>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-lws> ;
    schema:position 7 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Access tokens

How does an app get to read a protected resource? Through an **authorization server**, a service that hands out access tokens for the storage:

1. The app requests the resource. The storage answers `401`, and its `WWW-Authenticate` header names the authorization server (`as_uri`). That server's metadata lives at `/.well-known/lws-configuration`.
2. The app sends its credential (an ID token or a self-signed JWT) to the authorization server using **OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange** (RFC 8693).
3. The authorization server returns an **access token**: a short-lived JWT (300 seconds or less is recommended) with a single `aud`, that storage.
4. The app retries the request with the access token.

The token carries `sub`, `iss`, `aud`, `exp` and `client_id`. Solid-OIDC already passes the client ID to the pod, but Web Access Control (WAC) cannot use it and only Access Control Policy (ACP) can. LWS makes `client_id` a **required** claim in every storage token, so whatever enforcement layer a server uses can rely on it."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-lws-8a> ,
                         <#q-lws-8b> .

<#q-lws-8a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "In Solid 0.11, Solid-OIDC already passes the client ID to the pod. What do the W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) drafts (October 2026) add by making `client_id` a required access-token claim?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Any enforcement layer can count on knowing the app."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "In Solid 0.11 the client ID reaches the pod but only ACP can match on it; LWS requires it in every access token, so any enforcement layer, not just ACP, can restrict by app."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-8a-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-8a-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-8a-d3> .

<#q-lws-8a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The user can log in without any identity provider."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is the self-signed identity suite; `client_id` identifies the app."@en .

<#q-lws-8a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The token becomes valid on every storage the user has."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The token has a single audience, one storage."@en .

<#q-lws-8a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The app's identity replaces the user's identity in it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The token still carries the user in `sub`; `client_id` adds the app."@en .

<#q-lws-8b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) access token (drafts, October 2026) was issued for `https://storage-a.example/`. What happens if the app sends it to `https://storage-b.example/`?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Storage B rejects it, since it names another audience."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Each LWS access token has a single audience, so an app needs a separate token exchange for every storage it talks to."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-8b-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-8b-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-8b-d3> .

<#q-lws-8b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Storage B accepts it, since the same user signed in."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The `aud` claim limits the token to storage A, whoever the user is."@en .

<#q-lws-8b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Storage B accepts it until its 300 seconds run out."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The lifetime limits when a token works, the audience limits where."@en .

<#q-lws-8b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Storage B forwards it to storage A to have it checked."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Each storage validates the tokens it receives; nothing is forwarded."@en .

<#ch-lws-9>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-lws> ;
    schema:position 8 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Access requests and grants

An app that lacks access can ask for it. An **AccessRequest** is "a request by an agent for access to resources"; an **AccessGrant** is "an authorization by a storage controller granting access to resources". The storage description names the endpoints for both (`AccessRequestService`, `AccessGrantService`). So the app asks, and the storage's controller, not the app, issues the grant.

Their contents follow an **ODRL-based profile**. ODRL is a W3C vocabulary for permissions with conditions: who may do what, for which purpose, until when. An access entry has a type (`AccessPolicy`), actions (`read`, `modify`, `create`, `delete`), an `assignee`, a `target` and constraints. A constraint compares a `leftOperand` (such as `purpose`, `client` or `dateTime`) to a `rightOperand` (the allowed value). A grant, abridged from the draft's example:

```json
{ "@context": ["https://www.w3.org/ns/lws/v1"],
  "type": ["AccessGrant"], "storage": "https://storage.example/",
  "access": [{ "type": ["AccessPolicy"], "action": ["read"],
    "assignee": "https://id.example/agent",
    "target": { "type": "StorageResource",
                "value": ["https://storage.example/root/projects/"] },
    "constraint": [{ "leftOperand": "purpose", "operator": "eq",
      "rightOperand": "https://purpose.example/collaboration" }] }] }
```

LWS does **not** mandate WAC or ACP; how grants map onto an enforcement layer is out of scope."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-lws-9a> ,
                         <#q-lws-9b> .

<#q-lws-9a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Your Solid server implements Web Access Control (WAC). Do the W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) drafts (October 2026) require you to replace it with Access Control Policy (ACP)?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "No, LWS mandates neither WAC nor ACP."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "LWS standardises tokens and access requests and grants, but leaves how a server enforces them (WAC, ACP or something else) to the implementation."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-9a-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-9a-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-9a-d3> .

<#q-lws-9a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, LWS standardises ACP as the only model."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The draft deliberately leaves the enforcement layer out of scope."@en .

<#q-lws-9a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, WAC is forbidden because it lacks deny rules."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "LWS doesn't forbid any access control language."@en .

<#q-lws-9a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, LWS requires WAC on every server."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "LWS doesn't mandate WAC either; the charter only lists WAC and ACP as candidate inputs."@en .

<#q-lws-9b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front """In this W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) access grant (draft, October 2026), what does the `purpose` constraint express?

```json
{ "type": ["AccessGrant"],
  "access": [{ "type": ["AccessPolicy"], "action": ["read"],
    "assignee": "https://id.example/agent",
    "target": { "value": ["https://storage.example/root/projects/"] },
    "constraint": [{ "leftOperand": "purpose", "operator": "eq",
      "rightOperand": "https://purpose.example/collaboration" }] }] }
```"""@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Read access holds only for collaboration use."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A constraint compares a `leftOperand` (`purpose`) to a `rightOperand` (the collaboration purpose), narrowing the `read` grant to that use."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-9b-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-9b-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-9b-d3> .

<#q-lws-9b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The agent may also modify the shared projects."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The only action granted is `read`; a constraint narrows access, it never adds actions."@en .

<#q-lws-9b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Every app may read the projects for any reason."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The grant names one `assignee`, and the constraint limits the purpose."@en .

<#q-lws-9b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The grant expires once the collaboration ends."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "An end date would be a `dateTime` constraint; `purpose` says what use is allowed."@en .

<#ch-lws-10>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-lws> ;
    schema:position 9 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## What carries over and what doesn't

LWS renames and reworks a lot, but the core ideas of Solid stay:

- **Decoupling.** Storage, identity, access control and apps can each come from a different provider.
- **URI identity.** You are a URI that dereferences to a document listing whom to trust (WebID profile today, CID document in LWS).
- **Permissioned access.** Data stays private unless access is granted.
- **Following links.** Clients discover storage, parents, metadata and capabilities through Link headers and descriptions.

Some of Solid's open problems stay open: LWS defines **no query**; **shapes and schema drift** are untouched because data formats are out of scope; **business models** and **GDPR roles** are not protocol questions.

The habit that transfers best: **never hard-code URL patterns**. Find the storage from a Link header, the metadata from `describedby` (later `linkset`), the patch format from `Accept-Patch`, and the parent from links rather than path cutting. Code written that way needs fewer changes when LWS lands."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-lws-10a> ,
                         <#q-lws-10b> .

<#q-lws-10a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Which coding habit in a Solid 0.11 app will carry over best to the W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) protocol (drafts, October 2026)?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Read URLs and formats from links and headers."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Code that follows links and reads what a server announces keeps working when the details change, and those details are exactly what differs between Solid 0.11 and LWS."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-10a-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-10a-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-10a-d3> .

<#q-lws-10a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Build `.acl` and `.meta` URLs by adding suffixes."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Those suffixes are only a server convention even today; LWS moves metadata to linksets and defines no ACL files."@en .

<#q-lws-10a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Treat each URL path segment as a container."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "LWS says URI structure should not be assumed to reflect containment."@en .

<#q-lws-10a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Require N3 Patch for every update the app makes."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "LWS only guarantees JSON Patch, so the format should come from what the server advertises."@en .

<#q-lws-10b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A manager hopes the W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) protocol (drafts, October 2026) will make your Solid app GDPR-compliant once it is a standard. What is the accurate reply?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "No, GDPR roles lie outside a storage protocol."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "LWS standardises storage, authentication and authorization; GDPR roles, like business models, remain outside it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-10b-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-10b-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-10b-d3> .

<#q-lws-10b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, because LWS grants cannot state a purpose."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Grants can carry a `purpose` constraint, but that still doesn't settle GDPR roles."@en .

<#q-lws-10b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, LWS access grants make the app a processor only."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Apps that decide purposes are still likely controllers, and a protocol can't change that."@en .

<#q-lws-10b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, because data never leaves the user's storage."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Apps still read and process the data, and GDPR roles follow who decides the purposes."@en .

<#q-lws-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A Solid 0.11 app finds a resource's parent by cutting the last path segment, and its metadata by appending `.meta`. Which two changes in the W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) drafts (October 2026) make these tricks unsafe?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Containment via `rel=\"up\"`, and linksets found by link."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Both tricks were only conventions even in Solid 0.11. LWS says clients SHOULD NOT assume URL containment and gives the linkset URL only through `rel=\"linkset\"`."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-r01-d3> .

<#q-lws-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "JSON Patch for writes, and ETags required on every read."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "These affect how the app writes, not how it finds parents or metadata."@en .

<#q-lws-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The `sub` claim, and OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "These are authentication changes; they don't touch URL discovery."@en .

<#q-lws-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Access grants, and the two-implementation rule."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Neither changes how resource URLs relate to each other."@en .

<#q-lws-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Which pair best shows the W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) drafts (October 2026) keeping a Solid 0.11 idea while changing its mechanism?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The WebID's issuer triple becomes a CID service entry."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "In both cases you dereference the user's identifier to find which issuer to trust; only the document type and the term change."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-r02-d3> .

<#q-lws-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "`ldp:contains` becomes `items`, still derived from paths."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "LWS decouples containment from URL paths, so the idea changes too, not only the name."@en .

<#q-lws-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "N3 Patch becomes SPARQL Update as the required format."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "LWS requires JSON Patch, not SPARQL Update."@en .

<#q-lws-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Access control moves from the server into each app."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The storage still enforces access, using tokens and grants."@en .

<#q-lws-r03>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Which known difficulty of Solid 0.11 is the W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) effort (drafts, October 2026) designed to reduce, rather than leave open?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Servers behaving differently for one feature."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The charter's two-interoperable-implementations rule and open test suites target server divergence; query, shapes and business models remain open."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-r03-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-r03-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-r03-d3> .

<#q-lws-r03-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The lack of any query mechanism in the protocol."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "LWS defines no query; listings only add metadata and paging."@en .

<#q-lws-r03-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Schema drift between different apps' data shapes."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Data formats are out of the charter's scope."@en .

<#q-lws-r03-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Unclear business models for storage providers."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is an economic question no protocol settles."@en .

<#q-lws-r04>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Which sequence matches the W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) drafts (October 2026) for an app reading a protected resource?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "401, token exchange at the authorization server, retry."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The storage's `401` names the authorization server; the client trades its credential via Token Exchange for a short-lived, storage-specific access token and retries."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-r04-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-r04-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-r04-d3> .

<#q-lws-r04-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Send the ID token straight to the storage on every request."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "LWS exchanges credentials at an authorization server for an access token first."@en .

<#q-lws-r04-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Read the resource's ACL, then fetch with the user's password."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "LWS doesn't use passwords at the storage or mandate ACL files."@en .

<#q-lws-r04-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Get one access token, then reuse it on every storage you use."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Access tokens have a single audience, one storage."@en .

<#q-lws-r05>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Solid Memo, a Solid flashcard app, follows the type index to its decks, never guesses a deck's path, and saves edits to Turtle documents as SPARQL Update deltas sent with `If-Match`. Which part would need the most change under the W3C Linked Web Storage (LWS) drafts (October 2026)?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Saving edits as SPARQL Update deltas to Turtle documents."@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "LWS guarantees only JSON Patch, so SPARQL Update works only where the server advertises it; the app would need to check and fall back, for example to a conditional PUT of the document as read plus the edit. Its link-following and conditional writes carry over."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-lws-r05-d1> ,
                          <#q-lws-r05-d2> ,
                          <#q-lws-r05-d3> .

<#q-lws-r05-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Following the type index to find its decks."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Following links instead of guessing paths is the habit that carries over best."@en .

<#q-lws-r05-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Sending `If-Match` with the ETag each edit was read with."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "LWS makes ETags mandatory, so conditional writes work better, not worse."@en .

<#q-lws-r05-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Refusing to guess a deck's path from its name or date."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Not hard-coding URL patterns is exactly what LWS rewards."@en .

<#turtle>
    a dcat:Distribution ;
    dcat:accessURL <> ;
    dcat:downloadURL <> ;
    dcat:mediaType <https://www.iana.org/assignments/media-types/text/turtle> ;
    dcterms:format <http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/file-type/RDF_TURTLE> .
